window.FD_PAGES = [["Nonprofits doing business with their own officers.","Grants with little organization behind them.","Funded while suspended, revoked or failing audits.","The same cost covered by City and federal money.","What the top officers are paid.","Paid-for capacity standing empty.","Service counts from tax returns, year against year.","Stated counts against who actually lives there.","One number for the City, another for the regulator.","City money passed along a chain of organizations.","Federal audits are on file for 161 City-paid organizations (816 audit reports, 2019 to 2025). Auditors reported findings at 43 of them, and at 7 in three or more separate years; those 7 were paid $351 million by the City since FY2019. 22 of the 122 findings were marked as repeats of an earlier one, 30 were graded material weaknesses and 16 involved questioned costs. 3 parent organizations that file one audit covering several City payees (hospital systems among them) are listed last and left out of these counts.","25 findings at 12 organizations were on federal grants that the auditor listed as passed through the City and County of San Francisco.","5 audit reports of City-paid organizations include a going-concern paragraph and 6 carry a qualified opinion on the financial statements.","What organizations are funded for, set against limits somebody else wrote down: how many people a building is licensed to hold, how many attended, how many meals were claimed, how many staff are on the payroll, and what the federal inspector found. A mismatch is a question to put to the organization or its funder, not a finding.","10 City-paid organizations held 1,027 State-licensed residential beds at the start of 2022 and hold 968 now, while City payments to the same organizations went from $107.6 million to $147.4 million. 2 lost a tenth or more of their beds, 1 of them while City payments rose by a tenth or more; the largest loss is Catholic Charities: 77 beds to 32 (closed Leland Avenue Project (45, 2022-01)).","Since in-person rules returned in October 2022, San Francisco centres reported average daily attendance above licensed capacity in 1 of 542 centre-months. Before that, while services were delivered remotely, they did so in 41 months, in line with centres statewide. In 2026 the middle San Francisco centre ran at 69% of capacity against 69% statewide.","Of 410 City-paid nonprofits with payroll counts on their tax returns (276 also file a staff benefit-plan return), 13 saw City payments rise by a quarter or more while their payroll count fell by a tenth or more; the largest is Catholic Charities, where City payments went from $20.7 million to $33.4 million as the payroll count went from 602 to 537. 10 organizations paid $1 million or more a year have fewer than one person on payroll for every $250,000 of City money, led by Reality House West, Inc with 1 on payroll and $3.6 million from the City; the middle organization of that size receives $42,240 per person.","24 top-contractor listings point back toward the organization itself: 14 where the contractor carries the full name of someone the same return lists as an officer, director or key employee (or is registered to one), the rest sharing an uncommon surname or the organization’s own address. In 8 of the 14 the person is listed with no pay and the payment is not found on the insider-transactions schedule, so the contractor line is the only place it shows; the largest is Sunny Schwartz at Five Keys Schools And Programs ($207,262).","30 organizations qualify: 16 that get at least a quarter of their revenue from the City raised their top earner’s pay by at least a fifth while revenue or the number of people served fell, and 14 pay their top earner more than the typical City department head ($273,274 in fiscal 2025) while drawing at least half their revenue from the City.","The tax returns of 3,246 Bay Area and large California grantmakers show $2.10 billion paid in 2020 to 2025 to 442 organizations the City also pays, set here beside what those organizations reported on their own returns. Most overlaps are ordinary; the screens measure the overlap, the names on both sides, and whether the money fits inside the recipient's books.","Of the 100 nonprofits the City pays most, 6 received no grant at all from the 3,246 grantmakers on file in 2020 to 2025, and for 22 private grants were under 1% of City and private money combined; the City paid those 22 organizations $1.86 billion in fiscal 2020 to 2025. The largest with none: Richmond Area Multi-Services Inc ($163.1 million), Baker Places Inc ($109.9 million), The Yerba Buena Gardens Conservancy ($52.9 million), Providence Foundation of San Francisco ($38.3 million). The middle organization of the 100 drew 4.8% of the combined total from these funders.","10 of the 100 drew at least a quarter of their City-plus-private total from these funders, led by Tides Center ($227.6 million from 97 funders, 73%); San Francisco Symphony ($40.3 million from 108 funders, 60%); First Place For Youth ($18.5 million from 42 funders, 52%); Jewish Voc & Career Counseling Svc (Jvs) ($15.4 million from 15 funders, 48%). Across all 100 the City paid $6.33 billion and these funders paid $736.6 million, of which $227.6 million went to Tides Center, which a case file in this project describes as a fiscal sponsor.","959 grantmakers paid $2.10 billion to 442 City-paid organizations in tax periods ending 2020 to 2025; the ten largest account for $1.19 billion (57%). The largest: Silicon Valley Community Foundation $243.3 million to 231 organizations; Crankstart Foundation $238.3 million to 126 organizations; The San Francisco Foundation $196.9 million to 289 organizations; East Bay Community Foundation $99.3 million to 112 organizations. $745.2 million came through community foundations and other public-charity grantmakers, and $227.6 million of the total went to one recipient, Tides Center. Among the 60 largest funders, 13 sent more than half of their City-paid-organization giving to a single recipient.","367 organization-years across 135 organizations pair City payments ($883.7 million) with grants from two or more private funders ($235.8 million) for the same kind of work. In none of them do the City payments and private grants for that one purpose exceed the organization's whole reported spending for the tax year ending that year: on this test the totals fit (the tightest is Episcopal Community Svcs of SF Inc in 2022, where housing and homelessness money from both sides is 94% of spending). Counting every City payment and every private grant found, whatever the purpose, 3 of these organization-years exceed reported spending (1 of them already listed, for City payments alone, in the 'Do the books agree?' screens): Counterpulse 2021 ($1.4 million against $968,014 spent); The SF Housing Accelerator Fund 2023 ($5.6 million against $4.9 million spent).","Of 1,751 organization-years where both sides are on file, 2 show grants from these funders alone larger than every contribution and grant the recipient reported in that year and the years either side: San Francisco Public Health Foundation 2021 ($2.3 million paid by 4 funders, $203,030 reported, total revenue $1.5 million); African Advocacy Network 2021 ($154,000 paid by 3 funders, $112,461 reported, total revenue $800,916). A further 27 organization-years (19 organizations, $30.6 million) fit inside total contributions only if the private grants were entered on the return's government-grants line; the largest gaps are Heluna Health 2024 ($3.6 million in private grants, $371,286 reported outside the government line); Young Community Developers Inc 2021 ($2.7 million in private grants, $42,887 reported outside the government line); Main Street Launch 2022 ($2.5 million in private grants, $0 reported outside the government line).","74 funder-and-recipient pairs share at least one rare full name, and the funders in those pairs paid the recipients $39.3 million in 2020 to 2025, $33.9 million of it in years when the name was on both returns. The largest: The Ann & Gordon Getty Foundation for the Arts to San Francisco Symphony ($15.0 million; Gordon P Getty); Kenneth Rainin Foundation to Community Arts Stablization Trust ($4.0 million; Moy Eng; Eric Rodenbeck); Ann & Gordon Getty Foundation to San Francisco Symphony ($2.0 million; Gordon P Getty). In 5 pairs the shared name is a paid officer or employee of the recipient, and in 16 the recipient takes at least half of everything the funder gave to City-paid organizations; no pair shares three or more names.","9 foundation-and-official pairs share a rare name between a foundation's officers or trustees and a City Form 700 filer whose own department also pays an organization the foundation funded in the same years; those grants total $22.3 million. The largest by grant dollars: Tipping Point Community and a filer for Mayor Office of the ($14.2 million to 25 organizations that body's department pays); The San Francisco Foundation and a filer for Public Utilities Commission ($5.3 million to 13 organizations that body's department pays); Mimi and Peter Haas Fund and a filer for Children & Families First Commission ($2.2 million to 11 organizations that body's department pays). Of 332 payor entries in the City's behested-payment reports, 8 official-and-funder pairs ($478,500) name a grantmaker on file as the payor; the largest single payment is $300,000 from Crankstart Foundation to Housing Economic Rights Advocate in 2022, reported by Joaquín Torres (Assessor-Recorder). In 1 of those pairs the funder's own returns list grants to a recipient of the same name, and in none does the funder also pay an organization that the requesting official's own department pays.","66 go-betweens received $1.86 billion from the funders on file in 2020 to 2025 and themselves granted $930.5 million to City-paid organizations; the largest by money received are Silicon Valley Community Foundation ($319.8 million in, $243.3 million on to City-paid organizations); Tides Center ($227.6 million in, $41.0 million on to City-paid organizations); The San Francisco Foundation ($209.7 million in, $196.9 million on to City-paid organizations); California Community Foundation ($118.5 million in, $49.6 million on to City-paid organizations). In 2 funder-and-go-between pairs ($5.3 million) the first grant's printed purpose names a City-paid organization that the go-between also paid, so the chain can be read from the two returns: Crankstart Foundation through The San Francisco Foundation (The SF Housing Accelerator Fund); The San Francisco Foundation through Asian Amer Advncng Jstc-Asian Law Caucus (Chinese For Affirmative Action). A further $5.67 billion in 589 grants went to four national donor-advised-fund sponsors whose own returns are not on disk; where that money went next cannot be followed here.","74 of the 85 City-paid nonprofits that carry an official finding, a charge or a proven case in this project received $343.1 million from the funders on file in 2020 to 2025; the 1 tied to a charged or proven case received $0, the most going to Providence Foundation of San Francisco ($0, 0 funders). Whether funding stopped can be tested for only 3 organizations with grants on file and a dated enforcement step early enough for a later year of returns: for 1 no later grant was found (Bayview Hunters Pt Fndtn For Comm Improv, after “Controller's audit of the UCHS grants, for which the foundation had been …” on 2022-11-17: no grant found in tax periods ending after 2022; $219,886 before), and 2 received grants in later years (Community Housing Partnership, after “Controller publishes the audit.” on 2024-04-02: 3 funders paid $653,300 in tax periods ending after 2024; $3.9 million up to then; Urban Ed Academy, after “District Attorney charges Jones and Henriquez.” on 2023-08-29: 8 funders paid $810,841 in tax periods ending after 2023; $2.7 million up to then).","681 rows from 147 funders name no recipient and carry $18.95 billion of the $93.27 billion paid in tax periods ending 2020 to 2025 (20%). $16.21 billion of that is one foundation's donated prescription drugs. Setting those aside, $2.74 billion (3.6% of grants paid) has no named recipient; the largest are The Roberts Foundation $225.9 million; Hellman Foundation $208.4 million; Community Foundation for Monterey County $195.4 million; Craig H Neilsen Foundation $162.3 million. 62 funders name no recipient for any of the money they paid. None of these dollars is in any other screen here, so every total of private money to City-paid organizations is a floor.","68 private organizations had all three in at least one of fiscal years 2023 to 2025, out of 313 that had a State grant and City payments; for 15 of them every one of the three came to $100,000 or more. The largest by their smallest leg: Wu Yee Children's Services (FY2024: City $1,643,167, State $1,967,344, federal $30,170,951); Mission Neighborhood Centers Inc (FY2023: City $18,189,159, State $1,500,000, federal $2,000,000); Urban Alchemy (FY2023: City $10,192,702, State $1,440,000, federal $2,000,000). The question is whether one cost was charged to two funders; nothing here shows that happened.","184 organization-years pair a State grant serving San Francisco with a City contract on the same theme (35 outside the arts); in 22 both sides are $100,000 or more. The largest: YMCA of San Francisco, fiscal 2024, child care places and buildings (State $1,999,999; City $2,156,405); Urban Alchemy, fiscal 2023, shelter and homeless services (State $1,440,000; City $3,070,767); Horizons Unlimited of SF, fiscal 2024, mental health and drug treatment (State $1,000,000; City $1,223,382); Wu Yee Children's Services, fiscal 2024, child care places and buildings (State $1,000,000; City $751,303). Charging one cost to two is not, and a shared theme does not show a shared cost.","136 flagged organizations hold State grants on file worth $917.3 million. 26 of them carry something stronger than a lead (a proven or charged case, or an official finding), with $149.7 million in State grants: Healthright 360 (official finding, $62.1 million); Community Partners (official finding, $35.0 million); Brilliant Corners (official finding, $23.6 million); Hunters Point Family (official finding, $5.0 million); Centers For Equity And Success, Inc (official finding, $3.0 million).","36 awards worth $93.8 million are on file: $68.4 million to public agencies, led by San Francisco Police Department ($15.3 million: Suppression of Organized Retail Crime and Motor Vehicle Accessory Theft …), and $25.4 million to 18 nonprofit awards, led by Hunters Point Family ($5.0 million). 13 of the nonprofit awards went to organizations the City also pays, and 5 to ones carrying warning signs here: Hunters Point Family ($5.0 million); Juma Ventures ($4.5 million); Special Service For Groups Inc ($1.4 million); Five Keys Schools And Programs ($329,082).","Only one regional body publishes a contract list that could be read: the Transbay Joint Powers Authority, with 199 awards to 170 firms since 2004. 56 of those firms are also City payees ($3.70 billion from the City in fiscal 2019 to 2026), and 19 of them carry warning signs here: Anvil Builders, Inc.; AECOM Technical Services, Inc.; Allied Universal; Allied Universal Security Services; Stantec Consulting Services Inc.; Macias, Gini & O’Connell; Civic Edge Consulting; Otis Elevator Company. None of the 57 contractors on the State Labor Commissioner's public-works debarment list matches a City vendor or a Transbay awardee by name.","The City's ledger says what it paid each nonprofit; the nonprofit's own sworn tax return says what it took in, spent, kept and paid its people. This sets the two side by side, year by year, and lists where they cannot both be right, where a return contradicts itself and where money visibly stops moving; a gap is a reason to ask for the audited statements, not a finding.","Across 2,001 organization-years in which the City paid 463 organizations $100,000 or more, 12 organizations had at least one year in which City payments alone exceeded the total revenue on the organization's own tax return, by $28.6 million in their worst years combined; the largest is San Francisco Public Health Foundation, paid $13.5 million in the tax year ended 30 June 2022 against reported revenue of $1.6 million. For 7 of them the City's payments exceed reported revenue over every year on file added together, for 2 the gap is covered by a fall in money owed to them on their own balance sheet, and for 3 the return offers no clue to the reason. A further 9 organizations reported enough revenue but spent less in total than the City alone paid. 21 other one-year gaps disappeared when the year was pooled with its neighbour and are not listed.","33 organizations had at least one year in which the City paid $250,000 or more and that sum was larger than the government grants and programme fees on the return put together, by at least a tenth; 13 of them left the government-grants line blank or at zero in the year shown. The largest difference is at Five Keys Schools And Programs: $54.3 million paid by the City in the tax year ended 30 June 2025, against $44.9 million of government grants and $0 of programme fees reported. Years already listed in the first screen are left out.","Of 2,343 returns tested, 16 from 9 organizations leave $25,000 or more (and at least 1% of the year's spending) unexplained, $10.6 million in all. The largest is St James Infirmary: net assets of $5.9 million at the start of the year ended 30 June 2024, a reported surplus of $68,843, and $204,560 at the end, leaving $5.8 million with no entry. In 7 of the 16 the net-asset lines are simply blank or zero, which points to an incomplete form.","Among 1,851 pairs of consecutive returns, 47 pairs from 39 organizations disagree by $25,000 or more (and 1% of spending) on net assets, total assets, or last year's revenue or expenses. The largest is Brilliant Corners, for the year ended 30 June 2022: a change of $263.5 million between what was filed and what the next return says was filed. 31 pairs carry no amended return, prior-period adjustment or other sign of why.","The middle organization among 466 with full returns held 4.6 months of spending in cash and investments at its latest year-end; the middle one among the 120 that get half or more of their revenue from the City held 2.3 months. 28 organizations with at least a quarter of revenue from the City added to reserves in each of the last three years or more, by $56.3 million in total while the City paid them $342.8 million; the largest rise is Community Youth Center San Francisco, from $7.2 million to $15.8 million. 29 mostly City-funded organizations held six months of spending or more, 8 of them a year or more; the largest reserve in that group is Self-Help For The Elderly with $20.0 million, 7.2 months of spending.","At the middle organization among 457 with $250,000 or more of spending, 82% of spending is reported as programme and 13% as management. 25 report a management share of 30% or more that is also at least twice the middle figure for their peers, led by Phatt Chance Community Services Inc at 96% against 13%. At 21 the management share rose by seven percentage points or more while City money rose by a quarter or more. 1 organization spending $1 million or more reports no management cost at all. On 5 returns the three columns do not add up to the total on the same page, or are left empty; the largest miss is $1.3 million at Southwest Community Corp.","The middle organization among 427 with at least three employees spends $61,213 in salaries and benefits per employee counted. 13 report a payroll of $100,000 or more with no employees, and 0 report five or more employees with no payroll. 21 come out at two and a half times their peers or more, led by Applied Technology Council at $253,545 for each of 7 employees; 15 with twenty or more employees come out at a fifth or less. At 20 with five or more employees, officers and key employees take 40% or more of all pay, and at 20 their pay rose by a quarter or more while programme spending fell by a tenth or more.","The returns name 1,612 different contractors in 1,798 organization-and-contractor pairings. In 30 pairings one contractor took a quarter or more of everything the organization spent in a year, most of all Resilient Strategies LLC at Chinese Progressive Association (100%, $18.3 million). In 34 the contractor's address is the organization's own. In 58 the contractor has the name of one of the City-funded nonprofits in this study, and in 66 the name of an entry on this project's map of warning signs; these are name matches, and where no address joins them the records do not show whether the contractor and the named organization are one organization or two with the same name. 8 contractors are named by five or more City-funded organizations, the most widely named being Armanino LLP (13).","Of 485 organizations checked, 21 were paid $100,000 or more by the City in at least one fiscal year for which no return is on file, $106.8 million in those years; for 11 of them ($5.9 million) the records give no reason such as church status or a first return in a later year, the largest being The Gubbio Project ($1.4 million, fiscal 2025). 12 returns were filed more than a year after the last date allowed with an extension, the latest 577 days after it (Bindlestiff Studio, year ended 31 December 2023); 34 were more than 180 days after it.","75 tax years have more than one version of the return on file; in 32 the amendment moved revenue, expenses or year-end net assets by $25,000 or more (and 1% of expenses), most of all at Episcopal Community Svcs of SF Inc for the year ended 30 June 2023, where year-end net assets went from $20.0 million to $40.5 million. 19 returns report a different accounting method from the return before. 19 returns follow a change of financial year, and in 3 of those cases months are left that no return on file covers.","Among 477 organizations with full returns, there are 41 years in which a balance of $250,000 or more owed to the organization (and at least 15% of the year's revenue) fell by three-quarters or more. In 15 of them cash and investments rose to match, so the money appears to have been collected; in 2 net assets fell with no line explaining it, the largest being St James Infirmary, where $6.5 million owed at the start of the year ended 30 June 2024 was $1,185 at the end; the other 24 were set against debts, absorbed by a deficit, or cannot be traced from the return. Separately, 9 organizations show a balance that has risen at each of the last four year-ends or more and faster than revenue, $28.4 million in all, led by Horizon Services, Inc with $7.7 million, 36% of a year's revenue.","132 organizations have at least one catch-all line that is large for their size: 44 with \"other changes in net assets\" of $100,000 or more and a tenth of expenses, 28 with a prior-period adjustment of that size, 10 with undescribed \"all other expenses\" above 10% of spending, 33 with one self-described other expense above 40% of spending, 20 with \"other fees for services\" above half, and 28 with \"other assets\" of $1 million or more that are over half of everything they own. 38 of the 163 rows carry no description the script could find; the largest of those is a prior-period adjustment of $81.4 million at Yerba Buena Center For The Arts in the year ended 30 June 2025, against $11.6 million of expenses.","The 150 received $10.44 billion from the City over fiscal 2019 to 2026. 79 trip none of the eight tests, 42 trip one, 20 trip two and 9 trip three or more; the most is 4, at Hyde Street Community Services Inc. The tests tripped most often among them are reserves (22), receivables or other lines (21), filings (16). 2 of the 150 have no return on file to test. This page runs from Hyde Street Community Services Inc (4 tests) to Centers For Equity And Success, Inc (1).","The 150 received $10.44 billion from the City over fiscal 2019 to 2026. 79 trip none of the eight tests, 42 trip one, 20 trip two and 9 trip three or more; the most is 4, at Hyde Street Community Services Inc. The tests tripped most often among them are reserves (22), receivables or other lines (21), filings (16). 2 of the 150 have no return on file to test. This page runs from First Place For Youth (1 tests) to Bay Area Community Resources (0).","The 150 received $10.44 billion from the City over fiscal 2019 to 2026. 79 trip none of the eight tests, 42 trip one, 20 trip two and 9 trip three or more; the most is 4, at Hyde Street Community Services Inc. The tests tripped most often among them are reserves (22), receivables or other lines (21), filings (16). 2 of the 150 have no return on file to test. This page runs from Edgewood Center For Children And Familes (0 tests) to Kimochi Inc (0).","The City publishes a dataset of its spending on nonprofits and a table of every payment it makes. For most organizations they agree. The dataset is narrowed on purpose to contracted services. This page lists where that leaves out the most, because the organization pages on this site use the dataset and are too low for those organizations.","In 122 organization-years at 35 organizations the payment table is higher than the nonprofit-spending dataset by a quarter and $1 million or more, fiscal 2019 to 2026. The largest differences over those years are Children's Council of San Francisco ($802.3 million), Delta Dental Plan of California ($508.0 million), Homebridge Inc ($237.8 million). The organization pages on this site use the dataset, so for these organizations they understate what the City paid; the contract titles in the last columns show what the extra money was paid under.","For the 443 of 444 profiled organizations found in the payment table, the dataset shows $10.22 billion paid fiscal 2019 to 2026 and the payment table $12.38 billion: $2.16 billion more. In fiscal 2026, the latest year in both, the figures are $1.67 billion and $1.96 billion.","The payments in those 122 organization-years sit under 407 contract titles. The largest are “No contract title printed” ($758.3 million); “ECE Integrated Services 22-25” ($339.4 million); “ECE Integrated Services 17-22” ($185.0 million). The titles are the City’s own and say what the money was for; they include all of an organization’s payments in those years, not only the part the dataset leaves out.","28 City-paid organizations say on their own returns that they sponsor other projects; the City paid them $552.1 million from fiscal 2019 to 2027 to date, led by Heluna Health ($165.5 million); Tides Center ($106.7 million); San Francisco Public Health Foundation ($72.7 million); Richmond District Neighborhood Ctr Inc ($28.3 million). Only 9 of the 28 show the fee as a line of its own: Northern California Grantmakers ($410,444, 6.7% of its revenue); Dancers' Group ($302,887, 7.0% of its revenue); Z Space Studio ($215,892, 9.1% of its revenue); New Community Leadership Foundation ($150,000, 8.9% of its revenue). For the rest the fee is inside a general line such as service fees, so the share kept cannot be read from the return. 8 of the 28 are also among the large onward grant-makers listed in the grant-chains file; 1 more organization says it was itself a sponsored project, and 9 show only an expense line with the words.","34 contract titles paid since fiscal 2019 use those words, $111.5 million in all to 19 payees: $92.3 million as fiscal intermediary, $11.4 million as fiscal agent and $7.8 million as fiscal sponsor. The largest: Healthright 360 ($77.7 million); Abode Services ($11.4 million); Community Initiatives ($7.8 million); San Francisco Study Center Inc ($5.5 million). In these contracts the payee is paid to pass money on, to people or to a named project, and the payment file stops at the payee.","1 City-paid organization says on its own return that it operated as a sponsored project of another City-paid organization: Housing Rights Committee of San Francisc (paid $8.2 million) under San Francisco Study Center Inc (paid $58.3 million). Of 127 project and trade names registered under the sponsors’ own business accounts, 0 appear as a City payee in their own right: by that test, nothing unusual. 9 more payees share only a suite-level address with a sponsor (SisterWeb at Heluna Health; San Francisco Youth Theatre at Community Initiatives; Chinatown Media and Arts Collaborative at Chinese For Affirmative Action; City Youth Now at Centers For Equity And Success, Inc); an address is a lead, not a link. The grant-chains file lists, pair by pair, the City-funded organizations that also receive onward grants from other City-funded organizations; those rows are not repeated here.","The payment file shows 19 district organizations that received assessments collected by the City, $498.9 million since fiscal 2019 ($228.7 million of it to the tourism district paid by hotels); the City’s own list names 16 community benefit districts. On top of the assessments the City paid the same organizations $69.7 million in grants. For the neighbourhood districts grants rose from $11.9 million in fiscal 2019–2022 to $36.5 million in fiscal 2023–2026. In 5 districts City grants in the later years came to half or more of the assessment itself: Excelsior Action Group (205%); No Of Market/Tenderloin Com Benefit Corp (106%); Castro Upper Market Cmnty Benefit Dist (66%); Fisherman's Wharf Association Of SF (62%); Noe Valley Association (55%). Organizations the files tie to a district were paid a further $18.7 million (San Francisco Travel Association ($13.0 million); Union Square Foundation ($5.6 million); Ingleside Community Fund ($119,300)).","The districts’ returns for tax years 2021 to 2025 name 38 contractors paid $100,000 or more in a year, $101.9 million in all. 5 firms are named by two or more districts: Block by Block (8 districts, $41.7 million); Streetplus (3 districts, $23.2 million); A3 Visual (2 districts, $2.5 million); Nextstreet Cleaning Sanitation Inc (2 districts, $1.8 million); TL Squared LLP (2 districts, $539,063). Street cleaning and ambassador work for most districts is in the hands of a few firms. One district’s own return discloses an insider tie to its contractor: Lower Polk Community Benefit District lists payments of $1.4 million over 3 returns for cleaning and ambassador services to a firm whose chief executive it describes as its own former executive director.","3 pairs share people and have a second link on file. SF Tourism Improvement Dist Mgmt Corp and San Francisco Travel Association: 14 people on both returns, 3 of them on the payroll of one (EVP, COO; EVP, Chief Financial Officer); apart from assessments the City paid the first $39.4 million and the second $13.0 million, and the returns record $45.9 million passing between them. Union Square Foundation and Union Square Business Improvmnt District: 13 people on both returns, 8 of them on the payroll of one (Assoc, Finance/HR; Assoc, Marketing; CEO; Deputy Director); apart from assessments the City paid the first $5.6 million and the second $2.9 million, and the returns record $5.7 million passing between them. No of Market/Tenderloin Com Benefit Corp and Mid Market Foundation: 3 people on both returns, 1 of them on the payroll of one (Executive Dir.); apart from assessments the City paid the first $16.3 million and the second $82.4 million, and the returns record $2.2 million passing between them. 19 further pairs share at least one name on their boards with no other link on file; they are not listed, because a shared name alone is not evidence of the same person.","Of 12 district organizations that can be compared, 1 pays the highest-paid person one and a half times the middle figure for organizations of like size or more: Union Square Business Improvmnt District (Marisa Rodriguez, CEO, $394,479; 1.72 times). The middle district pays 0.93 times its peers: nothing unusual on the whole. The highest figure is $394,479. 2 district organizations list no officer or key employee paid by the organization itself in the latest return.","The year-end reports show $108.4 million granted in 2024-25 and $110.0 million in 2025-26 across 8 service areas (255 programs), with $104.7 million and $104.1 million spent. The sharpest change is in Justice Services: participant service days fell from 194,889 to 36,382 (-81%) while spending was $13.1 million in both years and unique participants went from 1,179 to 1,126; spending per participant day rose from $67.43 to $359.89. Family Empowerment grants rose from $6.2 million to $6.6 million while spending fell from $5.9 million to $5.8 million (13% of the grant unspent).","In 2025-26, 181 of 215 programs met the enrolment target (at least 90% of the participants each program projected), so 34 fell short; strategy averages run as high as 200% of projection, so some programs served far more than they projected. Programs met 45% of outcome targets (grades, credentials, assessments and the like), 79% of the required mid-year and year-end narrative reports, and 64% of survey targets. Weakest outcome results: Academic Supports: Percent of participants who have a completed individual learning plan. (7 of 14 met, average 64% against a 85% target); Academic Supports: Percent of participants who pass their courses. (Disconnected transitional-age young adults (TAY/A) Only) (0 of 2 met, average 27% against a 75% target); Alternative Education: Percent of participants who have a completed individual learning plan. (2 of 5 met, average 71% against a 85% target).","The reports account for $104.7 million spent in 2024-25 and $104.1 million in 2025-26. The City's payment table shows the department (code CHF) paying $138.1 million and $139.8 million under those year labels ($135.1 million and $139.8 million by the year of the voucher number), so the reports cover about 76% and 74% of it. Payments by the department peaked at $179.0 million in fiscal 2023 (by voucher year) and were $110.7 million in fiscal 2019.","The youth department's grantee-level pages trace most of the reported fall in youth justice service days to one program's count. Commission minutes, State payment reviews and grant evaluations add what authorities wrote down about San Francisco's child and youth money.","Justice Services participant service days went from 194,889 in 2024-25 to 36,382 in 2025-26. School Crisis Support alone went from 166,096 to 4,508, and one program in it — Community Youth Center of San Francisco, School Violence Interrupters — reported 163,663 days in 2024-25 (84% of the whole service area) and 943 in 2025-26. Its page shows no participant count, 126 service dates, 26 school incidents and 53 supports in 2024-25: about 1,299 participant days per service date. Without that program, the service area's days rose from 31,226 to 35,439 (+13%).","12 of the 33 Justice Services programs were saved as program pages (both years); they account for $5.4 million of spending in 2024-25 and $5.4 million in 2025-26. 4 program-years left 15% or more of the grant unspent. Highest spending per participant: Catholic Charities CYO of the Archdiocese of San Francisco, Boys Home Short-Term Residential Therapeutic Program (2025-26): $794,317 for 10 participants, $79,432 each.","5 of 12 saved programs match exactly one youth-department agreement by title; 7 share an agreement with other programs (for example titles such as 'Productive Adult-24-29') or have none named, and are left out. None of the matched programs was paid in fiscal 2025 and 2026 more than 5% above the spending its pages report for 2024-25 and 2025-26; payments under those two year labels run at 0.84 to 0.93 times reported spending. Adding fiscal 2027 lines paid so far (which mix late 2025-26 claims with the new year's) takes every one of them above reported spending, so the two records agree within the lag of reimbursement.","3 program-years did not complete a required mid-year or year-end narrative report (Catholic Charities CYO of the Archdiocese of San Francisco, Boys Home Short-Term Residential Therapeutic Program 2024-25; Catholic Charities CYO of the Archdiocese of San Francisco, Boys Home Short-Term Residential Therapeutic Program 2025-26; Centers for Equity and Success, Inc., Credible Messengers 2025-26). 4 program-years show 'No Data' against survey targets, and 8 had no performance measures at all (the department's note: none set for that year).","San Mateo County's index shows 3 deeds from Action Associates Inc to WeHOPE in East Palo Alto: one on 28 September 2018 (transfer tax $1,760) and two on 18 October 2024 (transfer tax $4,950 on one, $0 on the other). WeHOPE's land and buildings on its own returns went from $1,812,293 at the end of 2023 to $6,781,102 at the end of 2024, up $4,968,809; the 2024 return reports the $4,555,548 building transaction. The transfer tax comes to $1.00 per $1,000 of that rise, and the 2018 tax to $0.97 per $1,000 of the 2020 book value: the two purchases are in proportion. The same index lists 3 notices of State tax lien against the name WEHOPE (2012, and twice in 2021), each later released; found by a name search, the records do not show whether they concern this organization or another party of the same name.","Two City-funded nonprofits had serious findings. In June 2026 the Justice Department's inspector general questioned $579,145 of Huckleberry Youth Programs' victim-assistance spending and $145,433 of its match, found it could not track spending by funding source, and found part of the match was paid with a City grant that was federal money, which the rules forbid; the City paid Huckleberry $30.3 million in fiscal 2019-2026. In 2016 it found Asian Pacific Islander Legal Outreach had commingled grant funds and overstated victims served (203 reported, 92 supported), and questioned $500,000 of drawn costs. A separate $455,000 award remained undrawn at the cutoff; a City supplier named Nihonmachi Legal Outreach dba API Legal (name match) was paid $16.4 million in fiscal 2019-2026. The federal transit audits also questioned costs at SFMTA for procurement in FY2017 and cash management in FY2019.","Of 15 charities checked on 20 November 2026, 6 are delinquent or revoked and 2 more registered but past due. Still paid in fiscal 2026 or 2027 while past due: Chinese Progressive Association $956,433; Mid Market Foundation $3.6 million. The delinquent ones (Oakes Children's Center, last paid FY2023, San Francisco Safe, last paid FY2024, GlobalSF, last paid FY2023, Hearing and Speech Center of Northern California, last paid FY2023, Southeast Consortium for Equitable Partnerships, last paid FY2023) are no longer paid under their own names. United Council of Human Services was revoked on 09/18/2023 after more than a year suspended (letter in charity-filing-facts); the City paid for its programs through fiscal sponsors.","St. James Infirmary's books, filed with the registry for its dissolution, give the missing money an account name: $5.7 million booked as \"Payments to Affiliates\" in its last year, against $2.0 million of government contracts; the books also show $204,327 of liabilities, so its equity was $233.26, not $204,560. The Chinese Progressive Association owed Resilient Strategies $4.2 million at the end of 2022 and reports Resilient's Oakland suite as its own address. Mid Market Foundation reported $7,500.00 of revenue and $732.00 of assets for 2020; in fiscal 2022 the City paid it $12.2 million. United Council of Human Services lost its registration in September 2023.","Across 2,343 returns for tax years ending 2019 or later, 31 names (people and 5 outside firms) sign as officer, are named principal officer, or hold the books for two or more funded organizations that do not report each other as related; 16 do so in the same tax year. Most organizations: Easy Office DBA Jitasa (4).","57 rare names are listed as chief executive, finance or operating officer on the notices of two or more contractors that share no other officers; for 2 the date ranges overlap by more than three months, and 4 share a name with a former City employee.","21 names are paid by two unrelated funded organizations in one tax year; 4 in two or more years. In 10 both returns give 35 hours a week or more and neither title notes a part year. Most are what a job change looks like; on this test little unusual was found.","41 sponsor-and-project pairs are written into the contractor line of City contract notices: the payee is the sponsor, the money is for the project. The sponsors named most often: San Francisco Study Center Inc. (6 projects); Filipino-American Development Foundation (4 projects); St James Infirmary (3 projects); Somarts (2 projects). In 4 pairs the sponsor was last paid before fiscal 2026: St James Infirmary for Compton'S Transgender Cultural District (sponsor last paid fiscal 2024; not paid under this name; a payee with a like name, The Transgender District, was first paid in fiscal 2024 ($3.2 million since)); San Francisco Parks Alliance for Urban Sprouts (sponsor last paid fiscal 2025; the project has never been paid in its own name); St James Infirmary for Transgender Gender Variant Intersex Ju (sponsor last paid fiscal 2024; the project became a City payee in its own name in fiscal 2022); St James Infirmary for Transgender Cultural District (sponsor last paid fiscal 2024; not paid under this name; a payee with a like name, The Transgender District, was first paid in fiscal 2024 ($3.2 million since)).","9 City payees share a name and city with an entry on the State tax board's list of revoked exemptions and were paid afterwards, $28.8 million in all; the list carries no employer number, so each is a name-and-city match: the records do not show whether the listed entity and the City’s payee are the same organization or two with the same name, and the State entity number would settle it. The largest: United Playaz Inc. (listed 2008-10-30, $20.9 million since); Excelsior Action Group (listed 2021-08-20, $3.2 million since); Freedom West Homes Corporation (listed 2019-04-02, $1.8 million since); Asian Pacific Islander Cultural Center (listed 2016-08-25, $1.6 million since). 2 organizations matched by employer number are on the Attorney General's may-not-operate list and were paid in fiscal 2026 or 2027 ($4.6 million). 3 lost the federal exemption for not filing and regained it under a new ruling; 0 with no later ruling in the master file were paid afterwards.","The City ledger, federal award files, the State grant portal, the federal audit clearinghouse and the organizations’ own tax returns each record the same money from a different side. Joined on employer numbers, programme numbers and year ends, they mostly agree; these screens list where they do not, and where one record says a check was done that another record does not hold.","Of 1,982 returns for years ending June 2019 to December 2024, 77 from 34 organizations answer yes to the single-audit question for a year with no audit in the clearinghouse under the organization's employer number, its name or a listed additional number. 21 of those organizations do not describe their statements as audited with a group; the City paid them $239.0 million in the years concerned, and 9 have no audit on file for any year since 2016. The largest by City money: Instituto Familiar De La Raza Inc (Jun 2021, Jun 2022, Jun 2023, Jun 2024; $49.2 million); Bayview Hunters Pt Fndtn For Comm Improv (Jun 2022, Jun 2023; $32.5 million); Providence Foundation of San Francisco (Dec 2021, Dec 2022, Dec 2023, Dec 2024; $30.2 million); Young Community Developers Inc (Dec 2024; $16.5 million). 3 say on the return itself that the required audit was not done: Bayview Hunters Pt Fndtn For Comm Improv (Jun 2023); Young Community Developers Inc (Dec 2024); Housing Rights Committee of San Francisc (Jun 2024).","33 returns answer no, or leave the question blank, for a year in which the clearinghouse holds an audit of the same organization. The largest by federal spending in the audit: Bay Area Community Services (year ended 2023-06-30, $20.9 million); Bay Area Community Services (year ended 2024-06-30, $17.0 million); Conard House Inc (year ended 2024-06-30, $9.4 million); Conard House Inc (year ended 2023-06-30, $9.4 million).","Of 86 organizations whose audits show $250,000 or more received through the City, 21 with $1 million or more ($158.1 million together) are named in no City subaward report. The largest differences: Children's Council of San Francisco ($77.3 million audited, $275,000 reported); Episcopal Community Svcs of SF Inc ($66.4 million audited, $2.2 million reported); Healthright 360 ($56.4 million audited, $319,002 reported); The Yerba Buena Gardens Conservancy ($52.9 million audited, $0 reported); Heluna Health ($45.4 million audited, $288,133 reported).","Almost none. In 444 audited organization-years with federal money from the City, 4 at 2 organizations show more in the audit than the whole of the City’s payments for the same months, by $100,000 and a tenth or more: The Yerba Buena Gardens Conservancy (year ended 2025-06-30: $11.2 million audited, $7.8 million paid); Main Street Launch (year ended 2025-06-30: $1.8 million audited, $282,601 paid); The Yerba Buena Gardens Conservancy (year ended 2021-06-30: $6.9 million audited, $5.7 million paid); Main Street Launch (year ended 2022-06-30: $1.5 million audited, $1.1 million paid). In the rest the audited figure fits inside the ledger.","16 audits of 11 City-paid organizations for fiscal years ending 2019 to 2025 carry a finding on a programme the City passed through; 6 involve a material weakness, questioned costs or a repeated finding. In 4 of the 16 the City’s monitoring for the same year records no findings, a waiver or an unfinished review (PRC 2022: monitoring not complete; PRC 2024: no findings; Compass Family Services 2019: no findings; Shanti Project 2022: monitoring waived). Examples: Eviction Defense Collaborative Inc fiscal 2024 (1 material weakness, 1 with questioned costs, 0 repeated; City paid $25.0 million, then $21.6 million); Baker Places Inc fiscal 2021 (0 material weakness, 0 with questioned costs, 2 repeated; City paid $18.0 million, then $19.4 million); PRC fiscal 2021 (0 material weakness, 0 with questioned costs, 2 repeated; City paid $4.8 million, then $5.5 million); Mission Action Inc fiscal 2021 (1 material weakness, 0 with questioned costs, 0 repeated; City paid $10.6 million, then $13.1 million).","The Controller’s reports for fiscal 2019 to 2025 name 24 profiled organizations as elevated concern, red flag, tier 2 or tier 3. 6 of them have State or federal awards dated after the fiscal year of their first rating: $35.0 million from the State and $9.1 million from federal agencies, beside $19.4 million of federal subawards from the City itself. The largest: Friendship House Assoc of American India (Tier 2, fiscal 2024; then Department of Health Care Services: The Village SF Wellness Center ($31.4 million, 2025-05-06)); PRC (Red Flag, fiscal 2022; then Governor's Office of Business and Economic Development: California Community Reinvestment Grants Program ($3.0 million, 2024-06-01)); Baker Places Inc (Red Flag, fiscal 2022; then Department of Health and Human Services: Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Projects ($2.5 million, 2022-09-15)).","Often, and mostly for an ordinary reason. 250 audit-year and programme pairs at City-paid organizations show money through the City and by another route; in 147 pairs at 32 organizations both sides are $100,000 or more. Most second routes are other counties or the State, for organizations that work in several places. In 21 pairs the second route is a direct federal award, as at Alzheimer's Association; Community Housing Partnership; Grid Alternatives Inc; Healthright 360; Heluna Health.","19 pairs, $7.4 million in all, in the federal subaward register for fiscal 2019 to 2026. The largest: American Institutes For Research to Wested ($4.2 million, 2018-2024, Second Chance Act Reentry Initiative); Mission Economic Development Agency to Open Door Legal ($1.0 million, 2025, Eviction Protection Grant Program); Public Health Institute to Richmond Area Multi-Services Inc ($617,134, 2022, Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Projects of Regio); Enterprise Community Partners Inc. to Mission Economic Development Agency ($565,000, 2020-2026, Section 4 Capacity Building for Community Development and Af). 1 register row was set aside because its amount repeats its own subaward number and is larger than the whole prime award: an entry error in the source, not a payment.","In nearly every case it does. Of 542 return-years with both an audit and City payments, 11 at 9 organizations show the two together above total revenue by $250,000 and 5% or more: Main Street Launch (year ended 2021-06-30: $4.1 million City plus $76.4 million other federal, against $12.8 million revenue); Children's Council of San Francisco (year ended 2023-06-30: $166.5 million City plus $21.0 million other federal, against $174.8 million revenue); The SF Housing Accelerator Fund (year ended 2025-06-30: $44.7 million City plus $5.0 million other federal, against $42.5 million revenue); Conard House Inc (year ended 2025-06-30: $18.5 million City plus $3.5 million other federal, against $19.8 million revenue).","On 21 November 2026 the Attorney General's registry records of about ninety City-funded charities were added: yearly reports signed under penalty of perjury, the registry's letters and notices, and copies of returns and audits. These screens set each charity's answers to the State beside its own federal return and the City's payments for the same year.","162 yearly reports from 66 organizations were read; 94 could be set beside the organization's e-filed tax return for the same year. 9 reports from 6 organizations answer \"No\" to transactions with officers or directors although the return for that year lists such transactions on Schedule L ($1.2 million in all), the largest at Project Wreckless for the year ended 2025-12-31 ($584,180). 14 answer \"No\" to government funding against the return or the City's ledger for the same twelve months, 6 claim an audit the return says was not done, and 4 answer \"No\" on restricted funds against the return's balance sheet.","86 incomplete-report letters to these organizations were read. 13 objections go to the substance of a report rather than a fee, a signature or a missing page: 6 years at 2 organizations (Freedom West Homes Corporation, La Cocina) in which the registry wrote that an independent audit was required and the report said none was done; 1 in which a \"No\" on transactions with officers or directors was contradicted by Schedule L; and 5 in which a \"No\" on government funding was contradicted by the return.","Of 95 registry records joined to a City payee, 48 were sent 93 delinquency or suspension notices since 2017. The City paid those organizations $65.2 million in the fiscal years the notices fall in, most to Low Income Investment Fund ($33.4 million, 2 notices); PRC ($5.5 million, 3 notices); Lavender Youth Recreation & Info Ctr ($3.2 million, 1 notice). 5 are shown as delinquent or revoked on 21 November 2026.","4 organizations registered only after at least one full fiscal year of City payments, $13.6 million in those earlier years: The SF Housing Accelerator Fund (registered 3 May 2021, $10.0 million before); SF New Deal (registered 11 February 2021, $2.5 million before); Excelsior Action Group (registered 16 September 2021, $626,555 before); Southeast Consortium For Equitable Partn (registered 25 September 2019, $423,848 before). 3 were sent a notice to register by the registry.","82 copies of returns in the registry files could be placed beside an e-filed return for the same year. 79 agree on revenue and expenses (to within $25,000). 3 are a first version that the organization later amended with the IRS, and 0 match no version the IRS holds. 6 summary pages could not be read reliably (revenue less expenses did not equal the printed difference) and are not counted.","3 organizations in the batch filed dissolution papers with the registry or a final federal return. Oakes Childrens Center: no dissolution document on its registry list; final return for the period ended 2022-07-31: to Community Behavioral Health Services (1380 Howard St): Cash and Accounts receivables $19,309; Buildings, net accumulated depreciation $12,792; Investments $2,924; Accounts payable $195,474; Notes payable and other liabilities $647,220; Net assets $-807,623. Urban Ed Academy: 2026-03-18 dissolution request; 2026-10-14 registry asked for more before it will waive; no final return on disk. California Partnership For Safe Communit: 2025-08-06 dissolution request; 2026-02-12 waiver: no assets; 2026-04-30 dissolution documents; 2026-10-16 file closed; final return for the period ended 2025-06-30: to University of Pennsylvania (3451 Walnut Street): Balance in Bank Accounts $206,664.","Four filings describe a ticket-cash shortage or unauthorized bank transactions. Two organizations report a refund or reversal. One reports bank and insurance recoveries subject to a deductible.","The youth department's report app was saved whole on 21 November 2026: 254 program pages for 2024-25 and 255 for 2025-26. These screens set the programs against each other and against what the City paid.","131 payees hold 2024-29 agreements that match program pages. Under the fiscal 2025 and 2026 labels they were paid $178.7 million against $208.8 million of spending their pages report for 2024-25 and 2025-26 (0.86 times); none were paid more than 5% above it once fiscally sponsored programs are counted with their sponsor. Of $115.2 million the department paid nonprofit-flagged payees in fiscal 2026, $105.0 million (91%) went to payees with a program page; the largest without one is City College Of San Francisco ($9.5 million).","In 2025-26, 13 programs reported more participant days than their individually counted participants could have attended on every service date; together 569,646 of the department's 2,620,482 days (22%), against 624,706 of 2,712,571 (23%) in 2024-25. The largest is Playworks Education Energized - Playworks San Francisco Safe and Healthy Play: 479,298 days from 181 participants on 193 dates, 18% of all the department's days and 55% of its service area's, on $303,000. Without it the service area's spending per participant day is $45.25, not $20.74.","8 program-years across all service areas record a required mid-year or year-end narrative report as not completed: 6 in 2025-26, which spent $4.9 million. The largest: Community Youth Center of San Francisco - Coalition for Community Safety and Justice (2025-26, $1.1 million); Samoan Community Development Center - Pasifika Urban Roots (2025-26, $963,643); Young Community Developers - Summer Youth Academic and Employment Program (2025-26, $853,594).","19 program-years. Highest: Rocking the Boat, Inc. - Rocking the Boat (2024-25): $250,000 for 7, 47.1 times the strategy middle; Rocking the Boat, Inc. - Rocking the Boat (2025-26): $252,500 for 23, 11.3 times the strategy middle; Project Level - Project Level (2024-25): $294,830 for 20, 9.6 times the strategy middle.","12 program-years differ by 100 or more, or by 30% or more. Largest: Japanese Community Youth Council - Mayor’s Youth Employment and Education Program (2025-26): 1,499 participants, 1,042 served in the measure; Samoan Community Development Center - Pacific Islander Youth Alliance (2025-26): 164 participants, 370 served in the measure; Young Community Developers - Summer Youth Academic and Employment Program (2024-25): 218 participants, 423 served in the measure.","Of 252 programs with pages for both years, 2 reported the same participant days in both: Flyaway Productions - Girlfly (same participants, service dates, participant service dates; 300 days); Embarcadero YMCA - Treasure Island Out of School Time Programs (same participant service dates; 4,656 days).","15 programs fell short in both years; 15 of them had the same or a larger grant in 2025-26. Lowest: Community Education Partnerships - One-on-One Academic Tutoring for McKinney-Vento Students (16%, 28%); Friendship House Association of American Indians - Friendship House Youth Program (35%, 21%); Bayview Hunters Point YMCA - CARE Middle School - Bayview (40%, 37%).","In 82 of 322 activity-years of the published records, every record has an identical twin (same activity, year, age band, income band, race group, neighbourhood and client number): 66,228 records, 33,114 of them second copies, 10.6% of all 311,824. In the other activity-years a typical 3% of such cells hold an even count and none more than 50%. The office's own reports to HUD print half the paired count for 13 of 13 paired activity-years tested, and the full count for 10 of 10 unpaired controls.","The office's reports to HUD print half the published count for 13 of 13 paired activity-years tested, and the full count for 10 of 10 activity-years whose records are not paired.","Near-identical yearly counts show no sign of records carried forward. 8 activity year-pairs differ by 1% or less; in none is the mix of age, income, race group and neighbourhood the same (80% at most overlaps), and client numbers rarely recur. Moving-cost and deposit help, for example, has 338 and 340 records in 2015-16 and 2016-17 with 36% of the mix in common.","The neighbourhood and district versions hold the same 311,824 record numbers. 1 record is dated to a different fiscal year in each, which is why the 2015-16 and 2016-17 totals differ by one. 2,513 of 272,196 records placed in a named neighbourhood and district (0.9%) pair a neighbourhood with a district that holds under 2% of that neighbourhood's records. 111,120 records (36%) have no client number. The file was last updated 2020-05-21 and stops at fiscal 2018-19.","The site layer lists 31 public spaces (15 labelled Rec and Park, the rest Public Works, transit, BART, Port, real-estate or private). Economic and Workforce Development paid the Alliance $4,472,507 under two grants titled 'Citywide Public Space' (FY2019-FY2023), the largest of its City funding lines; grant 1000013629 shows $4,083,416 agreed, $3,982,507 paid and $354,510 more committed, leaving the balance $253,601 below zero. Rec and Park itself paid the Alliance $861,436 of $19,782,241 in FY2019-FY2025.","Nothing unusual. The 12 scored sites rose 4.3 points from FY2018 to FY2023 against 1.3 for other parks (a random set of 12 parks does as well or better 9% of the time), and moved -1.5 points from FY2023 to FY2025 against +1.1 (10% by chance).","TODCO, filing as a major donor together with its affiliate Yerba Buena Neighborhood Consortium, reported $2,448,204 in contributions and campaign payments from 2014 to 2024: $1,126,393 to committees it sponsors (Build Affordable Faster CA; Housing Forward SF) and $1,321,810 to other committees and campaign vendors. $62,329 went to political clubs’ committees with no measure named on the filing. The City paid TODCO $3,654,566 in fiscal 2019 to 2026. In the year to June 2024 its filings show $80,500 of campaign money against $51,000 of lobbying on its tax return; in the other years from 2021 the return’s figure is the larger.","Follow every leg of a three-organization grant relationship back to the filing that reported it.","2,311 latest Form 990 filings yielded 26,352 positive cash-grant entries with a different recipient EIN. They contain 9 three-organization cycles across 8 distinct directed groups. Distinct groups recurring in more than one period: 1.","The same-period checks identified 11 reciprocal pair-periods, 5 four-organization circuit-periods and 114 origin/destination pairs with multiple intermediaries across the periods reviewed.","5 circuits and 114 two-route origin/destination pairs have matching reporting dates. Each group below links to all four reported grants.","Of 471 comparable returns, 192 agree exactly and 11 exceed both thresholds for review."],["Among 23 San Francisco operators holding State contracts or City early-childhood payments, the money for 2025-26 works out at a middle figure of $26,457 per licensed place. 3 come out at twice that or more, led by Wu Yee Children's Services at $144,539 a place ($77.3 million against 535 licensed places). 1 contract holder with $2.6 million listed for 2025-26 has no open licence in the State file: Companeros Del Barrio (closed Companeros Del Barrio, Inc. (44, 2024-12)).","In 2024-25 San Francisco child-care and after-school sponsors reported 57,513 children enrolled but 11,147 fed on an average day, 5.2 enrolled for each one fed; the middle large county reports 2.9, and San Francisco ranks 7 of 40. Enrolment was 16,835 in 2016-17 and 126,299 in 2019-20, more than the 115,828 people under 18 then living in the city. Adult day care is consistent: sponsors claimed meals for 581 people a day, and adult day health centres separately reported about 597 attending a day.","Every licensed child-care centre in San Francisco set beside the public money that reaches it, and the money followed through the agencies that pass it on. Most of it cannot be followed to a centre or a child from any public record, and the screens say where the trail stops and which record would continue it.","San Francisco had 400 open child-care centre licences with 20,650 places on 8 November 2026; 26 more licences with 1,329 places were pending, many of them replacing a closed licence at the same address, and 20 with 3,172 places were inactive. Of the open licences, 202 centres (9,949 child places) are on the City's list of funded centres, 126 centres (6,747 child places) belong to an operator holding a State child-care or preschool contract, 31 centres (1,090 child places) sit at a Head Start address and 147 centres (7,674 child places) at a federal meal-programme site; 160 centres draw on two or more of these and 124 on three or more, while 157 centres (7,895 child places) show none. By kind of operator: nonprofit 170 centres (8,782 child places, 113 centres in the City network); private company 70 centres (3,601 child places, 31 centres in the City network); corporation, tax status not found 47 centres (2,403 child places, 7 centres in the City network); public body 31 centres (2,439 child places, 24 centres in the City network); church or religious body 26 centres (1,692 child places, 6 centres in the City network); not determined from the records 25 centres (1,091 child places, 11 centres in the City network); individual licensee 16 centres (493 child places, 4 centres in the City network); small centre, 14 places or fewer 15 centres (149 child places, 6 centres in the City network).","No public record counts funded children at each centre, so three proxies were tested. 3 of 23 operators hold State contracts for 2025-26 that would pay for more full-time children than they have licensed places even at the City's highest rate ($36,324 a year for an infant), led by Faces SF: $15.6 million, or 428 children against 271 places; 6 more exceed their places at the preschool rate. 0 Head Start comparisons show more funded places than licensed places. In 2019 the City Controller, using enrolment data the public cannot see, found 69 of 579 subsidised providers (12 percent) potentially over capacity.","No San Francisco child-care centre is on probation or has a revoked licence in the State file of 8 November 2026. 8 licences on the City's list of funded centres are no longer open (1 closed before the list was dated and not replaced at the address). 1 operator with a State contract has no open licence: Companeros Del Barrio, whose licence closed on 2024-12-10, is still listed for State money in later years. 1 site on the State's current meal-programme list has a closed licence. 10 publicly funded centres carry two or more citations of the most serious class.","The State publishes meals, children fed and enrolment only as county totals, so no sponsor can be tested. At that level the numbers fit: in 2024-25 centres and after-school sites claimed 3,547,122 meals for 11,147 people fed on an average day, 1.22 per person per weekday, and family child-care homes 2.54, against a federal ceiling of three; no year exceeds three, and the average paid per meal never exceeds the top federal rate. Enrolment is the number that does not behave: 23,993 in 2018-19 to 126,299 in 2019-20 while sites went from 243 to 251 and children fed on an average day from 12,104 to 9,750; 19,697 in 2020-21 to 65,919 in 2021-22 while sites went from 303 to 311 and children fed on an average day from 13,743 to 15,091. Of 119 meal sites of a child-care type, 7 have no centre licence at the listed address (7 of them school-district sites). The State's count of family-home meal sites reads 5,405 in 2022-23 against 351 in 2024-25, which looks like an error in the source.","In fiscal 2025 the City paid Children's Council of San Francisco $113.5 million (or $149.2 million, counting $35.7 million that the City's file labels fiscal 2025 although its voucher numbers and dates fall in June 2024) and the State promised it up to $35.9 million; its tax return shows $209.7 million of government money received, $186.8 million paid onward (89% of spending), $17.6 million in pay and benefits and $6.3 million in other costs, so the return balances to a surplus of $2.1 million. Onward payments rose from $96.8 million in 2021. The grants schedule names no onward recipient: the whole sum is reported as grants to individuals in 4 lines (\"Child care Subsidies and Child care Provider Stipends\": 2,547 recipients, $100.6 million; \"Quality Grants\": 119 recipients, $31.9 million; \"ECE Workforce Compensation Enhancements\": 2,551 recipients, $52.4 million; \"Nutrition\": 222 recipients, $1.9 million; ECE on the return stands for early care and education). The same is true of Wu Yee Children's Services ($49.4 million paid onward in 2025). The only providers either return names are its five highest-paid contractors (next screen); the Low Income Investment Fund names its recipients (the screen after). The City's own nonprofit-spending dataset shows $4.4 million to Children's Council for the same year, because it leaves out the two largest agreements. Its City agreements on file list 172 objectives, and no published result against them was found.","Each return names five payees: the agency's highest-paid contractors. For Children's Council they are child-care providers. In 2024, when it paid 238 contractors more than $100,000 each, the five were Wu Yee Children's Services ($3.3 million); Kai Ming Inc ($2.6 million); Cross Cultural Family Center ($2.6 million); Mission Neighborhood Centers Inc ($2.2 million); Holy Family Day Homes Of San Francisco ($1.9 million); its 2025 return names none and counts no contractor above $100,000. Across both agencies and five years, 5 private companies or corporations with no tax-exempt record found are among the named providers ($7.4 million), led by Bright Horizons with $4.6 million in 2021, 2022, 2023, 2025. 11 more top-five places went to home-based or individual providers ($3.1 million); they are not named here.","The City paid the Low Income Investment Fund $79.9 million in fiscal 2021 to 2025 for child-care buildings. Its tax returns for those years name 39 San Francisco centre operators and City-paid nonprofits receiving $48.4 million, led by Wu Yee Children's Services with $8.9 million, itself one of the two referral agencies. 5 of the named recipients ($1.2 million) are private companies, individual licensees or corporations whose tax status was not found. A further 57 San Francisco recipients received $5.0 million; they include home-based providers and people and are not listed. Together that is $53.5 million of grants to San Francisco organizations against the $79.9 million the City paid in. 1 recipient shares an officer's name with the fund: Felton Institute, granted $7.9 million. Name match: the fund's returns for 2024–2025 list Yohana Quiroz as director; this organization's returns for 2021–2025 list the same name as chief operating officer. The fund's grants to it in the years the name is on the fund's board come to $3.5 million.","31 privately owned centre operators (companies, individual licensees and corporations with no tax-exempt record found) are attached to at least one public stream; 27 of them, with 2,900 licensed places, are on the City's list of funded centres, the largest being Bright Horizons Children's Center, LLC with 821 places, and 10 draw on two or more streams. None holds a State contract and none is paid by the City's early-childhood departments by name: what each receives is paid through the two referral agencies, whose returns name only their five largest payees a year. The City's voucher table names 5 of them as direct payees, $194,344 in all; for 4 the payment is booked to a Human Services Agency wage-subsidy account, not to a child-care account. Separately, early-childhood departments paid $19.9 million to payees the voucher table does not name at all (\"Single Payment Payees\").","City early-childhood payments went from $117.7 million in fiscal 2019 to $297.3 million in fiscal 2026 while children under five fell from 36,864 to 32,320: $3,192 per child then, $9,198 now. State contracts to San Francisco agencies add $219.6 million in 2025-26, making $15,993 per child under five, or $20,732 for each of the 24,933 centre places with a licence in force in July 2025 (23,800 in July 2020). 83% of the City money in fiscal 2026 went to three pass-through agencies. In fiscal 2025 the City counted 9,150 children receiving funded care, $20,690 each ($24,922 if payments are counted in the year the City's file labels them); the City's published full-time preschool rate is $25,380 a year. In 2010 the budget analyst put the whole City early-care budget at $66.2 million.","The State counted 884 licensed family child-care homes with 8,504 places in San Francisco on 29 June 2023. Its current file lists only large homes: 292 open with 4,028 places, against 278 at the start of 2022. The City's network of funded programmes lists 372 homes (June 2025), more than twice its 177 centres; they cluster in ZIP codes 94112, 94134, 94124. Money that can be tied to homes as a group: federal meal payments of $3.7 million for 2,251 children fed on an average day in 2024-25, and $2.1 million of State contracts for home networks in 2025-26. Voucher and City money paid to homes cannot be separated from money paid to centres at any level: it is inside the referral agencies' unnamed onward payments.","9 official documents are on file. The Controller's 2019 audit found subsidies properly granted but 69 of 579 subsidised providers potentially over licensed capacity, and reported in 2022 that its recommendations were carried out. The school district's auditor could not find documents for 28 of 47 sampled child-care grant charges in fiscal 2022 (likely questioned costs $6,711,381). Federal audits of the three pass-through agencies report no findings in 21 audit years; among State contract holders, auditors reported findings at Glide Foundation, Compass Family Services, none of them on a child-care, Head Start or meal grant. One criminal case alleging payment for care not provided was charged in 2025 and is unresolved.","This section reads the State's licence records for child care and residential care, the youth department's year-end reports, and the State's child-care counts for San Francisco. Each pattern is a question for the records named, not a finding of wrongdoing.","Of 400 open San Francisco centres in the State file of 15 November 2026, 220 are on the City's funded-centre list or run by an operator with a State child-care or preschool contract for 2025-26. 32 of them carry at least one Type A citation (the State's more serious class) and 9 carry two or more; 32 had a complaint allegation upheld since July 2023. Funded centres average 0.19 Type A citations and 0.23 upheld allegations each, against 0.23 and 0.26 for open centres with no public stream found. The most cited is Nihonmachi Little Friends Sutter Site (Nihonmachi Little Friends): 3 Type A citations, 2 upheld allegations since July 2023. None is on probation.","San Francisco's 400 open centres carry 20.8 Type A citations per 100 centres against 42.5 statewide (rank 20 of 26 counties with 100 or more open centres, most cited first). 24% of complaint allegations against them were upheld, against 32% statewide (rank 25 of 26). 78% were visited in the last twelve months (77% statewide); 0 show no visit in three years.","The State files list 9 children's 24-hour residential licences, 73 adult residential and adult day-program, 74 elder-care and 57 home-care licences in San Francisco. 43 are held by an organization the City paid in fiscal 2025 or 2026. 2 San Francisco licences in these files are on probation, none held by a City payee. Unity Care Group (Unity Care Group, transitional housing placement program, licensed): 4 Type A citations and 9 upheld complaint allegations. San Francisco Boys And Girls/ Shelter (Catholic Charities, short term residential therapeutic program, licensed): 3 Type A citations and 5 upheld complaint allegations. Peter Claver Community (Catholic Charities, residential facility chronically ill, licensed): 4 Type A citations and 1 upheld complaint allegations.","292 large family child-care homes are licensed in San Francisco with 4,028 places; 52 carry a Type A citation (21.9 per 100 homes, against 20.8 per 100 centres). 82 large-home licences in the file are closed.","San Francisco reported an average of 667 children a month in Stage 1 child care in 2018-19 and 978 in 2026-27 (989 in 2026-08). In 2026-08, 67% were in license-exempt care (the report's third setting, beside centres and family child-care homes), against 56% statewide. Reimbursed spending is reported through 2023-11 and is zero from 2023-12 on, as is the statewide row; in 2022-23 it averaged $1,060 a child a month ($813 in license-exempt care, $1,240 in centres).","The State lists 22 San Francisco preschool contracts worth $93.4 million for 2025-26, 2.1% of $4,386.0 million statewide; the San Francisco Unified School District holds $35.8 million. 22 of 23 amounts match the State figures already used in the child-care screens. The file also lists South San Francisco Unified School District ($1.4 million) under San Francisco County. Measured against open licensed places under the same name, the highest is Mission Child Care Consortium at $23,994 a place. ","The State's error-rate studies for 2021-22 and 2022-23 sampled 6 San Francisco contractor-years; 2 had errors. Highest: Felton Institute (2022-23, center-based), 25% of sampled cases in error, $7,185 of $29,070 sampled payments. Statewide rates: 2021-22 voucher 1.16%, centre 1.84%; 2022-23 voucher 2.91%, centre 5.19%."],["Contracts sized just under the Board-vote threshold.","One purchase split into many.","How much is awarded without competition.","For-profit suppliers paid without a bid.","Contracts the Board approved after work had begun.","Duplicates, round numbers and year-end rushes.","Voter-approved projects that run late.","The original bid sheets, City team register and California public works registrations identify firms that bid for the full project and appear on the winning team.","8 bidder–project pairs on 7 projects have a named City subcontractor row. 4 carry a positive payment figure. The original bid paperwork also identifies 6 of the commitments.","Reviewed all 108 source sheets. 107 have a matched City contract, 83 list at least one City subcontractor, and 103 have a corroborating state project registration.","The complete 420-row file retains winning firms, unmatched names and absent teams.","105 state registrations support 103 project identities. Multiple state records for one project remain separate rows.","78 audit titles addressed leases, rent, royalties or concessions. 17 titles mentioned a financial exception; the other reports provide context for interpreting those cases.","78 audit titles addressed leases, rent, royalties or concessions. 17 titles mentioned a financial exception; the other reports provide context for interpreting those cases.","Compare voter-approved bond components with their original schedules. Completed work and projections have separate tables. The download includes every component and the rows with missing dates.","Of 16 components with comparable dates, 15 finished after the original date and 9 by at least 365 days. The median difference across the whole cohort is 485.5 calendar days.","Of 47 components with comparable dates, 38 are projected to finish after the original date and 31 by at least 365 days. The median difference across the whole cohort is 1308 calendar days.","7 of 108 bid lists had one bidder (and 16 more had two), worth $93.9 million; the lone bid ran a median +14% against the estimate, where the low bid on contested contracts ran -7%.","Of 86 lists with an estimate, the lowest bid was 25% or more under it on 12 and 15% or more over it on 9; the widest gaps are +90% (Pier 80 Mooring and Berthing Improvements (contract 2871)) and +77% (1 SOUTH VAN NESS AVE. ELEVATOR MODERNIZATION (REBID)).","On 15 of 92 contested lists the second-lowest price was within 2% of the lowest; the closest pair were $1,064 apart on a $2.7 million job (Michael O'Shaughnessy Construction, Inc. and A. Ruiz Construction Company).","On 16 of 86 lists with a recommendation the firm put forward was not the lowest tabulated price, a difference of $12.3 million in total. On 3 the lowest bidder asked to withdraw its bid after opening ($8.8 million more to the next firm); on 7 the gap sits inside the local-business bid discount ($1.6 million).","Six firms (R&S Construction Management, Inc., Mitchell Engineering, Precision Engineering, Inc., Esquivel Grading & Paving, Inc., A. Ruiz Construction Company, Ronan Construction, Inc.) account for 132 of the 420 bids; at least two of them meet on 32 of 71 Public Works lists, and one of the six is recommended on 21 of those. Esquivel Grading & Paving, Inc. was recommended on 10 of the 22 lists it entered; R&S Construction Management, Inc. on 3 of 27.","516 City-paid firms match a State contractor licence by name; 13 of those licences are shown as suspended, 10 of them for firms paid $65.1 million since July 2024; 1 City-paid firm shares a State-numbered licensed person with a firm the City has barred, and 10 with a suspended licence.","Of 289 published State decisions on prevailing-wage assessments since 2011, 3 concern a San Francisco project: two against Shamrock Structures, Inc. affirming $101,352 in unpaid wages on two branch-library jobs for Public Works, and one penalty against East Bay Floorcovering, Inc. that the State dismissed.","159 agreements at least doubled and grew by $10 million or more through amendments the Board approved from 2014 to 2026; 23 grew five times or more. 80 of the 159 began between $9 million and $10 million, just under the amount at which the Board must approve a contract, and now stand at $2.97 billion together. 63 are with profiled nonprofits and 141 are with payees that carry other warning signs on this site.","1 business(es) registered at the exact address of an organization on the City’s suspended, debarred or agreed-not-to-contract lists began receiving City payments around or after the action; the largest, OBS Engineering Inc, shared the suite from 2018-07-02 to 2020-02-01 and has been paid $3.5 million since Azul Works, Inc’s 2020 action (2 long-standing co-tenants that were already City payees are left out). Separately, 8 City-paid businesses took over the address and part of the name of a closed business; those read as ordinary reorganizations (a sole owner incorporating, a sale, a merger) and are not listed.","27 San Francisco-registered payees with at least $250,000 in City payments give a business start date no more than 90 days before their first City contract; 10 of those first contracts are not recorded as competitively bid and those payees have been paid $88.8 million since.","The City’s ordinary rules say work is advertised and competed for, approved before it starts, and staffed from the civil-service list; each rule has a written exception. These screens count how often each way around is used, by which departments, for which suppliers and for how long. An exception that is used every year, for the same supplier, is a question to put to the department, not a finding. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","From fiscal 2019 to fiscal 2026 the City paid $4.08 billion under rules that set competition aside: $1.49 billion under emergency powers, $1.52 billion as sole source, $761.8 million where an ordinance waives bidding and $303.0 million under other \"not bid\" rules. That is 7.5% of the $54.52 billion of payments that record any rule; 51% of all payment dollars record none. The total peaked at $703.4 million in fiscal 2021 and was $695.4 million in fiscal 2026, when payments under ordinance waivers ($394.5 million) had replaced most of the emergency spending.","Citywide the share is 7.5%. At Homelessness Services it is 43% ($1.45 billion of $3.34 billion), mostly emergency powers; Assessor - Recorder follows at 24% and Health Service System at 17%. 1 of the 39 departments compared put a third or more of their recorded purchasing through such routes.","983 suppliers hold at least one contract awarded without competition since July 2018. 56 of them were given a new one in four or more different fiscal years, and 4 in at least eight of the nine. The longest runs: Five Keys Schools And Programs (9 years, 20 contracts, $224.2 million); One Treasure Island (9 years, 10 contracts, $19.2 million); Rubicon Enterprises Inc (9 years, 9 contracts, $16.6 million); Toolworks Inc (9 years, 9 contracts, $2.9 million).","76 contracts of $10 million or more were awarded without competition since July 2018, worth $2.81 billion between them. The largest is Tenderloin Housing Clinic Inc’s with Homelessness Services ($303.6 million, bidding waived by ordinance), then New Flyer Of America Inc’s with Municipal Transportation Agency ($220.4 million, other \"not bid\" rule).","118 suppliers with $1 million or more in no-competition contracts since July 2018 also carry warning signs elsewhere in this project. For 39 of them the strongest sign is an official finding, a charge or a proven case; together those 39 hold $1.25 billion in such contracts.","The City paid $1.51 billion under emergency purchasing rules from fiscal 2019 to fiscal 2027 to date: $65,177 in fiscal 2019, $443.3 million at the peak in fiscal 2021, and still $198.7 million in fiscal 2024, $100.8 million in 2025 and $71.9 million in 2026. Homelessness Services paid under the wording \"grants only, no bid, emergency procurement\" in every year from fiscal 2020 to fiscal 2027 ($726.9 million in all, $305.9 million of it since July 2023). Emergency public-works contracts, almost unused before fiscal 2024, have drawn $62.6 million since.","25 contracts that started before July 2022 were still being paid under an emergency purchasing rule in fiscal 2026 or 2027, drawing $34.8 million in those two years. 25 of the 25 are Homelessness Services grants. 23 contracts awarded under emergency rules since July 2018 have a term of five years or more ($220.6 million agreed); the latest is scheduled to end on 2030-06-30.","68 suppliers paid $1 million or more under emergency rules got at least half of all their City money that way ($489.4 million between them); for 42 it was 95% or more. The largest: Hotel Whitcomb ($62.8 million, 71%, fiscal 2020–2022); Color Health Inc. ($48.5 million, 59%, fiscal 2020–2022); Chinese Hospital Association ($29.1 million, 54%, fiscal 2020–2023); Off The Grid Services LLC ($28.7 million, 98%, fiscal 2020–2022).","The Board’s files since 2014 show 8 local emergencies that the Board concurred in or ratified. The coronavirus emergency runs through 39 Board files over 833 days (2020-02-25 to 2022-06-07); the next longest is Rim Fire (16 files over 427 days). Separately, the Board approved 50 emergency repairs or works declared by departments, with stated costs of $229.8 million; 12 of them, worth $75.8 million, date from 2023 or later. The middle case took 45 days from introduction to the Board’s vote, with the work already under way.","27 Board files from 2014 to November 2026 waive competitive bidding or approve sole-source dealing with a named party; the amounts stated in their titles add up to $504.1 million. 17 date from 2022 or later, the largest of those being Low Income Investment Fund ($67.7 million).","850 contracts worth $1.87 billion were awarded as sole source to 583 suppliers since July 2018. Public Health accounts for 29% of the value and Airport Commission for 19%. The largest holders: Alstom Transport USA Inc ($219.5 million, Airport Commission); Curascript Specialty Distribution ($98.5 million, Public Health); Low Income Investment Fund ($67.7 million, Department of Early Childhood); BEUMER Lifecycle Management LLC ($65.3 million, Airport Commission).","35 suppliers hold a sole-source contract of $250,000 or more with one department and a competitively awarded contract of the same kind with another, both started since July 2018; the sole-source side adds up to $119.5 million. The largest: Carahsoft Technology Corp (sole source for Technology, $28.3 million; competed at Police); San Francisco Food Bank (sole source for Human Services Agency, $23.0 million; competed at Public Health); Seneca Family Of Agencies (sole source for Public Health, $10.0 million; competed at Human Services Agency).","59 suppliers got a first City contract without competition within a year of their earliest registered business start date; their no-competition contracts since add up to $401.9 million. 10 of the first awards were made under emergency powers and 49 by sole source, ordinance waiver or another \"not bid\" rule. The largest: Color Health, Inc. (0 days, $84.4 million, emergency powers); BEUMER Lifecycle Management LLC (318 days, $65.3 million, sole source); SF New Deal (56 days, $58.9 million, emergency powers).","The Health Department’s list for fiscal 2024-25 shows 28 contracts excused from competition under section 21.42, with ceilings adding up to $250.7 million. For 23 of them ($239.3 million) the reason written beside the waiver is that the department needs more time to run a competition; 6 of those 23 contracts have terms of five years or more, the longest running from 2018-07-01 to 2027-06-30. The department’s summary for the year puts all its sole-source approvals at $436.4 million.","The City’s two posted lists show 108 contracts worth $152.3 million excused from the local-business rule in fiscal 2022-23 and 131 worth $182.7 million in fiscal 2023-24. City Administrator accounts for $125.4 million and Public Health for $106.2 million. 3 of the 239 contracts were also awarded without competition, according to the Supplier Contracts table. The largest is Lystek International Limited (TC63002 PUC Biosolids Mgmt Svc, $15.9 million).","10 firms and organizations named on the City’s suspension and debarment lists appear in the payment file, paid $126.4 million between them since fiscal 2019. 1 of them received $100,000 or more under rules that set competition aside, $40.5 million in all; the largest is Providence Foundation Of San Francisco ($40.4 million, emergency powers; bidding waived by ordinance; Health Department sole-source list). Payments issued in fiscal years after the year of the action add up to $11.9 million.","220 suppliers carry two or more of six kinds of exception; 73 carry three or more and 38 carry four or more. Those 38 were paid $4.76 billion since fiscal 2019. At the top: Healthright 360 (6 kinds, paid $488.9 million); Baker Places Inc (6 kinds, paid $143.8 million); Chinese Hospital Association (6 kinds, paid $54.3 million); Tenderloin Housing Clinic Inc (5 kinds, paid $439.6 million).","10 reports from 2012 to 2026 address these routes directly, 6 of them since 2024. The same criticism recurs: a waiver granted because a competition was not planned in time. The budget analyst wrote in 2012 that the Health Department “extends sole source contracts beyond their initial terms”; the Controller wrote in 2024 that “poor planning for procurements is not an appropriate justification”; and the Health Department’s own fiscal 2024-25 list gives time to run a competition as the reason for 23 of its 28 waivers.","These screens follow every outside payee through eight fiscal years and every contract file through the Board of Supervisors, to see who keeps winning, who is excused from competition each time, and whose approvals come faster. A payee that wins every year is a question about how the work was awarded, not a finding, and most have an ordinary answer. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","Across all departments the ten largest payees of each took 63% of outside spending in fiscal 2019 and 57% in fiscal 2026. On average 3.8 of a department’s ten were still the 2019 names in 2026, and those 2019 names received 33% of the 2026 money; payees new to their department took 4.4%. Of 30 departments compared, the circle is most closed at Adult Probation (90% of fiscal 2026 money to its 2019 top ten; 6 of ten names unchanged), Assessor - Recorder (77%) and City Attorney (76%); it is most open at Port (2%), Fire Department (6%), Controller (10%).","2,641 payee-and-department pairs were paid in every one of the eight years; in 46 of them (1.7%) the payee’s share rose in six or seven of the seven year-to-year steps. Chance alone would give about 165, so a share that climbs without pause is rarer than a coin toss would make it, not commoner. The test can turn on a move of a few hundredths of a point, so a pair is listed only if it also meets the test when each payment is counted in the year the file lists it under: 30 pairs meet it both ways. 13 of the 30 were paid $500,000 or more in fiscal 2026: Bar Association Of San Francisco at Superior Court (10.2% to 16.7%, $772,079 to $1.0 million); Moffatt & Nichol at Recreation & Park Commission (0.0% to 5.5%, $18,545 to $3.3 million); Community Youth Center San Francisco at Children, Youth & Families (2.9% to 5.9%, $2.4 million to $6.6 million). 2 of the 13 have nothing recorded as competed and 6 carry a warning sign elsewhere in this project.","2,110 payees were paid in all eight years and received $33.67 billion of the $44.69 billion of outside spending. 161 of them, paid $1 million or more ($6.43 billion together), have no payment recorded as competed. Most of that money sits under rules that do not say either way: construction contracts $4.50 billion; power for resale $446.4 million; property rent $353.0 million; management consulting services $334.4 million. Only 25 got most of their money under a rule that expressly sets competition aside, and 21 did so in every one of the eight years ($190.1 million), led by Epic State and Local Government LLC ($124.4 million, systems consulting services), Hyland Software Inc ($9.3 million, systems consulting services), R T Z Associates Inc ($6.5 million, systems consulting services). The departments that account for the 161: Public Utilities Commission $4.01 billion, Public Works $902.9 million, City Administrator $555.3 million, Public Health $346.2 million. Separately, 87 payees of any kind were paid under a no-competition rule in all eight years ($1.14 billion under those rules); the exceptions screens list them by contract.","Of 708 agreements with outside parties that the Board amended at least once from 2014 to 2026, 19 were amended in three or more different years; their latest approved ceilings add up to $1.90 billion. 13 of the 19 were amended in at least half the years between their first and latest Board file. The longest runs: CCT Technologies, Inc. with Office of Contract Administration (4 amendments in 4 years, 2019–2023, $20.0 million to $66.5 million); DFS Group, L.P. with Airport (5 amendments in 4 years, 2018–2025); Xtech JV with Office of Contract Administration (3 amendments in 3 years, 2016–2018, $32.0 million to $109.0 million); Golden Gate Petroleum with Office of Contract Administration (3 amendments in 3 years, 2022–2024, $45.0 million to $102.3 million). Where the contract table holds an agreement of the same size for the same party, 4 are keyed as competed and 2 under a rule that sets competition aside.","Of 24,301 contract numbers in the payment file, 232 were paid in every one of the eight years, $9.37 billion between them; 119 of them have a term of ten years or more in the contract table. 152 are keyed as competed and 18 under a rule that sets competition aside ($170.0 million). The largest of those 18: Epic State and Local Government LLC (Public Health, $124.4 million, sole source, term 2018–2027); Carahsoft Technology Corp (Assessor - Recorder, $8.7 million, sole source, term 2018–2030); Tides Center (Homelessness Services, $7.4 million, sole source, term 2018–2026); 21 Tech (Treasurer-Tax Collector, $6.7 million, sole source, term 2013–2033).","2,326 such files introduced from July 2018 to 2026 reached a final vote; the typical one took 35 days, and 75% took between three and seven weeks. 92% had exactly one committee hearing. By kind the middle value is 35 days for a contract, 35 days for a grant the City accepts, 42 days for a lease or property, 35 days for a grant agreement, 49 days for a settlement, 21 days for a loan; by calendar year of introduction it runs from 28 to 49 days. Against like files, the quickest departments are Real Estate Division (-10 days over 186 files); Mayor's Office (-8 days over 49 files); Treasure Island Development Authority (-5 days over 17 files); and the slowest Fire (+29 days over 19); Children, Youth and Their Families (+27 days over 25); Public Works (+22 days over 48). 2 of the 29 departments are faster, and 5 slower, than random sets of files of the same size would be 95% of the time (faster: Real Estate Division; Mayor's Office) (slower: Fire; Children, Youth and Their Families; Public Works; Police; Recreation and Park); chance alone would put one or two of 29 outside that range, so some departments’ files do move at their own pace.","68 City-paid organizations are named in three or more timed contract, grant, lease or loan files. For 31 of them the typical gap against like files is under a week either way. 10 ran a week or more faster with no file slower than its peers (Aramark Correctional Services LLC, -14 days over 3 files; Prologis L.P., -14 days over 3 files; SF New Deal, -14 days over 3 files; Chinese Hospital Association, -9 days over 4 files), and 5 a week or more slower with none faster (Self-Help For The Elderly, +21 days over 3; San Francisco Market Corporation, +14 days over 3; Cdm Smith Inc, +7 days over 3; Alliant Insurance Services Inc, +7 days over 3). 3 of the 68 lie outside the range that random sets of the same number of files fall in 95% of the time (Urban Alchemy, slower; Japanese Community Youth Council, slower; Cross Country Staffing, slower), which is about what chance alone gives (3 of 68). No organization’s files stand out as fast-tracked.","979 of the 2,326 timed files name no sponsor and 466 name the Mayor. Among the 19 supervisors with ten or more sponsored files, the average gap against like files runs from -9 days (Matt Haney, 47 files) to +23 days (Stephen Sherrill, 12 files); 13 of the 19 are within a week of their peers. 1 of the 19 lies outside the range that random sets of the same number of files fall in 95% of the time (Matt Haney, faster, -9 days), where chance alone would give about 1.","Of 2,326 timed files, 29 show no committee hearing before the final vote and 36 were voted within 14 days of introduction; 107 more were sent from committee to the full Board “as a committee report”, a wording in the Board’s own history. 17 of the 49 files listed are grants the City accepts, not payments to anyone. Speed does not track approval after the fact: among contract, grant, lease and loan files, those approved after the work or term had begun took a typical 42 days (189 files) against 35 for the rest (1,309 files), and ran +7.1 days against like files compared with +2.0. 7% of the fast-tracked contract-type files were approved after the fact, against 13% of all such files.","52 payees drew three or more different kinds of exception spread over four or more of the eight fiscal years; 17 drew at least one in every year. Together they were paid $6.25 billion, $1.73 billion of it under rules that set competition aside; 45 are nonprofits and 47 carry warning signs elsewhere. At the top: Tenderloin Housing Clinic Inc (5 kinds over 8 years); Healthright 360 (5 kinds over 8 years); Community Housing Partnership (5 kinds over 8 years); Felton Institute (5 kinds over 7 years); Homeless Prenatal Program (5 kinds over 7 years). The exceptions screens count how many kinds stack on each supplier; this one shows the order they came in.","From fiscal 2020 to 2026, 164 payees received $1 million or more in the first year they appear in the ledger, $660.1 million in all; 34% of that first-year money is recorded as competed and 16% under a rule that sets competition aside (2020: 31%, 2021: 38%, 2022: 26%, 2023: 9%, 2024: 5%, 2025: 4%, 2026: 12%). The largest: PRG-Potrero Properties LLC ($65.0 million in fiscal 2026, all under a rule that does not say); Granite Construction Company ($58.6 million in fiscal 2026, all competed); City Gardens Bridge LLC ($46.9 million in fiscal 2023, all under a rule that does not say). Of the 164 listed, 54 grew, 37 stayed about the same, 16 shrank, 35 vanished and 22 are too new to say.","101 payees that received $5 million or more since fiscal 2019 ($2.84 billion together) have no payment of any kind after fiscal 2024. The largest are Tutor Perini Corporation ($598.4 million, last paid fiscal 2023), Goodwill SF Urban Development LLC ($236.5 million, last paid fiscal 2023), Skanska Constructors ($141.2 million, last paid fiscal 2021). 18 were paid mostly or wholly under emergency powers, 15 of them last paid in fiscal 2021 to 2023. 33 carry a warning sign elsewhere in this project. For 2 a newcomer sharing a distinctive word of the name was first paid by the same department, for the same purpose, the next year (Arthur J Gallagher & Co Ins Brk Of Calif then Arthur J. Gallagher Risk Mgmt Svcs LLC; 350 Rhode Island Operating Company LLC then KAIM 350 Rhode Island LLC); for 14 more a newcomer without a shared name was paid a similar amount. Each is a lead to check, not a match.","108 payees of $1 million or more are named in the rising-share, never-competed, amended-again, one-contract, faster-at-the-Board or repeated-exception screens. For 82 of them none of the four ties appears. For 21, the organization or people listing it as their employer gave $1,000 or more to committees of candidates for supervisor or mayor since 2017 (7 gave to a supervisor who sponsored one of the payee’s own files); the giving by all 108 adds up to $71,831, of which $0 came from an organization itself and the rest from people who list it as employer; 7 had lobbyists report contacts with supervisors or the Mayor’s office; 7 appear in gift disclosures by staff of a department that pays them; 0 are named in a budget add-back. 8 show two or more kinds of tie: Pacific Gas & Electric Co (an exception every time); San Francisco Aids Foundation (amended again and again; an exception every time); Healthright 360 (an exception every time); Safeway Inc (never competed).","2,045 payees of $1 million or more were tested: 126 with campaign giving, 45 with lobbying contacts, 39 with a gift to paying staff, 8 with an add-back. Of 15 comparisons with enough payees, 1 differ from like payees by more than chance and 14 do not. Beyond chance: payees with lobbying contacts rank higher on years with an exception (position 58.8% against a chance range of 43.1% to 57.2%; 2.2 years on average against 1.1 years on average). No tie of any kind goes with a difference in: days to the Board’s final vote against like files; share of payments under no-competition rules; growth of its share of its main department’s spending. With this many comparisons one crossing of the 95% line is expected by chance alone.","Outside spending rose from $5.13 billion in fiscal 2019 to $6.90 billion in fiscal 2026 (+34%), while the number of payees went from 5,752 to 5,511 and the 100 largest took 66% and then 61%. Nonprofits received 18% and then 28%. The share keyed under rules that set competition aside was 3.6% in fiscal 2019, peaked at 11.1% in fiscal 2021 and was 9.9% in fiscal 2026; the share keyed as competed went from 57% to 55%. Of contract-type files sent to the Board, 16% were approved after the fact in fiscal 2019 and 8% in fiscal 2026.","Most of the money and approvals in a city government never come before anyone elected. These screens measure how much moves that way, whether the share is growing, and who is on the automatic side each time. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","Of 26,508 contracts on the City’s books that began in 2018 to 2026, worth $71.77 billion as agreed, 925 (3.5%) are large or long enough for the Board’s line to reach them; they hold $39.64 billion. The other 25,583 hold $32.13 billion, 45% of the dollars: $21.29 billion in 24,261 contracts under $10 million, and $10.84 billion in 1,322 construction contracts, which follow their own rules whatever their size. The share of dollars outside the line was 32% for contracts that began in 2019 and 51% for those that began in 2025. A separate screen counts 364 contracts sized between $9.5 million and $10 million against 39 just above (the “contract thresholds” results).","Across 34 departments with at least 20 contracts and $25 million agreed since 2019 (construction left out), the most money below the line sits with City Administrator ($2.94 billion in 3,789 contracts, 49% of its dollars); Public Health ($2.89 billion in 1,098 contracts, 32% of its dollars); Homelessness Services ($2.39 billion in 496 contracts, 51% of its dollars); Public Utilities Commission ($1.75 billion in 1,032 contracts, 21% of its dollars). 11 departments have nothing above the line at all. Comparing contracts that began in 2019 to 2021 with those that began in 2023 to 2025, the below-the-line share rose by more than five points in 8 departments and fell by more than five in 5; the largest rises: Adult Probation (55% to 100%); Children, Youth & Families (33% to 66%); Public Health (19% to 51%).","The November 10 preliminary count showed 63.76% yes. On November 9 the City Attorney expected the Charter changes to take effect in mid- to late December after formal filing.. Applied to contracts that began in 2023 to 2025, 312 were reached by the old line and 225 would be reached by the new one: 90 contracts worth $1.39 billion sit between $10 million and $25 million. 33 of the 90 are with nonprofits and 8 are keyed under a rule that sets competition aside. Most are held by Public Utilities Commission (18); City Administrator (16); Airport Commission (11); Public Health (10). The Board’s own analyst, counting the items the Board actually voted on (new contracts and amendments), put it at 304 items falling to 115 and about $2.51 billion no longer reviewed. First-crossing amendments still require approval. After approval under the new rules, further approval is required when cumulative increases since the last approval reach 50 percent of the original amount. This table cannot test amendments.","201 contracts carry a term of exactly ten years; 17 run up to a month longer, and 82 between ten years and eleven in all. 152 of the 201 are also under $10 million ($474.9 million in all), so neither line reaches them; 9 of those are within 10% of the dollar line as well. They sit mostly with Public Health (54); Public Utilities Commission (23); Homelessness Services (13); Airport Commission (9).","Of 8,069 resolutions, motions and ordinances the Board adopted from 2019 to 2026, 1,734 (21%) were “referred for adoption without committee reference” and adopted with no committee hearing, typically 7 days after introduction. The share has stayed between 18% (2019) and 25% (2021) with no steady rise. Only 17 of the 1,734 name $100,000 or more in their title. 95% of all resolutions and motions were settled at the first Board meeting that took them up. On the land-use files whose vote counts are on disk, 95% of 773 final roll calls had no vote against. Among contract, grant and lease files alone, the site’s “The same winners, year after year” page counts 29 with no committee hearing and 107 sent to the full Board as a committee report.","68% of the 1,734 files adopted without committee reference are commemorations, positions on bills, appointments to advisory bodies or the Board’s own meeting business. 67 are money items, 8 of them naming $100,000 or more ($3.47 billion in all, most of it one bond authorisation shown in the next screen). The largest groups: positions (550); commemorations (381); land-use steps (331); meeting business (138).","17 files adopted without committee reference since 2019 name $100,000 or more. 4 of them act on the money; the rest schedule a hearing, apply for a grant or take a position on a bill. The largest: Issuance of Revenue Bonds - Public Utilities Commission - Purchase of Electricity Distribution and Transmissio ($3.07 billion, 2020); Supporting California State Assembly Bill No. 1505 (Rodriguez) - $250,000,000 Seismic Retrofitting Program for ($250.0 million, 2023); Committee of the Whole - Loan Agreement - Metropolitan Transportation Commission - Public Transit Operating Pu ($200.0 million, 2026).","58 contracts and grants worth $207.8 million are keyed under the purchasing rule for “short-term extensions”; $17.9 million has been paid under that rule since fiscal 2019, to 33 payees, 26 of them in two or more years. These are not short contracts: all 58 show a term longer than a year, typically 4.6 years from start to today’s end date, so the extension rule is the latest step in a long run. They sit with City Administrator (41); Public Health (4); Human Services Agency (4); Economic & Workforce Development (3). This is the only place the contract table marks an extension as such. For agreements large enough to need a Board vote, the site’s “The same winners, year after year” page lists 19 amended in three or more different years, and 232 contract numbers paid in every year from 2019 to 2026.","119 contracts show payments in a fiscal year that began at least twelve months after their recorded end date, $20.7 million in all. 29 are construction contracts ($4.9 million), where late final payments are routine. The other 90 hold $15.9 million; 31 are grants. They sit with City Administrator (15); Public Health (15); Mayor (10); Human Rights Commission (7). Set against $71.77 billion in contracts, this is small: the table does not show widespread payment on expired contracts.","The Planning Department’s records mark 183 housing project applications since 2017 under a State streamlining law or describe them as ministerial: 11,117 homes proposed, 7,733 of them in the 91 applications approved so far, 6,339 of them below market rate. The count rose from 8 applications opened in 2019 to 52 in 2025. The laws named most: SB 35 (76); SB 9 (55); SB 423 (22); described as ministerial (22); AB 2011 (12). Building permits linked to these applications declare $2.79 billion of construction. The City’s own reports to the State list 74 projects (5,907 homes) as approved under a streamlining law from 2018 to 2025.","The 2025 roll carries $16.04 billion of institutional exemptions on 1,981 parcels, 4.5% of the roll’s $353.62 billion; in 2019 it was $12.37 billion on 1,818 parcels (4.5%). The charitable (“welfare”) exemption is by far the largest: $12.81 billion on 1,337 parcels, up from $10.15 billion on 1,174 in 2019. At the basic 1% rate printed in the City’s financial report, $16.04 billion of exempt value is about $160.4 million a year of tax not charged. The whole roll grew 1.29 times over the same years and institutional exemptions 1.30 times, so their share of the roll has not risen; the fastest-growing large exemption is college/university ($1.04 billion to $1.99 billion).","Ten addresses hold $5.26 billion of the $16.04 billion exempted, 33% of it. The largest are 1101 Van Ness Avenue (hospitals, welfare exemption, $2.67 billion); 3555 Cesar Chavez Street (hospitals, welfare exemption, $693.8 million); 151 3rd Street (commercial/mixed use, welfare exemption, $309.3 million); 2130 Fulton Street (schools, college/university, church exemption, $293.6 million). By the roll’s own description of use, the exempt value sits mostly in hospitals ($4.60 billion); apartment 15 units or more ($3.48 billion); schools ($1.72 billion); office ($1.37 billion).","Of 349 flagged nonprofits that could be placed at a San Francisco address, 127 are registered at an address with an institutional exemption on the 2025 roll, $4.06 billion of exempt value between them. 2 of the 6 organizations whose federal exemption was revoked or whose State charity registration is not current are among them: The Gubbio Project (1661 15th St, $519,791; State charity registration: Delinquent); Oakes Childrens Center (98 Bosworth St, $170,208; State charity registration: Delinquent). The largest: California Pacific Medical Center (1101 Van Ness Ave, $2.67 billion); Chinese Hospital Association (845 Jackson St, $245.6 million); Saint Francis Memorial Hospital (900 Hyde St, $157.4 million); SFjazz (201 Franklin St, $72.3 million).","84 ordinances passed since 2018 waive, end, reduce or refund a fee, or forgive rent: fee waived (62); fee ended (8); fee reduced (7); fee refunded (4); rent forgiven (3). Dollars are on file for one programme. The Board’s analyst reported that First Year Free, which waives first-year permit, licence and registration fees for small businesses, waived $1,649,977 in fiscal 2022-23 and $1,564,250 in the first ten months of 2023-24 (about $1,877,101 for the year), had enrolled 7,467 businesses by June 17, 2024, and would cost about $30.3 million over ten years if made permanent; it also noted a plan to waive about $10 million in licence fees. For the other ordinances the titles name the fee but not the sum. What each permit fee is, and how fees changed, is in the site’s “What the rulebook requires” page.","Since fiscal 2015 the Board has approved 114 settlements of tax-refund claims naming $262.6 million: $10.4 million in the six years to fiscal 2020 and $252.2 million since. The largest: General Motors Company ($71.1 million, 2026); Stripe, Inc. ($49.0 million, 2025); Twilio Inc. ($18.0 million, 2023); WeWork Companies Inc. ($13.3 million, 2023); Levi’s Plaza TIC Trust ($10.2 million, 2021). 8 of the files settle a property-tax suit by agreeing an assessed value, with or without a stated refund.","The City’s financial report puts exemptions that the State does not repay at $22.32 billion of assessed value in fiscal 2025, 6.0% of all assessed value, up from $8.25 billion (4.1%) in fiscal 2016: about $223.2 million a year at the 1% basic rate, against $82.5 million. Separately, collections on earlier years’ property tax turned negative: the report shows net givebacks of $12.9 million for the fiscal 2023 levy; $35.6 million for the fiscal 2024 levy; $87.3 million for the fiscal 2025 levy, which it attributes to exemptions being applied to earlier years and to Assessment Appeals Board decisions that offices, hotels and retail property had been over-assessed. The Board also approved 96 settlements of tax-refund claims from fiscal 2016 to 2025, $170.8 million in all.","The City’s financial report shows claim payments of $70.1 million in fiscal 2019 and $201.5 million in fiscal 2025, $767.8 million over the seven years. Settlements the Board passed in those years in which the City pays name $238.5 million, 31% of that. Until October 2026 the City Attorney could settle up to $25,000 without the Board; ordinance 205-26, passed on 2 October 2026, raised that to $100,000 with a yearly report in place of a vote. 228 of the 502 settlements the Board passed since fiscal 2019 (45%) were at or under $100,000, $13.6 million in all: files of that size will no longer come to the Board. By subject the files are injury or property damage (263); other (99); employment (97); civil rights (43). 2 further files since fiscal 2015 gave a department and the City Attorney standing authority to settle every claim from one event (a landslide, a pipeline break) up to a ceiling, $12.0 million between them.","The City’s ledger charges $557.7 million to its judgments and claims account from fiscal 2019 to 2026. The most sits with General City - Unallocated ($217.0 million); Public Utilities Commission ($110.4 million); City Attorney ($106.0 million); Municipal Transportation Agency ($98.4 million); Public Health ($12.5 million). By type the account holds judgements - legal fees ($315.9 million); litigation expenses ($86.8 million); litigation expenses-legal fees ($79.5 million); judgements - claims ($69.3 million).","The Board passed 671 accept-and-expend files from fiscal 2019 to 2026, naming $3.56 billion. In fiscal 2024 they named $437.6 million, against the $2.81 billion in grants and contributions the Board’s analyst counted in City revenue that year (16%): the rest came in through the annual budget, or to the transit agency, whose grants need no Board approval. Ordinance 135-26, passed on 23 July 2026, raises the line for a separate vote from $100,000 to $1,000,000 from October 1, 2026. 284 of the 671 files since 2019 (42%) were for less than $1,000,000 and would no longer come to the Board; they hold $108.9 million, 3.1% of the dollars.","Of 2,367 ordinances passed since 2018, 99 do one of these things by their own titles: removes a hearing, notice or permit step (53); hands the Board’s own approval to an official (16); lets a department approve or sign on its own (11); sets competition or contracting rules aside (11); raises a dollar line (5); takes effect or renews by itself (3). 35 delegate, raise a line or take effect by default; 13 of those passed in 2018 to 2021 and 19 in 2023 to 2026. Three passed in the last two years bear directly on money: ordinance 010-25 (12 February 2025) lets the Mayor approve certain contracts, grants and leases for homelessness and related work if the Board has not acted within 45 days; ordinance 135-26 (23 July 2026) raises the line for a Board vote on accepting a grant from $100,000 to $1,000,000; ordinance 205-26 (2 October 2026) raises the City Attorney’s authority to settle claims from $25,000 to $100,000. Voters then raised the contract line itself on 3 November 2026.","10 reports by the Controller and the Board’s Budget and Legislative Analyst from 2020 to 2026 address approval without a vote. The most recent, on the measure voters passed in November 2026, found that raising the Board’s contract line to $25 million would have cut its review from 304 to 115 contracts in 2023 to 2025; purchasing ordinances proposed by the City Administrator would take effect in 60 days unless disapproved; and nothing new is proposed to replace the review that goes. The common thread is not that delegation is wrong but that where it exists the written procedures, monitoring or reporting meant to stand in for a vote were often missing.","The City's contract table keeps only today's ceiling and end date, so growth is measured where another record holds the first figure: Board of Supervisors files, commission items that modify or close out construction contracts, bid sheets and capital reports. Most growth in service agreements is a service extended for more years; on construction, price increases cluster by department and by kind of contract, and no builder stands out by more than chance would give. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","Of 622 agreements with a first and a latest ceiling in the Board's files, 369 grew: from $9.09 billion to $19.04 billion together, $9.95 billion more. 305 grew by a quarter or more and 158 at least doubled. Read by their dates, 191 of the 369 are more years at about the same yearly cost, 72 are more years at a higher yearly cost, 79 are a higher ceiling for the same term, and 27 cannot be told. The largest rise in percent is APX Inc. (Public Utilities Commission, $5.0 million to $149.9 million); the largest in dollars is Mckesson Plasma And Biologics LLC (Public Health, $295.9 million to $1.47 billion). Where the first approval itself is on file, so that the agreement is followed from the start, 46 of 304 (15%) later grew by a quarter or more.","Grants to nonprofits account for the most added money: $3.99 billion across 280 measured agreements, 60% of which grew by a quarter or more (typical rise among those that grew: 92%). Goods and general services: 21 of 52 grew by a quarter or more (40%), $3.02 billion added; of those that grew, 8 read as more years at the same yearly cost, 8 as more years at a higher yearly cost and 11 as a higher ceiling for the same term. Other services: 33 of 114 grew by a quarter or more (29%), $1.40 billion added; of those that grew, 25 read as more years at the same yearly cost, 7 as more years at a higher yearly cost and 12 as a higher ceiling for the same term. Software and technology: 41 of 87 grew by a quarter or more (47%), $935.5 million added; of those that grew, 21 read as more years at the same yearly cost, 10 as more years at a higher yearly cost and 14 as a higher ceiling for the same term. Professional services: 41 of 83 grew by a quarter or more (49%), $599.7 million added; of those that grew, 16 read as more years at the same yearly cost, 5 as more years at a higher yearly cost and 21 as a higher ceiling for the same term. Construction: 0 of 6 grew by a quarter or more (0%), $8.6 million added; of those that grew, 0 read as more years at the same yearly cost, 0 as more years at a higher yearly cost and 1 as a higher ceiling for the same term. Only 6 construction agreements are in the Board's files at all: construction is awarded and changed under authority delegated to departments and their commissions, which is what the Budget and Legislative Analyst's two audits examine. The bid-sheet and official-review screens are the evidence for construction.","Both a first and a latest end date can be read for 454 agreements. 252 now end a year or more after the first date and 199 two years or more; the typical extension among the 252 is 30 months. 178 of them read as more years at about the same yearly cost and 66 as more years at a higher yearly cost. The longest: Motorola Solutions Inc (Emergency Management), first due to end 2027-11-30 and now running to 2038-11-30. Where the agreement can be found in the payment file, 114 were still being paid two or more fiscal years after the first end date.","Hardly any. Of 19,284 contracts that ended between July 2018 and June 2025 and were paid, 92 (0.5%) drew payments two or more fiscal years after their end date, $15.8 million in all; the highest rate is in construction (15 of 884). The table's end date is the present one: when a contract is extended the date is moved, so this test can only find payments that outlived every recorded extension. Read from the fiscal-year label on each payment instead, the count would be 149: the payment file carries some earlier payments under a later year, mostly on purchase orders in multi-year capital funds ($5.52 billion of $119.25 billion, 4.6% of all payments since fiscal 2019, and 9.3% of payments on contracts, carry a later year than their voucher number belongs to).","71 contracts, $5.2 million above their ceilings between them; on 59 of the 71 the excess is under 10%, and 1 is over by both 10% and $100,000. The largest is ConvergeOne, Inc. (Technology, Citywide Cisco EA 2019-2026): $65.6 million paid against a ceiling of $65.0 million. Gross vouchers include $2,526,324.82 coded Sales-Use Tax. The other vouchers total $63,030,075.55. Check the signed terms for how the limit treats tax. The raw table suggests far more: read from the prime contractor's row alone, 1,026 contracts look overpaid, but 879 of those are an effect of how the table splits one award across the prime and its subcontractors. Of the 151 that remain when every row is added up, 3 were dropped because the prime's own row is negative, 47 as rents and memoranda and 28 because a neighbouring contract number of the same firm holds the unspent room. A further 37 paid contracts show no ceiling at all, and on 164 the table's own payment column exceeds the ceiling where the payment file does not. 2 more were dropped because the department's own commission records show a larger approved amount than the table does, so the table is out of date: Clark Construction Group - Calif LP (AIR-11918.66-DBCargoBldg626.1): $128.8 million paid, table ceiling $104.2 million, but the Airport Commission's items put the contract at $257.2 million by 2026-05-19; PGH Wong & Partners (AIR-10072.41 -PMSS Courtyard 3): $36.1 million paid, table ceiling $17.2 million, but the Airport Commission's items put the contract at $45.4 million by 2024-01-16.","12 contractors have five or more agreements with a first and latest ceiling in the Board's files (42 more have three or four). Across all 622 measured agreements 49% grew by a quarter or more and 56% of those with dates ran a year or more long. None of the 12 sits above the rate for its own kind of work and department at the one-in-twenty level; about 0.6 would be expected to by chance alone. A second test on the 364 agreements whose contractor has at least one other: the contractor's other agreements predict growth worse than the department and kind of work do (average squared error 0.232 against 0.216; 0.251 knowing neither). On this evidence growth in Board-approved agreements goes with the department and the kind of service more than with the contractor; construction, where the question matters most, is barely in these files. For construction, the commission records added since give a direct test. Of 43 builder-and-department pairs with five or more construction contracts, 3 needed commission-approved price increases more often than their department's rate at the one-in-twenty level, where about 2.1 would by chance (Sierra Mountain Construction Inc, 6 of 14 at Public Utilities Commission; Pilot Construction Management Inc, 3 of 12 at Airport; Mitchell Engineering, 4 of 8 at Public Utilities Commission); a builder's other contracts predict a rise of a quarter or more worse than its department does. After such a rise 43% of builders were awarded new construction contracts by the same department, against 50% of those that closed out near the original. So, plainly: on the records now on disk, overruns on construction do not follow particular builders beyond what chance and the department explain; they follow the department, the kind of contract and the job. The test is weak for small builders, and individual change orders are still not public.","Of 24,716 paid contracts with a ceiling, 148 (0.60%) were paid above it or two fiscal years past their end date. Among 1,222 contractors with five or more such contracts, 10 have two or more of the 148. 3 of them have more than their mix of work and departments would give at the one-in-a-hundred level; with 1,222 contractors tested, about 12 would cross that line by chance. At the top: Core & Main LP (3 of 11 contracts, 0.27 expected); San Francisco Women Against Rape (3 of 19 contracts, 0.34 expected); Towne Ford Sales (2 of 5 contracts, 0.12 expected). That is no more than chance would produce. 8 of the 10 repeat cases are suppliers of goods, where a ceiling is a limit on orders.","139 contractor-and-department pairs had such an agreement at least a year ago and can be followed in the contract table (31 more contractors could not be found there under the name the Board title uses). 87 of them (63%) were given new contracts by the same department afterwards: 332 contracts with ceilings of $5.73 billion, the first typically 508 days later. By the purchasing rule keyed on them, 204 were competed, 99 were not ($1.61 billion) and 29 carry a rule that does not say. For comparison, among 124 pairs whose agreement neither grew by a quarter nor ran a year long, 60% were given new contracts afterwards. Leaving nonprofits out, the figures are 51% of 79 after an overrun and 53% of 88 without one. The most new work followed APX Inc. (Public Utilities Commission): 4 new contracts, $1.35 billion. An agreement that grew is not a bar to new work, and nothing in the files suggests it is treated as one.","Of 125 contractor-and-department pairs whose agreement crossed that line at least two years ago, 44 (35%) show no new contract from the same department since. 26 of those 44 were still being paid by that department in fiscal 2026 or later, usually under the same agreement, which runs on; 11 hold newer contracts with other departments. The files give no reason in any case: no record on disk says a contractor was passed over because an agreement grew.","More clearly than it follows contractors. Among 18 departments with five or more measured agreements the share that grew by a quarter or more runs from 0% (Mayor's Office of Housing and Community Development) to 86% (Homelessness and Supportive Housing). 3 of the 18 are above what their mix of work would give at the one-in-twenty level, where about 0.9 would be by chance: Public Health (95 of 129, 46% expected); Homelessness and Supportive Housing (49 of 57, 54% expected); Airport (26 of 42, 38% expected). For construction the only department figures are the Budget and Legislative Analyst's: across 297 construction contracts, July 1, 2021 to June 30, 2024, change orders added 8% to the first contract value, from 33% at Recreation and Park, 10% at Municipal Transportation Agency, 9% at Airport, 4% at Public Works, 3% at Port. The highest department is 11 times the lowest, a wider spread than anything these files show between contractors.","54 of 98 bid sheets on disk could be matched to a contract in the table, and 42 of those began at least a year ago. Their ceilings now stand a median +2.3% against the winning bid; 2 of the 54 have grown by a quarter or more. Winners that bid 10% or more under the engineer's estimate: 14, median growth since +2.8%, 0 now above the estimate; the other 27 with an estimate: median +1.3%. Won by 2% or less over the next bidder: 6, median growth +5.9%, against +1.6% for the 24 won by more. The largest rise: Bay Area Lightworks Inc, PW Cntrt No.36 Trffc Sgnl Mod (Public Works, bid $3.4 million in 2023, ceiling now $6.3 million, +82%). 8 of the matched contracts also appear in commission items that modify or close them out: Bay Area Lightworks Inc, PW Cntrt No.36 Trffc Sgnl Mod (bid $3.4 million, $6.5 million in the latest item, +90%; reason printed: work the owner added or changed); West Coast Arborists Inc, PW GAN G21 TREE SRVCS (bid $2.6 million, $3.8 million in the latest item, +50%); Buhler Commercial, PW 911 CALL CNTR RENO (bid $3.4 million, $4.1 million in the latest item, +20%). A rank test puts the chance of the low-bid group ranking that high with no real difference at 55%, and of the narrow-margin group at 21%. Neither gap is beyond chance on these few sheets, which are also too recent for most contracts to have finished.","Of 71 bond components in the Controller's reports, 33 are two years or more past the finish date first given (40 a year or more) and 1 has a bond budget a quarter or more above the amount first allocated; 33 meet one test or the other, with bond budgets of $2.20 billion. The latest: Final Design (65%) (Embarcadero Seawall Earthquake Safety (2018)), first due 2024-04-05 and now 2035-12-31 (not finished). Between the two reports on file (FY22-23 and FY24-25), 23 components moved their finish date back by six months or more. The project list counts 7,644 planned projects or housing units, 3,693 of them complete, and 49 entries with a completion date still ahead. No bond file names the builder, so none of this can be set against a contractor.","Of agreements first approved by the Board in 2014 to 2018, 11 of 121 (9%) grew by a quarter or more within three years; of those first approved in 2019 to 2023, 6 of 88 (7%). The later group is not higher. What has clearly risen is the volume of amendments: the Board passed 27 amendments a year to these agreements in 2014 to 2018 and 58 a year in 2021 to 2025. Those that state the amount before and after (110 and 268 of them) added $1.82 billion and $6.13 billion in those five-year spans. The typical single increase was 40% of the ceiling before it in the first span and 64% in the second.","192 contractors had such an agreement and 191 others in the same files did not. Lobbied City officials through a registered lobbyist: 13 of 192 (7%) against 12 of 191 (6%), a gap chance would give 51% of the time. Gave to a political committee in its own name: 21 of 192 (11%) against 15 of 191 (8%), a gap chance would give 20% of the time. Has staff who gave to candidates for City office: 69 of 192 (36%) against 40 of 191 (21%), a gap chance would give less than 0.1% of the time. Carries a warning sign elsewhere on this site: 86 of 192 (45%) against 52 of 191 (27%), a gap chance would give less than 0.1% of the time. So 2 of the four links are more common among the contractors with overruns than chance allows. But large nonprofits have their agreements extended most often, have the most staff to give, and are the organizations this site has examined most, so like has to be compared with like: nonprofit with nonprofit and business with business, in thirds by how much the City has paid them. Has staff who gave to candidates for City office: 69 with an overrun, where 67.7 would be expected from organizations of the same kind and size (chance 42%); among businesses alone 19 of 112 against 25 of 136. Carries a warning sign elsewhere on this site: 86 with an overrun, where 85.3 would be expected from organizations of the same kind and size (chance 48%); among businesses alone 27 of 112 against 21 of 136. Compared that way none of the gaps is beyond chance: the overlap reflects which kind and size of organization has its agreements extended, not better connections.","The commission items on disk cover 529 contracts; 248 are fixed-price construction contracts, 110 of those have a first and a latest figure, and their median rise is 19% (43 rose by a quarter or more). Job-order contracts are left out: 29 of the 65 on file were raised by exactly half, the one-time increase to $7.5 million that the Budget and Legislative Analyst describes as an option in the Administrative Code. Set against every fixed-price construction contract the same department held open in the same years, the share that needed a commission-approved increase is: Public Works 23 of 297 (8%); Public Utilities Commission 45 of 276 (16%); Airport 8 of 143 (6%); Port 8 of 59 (14%). Municipal Transportation Agency is left out of this test: the contract table keys too few of its contracts as construction to give a denominator. 43 contractor-and-department pairs hold five or more such contracts. 3 of them needed increases more often than their department's rate at the one-in-twenty level, where about 2.1 would by chance: Sierra Mountain Construction Inc at Public Utilities Commission (6 of 14, against 16%); Pilot Construction Management Inc at Airport (3 of 12, against 6%); Mitchell Engineering at Public Utilities Commission (4 of 8, against 16%). That is no more than chance. And among the 66 measured contracts whose builder has another measured one, the builder's other contracts predict a rise of a quarter or more worse than the department does (error 0.272 against 0.234; 0.229 knowing neither).","23 builder-and-department pairs had a construction contract that rose a quarter or more above its first figure at least a year ago. 10 of them (43%) were awarded new construction contracts by the same department afterwards: 42 contracts with ceilings of $308.1 million. Of 32 pairs whose contract was closed out with no commission-approved increase on file, 16 (50%) were awarded new contracts afterwards. A gap that size in favour of the builders that finished near the original would arise by chance 42% of the time; in favour of those that grew, 77%. The most new work followed Esquivel Grading & Paving Inc (Public Works; 6th Street Pedestrian Safety Project (1000015023) rose +30%): 13 new contracts, $97.5 million. Construction contracts above a threshold go to the lowest responsive bid under the Administrative Code, and the Controller's 2014 audit found that past performance is not considered in the award. Nothing in these records shows a builder shut out, or favoured, after a contract grew.","800 modification items are on file; 359 print a reason that can be sorted and 175 are further phases of Airport design-build contracts. Among the reasons printed: delay, claims and settlements in 58 items ($199.4 million added by those items; most often Tutor Perini Corporation (4)); more capacity on an as-needed or task-order contract in 58 items ($58.1 million added by those items; most often Mik Construction Inc. (2)); conditions found on site in 48 items ($43.7 million added by those items; most often Sierra Mountain Construction Inc (4)); work the owner added or changed in 36 items ($200.1 million added by those items; most often Tutor Perini Corporation (7)); design changes, errors and omissions in 13 items ($14.7 million added by those items; most often Blocka Construction Inc (3)); prices and bids in 7 items ($115.9 million added by those items; most often San Francisco Elevator Services, LLC (1)); final quantities and close-out in 4 items ($655,549 added by those items; most often Arana Group Inc (1)). An item can give more than one reason, and the dollars of an item are counted under each reason it gives. By dollars the largest are work the owner added or changed ($200.1 million) and delay, claims and settlements ($199.4 million). The words kept say what changed, not who was at fault.","44 construction contracts show five or more modifications or change orders in the items on file, 20 of them ten or more. The most: Tutor Perini Corporation, Central Subway Project (1300) (Municipal Transportation Agency), 671. 6 of the 44 have an item that says in so many words what staff may approve without a further vote, such as an aggregate dollar amount the board set or a contingency the commission approved; the wording is in the last column. No document on disk publishes the individual change orders with their amounts, so whether changes were sized to stay under such a limit cannot be tested; the Budget and Legislative Analyst found the Public Utilities Commission keeps no central record of them for projects under $10 million.","305 projects of $1 million or more appear in three or more dated reports. 77 now stand a quarter or more above their first figure and 114 are forecast to finish two years or more after the first date given; 0 rose in at least half of their reports and 27 rose in three or more. Leaving aside 13 continuing renewal programmes, the largest move is SEP Biosolids Digester Facilities Project (Public Utilities Commission), $1.68 billion to $2.84 billion over 14 reports from 2020-12 to 2026-06, finish moved +20 months. Programme totals for the projects present in both the first and the latest report: Water Enterprise CIP (6 projects, 2020-09 to 2026-06): $1.15 billion to $2.94 billion (+155%); Central Subway Project (Third Street Light Rail Phase 2) (9 projects, 2015-03 to 2022-09): $3.29 billion to $4.33 billion (+32%); Wastewater Enterprise CIP / SSIP (13 projects, 2020-09 to 2026-06): $1.68 billion to $2.04 billion (+21%); WSIP (Regional) / Appendix B project list (51 projects, 2024-09 to 2026-06): $3.33 billion to $3.33 billion (-0%). These are project budgets, which include design, management and land as well as construction; a rise can be added scope as well as higher cost, and the reports name the builder on few of them.","The Controller's close-out memorandum on the Public Utilities Commission's Crystal Springs/San Andreas contract with Kiewit Infrastructure West Company records an original amount of $99.8 million and a final amount of $132.4 million after modifications and change orders, +33%. The Central Subway's main contract with Tutor Perini Corporation was awarded at $839.7 million and stood at $1.20 billion at Modification No. 147; the 146 amendments before it are described in the item as \"a 38 percent increase\". The largest construction-manager contract, Southeast Water Pollution Control Plant Biosolids Digester Facilities Project (Biosolids Project) with MWH Constructors/Webcor Builders Joint Venture, is one that the Commission amended to add work and price: $938.8 million before the first amendment on file and $2.25 billion after the latest. At the Airport, 35 design-build contracts are enlarged by serial \"Phase\" approvals rather than modifications; the largest, with Turner Construction Company for Terminal 3 West, went from $152.5 million at its first phase on file (2019-02-19) to $2.06 billion at phase 21 (2026-08-25). A phase approval funds the next part of a job whose full size was known; it is not a change order.","14 findings from 10 reports, 2014 to 2026. The most recent: Approved change orders added $186.8 million (8%) to 297 construction contracts at five departments, July 1, 2021 to June 30, 2024; by department the increase ran from 3 percent to 33 percent of the first contract value. Departments that build public works make little use of the contract terms meant to protect the City against delay, cost overruns and poor work by contractors. The contractor evaluation database was still not in use; departments lacked the leadership, guidelines, procedures and training to use it. The reviewers describe weak records and weak enforcement inside departments, and a contractor evaluation database that the law requires and departments do not use; none of the reports on disk ranks contractors by change orders.","101 owner-groups hold two or more differently named City payees on record evidence (tiers 1 to 4): 41 through the same owner account or ownership name in the City business registry, 10 through a State-numbered licensed person, 48 through a filing that names one as the trade name or related organization of the other (23 of them declared on a tax return) and 2 through an address plus a telephone or named owner. The second and later names in those groups were paid $1.23 billion since fiscal 2019; the largest registry pair is Mckesson Corporation ($567.5 million) and Mckesson Plasma and Biologics LLC ($185.2 million). A further 199 pairs of payees share only a suite-level address (tier 5) or an address and a surname (tier 6) and are leads, not groups.","None of the 108 bid lists carries two bidders that these files tie to one owner-group, one State-licensed person or one address. Of the 121 bidder names, 90 were found in the City business registry and 89 on a State contractor licence, so the test could see most but not all of the field.","5 department-and-vendor pairs show the pattern, 19 times in all: 3 pair(s) tied on record evidence (tiers 1 to 4) and 2 that share only an address. The most frequent: Public Utilities Commsn with Ferguson Enterprises Inc. and Ferguson Waterworks, 5 time(s), 3 of the 16 orders just under the limit. 69,068 delegated purchase orders at or under the limit were tested; in 45 department-and-group combinations two differently named vendors of one group or address pair both received such orders.","50 as-needed or pool contract titles have 3 or more different prime contractors. In 3 of them two members are tied: 0 set(s) on record evidence (tiers 1 to 4), 0 on a shared address only, and 3 where a firm holds its own place and is also a member of a joint venture in the same pool.","1 newer payee names are tied to a troubled one: 1 to an organization on the City’s suspended and debarred lists, 0 to a firm whose State contractor licence is shown as suspended, and 0 to a registration the Treasurer closed. The largest: KM 106 Construction Inc., paid $1,811,649 since, tied to Azul Works, Inc (5: same address only). 1 older affiliates that were already City payees more than a year before the action are left out, as is the one row already shown in the site’s “People and roles” page. 30 listed organizations and 16 payees with a suspended licence were tested; for a suspended licence the tied firm is listed only if its own licence was issued, or its City payments began, after July 2019.","Of 1,111 names shown with local-business status in the City’s contract file, 135 are tied to another name and 4 of those ties are to a firm without that status that is large or registered only outside California: 1 on record evidence (tiers 1 to 4) and 3 on a shared address only. The largest: Ransome Company beside Silverado Contractors Inc. (2: same licensed person). 739 names carry both statuses in different contracts. The State’s certified disadvantaged-business directory could not be downloaded, so it was not tested.","Eight places where public money moves, or fails to arrive, without a contract award that anyone votes on: rent and debts not collected, claims settled, gift cards and stipends, charges between departments, fiscal sponsors, benefit districts, housing loans and emergency hotels.  these screens set the amounts, the repeat parties and the trend beside what the City’s own auditors and financial reports say, and name the record that would settle what the published files cannot. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","30 Board files passed from 2014 to November 2026 give a tenant or concession holder of the City a rent credit (10), forgive or waive rent (2), cut a minimum guarantee or percentage rent (10), or waive the market-rent rule for a lease (3). 5 of them are standing authorities: the Airport, the Port and the Real Estate Division were allowed to forgive or reduce rent tenant by tenant without coming back to the Board, so the sums forgiven under them are in no Board file. The clause that grants the relief carries a dollar figure in 14 of the 30 files, in terms too mixed to add up (a sum a month, a ceiling, a new guarantee). The Airport’s own estimate for its pandemic programme, printed in the City’s fiscal 2020 financial report, was $21.8 million. Rents and concessions collected by all departments fell from $688.8 million in fiscal 2019 to $321.3 million in fiscal 2021 and were $810.8 million in fiscal 2026.","Nothing unusual in the last report on disk. The Port’s report for 2020-02 lists 70 tenant lines that paid $34.1 million in rent on $282.6 million of sales over the twelve months before. Full-service restaurants paid a middle 7.0% of sales (lowest 5.1%, highest 24%). 1 line paid half or less of the middle share for the category, and 10 paid only the minimum rent with nothing on top. The reports on disk run from 2015-11 to 2020-02 (50 months); none was found after that, so rent forgiven or deferred from March 2020 on cannot be tested from the published records.","The City’s financial reports show $993.5 million of patient charges at San Francisco General Hospital written off as bad debt over the eleven years to June 2025: $79.1 million in fiscal 2015, a peak of $136.0 million in fiscal 2020, and $66.1 million in fiscal 2025. Set against what the hospital billed, the share fell: 2.7% of gross charges in 2015 and 1.3% in 2025. It is not rising. Charity care is counted apart: charges of $336.0 million were forgone under the charity policy in fiscal 2025. The report says “Patient accounts receivables are recorded net of estimated allowances, which include contractual allowances, allowances for bad debt, and administrative write-offs”.","The Board passed 84 settlements since fiscal 2015 in which the other side pays the City; the 82 that name a figure add up to $651.5 million, and the three largest are 73% of that: Walgreen Co. ($229.6 million, 2023); Pacific Gas & Electric Company ($190.0 million, 2021); Academy of Art University ($58.0 million, 2020). A typical year has 8 such files. The titles state what the City accepts, not what it first claimed, so the discount in each settlement cannot be read from them.","The Board passed 620 settlements in which the City pays from fiscal 2017 to 2026 (tax refunds left out), naming $325.9 million: $110.3 million in the five years to fiscal 2021 and $215.6 million in the five since. By the titles’ own description the most money went to injury on a street, sidewalk, park or other City property ($64.0 million in 145 files); employment ($52.4 million in 128 files); civil rights violations alleged ($46.2 million in 63 files); damage to a hotel used as a pandemic shelter ($44.5 million in 9 files). Kinds that grew by half or more between the two halves: employment ($12.3 million to $40.1 million); vehicle collision ($10.4 million to $18.6 million); medical or residential care at a City hospital ($2.4 million to $16.1 million). One kind did not exist before: 9 settlements with hotel owners for damage to a hotel used as a pandemic shelter, $44.5 million, all passed in fiscal 2023 (set out under the hotel screens).","The ledger charges $223.8 million to judgments and claims in fiscal 2019 to 2022 and $333.9 million in fiscal 2023 to 2026 (1.49 times as much). 2 departments carried at least half as much again in the later four years, and at least $5 million: Municipal Transportation Agency ($25.6 million to $72.8 million); Public Utilities Commission ($38.7 million to $71.7 million). The account fell most at Public Health ($11.3 million to $1.2 million).","The payment file shows $290.9 million paid from the judgments and claims account from fiscal 2019 to fiscal 2027 to date. $57.9 million of it went to “single payment payees” and $11.1 million to payees that read as private persons; neither is listed here. 91 firms were paid $1 million or more, or $250,000 or more across three or more years (6 public bodies that meet the same test are listed too); 55 of the firms were paid in seven or more fiscal years. 32 have a name that reads as a law firm ($70.4 million between them). On the line for the City’s own litigation fees the largest are Nossaman LLP ($7.7 million, 9 years); Beveridge & Diamond P.C. ($7.0 million, 7 years); Morgan Lewis & Bockius LLP ($6.6 million, 10 years); Renne Public Law Group LLP ($6.3 million, 10 years). On the lines for judgments and claims paid out the largest are Walkup Melodia Kelly & Schoenberger ($21.4 million); Americania Hotel ($4.7 million); The Good Hotel ($3.9 million); Greene Radovsky Maloney Share & Hennigh ($3.6 million).","Purchase orders coded as gifts, gift certificates or gift cards add up to $2.8 million from fiscal 2019 to fiscal 2027 to date, most of it at Public Health ($1.4 million) and Human Services Agency ($951,790); the largest seller is Safeway Inc ($1.8 million). The peak was fiscal 2020 ($1.0 million) and the figure for fiscal 2026 is $92,646: by this measure the spending is small and falling. The measure is incomplete. The Controller’s auditors counted $2,780,149 of gift cards bought by the Human Services Agency alone between August 15, 2017 and March 8, 2021, where the purchasing file’s gift lines for that agency in fiscal 2019 to 2021 come to $936,387; the audit says the cards included “Visa ReliaCards, which are prepaid debit cards”, which these commodity codes would not catch. For Public Health the auditors counted $487,060 over three years against $765,104 in the file, so there the file shows more than the programmes reported.","Three Controller audits since 2023 examined gift cards and each found controls that needed work. At the Human Services Agency ($2.8 million in under four years) the auditors found no site visits to the organizations handing cards out and one programme that used 1 of the 500 cards it was given. At Public Health ($487,060 over three years) 14 of 18 programmes reported not following at least one control, and 11 let one employee handle every step. At the Human Rights Commission $20,507 of cards was paid for with no approved policy and no proof of purchase. The reports record cards lost at two Public Health programmes and 25 cards handed to someone who was not a client; what all three have in common is records too thin to account for every card.","The ledger charges $16.7 million to its “Stipends” account from fiscal 2019 to 2026; the payment file, which removes many payments to individuals, shows $11.6 million through fiscal 2027 to date. The largest payee is a bank: US Bank National Association received $7.7 million, $7.1 million of it from Economic & Workforce Development in fiscal 2021 to 2025. The file does not say who received the stipends that passed through the bank, how many people, or in what form. $2.4 million went to payees that read as private persons (not named here), most of it from Arts Commission. Payments peaked at $2.9 million in fiscal 2023 and were $516,923 in fiscal 2026.","96 organizations paid $100,000 or more by the City in the year of their latest return report grants or assistance to individuals in that return, $571.5 million between them; most of that is food, child-care subsidies and rent paid on people’s behalf. 30 of them label $25,000 or more of it as stipends, cash, gift cards, incentives or financial assistance: $25.9 million in all, led by Catholic Charities ($16.9 million); Compass Family Services ($1.8 million); PRC ($1.3 million); Jewish Voc & Career Counseling Svc (Jvs) ($1.2 million). The returns do not say which funder’s money paid for these lines.","Departments charged one another $1.34 billion in fiscal 2026, against $909.7 million in fiscal 2019 (47% more). Day-to-day spending rose 46% over the same years, so the share barely moved: 7.6% then, 7.7% now, and between 7.0% and 8.9% in every year since fiscal 1999 that the ledger records in full. Nothing unusual in the total. What the Board’s analyst found in April 2025 was about management: “appropriations for interdepartmental services had between $53 and $80 million across all funds remaining at year-end”; of 48 charges sampled, “departments only provided MOUs for half”; and “At least two interdepartmental service agreements in our sample had hourly rates that exceeded at least some private-sector rates for similar services”.","Of 30 departments charged $5 million or more by other departments in fiscal 2026, 12 saw those charges grow at least 25 points faster than their own day-to-day spending: Emergency Management (charges 155%, own spending 62%; largest rise in the line “GF-Social Services”, $14.3 million); Juvenile Probation (charges 129%, own spending 37%; largest rise in the line “GF-Children;Youth;& Family Svc”, $2.6 million); District Attorney (charges 94%, own spending 38%; largest rise in the line “Leases Paid To Real Estate”, $7.0 million); Fine Arts Museum (charges 63%, own spending 7.0%; largest rise in the line “GF-PUC-Light Heat & Power”, $1.3 million); Retirement System (charges 43%, own spending -0.8%; largest rise in the line “GF-City Attorney-Legal Service”, $1.3 million). 5 went the other way. The largest buyer is Public Health, charged $210.8 million in fiscal 2026 (5.9% of its day-to-day spending).","5 departments recovered half or more of their day-to-day spending from other departments in fiscal 2026: Technology (89%, $157.0 million); Human Resources (85%, $128.7 million); Controller (77%, $68.4 million); City Attorney (71%, $94.3 million); City Administrator (63%, $325.3 million). Their cost appears in the budgets of the departments they bill, not as a budget line of their own, which is what an internal charge is for and also what makes it hard to see in a budget hearing. Recoveries at least doubled since fiscal 2019 at Emergency Management ($2.2 million to $15.8 million); Treasurer-Tax Collector ($4.2 million to $16.4 million); Homelessness Services ($5.5 million to $19.7 million); Fire Department ($5.9 million to $17.0 million).","In May 2021 the commission approved a contract with Cityspan Technologies for up to $340,000 from July 2021 through June 2026 (a base of $60,000 a year plus a one-time migration), described in the minutes as a sole source. The same agreement number (1000022730) now carries an agreed amount of $2.2 million in the City's supplier-contracts table; $1.1 million has been paid on it, $830,000 of that in fiscal 2026 and 2027 under the title 'DEC FY26 Contract Mgt System'. That is inside a later approval: on June 7, 2023 the commission approved an amendment for a total not to exceed $3.3 million from July 2021 through June 2028, for a new contract-management system for the Department of Early Childhood; the motion carried unanimously. Nothing unusual remains in the amounts; the contract is still a sole source.","The City's directory of 973 certified local businesses records, for 166 of them, affiliated firms whose participation they cannot be credited with. This section sets that record, and the firms' shared addresses, telephones and email domains, against the City's contract teams and payments.","The directory export lists 973 certified firms: 904 LBE, 53 PLBE, 16 NLBE. 295 are in District 10. 166 carry participation-credit restrictions naming 229 affiliated firms. By name, 662 directory firms appear on a team in the Controller's contract file and 444 were paid directly by the City since fiscal 2019 ($3.46 billion in all). No City file on disk carries the directory's supplier numbers, so every join is by name.","On 38 contracts, a subcontractor carrying the local-business flag works under a prime contractor or joint-venture member that the directory records as its affiliate (38 pairings, 8 distinct firm pairs, $6.4 million in listed subcontract shares). The most frequent: LAVINA TRUCKING, INC. under ESQUIVEL GRADING & PAVING INC, on 22 contracts ($5.1 million). 70 more pairings of recorded affiliates on one team are of other kinds (two subcontractors, or the certified firm as the prime).","166 directory firms name 229 restricted affiliates; 62 affiliate names are City payees or prime contractors by name. In 19 the affiliate is not itself certified and was paid at least $5.0 million and 5 times what the certified firm was paid since fiscal 2019; the largest: Architectural Material Solutions, Inc. beside CLARK CONSTRUCTION GROUP - CALIF LP ($708.6 million).","28 sets of two or more directory firms share an identifier (62 firms). 20 sets include a pair the directory does not record as affiliates, and in 5 of those all members appear on at least one contract team together (largest: AZUL WORKS INC; KM 106 Construction Inc., same street address, together on 4 contract(s)).","14 pairings of a directory firm with a larger or out-of-State firm: 12 without a recorded participation-credit restriction between them (1 through an owner-group on record evidence, the rest by a shared registry address). The largest undeclared: Urban West Construction Inc. beside Ranger Pipelines Inc. ($128.7 million; in one owner-group (the “aliases” analysis, evidence tier 2)).","114 names flagged as local businesses on teams starting on or after 2025-01-01 are not found by name in the directory export; the largest local-business share is MITCHELL ENGINEERING ($129.8 million). Each may be a spelling difference, a certification that lapsed after award, or a nonprofit the contract file flags; each is a check, not a finding.","1 directory firm is named in the Controller's debarment and suspension reports: AZUL WORKS INC (reports 2020-12-31 to 2025-06-30, certified to 2028-10-31, on the current page: no). 3 more directory firms are linked to a listed firm by a shared address, telephone, email domain or contact, or by a recorded affiliation.","The Federal Transit Administration’s profile gives the cost under the 2012 grant agreement as $1.58 billion, with $942.2 million federal, and service due in December 2018; in December 2017 the project was about 69% complete and a year late. By June 2022 the agency’s own report put the estimate at completion at $1.93 billion, $353.0 million (22%) above the original budget, with opening expected in November 2022, nearly four years after the baseline.","52 of 74 large firms tied to a certified local firm (recorded affiliate, same team or same address) turn up in other screens; 24 of them in a lobbying, donor, commissioner or permit-speed screen. Most often: HELUNA HEALTH (affiliate of Immaculate Concepts International LLC), in 12 groups: aliases: One owner, several names, all paid by the City; autopilot: Still paid after the term ran out; chains: Hired after a contract and 3 more.","5 contracts in the bid-sheet screens were won by a prime whose recorded affiliate is the certified local subcontractor on the same team; 1 of these was awarded inside a local-business bid discount rather than to the lowest bidder ($163,257 above the lowest bid in all). Across the contract file, LAVINA TRUCKING, INC. is listed under its affiliate ESQUIVEL GRADING & PAVING INC on 22 contracts, $5.1 million in listed subcontract shares.","4 directory firms are on, or linked to a firm on, the City's debarment reports. AZUL WORKS INC is itself listed (agreement not to do business with the city pending criminal case and stipulated stay of debarment proceedings, reports to 2025-06-30) and its certification runs to 2028-10-31; it appears in 9 published screens elsewhere. Terocons Inc., a recorded affiliate of the listed firm, also names SIERRA MOUNTAIN CONSTRUCTION INC as an affiliate (paid $106.9 million since fiscal 2019). ","The profiles put the project at 31% complete in January 2015, 53% in December 2015, 64% in December 2016 and 69% in December 2017: gains of 22, 11 and 5 points a year. Every profile repeats the 2012 grant agreement's $1.58 billion cost; the December 2016 one first says opening is a year past December 2018. Congress had appropriated $919.2 million of the federal share by then.","The core bus project was costed at $125.6 million in November 2012, $162.1 million when the federal agency rated it in 2014 and $189.5 million at its highest (October 2016). The whole Van Ness Improvement Project, with its sewer, water and signal work, went from $250.0 million (2014) to $346.0 million. Opening, set for 2018 in the 2014 profile, slipped to early 2019, late 2019, December 2021 and the first half of 2022; the project page says service began April 2022. The contractor claimed $6.7 million and $12 million for delays in 2019; the project started with $41.8 million in contingency and had $19.2 million left in January 2021. A June 2021 Civil Grand Jury report reviewed the delays.","19 award memos from 2015 to 2026 are on disk. 8 of them drew two offers or fewer, $31.0 million together, including the $135,000 state-lobbying contract (one bid, 2015), the $22.1 million Hillcrest Road construction contract (two bids, 2024) and the $4.35 million island path design (two proposals, 2024). Others drew many: 43 statements for the $8 million on-call engineering list, 31 proposals for the website.","Three design contracts with WMH Corporation started at $5.5 million together and stand at $25.0 million after the amendments the memos ask for: the west-side bridges contract went from $1.6 million in 2010 to $17.8 million in 2023 (11.11 times), Hillcrest Road from $2.7 million to $3.8 million, and the island path from $1.1 million to $3.4 million within two years. The bridges construction-management contract with WSP doubled, $5.5 million to $11.5 million. WMH was also the higher-ranked of two proposers for the 2021 freeway managed-lanes study, and the island path design drew two proposals. ","The four saved pages of the filtered search show 38 readable project numbers (the owner's note says the saved pages are the first 80 of 5,594 rows for 19 San Francisco awarding bodies). One project was opened in full: SFMTA Potrero Yard Modernization Project (Design / Build, San Francisco Municipal Transportation Agency, awarded 2026-03-24), listing 5 contractor entries. KPFF, INC. (listed as contractor): no City payment under this name; PHOENIX ELECTRIC COMPANY (listed as contractor): paid $7.1 million by the City since fiscal 2019; PRG - Potrero Properties LLC (listed as contractor): paid $65.0 million by the City since fiscal 2019; PRG - Potrero Properties LLC (listed as subcontractor): paid $65.0 million by the City since fiscal 2019; WEBCOR CONSTRUCTION L P (listed as contractor): paid $401.3 million by the City since fiscal 2019 as WEBCOR BUILDERS.","The Airport's procurement portal lists 10 closed construction solicitations; bid results are posted for 3. 12184.51 (As-Needed Agreement: Airfield Electrical Testing, Repairs an): 1 bid, lowest BASS Electric at $5.7 million against an engineer's estimate of $5.0 million (113%); 10672.61 (5kV Cable Replacement in Field Lighting Building (FLB) No. 2): 4 bids, lowest TJ Gardner Company at $2.5 million against an engineer's estimate of $5.1 million (49%); 11939.61 (NIP, EEI 2024 Phase, Group 1): 4 bids, lowest Pro-Ex Contstruction, Inc. at $644,000 against an engineer's estimate of $748,736 (86%). The only bidder on 12184.51, BASS Electric, was prime on 6 Airport contracts in the Controller's file, the latest with a like title being AIR11643.50;ASNAirfield Elec. (2021). 2 bidders on the same job also appear as prime and subcontractor to one another on other Airport contracts. Each portal entry says \"Awarded\" but the files do not say to whom.","The directory export lists 210 firms (228 certifications: 200 DBE, 26 ACDBE) in Alameda 93, San Francisco 71, Contra Costa 30, San Mateo 13, Marin 3; Caltrans certified 65, BART certified 62, SFMTA certified 52, SFO certified 15. By name, 43 were paid by the City since fiscal 2019 ($148.6 million), 68 appear on City contract teams and 54 are also in the City's local-business directory; the largest City payee is R&S Construction Management Inc. ($45.8 million, mostly Public Works). 10 certified firms share a telephone, street address or company email domain with another certified firm.","19 contracts carry an original amount and a later \"New Contract Amount\" in their notes; together they grew by $294,895,536, and 4 grew tenfold or more. Largest: Rudolph And Sletten Inc., $1,100,000 to $153,799,305 (139.82 times). The Student Success Center design-build contract with XL Construction began as $900,000 of schematic design and reached $119,130,502 after 21 change memos.","Of 6,436 purchase orders read (2021-01-04 to 2025-08-29), 257 are marked \"no bid\" ($2,500,316) and 139 \"sole source\" ($4,652,107). Largest sole-source suppliers: Ellucian Company LLC $1,295,320; Pacific Auxiliary Fire Alarm Co. $1,053,521; Alcatel-Lucent Enterprise USA Inc. $238,294; an individual contractor $199,500. \"No bid\" appears mostly in the 2021 reports, on small supply orders; from 2023 the notes cite bids, piggyback contracts or sole-source files instead.","3 clusters, $438,209 in all, 2023-2025. Largest: Blackhawk Network, Inc., 3 orders from 2023-04-18 to 2023-04-19 totalling $159,338 against a limit of $109,300.","All 7 companies are shown as active; none is suspended or forfeited. The filings confirm three ties the cases drew from tax returns: Resilient Strategies' only manager is Tim Ly, the Chinese Progressive Association's chief financial officer, at the Oakland suite the Association itself reports to the charity registry; Urban Place Consulting Group's chief executive is Stephen Gibson (Mid Market Foundation's unpaid executive director is \"Steve Gibson\"); and Jordan Langer runs NPU, Inc., the lender to Project Wreckless. They weaken one: JIA Technologies' filings name a different officer, not the former Lower Polk director. Azul Works Holding (the closest match to the debarred Azul Works, Inc) and OBS Engineering were incorporated three weeks apart in 2016 by the same incorporator, and OBS now lists the suite named in Azul's articles.","Each packet from 2022-11-07 to 2024-07-15 carries Human Resources’ cost summary of the personal-services contracts it processed in the fiscal year to date. Fiscal 2022-23 reached $3.60 billion by the meeting of 2023-06-05; fiscal 2023-24 reached $4.45 billion by 2024-06-03, including $444.9 million it lists as expedited approvals. The largest single packets were 2023-07-17 ($1.63 billion), 2023-03-06 ($976.1 million), 2024-07-15 ($430.1 million). The packets after July 2024 stop printing the summary.","The packets on disk list 960 requests at 68 meetings from 2022-11-07 to 2026-11-16: 692 new and 268 amendments of earlier approvals. The amounts that can be read add to $10.74 billion (50 requests without a readable amount). Airport Commission $4.38 billion over 100 requests; Public Health $1.65 billion over 154 requests; Public Utilities Commission $1.25 billion over 160 requests.","The largest: Airport Commission $1.50 billion (2023-07-17, West Field Area Projects); Airport Commission $700.0 million (2023-03-06, 40697-14/15); Municipal Transportation Agency $351.0 million (2024-07-15, Train control system design and procurement). 15 requests were for $100 million or more.","Of 865 request forms read, 181 ($1.79 billion) answer that City job classes or the department’s own employees have the skills: on the newer form, 164 of 242 answers to “Does City have classifications with the required specialized skills” are yes. A further 225 older forms name the civil-service classes that “normally perform” the work. The largest of the first kind: Public Health $162.6 million (Behavioral health services in locked facilities); Public Health $86.2 million (Mental Health Services Act Peer Programs); Public Utilities Commission $75.0 million (PRO.0333 As Needed Environmental Services). Across all forms, 40 requests ($726.5 million) give vacancies, attrition, a hiring freeze or too few staff as a reason for going outside; 8 mention the freeze.","289 requests say the work was contracted out before (in the last three years, or under an earlier approval). Of 301 newer forms that answer Q5, 241 call the need long-term rather than temporary; of 241 that answer Q5f, 209 say there is no plan to transition the work back to City staff.","93 amendments heard from 2022-11-07 on enlarge an approval first given six or more fiscal years earlier (by the fiscal year in its number). Airport Commission 40697-14/15 to $1.60 billion; Public Health DHRPSC0004810 to $647.9 million (Substance Use Disorder (SUD) Treatment Services); Airport Commission 43332-16/17 to $420.0 million (Project Management Support Services & Design Build for …).","25 new requests give a start date more than 30 days before the meeting that heard them. Technology 44123-22/23 ($7.5 million, started 2015-11-01, heard 2022-11-07); City Administrator 46300-23/24 ($2.5 million, started 2020-08-01, heard 2024-01-30); Technology 46561-22/23 ($5.2 million, started 2020-08-28, heard 2023-01-25).","The packets record 107 objections or appeals against a request (45 tied to a request read here). Most often named: IFPTE Local 21 (44), SEIU Local 1021 (34), Local 6 (3). Largest: Municipal Transportation Agency $351.0 million (2024-07-15); Homelessness and Supportive Housing $77.0 million (2025-03-03); Public Health $53.0 million (2025-08-11).","6 requests went to the Commission’s regular agenda as separate items, worth $86.4 million. In one, a factfinder found the transportation agency had not shown a compelling reason, and the agency acknowledged it did no formal study of using City staff; in two, the Commission had asked the department to look at doing the work itself, and both departments recommended staying with the contractor.","1,175 certified firm names (the City's local-business directory and the State directory for five Bay Area counties) were set against 1,725 contractor names on contract notices and against the business registry. None shares a telephone or email domain with a larger uncertified City payee beyond the affiliates the directory itself records. 16 pairings rest on a suite alone, each in an office that other registered businesses also use.","Of 1,015 payees paid $1 million or more since fiscal 2019 that could be placed at one registered San Francisco address, 34 are registered only on parcels the assessor classes as a house, condominium, small flats or an empty lot; for 10 a contract notice or directory entry gives the same address. The largest confirmed: Treeline Security Inc. ($22.8 million, mostly GSA - City Administrator, house, condominium or small flats). Most are consultants and small builders whose office is the owner's home; nothing here shows work billed from a place that does not exist.","462 judgments name a San Francisco address; 181 give a suite. At 3 suites a differently named business the City paid $100,000 or more after the judgment is registered beside the debtor. In 1 the two also use one trade name: Zenith Health Network LLC ($747,541 paid since) beside Mega Protect Services LLC (judgment J-93501 of 2025-01-27, $25,998, status open/unpaid; 2 more against people at the same suite on the same day). The rest are neighbours in multi-tenant suites.","The City, the schools, the transit agencies, the State and Washington each keep their own lists of contractors, bids, wage findings and barred firms. This page joins them: who was suspended, charged or excluded on one list and kept working on another.","33 firms on the City's suspension and debarment reports were tested. 1 firm appears on City contract teams that began after the City acted: Kwan Wo Ironworks Inc.. Kwan Wo Ironworks Inc. is listed as a subcontractor on 4 Public Works contracts that began between 19 December 2024 and 19 February 2026, $3.4 million agreed to it and $452,237 recorded as paid; 4 of the 4 began before 1 March 2026, the end date printed for the debarment. The Controller's reports list a suspension of the firm, its owner and SFR Recovery Inc. \"issued and effective on 3/1/2021\" (6 reports, June 30, 2021 to December 31, 2023); the reports from June 30, 2024 to December 31, 2025 instead print \"Florence Kong, Kin Wo Construction, Inc. and SFR Recovery, Inc.\", debarred for five years to 3/1/2026. Kin Wo Construction Inc is a separate registry account (0300631) that ended on 2020-12-31; Kwan Wo Ironworks Inc. (account 0913293, State licence 643621, clear) is still registered. 1 listed firm received City payments in a fiscal year after the year of the action: A&B Construction (Andrew M. Jordan, Inc.) ($10.1 million; agreed in a stipulation of 4 December 2023 not to take part in City contracting for three years).","2,293 federal press releases since 2015 were searched for the names of City payees and contract-team members. 4 firms are tied to a release by an officer or owner who was charged or convicted, and were paid by the City or joined a new City contract team afterwards; 1 more is named in a headline about a charge, conviction or settlement. Empire Engineering & Construction Inc: its president was charged on 7 April 2017 and convicted on 20 February 2019 in a scheme to submit rigged bids for a federal building contract; the federal exclusion list shows the firm suspended since 6 November 2017 (SBA, \"Ineligible (Proceedings Pending)\", no end date). In the fiscal years after the charge the City paid it $7.8 million and it joined 8 new contract teams ($5.9 million agreed); 5 of those began after the federal suspension ($4.6 million). It is in none of the City's twelve debarment reports on disk. Townsend Management Inc: a vice president was charged in the same release and convicted on 20 February 2019; in later fiscal years the City paid the firm $5.6 million and its joint venture TMI/CMP JV $10.7 million. The State licence file says of that person: left State licence 861127 on 22 October 2018.","The federal list holds 35,252 records for firms and other organisations. 5 names on it match a City payee (paid $5,000 or more since fiscal 2019), a contract-team member, another body's contractor or a bidder; 1 match is confirmed by the same street address: Empire Engineering & Construction Inc. (SBA, ineligible (proceedings pending), active 2017-11-06; City paid $7.2 million since fiscal 2019, $7.2 million of it in later fiscal years, 5 contract teams begun afterwards). For the other 4 the records do not show whether the excluded firm and the City payee are the same organisation; most are excluded in another State or country. None of the matches is a contractor of another local body or a bidder on the bid sheets.","462 Labor Commissioner judgments against San Francisco employers, 451 federal wage cases in the Bay Area closed since 2015 (those on government contracts, or with $10,000 or more of back wages), 295 State prevailing-wage decisions and 57 State public-works debarments were set against City payees, contract teams, the other public bodies' contractors and the bid sheets. 14 judgments, 19 federal cases, 6 State decisions and 0 State debarments match a name; 19 of the 39 matches are confirmed by the same street address. 4 of the matches (two firms) are contractors of another public body; none is a bidder on the bid sheets. The most recent case on public work: Mark Scott Construction, Inc., 3 federal cases with findings periods from October 2024 to May 2025, $74,354 of back wages under the federal construction-wage law; the City paid the firm $6.4 million since fiscal 2019, last in fiscal 2026, almost all before those cases. No firm with a State debarment or a recent prevailing-wage finding is shown winning new City work afterwards.","Of 39 matched contracts won by the lowest bidder where the second bid is known, payments already exceed the second-lowest bid on 3: Bay Area Lightworks Inc, PW Cntrt No.36 Trffc Sgnl Mod (Public Works): bid $3.4 million, next bid $4.4 million, paid $5.7 million; Hoseley Corporation, PRT CT2786 P94 BACKLANDS (Port): bid $6.7 million, next bid $6.8 million, paid $7.5 million; Chiang C.M. Construction Inc, PW SF RENT BOARD TNNT IMPR (Public Works): bid $1.4 million, next bid $1.5 million, paid $1.6 million. On 6 more the ceiling, which includes contingency the contractor may never be paid, stands above the second bid. Together the 3 were paid $2.2 million more than the runner-up's price.","23 pairs of firms met on six or more of the 92 contested bid sheets. The pair that most often took the low bid between them is Esquivel Grading and Paving, Inc and Ronan Construction, Inc.: one of them was lowest on 9 of the 10 sheets they both entered (5 and 4), where their records on other sheets would predict about 5.0; the other was second on 4, and the typical gap between them was 13%. 1 pair has two or more sheets where the loser was within 2% of the winner. No pair shows the pattern of a cover bid, a strict alternation with the loser a few percent above. 26 firms bid at two or more of the three agencies.","3 firms paid $100,000 or more by the City since fiscal 2019 under a no-competition rule also appear in another body's papers as a sole-source, single-source or no-bid purchase: 3 firm-and-body pairs, $211,944 as read at the other bodies and $11.6 million at the City. The overlap is small and ordinary: Seneca Family of Agencies (City College (purchase-order report), $184,033; City $10.2 million); Wested (City College (purchase-order report), $27,911; City $560,843); Oclc Inc. (City College (purchase-order report), $0; City $802,508). None of the large sole-source purchases at BART or the bridge district is from a firm the City also pays without competition. 6,194 City College order lines were read for this.","The Parks Alliance public-space grant, the park scores at its sites, and the citywide pest contractor's records set against its pay. Park scores show nothing unusual; the pest records thin out in one year while payments do not.","In FY2022 the feed holds 725 treatment records, against a median of 4007 in the other full years, while the Public Utilities Commission paid Agurto Corporation (Pestec) $825,549 from its wastewater operating fund, within the range of other years ($620,777 to $967,176). Empty months are common in every year; FY2022 stands out for volume, at 18% of the median year.","The City Administrator's citywide pest management contracts with Agurto Corporation (Pestec) were set at $9,990,000 (2012), $9,900,000 (2017) and $9,950,000 (2021); the fourth, from February 2025, is $15,000,000 and went to the Board. The 2017 and 2021 contracts paid out $8,818,892 and $8,728,766. Across all departments the contract file holds 364 contracts from $9.5 million to just under $10 million against 39 from $10 million to $10.5 million.","Most organisations the City pays to cook or hand out food have clean food-safety records. Two programme kitchens were closed once each, and the inspection files are nearly empty for the first months of the emergency meal contracts.","48 City-paid food vendors were found in the health department's inspection files on two keys (name and their own address), 185 facilities in all. Of the 23 paid at least $250,000 under meal or food programme contracts ($300.1 million, fiscal 2019 to 2027), 3 of 55 facilities (5.5%) ever had a closure, conditional pass or score under 71, against 13.1% of all 13,625 inspected facilities. Two programme kitchens were closed: the Salvation Army's 407 9th Street kitchen (15 and 16 May 2024) and Centro Latino's 1656 15th Street senior meal site (12 January 2023).","In 2019 the files hold about 972 inspections a month; April to June 2020 hold 70 in all, November 2019 holds 1, December 2019 to February 2020 and September to December 2023 hold none. The facilities at SMG's registered addresses (Moscone Center and Bill Graham Civic Auditorium) have 0 inspections dated April 2020 to June 2021 (76 in all years, none poor).","Of 115 Board files whose executed agreements and modifications from before the Board item are in this download, 19 had a last ceiling between $9.5 million and $10 million; 10 of them are Airport agreements.","17 department, supplier and start-date combinations each have at least two contracts with ceilings from $9.5 million to under $10 million, covering 37 contract numbers. The lower band contains 363 contracts across 343 combinations in total. The $10 million to under $10.5 million band contains 39 contracts across 39 combinations, of which 0 have at least two contracts."],["Spending against the official counts, system by system.","In the fiscal 2026 federal renewal list, 22 San Francisco projects with a stated number of homes have a middle budget of $36,028 a home a year. 3 are at one and a half times that or more, led by Housing For Survivors at $104,284 a home, of which 56% is services rather than rent. A further 7 housing projects worth $8.3 million list no number of homes at all.","Of 119 subsidised buildings inspected since 2022, 3 scored under 60 out of 100, the federal failing line, and 5 fell 15 points or more from their previous inspection; the middle score is 92. The lowest is Plaza (1230 Eddy St) at 29 on 2025-04-01, down from 82 in 2017. When the Housing Authority's own estates were last scored (2013–2017), 11 of 27 were under 60.","The City spends hundreds of millions of dollars on buildings through purchases, leases and housing loans that never appear as an ordinary contract. This page sets each deal beside its peers, the assessor’s record and the people on the other side. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","The Board approved 41 purchases of property inside the city since 2014, $1,045,663,961 in all (including one $326,690,953 deal to have a new City office building built); 9 of them, $370,000,000, were buildings for homeless housing. Where the number of homes is known (9 buildings: 7 of the homeless-housing purchases and 2 other residential buildings) the price runs from $200,000 a home (333-7th Street) to $852,941 (333-12th Street), with a middle value of $268,919. The two official counts of homes at 333-12th Street differ: the State’s Homekey award list gives 170 ($852,941 a home) and the Board’s Budget and Legislative Analyst, in the report on the purchase file and in the 2024 audit, gives 200 ($725,000 a home). The records do not say why the counts differ.","The Budget and Legislative Analyst counted 8 homeless-housing sites approved since 2020–21 that cost $395,650,000 to buy, before repairs. For the two it re-checked, repairs rose from $15,602,150 to $85,309,595, $69,707,445 more (447%); at 1000 Sutter Street the estimate went from $6,540,000 to $67,459,595.","4 finished buildings had last changed hands within four years of the public purchase, 3 of them within two. The clearest case: 681-687 Ellis Street last changed hands in 2021-06, was carried by the assessor at $14,280,000 afterwards, and was sold to the City for $19,900,000 in a file introduced in 2022-04, 39% more.","The Board approved 126 leases or lease changes since 2014 with the City as tenant, $116,674,873 a year in first-year rent where the title states it. Of the 59 with both rent and floor area, 3 cost at least 1.5 times the middle rent of their period; 2 renewals raised the rent by 8% a year or more over the previous file; 11 were approved after the term had already begun.","61 San Francisco developments in State tax-credit staff reports from 2018 to 2026 budget $5,950,063,584 for 7,158 homes. New-build cost per home has a middle value of $943,615; the middle rose from $750,174 in 2018 to $976,163 in 2026. The dearest is Sunnydale HOPE SF Block 7 at $1,203,262 a home.","5 developments returned to the Board for a larger City loan, $40,099,620 more in total than first approved. The largest increase was 4840 Mission Street (4840 Mission Associates, L.P.), from $18,510,000 in 2019 to $37,728,757 in 2023.","Across 228 City purchases, leases, loans and sales with a known address, 1 pairing with an official’s declared property within 150 metres turned up, involving 1 official; 0 sponsored the file. The nearest is Aaron Peskin (supervisor), who declared property on the 500 block of Filbert Street, 93 metres from 777 Broadway Street, 1525 Grant Avenue, 1207 Mason Street (city housing loan, 2022).","6 City-funded organizations told the IRS they have a lease or rent arrangement with a board member, officer, founder or relative in which the organization is not described as the landlord, $956,301 over the returns on file; the largest is 18 Reasons with Sam Mogannam (board member), $187,245. 2 more collect rent from insiders ($4,060,378; a wholesale market whose merchant tenants sit on its board, and a hospital renting to its doctors), and 31 reported leases with related organizations they control or are controlled by ($113,661,853).","The Real Estate Division’s report of April 2025 lists 128 leases with the City as tenant, from 100 named landlords (1 with no landlord named), covering 4,641,796 square feet where an area is given; 62% of that area is rented from other public bodies. Among the 79 private and nonprofit landlords, Trinity Center LLC has the most leases (3) and Hudson Pacific Properties -Hudson 1455 Market St. LLC the most floor area (157,154 square feet). 8 private landlord names match a lobbyist client or a campaign-donation record exactly: 200 Paul LLC; 33 Gough LLC; 333 Valencia Owner LLC; The Salvation Army; 101 South Van Ness, LLC; Larkin Street Youth Services; HealthRight360; SF LGBT Community Center. The report gives no rents, so none are shown.","The State's scorecard grows harsher with each round. Against its 2022 starting point (round 4) San Francisco shows progress on 6 of 7 measures; against 2023 (round 5) on 4; against 2024 (round 6) on 1, and the State's column for whether enough measures were met reads \"No\" for round 6. On its own target for people sleeping outside, San Francisco aimed to go from 5,180 (2020) to 5,000 by 2024 and the State file shows 4,354: met. For round 4 it aimed for 4,177 by 2025 from 4,397; the State file shows 4,354, short by 177. People leaving homelessness for permanent housing fell in every round's comparison, while placements from street outreach went from 187 in 2022 to 1,908 in 2025.","Among the ten largest areas San Francisco slid from near the top to the bottom of the State's scorecard. It was marked as progressing on 6, 4 and 1 of seven measures for rounds 4, 5 and 6, against a middle figure of 4.5, 4 and 2: 1 area(s) did better and 6 worse in round 4, 3 area(s) did better and 4 worse in round 5, 8 area(s) did better and 0 worse in round 6. 1 of the 9 areas with a round 4 target for people sleeping outside reached it (Sacramento City & County); San Francisco was 177 over its target of 4,177, a cut of 5% from its 2022 count where the middle target among these areas was a cut of 12%.","San Francisco reported $187.8 million of State homelessness grant money committed across rounds 1 to 5 as of 12/31/2025, $130.0 million of it spent. Two contractors account for 59%: Five Keys Schools And Programs ($75.9 million) and Urban Alchemy ($34.2 million). 1 contractor(s) with $1 million or more committed show nothing spent yet: Glide Foundation ($9.6 million, rounds 4, 5). 14 of the 28 named contractors carry warning signs elsewhere in this project. The State's fiscal snapshot shows the awards for rounds 1 100% spent, 2 100% spent, 3 100% spent, 4 82% spent, 5 39% spent, 6 0% spent.","The assessor’s roll records when each building last changed hands and the value that sale set. This page reads 19 yearly rolls, 2007 to 2025, for every building the City bought, rents or funds, and sets those dates and values beside what the public paid.","41 of the 43 purchases could be matched to a parcel; the rolls from 2007 to 2025 show at least one earlier sale date for 29 and a value re-set by that sale for 22. Among the 8 finished buildings that can be compared like for like, the widest gaps are 888 Post Street ($9,999,000 on the 2020 roll after a sale dated 3 July 2019, $29,000,000 paid 1.9 years later, +190%; work added in between); 3055-3061 16th Street ($1,967,514 on the 2016 roll after a sale dated 13 May 2014, $5,600,000 paid 7.3 years later, +185%; no change shown on the roll); 1000 Sutter Street (Hotel Granada) ($30,074,998 on the 2020 roll after a sale dated 25 July 2019, $46,000,000 paid 1.3 years later, +53%; too soon for the roll to show any change); 681-687 Ellis Street ($14,280,000 on the 2022 roll after a sale dated 14 June 2021, $19,900,000 paid 0.8 years later, +39%; too soon for the roll to show any change). At 888 Post Street the City was already the tenant when it bought (Base Annual Rent $1,500,000, Landlord Work $5,000,000 under file 200043). In 4 purchases the price was below the assessed value the roll carried just before, among them 1660 and 1670 Mission Street ($18,500,000 against $48,512,468). Where a later roll can be checked against a stated price it matches: in 2 of 2 nonprofit purchases the next roll equals the Budget Analyst’s price to the dollar ($46,000,000 and $50,000,000).","The prior sale date falls within one year of the public purchase in 2 cases, within two in 5 and within three in 8; 3 of those are finished buildings comparable like for like: 681-687 Ellis Street (295 days, +39%); 1000 Sutter Street (Hotel Granada) (477 days, +53%); 888 Post Street (727 days, +190%). 3 more fall between three and four years. 12 matched purchases show no earlier sale date on any roll from 2007 to 2025; for 3 of them the land value was nonetheless re-set in some year, which means a change of ownership or reassessment the roll does not date.","11 purchases of finished residential buildings have a known number of homes. For 11 of them at least three multi-unit buildings show a sale date in the two years before (10 within the same neighbourhood, the rest only citywide). The public price per home was 0.4 to 2.2 times the middle value per unit those sales set; 2 were at 1.5 times or more, led by 333-12th Street at $852,941 a home against $392,854 (40 sales). Against every multi-unit building in the neighbourhood, sold or not, the price per home runs from 0.6 to 5.4 times the middle assessed value per unit, which mostly measures how long the neighbours have been held.","122 of the 126 Board files with the City as tenant could be matched to a parcel, at 78 sites; the rolls give a sale date before the lease for 72. 7 new leases were introduced within two years after the building’s last sale date (945 Bryant Street, 162 days; 888 Post Street, 195 days; 1360 Mission Street, 256 days; 250 Kearny Street, 371 days; 258A Laguna Honda Boulevard, 475 days; 2000 Oakdale Avenue, 508 days; 752 Vallejo Street, 628 days); 2 buildings changed hands again within two years after a new City lease (945 Bryant Street, sale dated 1 May 2019, value set $42,791,550 against $27,540,000 on the roll before; 1076 Howard Street, sale dated 18 July 2022, value set $17,886,207 against $10,394,832 on the roll before); at 2 sites a sale date falls between the first and last lease file on record. Where a sale within three years set a value and the title states the rent (2 leases), a year’s rent is 6% to 10% of that value for the space the City took.","Of 178 San Francisco tax-credit applications since 2007 that could be matched to a parcel, 53 show a sale date in the application year or the three years before, and the roll shows a value set by that sale for 37. The application’s land and acquisition line is on disk for 5 of those: in 3 the roll value is within a tenth of that line, and in 1 the value the sale set is less than half or more than twice that line (2550 Irving: $9,000,000 on the roll against $1,190,308 in the application, where the City bought the land itself for $9,600,000). Lot lines were changed within three years either side of the application in 62 developments.","The tax returns give no address for the leased premises, so the address on the return stands in for it: 27 San Francisco buildings for 25 organizations, 7 of them for leases with an insider. 2 of those buildings show a sale date in the years the City was paying the organization (0 of them insider leases): Compass Family Services at 37 Grove Street (sale dated 10 September 2018, value set $9,500,000; reported lease with COMPASS QALICB); Hamilton Families at 2567 Mission St (sale dated 7 February 2023, value set $8,262,000; reported lease with HAMILTON FAMILIES QALICB). Both are related-organization leases, the usual form when a nonprofit buys its own building through a company it controls. The roll names no owners, so none of this shows that an insider or a related company was the buyer.","San Francisco has 11 of the 318 Homekey awards that list homes: $313,698,953 for 1,080 homes, $290,462 a home against $241,070 for the rest of the State. Homekey Round 1: the middle San Francisco award was $214,672 a home against $127,907 elsewhere (2 of 94 awards). Homekey Round 2: the middle San Francisco award was $331,337 a home against $285,129 elsewhere (4 of 116 awards). Homekey Round 3: the middle San Francisco award was $307,376 a home against $286,992 elsewhere (2 of 51 awards). Homekey+: the middle San Francisco award was $326,255 a home against $361,989 elsewhere (3 of 57 awards). The highest against its round is Homekey 2 (440 Geary St) at $223,077 a home, 1.74 times the middle award elsewhere and number 2 of 94.","Of 41 purchases with a roll history, 35 went off the tax roll as government property, most on the first or second roll after the Board file; 4 are too recent to see. In 2 the site was divided and part of it still carries an assessed value: 333-12th Street ($1,137,827 on the latest roll); 1500-1580 Mission Street ($410,110,178 on the latest roll). 1 still carried a value on two or more yearly valuations after the file before leaving the roll (450 Sixth Street). The rolls show a further sale date after the one that records the public purchase for 1: 921 Howard Street (30 March 2021). The two hotels a nonprofit bought stayed on the roll under a welfare exemption.","93 company names were screened (30 sellers and 65 landlords; individuals and public bodies left out). 10 match a campaign contributor or a contributor’s employer, $432,184 in all, most of it Pacific Gas and Electric Company ($354,584; its deal here: seller of 5000 Hunters Point Boulevard (2026, $575,000)); 8 match a lobbyist’s client (Pacific Gas and Electric Company, 964 reported contacts; McDonald’s Corporation, 39 reported contacts; 200 Paul, LLC, 21 reported contacts); 0 match a payor of a payment made at an official’s request; 1 filed a major-developer disclosure; 47 appear as City vendors, which for a landlord is the rent. Most sellers and landlords are single-purpose companies named after one address, and they match nothing: the people behind them are not in these records.","The City’s financial report for June 2025 says $3.16 billion of its $3.48 billion loan portfolio is not expected to be collected: 91%. Ten years earlier it was $1.00 billion of $1.08 billion (93%), so the portfolio has more than tripled while the share written down held steady. The report gives the reason: “Management has determined through policy that many of these loans may be forgiven or renegotiated and extended long into the future”. From fiscal 2019 to 2026 the ledger shows the housing offices lending $2.68 billion and taking back $176.9 million of principal and $73.5 million of interest: about 7 cents of principal for each dollar lent.","The payment file shows $2.62 billion of housing loans paid out from fiscal 2019 to fiscal 2027 to date. Counting partnerships the files tie to one owner as a single borrower, the five largest received 36%: Mercy Housing California ($351.5 million, 21 partnerships); Bridge Housing Corp ($250.8 million, 8 partnerships); Mission Economic Development Agency ($170.4 million, 4 partnerships); Chinatown Community Development Center ($99.5 million, 10 partnerships); 1064 Mission LP ($75.1 million). $134.0 million more was paid to title and escrow companies at closings, where the file does not show the borrower. Each development borrows through its own partnership, so the list of payees is long (154 names) while the list of owners is short.","Of 80 housing-loan files the Board passed since 2015, 41 change an earlier loan: 34 amend and restate it, 2 consolidate or restructure loans, and 5 state an increase, $31.7 million between them. 25 files state the loan’s term; the typical term is 57 years. The property file measured the same thing by address and found 5 developments whose loan limit rose between files, by $40.1 million in all. No file on disk forgives a housing loan by name, and none records a repayment.","The housing office’s only published loan table lists 662 loans and grants to 265 projects, $1.13 billion committed between 1981 and 2015. 96 projects have one loan; 56 have four or more, led by Hunters View (Phase 1) (17 loans, $42.7 million, 2007 to 2013). Whether any of them was repaid cannot be tested from the published records: the table’s only fields are project id, project name, loan id, funding agreement date and amount of loan commitment. It has no borrower, balance, rate, term or repayment, and it stops in 2015.","The payment file shows $297.6 million paid to 30 hotel payees on 31 room agreements that began under emergency purchasing rules, $228.7 million of it keyed under an emergency rule; the Board’s analyst counted 28 hotels, $224.7 million spent by June 28, 2022, and 2,288 rooms at the peak. Payments peaked at $115.6 million in fiscal 2021. The largest by far is Hotel Whitcomb: $88.1 million for 459 rooms, $191,846 a room over fiscal 2020 to 2023; the analyst put its average rate at $177.52 a room a night with meals and linen. Where the analyst’s table gives rooms (11 hotels), the total paid runs from $68,223 to $217,374 a room. 4 hotels were still being paid in fiscal 2024 or later (The Monarch Hotel, Adante Hotel, Cova Hotel and Days Inn San Francisco), and the Board approved 2 new one-year shelter leases of former programme hotels retroactively in 2025 (Lombard Hotel Group – Monarch Hotel; Roshan Investments LLC).","The Board approved 9 settlements with hotel owners for damage by shelter guests and resulting loss of use, $44.5 million in all, between 22 December 2022 and 23 June 2023. The largest is $19.5 million for the Hotel Whitcomb ($42,484 a room). Across the 9 that can be matched to a room agreement the settlements come to 27% of what had been paid for the rooms, from 7.9% to 57%. The analyst had warned of it: under the agreements “the City is responsible for all repair costs associated with restoring the hotel to its previous condition, even if they exceed the contingency amount”, and by July 2022 “at least two hotels, Hotel Union Square and Tilden, have demanded repair costs above the not to exceed contract amounts”.","The federal emergency agency’s public file lists 42 coronavirus projects for the City with $305.7 million of federal money obligated; 4 of them are for shelter outside group settings, the heading hotel rooms fall under, with $62.1 million obligated. That is 21% of the $297.6 million the City paid on the room agreements alone. The analyst reported in July 2022 that “Approximately 91 percent of expenditures to date on Shelter in Place hotel agreements are eligible for reimbursement by the Federal Emergency Management Agency”. The shelter projects’ own titles cover 16 March 2020 to 30 June 2020; 23 January 2021 to 30 June 2022; none in the file covers 1 July 2020 to 22 January 2021. The City’s fiscal 2023 financial report says federal grant revenue fell $262.2 million short of budget mainly because the agency had put other jurisdictions’ claims first. The ledger shows $412.0 million received from the agency for all purposes from fiscal 2020 to 2026.","Of 30 hotel payees, 5 appear, under their own name or a registered owner’s, as a registered lobbyist’s client (HTL 587: 23 contacts, $5,506 paid to lobbyists; Nob Hill Hotel: 27 contacts, $2,786 paid to lobbyists; Intercontinental San Francisco: 2 contacts, $100 paid to lobbyists; Cova Hotel: 1 contact, $3,500 paid to lobbyists; Hotel Epik: 5 contacts, $553 paid to lobbyists) and 12 appear in campaign filings since 2015, as a contributor or as a contributor’s employer, $34,900 in all. Most of the lobbying concerned permits. For 2 hotels the lobbyist reported contacts about the City’s own use of the property: HTL 587 (23 contacts; Homelessness and Supportive Housing, 2020: “Lease Negotiations”); Nob Hill Hotel (1 contact; Mayor, 2021: “City's Purchase of Client Property”). Measured against the sums paid for rooms the money is very small. These are matches on a name, and a contact is on file only because the lobbyist reported it.","These three files are the State housing department's Homekey program (for people experiencing or at risk of homelessness, as the files describe it). The racial-equity analysis and action plan is worth up to 10 points: 4 for a completed outcomes workbook (also a threshold to apply), up to 3 for the analysis and up to 3 for the plan; community engagement is scored separately, up to 10 points. Points go to the applicant's analysis of system outcomes and its plan, not to the race of any applicant, owner or tenant. The workbook saved here is the blank template (0 figures filled in).","The new 2026-27 assessor roll and eight earlier ones, read beside the buildings behind City purchases, leases and loans, the State Homekey grants and the tax-credit rules. The homelessness department's own dashboard shows how many supportive housing units stand empty. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","The nine workbooks agree with the portal on every one of 3,602 parcel-years the two share (value and sale date). They add 25 sale dates on 30 deal parcels that no 2007-2025 portal roll carried, 4 of them dated 2026. 7 are buildings the City rents, 2 are buildings the public bought, and the rest are tax-credit or nonprofit sites.","14 rented sites show a sale date after the City's first lease file (2018 on) or a value re-set on the 2026-27 roll with no date. 6 of the sales are new to the record from the hand-saved rolls: 752 Vallejo Street (31 March 2026, 1,421 days after the lease file of 10 May 2022); 333 Valencia Street; 333 Valencia (3 February 2026, 2,058 days after the lease file of 16 June 2020); 2000 Oakdale Avenue (2 January 2026, 3,559 days after the lease file of 5 April 2016); 141 Industrial Street; 141 Industrial (22 April 2026, 701 days after the lease file of 21 May 2024); 760 Harrison Street (1 April 2024, 3,289 days after the lease file of 31 March 2015); 3801 Third Street (4 October 2023, 1,443 days after the lease file of 22 October 2019). 1380 Howard Street: value $5,397,142 on the 2025-26 roll and $28,298,880 on the 2026-27 roll with no sale date.","20 deal parcels moved more than 3% and more than $250,000 between the 2025-26 and 2026-27 rolls: 10 up, 10 down. Largest rise: 1380 Howard Street; 1380 Howard ($5,397,142 to $28,298,880). 758 Pacific Avenue, which the Board approved buying in 2026 (no deed to the City is on the recorder's index saved in November 2026) after a sale dated 8 August 2023 that set no new value on four rolls, is now carried at $2,080,800 (from $805,696); the 2026-27 roll value is 96% of it.","9 rented sites carry an exemption on the 2026-27 roll, $353,304,129 of exempt value in all; the largest is 845 Jackson Street (welfare, $232,196,298).","9 awards, $236,844,933 for 718 homes; 8 tie to a Board acceptance file by the exact sum and 7 to a City purchase. All 4 Round 2 grants are within the notice’s caps (0 above): capital per home $230,531 to $289,312 against at most $300,000. 1321 Mission (1321 Mission Street): price $86,500,000, $540,625 a home, 1.29 times the 2021-22 roll value; Eula Hotel Apartments (3055-3061 16th Street): price $5,600,000, $224,000 a home, 2.6 times the 2021-22 roll value; Mission Inn (5630 Mission Street): price $17,000,000, $333,333 a home, 1.33 times the 2021-22 roll value; City Gardens (333-12th Street): price $145,000,000, $852,941 a home, 2.79 times the 2020-21 roll value. The Board accepted $7,480,080, not the $7,194,080 on the State's list; the Budget and Legislative Analyst's report on this file names the Eula Hotel.","On the dashboard updated 11/12/2026, 761 of 9,198 site-based units were vacant (8.3%, against the department's own 7% target): 90 available for referral, 308 with a move-in in progress and 363 offline. Over the twelve months from 2025-12 to 2026-11 vacancies averaged 778 a month (373 offline, 406 being filled) and never fell below 744; offline units went from 414 in 2025-12 to 363 in 2026-11 (low 329), while units being filled rose from 340 to a peak of 450. Largest offline reasons: janitorial maintenance 77, property hold 68, internal transfer 59. The excess over the 7% target is about 117 units. At the homelessness department's own pre-purchase estimate of $19,800 a unit a year for operations and services, an average of 373 offline units is about $7,385,400 a year of budgeted operating cost; at the $30,047 later estimated for one site, $11,207,531. A new building in lease-up, Harbor Haven, had 22 of 67 counted units empty (expected 125 units), outside these counts.","Not the two that were found: both (2022, applications of August 9, 2025, applications of May 20) were bond rounds, which the regulations hand to the Debt Limit Allocation Committee's own scoring (its Regulation 5106, not on disk). For the 9% rounds the regulations give San Francisco a 3.7% regional share and make a letter from the Mayor's Office of Housing the first tie-breaker among San Francisco applications; in the 9% rounds with an award list 9 of 11 San Francisco applications were funded and none was passed over.","10 of the 23 rented buildings that changed hands or carry an exemption also carry a landlord tie in older screens. Most: 845 Jackson Street (Chinese Hospital Association): autopilot: Exempt property where flagged organizations are registered (Chinese Hospital Association); exceptions: The same supplier, excused from competition year after year (Chinese Hospital Association); exceptions: The largest contracts awarded without competition (Chinese Hospital Association) and 3 more.","Of 9 State Homekey buildings, 5 appear in an older purchase, hotel or contract screen (Homekey screens left out). 1 was first paid as a shelter hotel: 685 Ellis, paid $4.8 million as a hotel, then bought for $19.9 million from a seller who had held it 295 days, with a Homekey award of $18.2 million.","The recorded deeds behind the City's building purchases, read against the Board's votes, and the homelessness department's own dashboards read against the cases. The deeds give dates and parties, never prices.","The recorder's index was printed for 40 of the purchases; 32 show the deed to the City on the printed lot. The median time from the Board vote to the City's deed is 40.5 days; none was recorded before the vote. 4 properties changed hands two or more times in the four years before the vote. At 490 South Van Ness Avenue the owner since 2009 deeded the property to four different holders between 22 December 2014 and 20 March 2015, months before the Board approved paying $18,037,500; the City's deed came from one of them. 1000 Sutter Street changed hands 3 times in the four years before a City grantee bought it with City-linked financing.","13 of the purchased properties carry such records in the five years before the vote: 601-617 Laguna Street (4 City lien (Public Utilities (water)); 3 City lien (Public Health); 3 order of abatement (Building Inspection)); 3055-3061 16th Street (1 order of abatement (Building Inspection); 6 loans from lenders that are not banks or government); 624 Laguna Street (3 City lien (Public Health); 1 mechanic's lien (a contractor)).","13 buildings carry orders of abatement or City liens recorded after the City's deed, loan or lease; 11 orders in all. 4 have Fire Department orders, among them 1000 Sutter Street / 1000 Sutter Street (Hotel Granada) (4 order of abatement (Fire Department)); 921 Howard Street (3 City lien (Public Utilities (water)); 2 order of abatement (Fire Department); 1 order of abatement (Building Inspection)); 440 Geary Street (3 City lien (Public Health); 1 order of abatement (Fire Department); 1 City lien (Public Utilities (water))). Several liens are the City's water utility liening buildings the City itself owns.","1044 Howard Street: the deed to United Playaz was recorded on 2022-12-07 (document 2022109688), 69 days before the Board's vote, with same-day deeds of trust to a private trust; COMMUNITY VISION CAPITAL & CON. The housing office's first recorded document followed on 2023-06-23 (fixture filing / declaration of cc&r to SFCC-MAYORS OFFICE OF HOUSING & COMMUNITY DEV), the same day a deed of trust was reconveyed. 758 Pacific Avenue went from a private trust to Chinatown Community Development Center in 2023 and to its partnership, New Asia Housing, in 2024, which the City then agreed to buy it from.","578 vacancy reports are in the file for tax years 2022 to 2025. 4 spaces reported vacant belong to a filer that is a City landlord, grantee or payee on more than a name; 1 of them for two or more years. 333 Valencia Owner LLC (321-335 Valencia St, 2022, 2023, 2024; the City paid it $13.7 million in those fiscal years, mostly Public Health). Self-Help for the Elderly (933 Clement St, 2024; the City paid it $39.1 million in those fiscal years, mostly Human Services Agency). Abode Property Management (1174 Folsom St, 2024; the City paid it $4.7 million in those fiscal years, mostly Homelessness Services). 2339 Market I7 LP (2337-2339 Market St, 2023; the City paid it $25,770 in those fiscal years, mostly Mayor). 4 more rows are name matches only and are labelled so.","49 City-funded organizations file as tenant of a commercial space in the vacancy-tax file; an owner filing is on the same parcel for 58. In 0 of them an owner filing carries a surname from the tenant's own list of officers and directors: this test found no grantee renting from an officer. In 3 the owner is itself an organization the City pays (by name), among them Baker Places Inc. at 1156-1164 Valencia St, owner Salvation Army ($53.3 million); Tides Center at 2176-2186 Mission St, owner Sonali Holdings LLC ($4.6 million); San Francisco Bay Area Theatre Company at 2761-2791 24th St, owner Brava for the Women In the Arts ($4.0 million).","The index for 64 parcels names 76 organizations on a deed (banks, title companies, utilities, public bodies and private trusts left out). 12 of them also gave to a local campaign committee in their own name or hired a registered lobbyist; 10 are not in the earlier sellers-and-landlords screen, which read only the names in Board files. City Gardens 333 LLC (333 12th Street; 333-12th Street): 36 reported lobbying contacts, 2020-2021: 333 12th; 333 12th Street. Bodies contacted: Planning, Mayor, City Attorney. Personality Hotels LLC (440 Geary Street): 2 reported lobbying contacts, 2015-2015: Hotel Union Square Sign. Bodies contacted: City Planning. Chinatown Community Development Center (758 Pacific Avenue; 772+758 Pacific Ave; 777 Broadway Street, 1525 Grant Avenue, 1207 Mason Street): gave $215,635.","The homelessness department's adopted budget grew from $284.5 million in fiscal 2019 to $785.6 million in fiscal 2026; its spending grew from $233.7 million to $636.1 million. It spent less than its adopted budget in 7 of 8 years ($4.13 billion of $5.19 billion over the eight years), and less than its budgeted grants to providers in 7 of 8. The adopted budgets for fiscal 2027 and 2028 are $824.5 million and $772.0 million."],["Dozens of provider numbers registered to one unit.","Agencies whose billing exploded.","Providers far outside their specialty.","On an exclusion list and still billing.","Chains of agencies behind one name.","The federal government now publishes what Medicaid paid every provider for every outpatient service from 2018 to 2024. San Francisco billers took in $594 million in 2023; these screens set each one against the national norm for the same service, against lists of barred providers, and against what the City pays the same organizations.","20 San Francisco provider numbers (13 of them outside City government and the University of California) were paid more than 99 in 100 billers nationwide for a service while also getting at least three times the national median per claim line and half as much again as other California billers: 126 service-years and $1,076 million in all, $32.0 million of it to the 13 others.","19 times a San Francisco biller at least tripled its Medicaid payments in one year to $500,000 or more; 5 provider numbers issued since 2018 took in $500,000 or more by the end of the following year; 3 billers taking $500,000 or more a year vanished from the file by 2022.","32 organizations the City pays also bill Medicaid under their own name ($614 million, 2018 to 2024). For 7 of them the Medicaid money is new or has tripled since 2021, from $0.0 million to $21.4 million in 2024, while City payments to the same 7 went from $30.0 million (fiscal 2022) to $46.7 million (fiscal 2026). Seneca Family of Agencies is not counted: only its own billing number is new, and the raw federal rows show Medicaid paid $40.1 million in 2021 for its services under another biller's number.","7 San Francisco health centers file yearly counts with the federal health-center program; for 2 of them the federal figures sit badly with the State filing, the Bay Area norm or the City ledger. Strongest: San Francisco Community Health Center (reports as Asian and Pacific Islander Wellness Center): cost per patient in 2025 was $6,137, 1.7 times the median of the 10 Bay Area centers whose cost is published; reported cost went from $5.4 million in 2023 to $16.6 million in 2024 while patients went from 1,938 to 2,720; it reported $3.8 million in local-government grants for 2025, and the City ledger shows about $10.9 million paid to it that year.","Of 16 listed provider numbers that appear anywhere in the San Francisco and Bay Area payment extract, 1 shows payments in a calendar year after the year of listing, $47,207 in all; the largest is St. Francis Medical Group, P.C. ($47,207).","Billers registered in San Francisco were paid $2,174 per enrolled resident in 2023 for outpatient and professional services, 1.47 times the California figure of $1,482; in 2018 it was $2,071 against $1,150 (1.8 times). City government's own provider numbers took 51% of the San Francisco total in 2023.","25 organizations the City pays appear by name in the federal Provider Relief Fund list for California, with $38.5 million between them; 8 of those also hold a federal paycheck-protection loan under a matching name ($14.9 million). The City paid the 25 a combined $545 million in fiscal 2020 to 2022. San Francisco recipients as a whole received $651 million in 1,318 payments.","The raw federal rows name two providers for each payment: the number that billed and the number named as giving the service. 15 San Francisco billing numbers stand apart from the ordinary pattern for their kind. Far the largest is the City's own: provider number 1417099789 of the City and County was paid $1,344.1 million from 2018 to 2024, $902.4 million of it for services that 41 organizations under other names are named as giving (the largest: University of California San Francisco $204.6 million; Progress Foundation $112.0 million; Felton Institute $70.1 million); $576.2 million of that was for 27 City-funded nonprofits. That is the pattern to expect where a county runs its own mental-health plan and bills for the organizations it contracts with. 11 of the 15 are outside City government and the University of California; 8 of those are there because services are attributed to provider numbers missing from the California registry file. On the other side, 6 organizations in the Bay Area extract are named as giving $100,000 or more of services under three or more unrelated billing names in one year; none of them is registered in San Francisco. Individual practitioners registered in San Francisco appear under one other billing name in 84% of practitioner-years, and 82 of them under four or more in a single year (up to 19); they are counted, not named.","Of 766 San Francisco billing numbers with payments, 29 were paid $10.7 million for months that no enrollment record under that number covers; 4 of the 115 whose first record begins in 2018 or later were among the top 10 in 100 billers nationwide for a service within twelve months (2 in the top 1 in 100); 35 numbers paid $3.5 million have no enrollment record at all; and 93 numbers paid $8.6 million are recorded in California only as “active, do not pay”. The third question cannot be answered from this file: none of the 665 San Francisco billing numbers with a California record has an end date, so a provider that left after a peak cannot be seen.","252 San Francisco billing numbers appear in the federal Medicaid drug file with $207.4 million from 2018 to 2024 ($19.9 million in 2018, $38.8 million in 2023). 19 of them show at least one pattern (16 outside City government and the University of California): 11 with four fifths of a year's drug money on one or two high-cost drugs and well above the middle biller of its kind, 9 with a jump or a sudden start, and 5 paid at least ten times the middle Bay Area biller of its kind for a drug. Concentration by itself is ordinary in this file: in 2023 the middle Bay Area pharmacy drew 68% of its drug money from two products and the middle provider of other kinds 52%. The three largest drugs by money to San Francisco billers are drug product code 00006-3026 (no name for this code in the files on disk), $36.8 million; Infliximab (sold as Remicade; product code 57894-0030), $15.2 million; Adalimumab (sold as Humira; product code 00074-0554), $12.9 million.","Across all services, billers registered in San Francisco were paid $2,174 per enrolled resident in 2023 against a middle figure of $1,379 for the other 16 counties with 200,000 or more enrollees (1.58 times; rank 1 of 17). By family, San Francisco is at least one and a half times the middle in 7 of 17, led by codes beginning S (6.09 times, $239 against $39; the largest code is mental health crisis intervention, per hour (code S9484)), and at or below two thirds of it in 6, lowest in drugs given by a provider (0.23 times). The single service furthest above the middle is medication training and support, per 15 minutes (code H0034): $73.57 per enrollee against $3.73 (19.8 times). San Francisco's enrollees are not like the other counties': 24% were seniors or people with disabilities in 2023 against a middle of 13%, and 20% were 65 or older against 8%.","55 nonprofits were paid $801.5 million by the Department of Public Health since fiscal 2019 under contracts titled for mental-health or substance-use services. 12 are in both the federal locator and Medicaid treatment billing, 24 in Medicaid only, 2 in the locator only and 17 in neither ($58.1 million from the City; the largest is Central City Hospitality House, $21.5 million). Most of the Medicaid money for their treatment services is billed by someone else: $507.6 million under the City's provider numbers and $455.6 million under other bodies' numbers (83% of it other counties and public bodies), against $8.6 million under their own, 2018 to 2024.","24 provider numbers in the project's universe are on the federal exclusion list by exact number; 24 belong to individuals and none to an organization. None of them appears in the Medicaid payment file in a year after the year of exclusion; 2 appear before it or in the same year. No excluded business on the list shares both a name and an address or city with an organization in the universe (9 share a name only, with addresses in other States, and are not shown). The list has 136 entries with a San Francisco address, most of them without a provider number.","The federal list of 334 integrity agreements names 2 companies whose exact name is on San Francisco provider numbers with a second link through the registry's mailing address (1 in the same city as the agreement, 1 in the same State only): Medi-Lynx Cardiac Monitoring (1 San Francisco number; 1 agreement, the latest listed as Closed Oct 05, 2022); Walgreen Co (65 San Francisco numbers; 2 agreements, the latest listed as Effective Apr 18, 2025). 2 more names match with no second link and are not shown. The 2026 federal freezes on new Medicare enrollment name hospices and home health agencies. The national registry has 33 hospice provider numbers in San Francisco (3 paid by Medicaid as billers, $3.8 million from 2018 to 2024) and 82 in its home-health group (8 paid, $10.6 million); 6 and 33 of those numbers were issued in 2022 or later.","145 Bay Area nursing homes (10 in San Francisco) are on the State’s list of 925 homes whose 2020 reports included a related company’s profit-and-loss statement. They name 110 related companies; 20 serve five or more Bay Area homes. The most named is PACS (16 Bay Area homes, 45 statewide). 15 of the names match a nursing-home chain in the federal chain file; the largest federal fine total among them is PACS Group with $8.9 million across 275 homes nationwide.","10 San Francisco nursing homes filed related-company statements with their 2020 reports. 4 named two or more related companies. 5 name a company that matches a chain in the federal chain file: Aspen Skilled Healthcare (35 homes nationwide, $732,404 in federal fines); PACS Group (275 homes nationwide, $8.9 million in federal fines).","Federal and State files not screened before: the federal hospice scoring index, nursing-home finances and daily staffing, medical-equipment suppliers and the providers who order from them, Medicare prescribing, and manufacturers' disclosures of what physicians own. Each screen is a reason to look and never a finding; the last screens list what authorities have already put in writing.","111 Bay Area hospices were scored for fiscal 2025. Their middle score is 5 of 16, against 6 for California and 6 nationally; 4 scored 10 or more, where the top tenth of the country begins (16% of California hospices do). The highest is Careplus Hospice Services, Inc, Livermore, at 12 (no continuous-care or inpatient days all year; few visits in the last two days of life; many stays of 180 days or more; many patients discharged alive); Medicare paid it $2.7 million in fiscal 2024. 8 scored 4 or more of 8 on the care-pattern half of the index, and for 4 Medicare paid other providers at least five times the California middle figure ($7.86) per hospice day. 4 of the 13 highest-scoring hospices are also in the existing new-or-fast-growing screen.","42 of 183 Bay Area nursing homes with a recent State financial report (40 of them investor-owned) record at least half of all their assets, and at least $3 million, as money owed to them by related parties: $1,021.5 million in all. Largest against its size among investor-owned homes: Garfield Neurobehavioral Center, Oakland, with $33.6 million owed to it in 2025, 87% of its assets and 3.3 times a year's revenue. 3 are in San Francisco ($63.4 million): San Francisco Post Acute, Victorian Post Acute, Central Gardens Post Acute. Across investor-owned Bay Area homes, the amount owed by related parties went from $900.4 million in 2018 to $958.4 million in 2025; the same homes reported $1,124.7 million of net income over those years.","14 chains have three or more Bay Area homes in the State reports; the largest is PACS Group with 31 ($738.4 million of revenue and $119.2 million of net income in 2025, $436.0 million owed to its homes by related parties). Across all 183 homes, revenue per resident day rose from $400 in 2019 to $591, net income from 3.8% to 12.9% of revenue, and nurse hours per resident day in the State reports went from 3.98 to 4.17. The chain with the largest share of its homes' assets owed by related parties is PACS Group (78%).","Over 2025Q3 to 2026Q2 the middle Bay Area home reported 4.19 nurse hours per resident day; the California middle is 4.25 and the national average in the federal chain file is 3.9. 51 of 188 Bay Area homes fall in the lowest quarter of California (below 4.01); the lowest is Inspire Behavioral Health, San Jose, at 2.49. 22 homes reported at least one day with residents and no registered-nurse hours; Bancroft Healthcare Center, San Leandro, reported 98 such days. The middle California home's weekend figure is 3.83 (3.4 nationally) and its registered-nurse figure 0.50 (0.7 nationally).","22 Bay Area homes are on both lists: below the California middle of 4.25 nurse hours per resident day, with half or more of their assets owed to them by related parties ($352.9 million between them, and $85.1 million of net income in their latest year). The State issued them 41 citations or penalties from 2019 to June 2024, 16 of them for nursing hours. Lowest on staffing: Marin Post Acute, San Rafael, at 3.69 hours with $18.9 million owed to it.","Medicare paid 696 Bay Area suppliers $85.1 million in 2024. 7 of those paid $250,000 or more show at least one of the patterns ($16.2 million between them). The largest is Medicare Drop LLC, Campbell: $10.9 million in 2024, first in the file in 2021, mainly for wheelchair accessory, power seating system, combination tilt and… (code E1007). 2 are supplied from the address of the provider who orders: the largest is Sah Orthopaedic Associates, Fremont, paid $1.9 million for collagen dressing, sterile, size more than 48 sq. in., each (code A6023), where Alexander Sah, MD (provider no. 1003966359), at the same street address, ordered $1.8 million of this item from 1 supplier. 6 first appear in the file in 2022 or later, or grew past $5 million after 2018.","Of 5,983 Bay Area providers whose equipment orders Medicare paid in 2024, 40 ordered $150,000 or more at five times or more the per-patient middle figure for their specialty; most are Neurology (8), Nurse Practitioner (7), Orthopedic Surgery (4). 7 ordered $150,000 or more through only one or two suppliers. In 4 cases the provider who orders is also the supplier, or shares a street address with a supplier of the same item: Alexander Sah, MD, Fremont ($1.8 million); Bryant Bonner, MD, Fremont ($168,560); Mher Vartivarian, D.P.M., San Francisco ($186,662).","Of 6,413 San Francisco prescribers in the 2024 Medicare drug file, 26 stand far above their kind on the share of claims that are opioids, 42 on the share that are brand-name, and 32 wrote $2 million or more at ten times their kind's cost per claim. Highest opioid share: Adam Lee, PHARM.D. (provider no. 1467544031), pharmacist, 91% of 3,007 claims (kind's middle 0.0%). 6 of the opioid outliers give one street address, 3838 California St. Highest brand share: Sharrie Ray, M.D. (provider no. 1689751372), 98% of 22,833 claims, most often Shingrix (8,099 claims). Highest cost: Dong Phuong Nguyen, PHARMD (provider no. 1780776443), $15.3 million, most of it Biktarvy ($6.0 million).","17 of the 10,061 provider numbers that appear in the San Francisco drug files for 2018 to 2024 are on one of the lists. For 5 of them the file shows prescriptions filled in a calendar year after the year of listing: the largest is Juan Posada (provider no. 1265496301): Medicare enrollment revoked, 2021-01-27 (424.535(A)(3) Felonies), with 6,865 claims costing $885,453 in 2022.","41 San Francisco physicians hold or held interests in 35 manufacturers in the federal disclosure files for 2019 to 2025; the 22 interests reported for 2025 are valued at $14.6 million. The largest at its latest report is Tony Chou's interest in Route 92 Medical, Inc. ($4.9 million, 2025). 5 physicians hold interests in one company, Route 92 Medical, Inc. 16 of the holders were also paid by the same company ($10.2 million in all), the most being Daniel Schwartz from RxSight Inc ($8.0 million, mostly \"compensation for services other than consulting, including serving as faculty or as a speaker at a venue other than a continuing education program\"). The Medicare drug file shows 0 of them prescribing a product the company names. These records are the manufacturers' own public disclosures.","Manufacturers reported $923.1 million in research payments to 593 San Francisco recipients for 2019 to 2025. 48% of it, $441.0 million, is recorded under one name, Langley Porter Psychiatric Hospital, although only 0.4% of those dollars are on studies whose lead investigator is listed under psychiatry; the largest single field is Internal Medicine, 14%. The manufacturers' label for the recipient looks wrong rather than the conduct. Next are UCSF Medical Center ($85.2 million), University of California San Francisco ($79.7 million), Board of Trustees of the Leland Stanford Junior University ($55.1 million). Research payments are disclosed.","701 San Francisco physicians are named as a lead investigator on industry research payments for 2019 to 2025. The largest total is on studies led by Alain Algazi: $119.2 million, Novartis Pharmaceuticals Corporation (96%), paid to UCSF Medical Center. 9 of the top 60 lead $5 million or more with 90% or more from a single company. 10 lead investigators also appear in the ownership file (Michael Blum: Vital Connect, Inc; Jeffrey Golden: Pulmonx Corporation; Luis Savastano: Endovascular Engineering Inc.; Endovascular Engineering, Inc.). The money goes to the institution, not to the investigator.","The inspector general's list holds 334 agreements, 43 with California parties. 3 are with parties based in the six Bay Area counties: Semler Scientific (Campbell, effective 2025); Braden Partners, LP Doing Business As Pacific Pulmonary Services (Novato, closed 2023); The County of San Mateo and San Mateo Medical Center (San Mateo, effective 2021). 4 more are with parties elsewhere whose name matches a Bay Area nursing-home chain, equipment supplier or hospice in the federal files. 3 of the 7 are in effect.","Of 604 enforcement entries the inspector general's site returns for California and San Francisco, 52 name a Bay Area place in the title or mention San Francisco on the page (20 by title); 14 are dated 2024 or later. The most recent is dated 2026-07-23: \"CEO Of Bay Area Home Health Agency Convicted Of Health Care Fraud Conspiracy Targeting Medicare\".","California's unit reported 186 convictions in fiscal 2018 and 43 in fiscal 2025, while the State's Medicaid spending went from $88,895.8 million to $173,402.7 million and the unit's staff from 205 to 290. That is 2.5 convictions for every $10 billion of Medicaid spending in 2025 (20.9 in 2018), against a middle figure of 20.8 for the five other largest programs (New York, Texas, Pennsylvania, Florida, Ohio); California ranks 49 of 53 units on that measure. It reported $102.1 million in recoveries in 2025, 1.4 times what the unit cost.","Of 143 county and service lines for the six Bay Area counties in 2025, 6 stand out. The clearest is independent diagnostic testing facility pt b in Marin County: $3.8 million in 2025, 6.1 times the 2020 figure of $628,566 (the State grew 1.3 times), $965 per user against $421 in the middle California county. Statewide, California had 2.8 hospices for every 10,000 people in traditional Medicare in 2025 against 1.3 nationally (813 hospices in 2020, 1,157 in 2025), and 6.0 home-health agencies against 2.1.","6 groups of two or more differently named Bay Area providers, each with at least one San Francisco address, are joined by owner numbers in the federal enrolment file; 4 have two or more names in San Francisco itself and 1 have two names at one street address. The largest holds 32 provider names and 96 billing numbers (Bay Area Master Tenant Llc; Bay Bridge Capital Partners, Llc; Hudson River Opco Llc; Pacs Group, Inc. and 4 more organizations). 3 of the groups include an owner already on the list in the “health” results; what this screen adds is the restriction to ownership interests and the count of names and billing numbers per group.","In three 2026 letters the federal Medicaid agency withheld $3.47 billion from California while it asks for documents, most of it on personal care. The national drug file sets San Francisco’s drug billers against all of California.","Three letters withheld $3.47 billion in federal share: quarter ended 2025-12-31: $1.34 billion ($1.18 billion benefits, $162.9 million administration; letter of 2026-05-13); quarter ended 2026-03-31: $867.5 million ($709.3 million benefits, $158.2 million administration; letter of 2026-07-21); quarter ended 2026-06-30: $1.26 billion ($1.12 billion benefits, $143.8 million administration; letter of 2026-10-20). Personal care (In-Home Supportive Services claimed as Community First Choice and personal care services) is $2.87 billion of it, new in the first letter and repeated in both later ones. Items about coverage by immigration status come to $43.4 million ($15.9 million; $24.8 million; $2.6 million by quarter); the drug-rebate item was on its 25th repeat in May, $120.2 million in all, and is absent from the October letter. New items: 2025-12-31: Pregnancy Gestation Related Emergency Claims, Overstated Costs, CFC-PCS - Lines 19D / 23A; 2026-06-30: Administrative Claims of Medical Services. Not in the October letter: Family Planning Related Emergency Claims, Insufficient Health Insurance Related Emergency Claims, Pregnancy Gestation Related Emergency Claims, Uncollected Drug Rebates. Labelled repeat but not in an earlier letter on disk: Family Planning Related Emergency Claims, Insufficient Health Insurance Related Emergency Claims.","The personal-care item is $1.13 billion for the quarter ended 2025-12-31; $646.4 million for the quarter ended 2026-03-31; $1.09 billion for the quarter ended 2026-06-30. Each time part is an estimate from growth faster than in other states, and part comes from tests on the State’s claims. Quarter ended 2025-12-31: $698.9 million statistical outliers; $13.2 million paid more than a year after the service. Quarter ended 2026-03-31: $237.5 million statistical outliers; $19.2 million paid more than a year after the service; $25.1 million one provider billing for four or more people; $3.8 million billed while the person was in hospital; $503,817 service after eligibility ended; $1,638 more than 283 hours in a month. Quarter ended 2026-06-30: $209.1 million statistical outliers; $16.0 million paid more than a year after the service; $19.9 million one provider billing for four or more people; $322,181 service dated after the person’s death; $591,611 billed by a provider after termination or exclusion; $3.8 million for people flagged as without satisfactory immigration status; $233,736 billed while the person was in hospital; $326,820 service after eligibility ended; $4,902 more than 283 hours in a month. In October it also asked about $305.6 million of employer payroll taxes and sick leave, and $209.0 million of “beneficiary services” it could not get explained.","In November 2025 the State budgeted $100.0 million of General Fund in 2026-27 to repay deferrals of benefit claims ($25 million a quarter) and $0 for administrative deferrals, saying the administrative deferrals would no longer continue. The federal letters then withheld $162.9 million, $158.2 million, $143.8 million of administrative claims in the three quarters ended 2025-12-31, 2026-03-31, 2026-06-30, and $15.9 million, $24.8 million, $2.6 million on immigration-status benefit items, close to the $25 million a quarter assumed. In May 2026 the State raised the 2026-27 figures to $200.0 million and $145.0 million, and its 2025-26 figures to $477.7 million and $582.4 million. Neither estimate has a line for the $2.87 billion withheld on personal care.","The November 2025 estimate added $819.3 million of General Fund in 2025-26 for State-only claiming adjustments for members with unsatisfactory immigration status, and $617.6 million for federal deferrals. It counts $66.4 million of recoupments on claims for these members, spread over three years, and cuts administrative claims by about $600.0 million a year from now on. The repayment of past federal claims (policy change 2415) is $397.7 million in 2025-26 in November and $686.6 million in May. Budget Act savings: Enrolment freeze, adults 19 and older without satisfactory status (from 1 Jan 2026), $77.9 million in 2025-26 and $3.30 billion in 2028-29; $30 monthly premiums, adults 19 to 59 with unsatisfactory status (from 1 Jul 2027), $695.7 million in 2027-28; End of clinic visit rates for State-only services (from 1 Jul 2026), $1.00 billion in 2026-27 and $1.10 billion in 2027-28; End of State-only dental cover, adults 19 and older (from 1 Jul 2026), $308.0 million in 2026-27 and $336.0 million in 2028-29; Drug rebate aggregator for State-only drug claims, $370.0 million in 2025-26 and $600.0 million in ongoing. Between November and May the State’s 2026-27 figures lost $1.58 billion of savings: Drug rebate aggregator (rebates for State-only drug claims), $435.0 million in November and nothing in May; End of clinic visit rates for State-only services (marked “DELETED” in the May estimate), $1.01 billion in November and nothing in May; End of dental cover for adults with unsatisfactory immigration status (marked “DELETED” in the May estimate), $134.6 million in November and nothing in May. Neither estimate gives one total for the cost of State-only coverage; the Legislative Analyst’s figure in the existing case remains the only total on disk.","The State told the federal agency it sent 408 credible-fraud referrals to the State fraud prosecutors in five review years; as of December 2025 charges had been filed on 34 of the 408 in its table, 13 ended in a judgment or restitution and 67 were declined. Over five years it imposed 97 payment suspensions and granted 339 exceptions not to suspend, all at the prosecutors’ request: for each suspension, 3.5 exceptions. Recoveries rose from $11.0 million in 2020-21 to $136.0 million in 2024-25. The federal letter of January 2026 says California is $1.60 billion of the $1.80 billion it is recouping from the eight jurisdictions with State-only programs.","Certified Medi-Cal enrolment was 14,781,191 in June 2025, 14,432,529 in December 2025 and 13,675,721 in June 2026: 756,808 fewer in the six months after the freeze on new adult enrolment in State-only coverage began, against 348,662 fewer in the six months before. 4,561,673 were adults in the federal expansion group (ages 19 to 64) in June 2026. The publication gives no count by immigration status, so it cannot say how much of the fall is the freeze.","252 San Francisco billing numbers drew $207.4 million of Medicaid drug money in the national file from 2018 to 2024 ($19.9 million in 2018, $48.9 million in 2024), against $8.06 billion for California. 3 stand above 99 in 100 California peers per claim line or per beneficiary-month in a year with $250,000 or more (2 outside City government and the University of California); the largest is SCRIPTSITE PHARMACY PARTNERS LLC ($527,644 in 2019, $1,609 per line against $49 for the middle peer).","12 San Francisco billing numbers show a product-year with $100,000 or more at three or more times the rest of the country’s payment per claim line for the same product, or with a quarter or more of California’s money for a product that 20 or more California billers are paid for (7 outside City government and the University); the largest is SF ROYAL MEDICAL SUPPLY, INC, $508,891 in 2019 for drug product code 85645-0002 (no name for this code in the files on disk) (29% of all California Medicaid money for this product that year, among 22 California billers).","Of 20 San Francisco pharmacies with $250,000 or more of Medicaid drug money in 2018-2024, 5 drew three quarters or more of it through one prescribing number; the middle pharmacy drew 50% through its largest prescriber. The largest is SCRIPTSITE PHARMACY PARTNERS LLC: 77% of $2.3 million through one number (individual clinician).","Of 29 private provider numbers named by the earlier screens (Bay Area drug peers, or national service outliers), 5 are flagged again by at least one of the three new drug screens: PLANNED PARENTHOOD SHASTA DIABLO, INC. (product price or share); NORTH EAST MEDICAL SERVICES (one prescriber); WALGREEN CO (one prescriber); WALGREEN CO (one prescriber); MISSION AREA HEALTH ASSOCIATES, INC (against California peers). The others do not stand out against California peers in the drug file; for the service outliers that is expected, since their flags were for services, not drugs.","The State's complete nursing-home financial reports itemise what each home paid companies under common ownership; this page reads them home by home, with federal owners and penalties, State hospice and home-health reports, and City payments. Each screen is a reason to look and never a finding; related-party dealings are permitted when disclosed.","Re-measured from the State's complete data sets, 42 of 183 Bay Area nursing homes carry half or more of their assets, and at least $3 million, as money owed by related parties at the end of their latest report: $1,021.5 million. The case figure of $1,021.5 million for 42 homes is confirmed. 3 of the homes are in San Francisco ($63.4 million: San Francisco Post Acute, Victorian Post Acute, Central Gardens Post Acute). The 42 homes reported paying related companies $72.3 million in 2025.","San Francisco's 13 nursing homes with a 2025 State report listed $19.0 million of transactions with related parties in 2025, 9.5% of their expenses (California: 8.6%). By year: 2019 $12.6 million, 2022 $20.7 million, 2023 $13.6 million, 2024 $13.0 million, 2025 $19.0 million. The largest single line in 2025: The Sequoias San Francisco to Sequoia Living, Inc (management, home office and administration), $3.4 million. Rent to related landlords came to $3.5 million and management or home-office charges to $12.8 million in 2025.","In 2025, 935 of 1070 California nursing homes with at least $2 million of expenses listed transactions with related parties; the middle one sent 7.6% of its expenses to them. 15 homes sent 25% or more (4 in the Bay Area). California homes listed $1,496.6 million of related-party transactions in 2025, against $1,173.1 million in 2019. The highest San Francisco share is The Sequoias San Francisco at 29.0%.","In 2025, 119 California nursing homes (21 in the Bay Area) reported a combined loss of $69.8 million while listing $232.0 million of payments to related parties; across the five report years, 481 homes did so at least once. In San Francisco it happened 6 times: City View Post Acute 2022 (loss $4.3 million, paid related parties $5.3 million); City View Post Acute 2023 (loss $1.6 million, paid related parties $3.2 million); Lawton Skilled Nursing and Rehabilitation Center 2019 (loss $312,852, paid related parties $1.5 million); San Francisco Health Care & Rehab 2024 (loss $653,754, paid related parties $1.8 million); The Sequoias San Francisco 2025 (loss $148,430, paid related parties $3.4 million); Tunnell Skilled Nursing and Rehabilitation Center 2019 (loss $1.6 million, paid related parties $2.8 million).","4 home-years in California list $250,000 or more of salary, wages or bonuses to related individuals among their related-party transactions. In San Francisco: San Francisco Health Care 2022, $1.3 million to 2 related people (net income $446,558); San Francisco Health Care & Rehab 2023, $1.7 million to 2 related people (net income $260,714).","California nursing homes named 1191 distinct related companies (names as each home spelled them, lightly cleaned). The largest in 2025 is PACS (management, home office and administration): $166.2 million from 136 homes. 16 of them were paid by San Francisco homes in at least one year, led by PACS ($166.2 million statewide in 2025), Aspen Healthcare Services, LLC ($34.8 million statewide in 2025), Theragen ($27.8 million statewide in 2025). A further $19.4 million in 2025 went to parties whose names may be people's and are not shown.","The October 2026 federal file lists 18 San Francisco nursing homes; 6 carry fines or payment denials in the last three years, $296,624 of fines in all (the middle fined California home: $35,840). The largest is San Francisco Health Care (2 fines, $152,404). Inspectors cited San Francisco homes 434 times in the file's inspection cycles, 1 of them at the immediate-jeopardy level; 0 homes carry the federal abuse marker.","The federal ownership file names 24 organizations as owners or managers of San Francisco homes; 15 of them also hold other homes. None has at least three other homes and fines per home at least twice the national average of $30,351. Highest fines per other home: Providence Administrative Consulting Services Inc (Victorian Post Acute): 70 other homes in 12 states, $3.6 million of fines, 49 immediate-jeopardy citations, 5 on special focus; Providence Group Inc (Central Gardens Post Acute, City View Post Acute): 31 other homes in 9 states, $1.3 million of fines, 21 immediate-jeopardy citations, 1 on special focus; Providence Group North LLC (San Francisco Post Acute): 11 other homes in 1 states, $330,358 of fines, 2 immediate-jeopardy citations, 0 on special focus.","6 City payees carry the exact name of a nursing home or of a related company named in a home's report: $234.8 million in all. Crestwood Behavioral Health Inc (DPH, 2017 to 2027, $191.1 million); Helios Healthcare LLC (DPH, 2017 to 2027, $29.5 million); Sequoia Living (HOM, HSA, MYR, 2018 to 2027, $12.8 million); Garfield Nursing Home Inc (DPH, 2027, $581,859); Eden Healthcare Center (DPH, 2027, $468,450); Medical Hill Healthcare Center (DPH, 2027, $336,220).","The State utilization files list 12 San Francisco hospices; 2 meet a test. Across the Bay Area 28 more do. In California the middle hospice discharged 48% of its patients alive in 2025 (35% in 2019) and 23% after 180 days or more. 9 Bay Area hospices filed no State report in two or more of the last three years; Medicare paid them $3.1 million in 2024.","15 San Francisco home-health agencies filed a State utilization report; 1 of them meets a test, and 41 more agencies across the Bay Area do. The middle California agency made 24.4 visits per patient in 2025. Highest in San Francisco: On Lok Senior Health Services (149.8).","From 1 January 2021 to 17 November 2026 the State issued 7,662 enforcement actions; 6,772 (88%) were long-term care citations and penalties. The largest dollar line is the nursing-hours penalty: 1,723 issued, $44.4 million initially assessed. Long-term care actions rose from 825 in 2021 to 1,417 in 2025, and long-term care penalty dollars from $7.4 million to $23.6 million.","Walgreen Co was under an integrity agreement from January 11, 2019 to July 16, 2024 (settlement $3.5 million) and signed a new one on April 18, 2025 for five years, covering its retail pharmacy operations across the United States, including controlled-substance dispensing; neither document names a San Francisco location, so the San Francisco stores are covered as part of the national retail business. Medi-Lynx Cardiac Monitoring's agreement ran from June 23, 2017 to October 5, 2022 (settlement $2.9 million) and is closed. Reportable events posted under the agreements include $512,923.78, $37,998.53, $71,595.27 in settlements with the Inspector General.","Checked; nothing unusual. San Francisco homes' combined net income was -1.4% of revenue in 2011 and 1.4% in 2015, against 3.7% for the rest of California in 2015. The file covers 2011 to 2015 only, so it gives history, not a test of the current figures.","5 of the 6 nursing-home operators the City pays by name also appear in the contract screens (growth, no-bid or sole-source): $222.0 million paid in all; 3 of the 5 are confirmed as the operator by their registered address, the rest are name matches. Crestwood Behavioral Health Inc and Helios Healthcare LLC are counted separately here, but their contract notices share a telephone, e-mail domain or officers, so they may be one management. The largest, Crestwood Behavioral Health Inc, was paid $191.1 million; its Public Health agreement grew to $224.2 million (190% over the first approval) and a further $26.3 million contract followed that was not put out to bid (bidding waived by ordinance).","19 City-paid operators hold State-licensed sites with serious citations or upheld complaints and are flagged in three or more older screen groups. Largest product: Catholic Charities (9 Type A, 8 upheld, 13 groups: autopilot, capacity, claims, daycare, exceptions, expected, federal_flags, leaks, pay, sidedoors, winners, xlink_money, youth_records).","29 related companies and owner chains named by San Francisco nursing homes were searched in the lobbying, donor, commissioner, requested-payment and gift screens: 3 found: Bmo Bank, N.A. (lobbyists: What clients pay lobbyists; lobbyists: The firms that are paid to lobby; lobbyists: Organizations the City pays that pay lobbyists); Front Porch (lobbyists: What clients pay lobbyists); Sequoia Living (statemoney: City payees in State politics). Nothing else: no management, staffing or landlord company of a for-profit San Francisco home lobbies, gives, or is declared by a commissioner in the published rows.","The homes' own disclosure reports show who their related companies are and where each year's profit went; older State data sets, federal archives and State enforcement and licensing files extend the health cases back in time. Each screen is a reason to look and never a finding; dealings with related companies are permitted when disclosed.","40 of 42 homes agree to the dollar on all three figures (amount owed by related parties, related-party payments on page 10.4, net income); 2 differ: Morton Bakar Center, San Jose Healthcare & Wellness Center. The 42 reports together carry $1,021.5 million owed by related parties and list $71.0 million of related-party transactions.","The 42 homes reported $177.7 million of net income for their 2025 report year; the amount owed to them by related parties rose by $305.2 million. Most of the rise is at the 4 Life Generations Healthcare homes (Cedar Crest, Plum Tree, Pleasanton, Vista Manor): their related balance went from $32.9 million to $179.2 million in one year, on $15.3 million of net income, while the cash in their own accounts fell from $137.6 million to $816,962. At the other homes the rise ($159.0 million) is close to their profit; in 35 homes it was at least three quarters of the year's profit. Owners' draws, dividends and related-party transfers on the equity page are blank in every report.","The 42 reports list $71.0 million of transactions with 32 related parties (names as written). The largest is PACS (25 homes, $31.6 million, support services). 10 lines are related-party building leases ($14.6 million), of which the reports themselves remove $11.7 million for Medi-Cal. Across all lines the reports' own adjustments net $-11.3 million: related-party prices are taken out and replaced by the related company's cost, including $40.9 million of home-office cost added on lines with no transaction amount.","10 groups. PACS: 25 of the 42 homes, $431.4 million owed by related parties; its reports list up to 136 other facilities under the same ownership. Humangood: 2 homes, $228.3 million; Life Generations Healthcare: 4 homes, $179.2 million; Aspen Healthcare Services, LLC: 3 homes, $60.4 million; Telecare Corporation: 2 homes, $55.4 million. 34 of the 42 reports leave the parent's name (page 3.1 line 15) blank although they answer that the home is a subsidiary.","Central Gardens Post Acute: $10.7 million owed in 2023, $18.1 million in 2025 (net income over the years $9.1 million); San Francisco Post Acute: $16.9 million owed in 2023, $25.0 million in 2025 (net income over the years $10.3 million); Victorian Post Acute: $12.6 million owed in 2023, $20.2 million in 2025 (net income over the years $8.9 million).","Checked; nothing unusual. All 35 reports that fill in the trust-account lines add up to within a dollar.","The 42 homes (owed $1,021.5 million at their latest report) were owed $318.0 million in 2012, $235.3 million in 2015, $371.9 million in 2018, $476.5 million in 2020, $503.4 million in 2021 and $717.2 million in 2024; the total rose 42% in the last year alone. 14 of them already met the test (at least half of assets and at least $3 million) in 2015 or earlier; 7 met it first in 2022 or later. The three San Francisco homes (each naming PACS as a related party from 2020 on) went from -$1.5 million in 2015 to $16.6 million in 2020 and $63.4 million in 2025: the balance grew by $46.8 million after 2020, against $49.9 million of net income the three reported in 2021 to 2025. Across the 41 homes with a 2020 report, the balance grew by $523.3 million after 2020 against $558.9 million of net income; for 25 of them the growth was within a quarter of the income. The 42 homes listed related-party transactions of $76.3 million in 2020 and $75.0 million in 2021 ($40.8 million in 2015, $72.3 million in 2025).","32 of 192 Bay Area skilled-nursing homes met the test in 2015 ($479.8 million); 44 of 187 in 2021 ($725.5 million); 42 of 182 in 2025. The years 2020 and 2021, missing from the case, sit on the same rising line.","11 Bay Area home-years since 2013 (45 in California) show cash and securities falling by $5 million or more and the net amount owed by related parties rising by at least three quarters of the fall: $200.8 million in all. 8 are in 2025, moving $173.3 million: Cedar Crest Nursing and Rehabilitation Center (cash $36.0 million to $0, owed by related parties $7.3 million to $45.6 million); Plum Tree Care Center (cash $35.2 million to $0, owed by related parties $6.5 million to $43.2 million); Vista Manor Nursing Center (cash $34.9 million to $0, owed by related parties $3.7 million to $41.0 million); Pleasanton Nursing and Rehabilitation Center (cash $31.5 million to $478,409, owed by related parties $15.4 million to $49.3 million); Smith Ranch Skilled Nursing & Rehabilitation Ctr (cash $13.2 million to $398,321, owed by related parties -$13.1 million to $605,414); Lawton Skilled Nursing & Rehabilitation Center (cash $9.1 million to $218,549, owed by related parties -$11.2 million to -$2.7 million).","Yes, one group stands out. The 27 California homes whose 2025 reports name Life Generations Healthcare / Generations Healthcare, Inc as parent (8 in the Bay Area, 1 in San Francisco) had $314.0 million of cash and securities at the end of 2024 and $6.1 million at the end of 2025 in the State’s pivot profiles. The profiles use a derived cash column. The corresponding figures on the 27 Life Generations homes’ submitted balance sheets (page 5.1, lines 5 and 10) were $314,215,627 and $6,598,810. Over the same year the net amount related parties owed them went from -$36.3 million to $298.8 million, while the homes reported $53.6 million of net income. No other parent with two or more homes shows the same pattern (cash down $10 million or more and the related balance up by at least three quarters of that).","San Francisco skilled-nursing homes listed related-party transactions of $25.6 million in 2015, $23.1 million in 2016, $20.5 million in 2017, $12.4 million in 2018, $12.6 million in 2019, $24.7 million in 2020, $17.4 million in 2021, $20.7 million in 2022, $13.6 million in 2023, $13.0 million in 2024, $19.0 million in 2025. Across all eleven years, 25 home-years show a loss smaller than what the home paid related parties; 19 of them are in the years added here ($48.1 million paid in those years): California Convalescent Hospital 2017 (loss $101,536, paid $144,092); Central Gardens 2015 (loss $599,304, paid $617,971); Central Gardens 2016 (loss $3,486, paid $392,371); Central Gardens 2017 (loss $108,180, paid $317,371); Central Gardens 2018 (loss $399,491, paid $694,912); City View Post Acute 2020 (loss $2.2 million, paid $5.1 million); City View Post Acute 2021 (loss $3.2 million, paid $4.9 million); Kindred Nursing & Rehabilitation Nineteenth Avenue 2017 (loss $1.0 million, paid $1.6 million); Kindred Transitional Care & Rehabilitation-Tunnell Center 2015 (loss $1.8 million, paid $6.2 million); Kindred Transitional Care and Rehabilitation-Lawton 2015 (loss $153,459, paid $3.9 million); Kindred Transitional Care and Rehabilitation-Tunnell Center 2016 (loss $889,836, paid $4.7 million); Lawton Skilled Nursing and Rehabilitation Center 2017 (loss $2.8 million, paid $3.8 million); Lawton Skilled Nursing and Rehabilitation Center 2018 (loss $644,087, paid $1.5 million); Lawton Skilled Nursing and Rehabilitation Center 2020 (loss $78,423, paid $2.2 million); San Francisco Health Care 2016 (loss $1.6 million, paid $5.2 million); Sequoias San Francisco Convalescent Hospital 2015 (loss $1.3 million, paid $2.1 million); Sequoias San Francisco Convalescent Hospital 2016 (loss $74,138, paid $2.2 million); Sequoias San Francisco Convalescent Hospital 2020 (loss $1.1 million, paid $2.4 million); Sheffield Convalescent Hospital 2015 (loss $324, paid $87,586).","25 home-years: California Convalescent Hospital 2017 (loss $101,536, paid $144,092); Central Gardens 2015 (loss $599,304, paid $617,971); Central Gardens 2016 (loss $3,486, paid $392,371); Central Gardens 2017 (loss $108,180, paid $317,371); Central Gardens 2018 (loss $399,491, paid $694,912); City View Post Acute 2020 (loss $2.2 million, paid $5.1 million); City View Post Acute 2021 (loss $3.2 million, paid $4.9 million); City View Post Acute 2022 (loss $4.3 million, paid $5.3 million); City View Post Acute 2023 (loss $1.6 million, paid $3.2 million); Kindred Nursing & Rehabilitation Nineteenth Avenue 2017 (loss $1.0 million, paid $1.6 million); Kindred Transitional Care & Rehabilitation-Tunnell Center 2015 (loss $1.8 million, paid $6.2 million); Kindred Transitional Care and Rehabilitation-Lawton 2015 (loss $153,459, paid $3.9 million); Kindred Transitional Care and Rehabilitation-Tunnell Center 2016 (loss $889,836, paid $4.7 million); Lawton Skilled Nursing and Rehabilitation Center 2017 (loss $2.8 million, paid $3.8 million); Lawton Skilled Nursing and Rehabilitation Center 2018 (loss $644,087, paid $1.5 million); Lawton Skilled Nursing and Rehabilitation Center 2019 (loss $312,852, paid $1.5 million); Lawton Skilled Nursing and Rehabilitation Center 2020 (loss $78,423, paid $2.2 million); San Francisco Health Care 2016 (loss $1.6 million, paid $5.2 million); San Francisco Health Care & Rehab 2024 (loss $653,754, paid $1.8 million); Sequoias San Francisco Convalescent Hospital 2015 (loss $1.3 million, paid $2.1 million); Sequoias San Francisco Convalescent Hospital 2016 (loss $74,138, paid $2.2 million); Sequoias San Francisco Convalescent Hospital 2020 (loss $1.1 million, paid $2.4 million); Sheffield Convalescent Hospital 2015 (loss $324, paid $87,586); The Sequoias San Francisco 2025 (loss $148,430, paid $3.4 million); Tunnell Skilled Nursing and Rehabilitation Center 2019 (loss $1.6 million, paid $2.8 million).","3 home-years: San Francisco Health Care 2020, $1.4 million to 1 related person (net income $2.8 million); San Francisco Health Care 2022, $1.3 million to 2 related people (net income $446,558); San Francisco Health Care & Rehab 2023, $1.7 million to 2 related people (net income $260,714).","Net income agrees to the dollar for 94% of homes in the 2019 trend pivot, 98% of homes in the 2020 trend pivot, 74% of homes in the 2021 trend pivot, 73% of homes in the 2022 trend pivot, 91% of homes in the 2023 trend pivot; 96% in the 2015 pivot, 96% in the 2016 pivot, 96% in the 2017 pivot, 98% in the 2018 pivot, 91% in the 2023 pivot, 98% in the 2024 pivot, 96% in the 2025 pivot. 36 Bay Area home-years differ by $1 million or more in net income or in the net related-party balance; in 35 of them the summary table holds one report where the complete set holds two or more (a change of operator in the year). The four Santa Clara homes whose operator changed on 1 August 2025 are the largest: the 2025 pivot keeps the outgoing operator's balance, the complete set the incoming one's.","The State's own staffing file stops in 2015, so it cannot test the 2025-26 federal figures. As history: of the 22 low-staff homes, 22 are in it; 10 of the 22 with a 2015 figure were below the California middle that year (3.60 nursing hours per patient day), 13 were below it in most years from 2011 to 2015 and 7 in every year they reported.","The 42 case homes are held by 41 different licensees. 10 licensee names follow one pattern (place name + \"IDENCE OPCO, LLC\", e.g. San Franciscoidence Opco, LLC; Golden Gateidence Opco, LLC), 4 begin \"GHC of\", and Garfield Neurobehavioral Center and Morton Bakar Center are both licensed to Garfield Nursing Home Inc. San Francisco: San Francisco Post Acute (San Franciscoidence Opco, LLC); Victorian Post Acute (Golden Gateidence Opco, LLC); Central Gardens Post Acute (Golden California Healthcare, LLC).","From January 2021 to 13 November 2026 the State issued 114 citations and penalties to 32 of the 42 case homes (66 after the archived file ends on 2024-06-28); 54 were nursing-hours penalties; final amounts $2.0 million. The 19 low-staff case homes with actions received 75 (46 after June 2024), 35 of them nursing-hours penalties (22 after June 2024). San Francisco homes: 42 actions at 12 homes, 2 class AA and 1 class A citations, $1.1 million.","Checked; nothing unusual for San Francisco. California: 7,662 actions since January 2021, 1,723 of them nursing-hours penalties. Bay Area: 650; San Francisco 55 (43 at nursing homes; final amounts $1.6 million).","5 of the 5 case hospices looked up are in the State listing. The licensee of \"Physicians Hospice Group\" (4625 1st St, Suite 230, Pleasanton) is A Plus Prime Care Hospice, Inc, which settles the name-and-address link that earlier screens could only suggest. Home Connect Hospice, LLC is licensed as Valor Hospice; Attaie Healthcare, Inc. runs Sunbright Hospice Care and, next door (Suite B), Sunbright Home Health Care; a sister company, Home Connect Home Health, LLC, holds a Fremont home-health licence. 3 of the listed hospices show no federal certification number in the listing.","11 Bay Area street addresses carry three or more different hospice or home-health licensees (0 in San Francisco); the most at one address is 7 (24301 Southland Dr, Hayward).","Alameda County had 27 licensed hospices in 2016-17, 58 at the peak in 2021-22 and 51 in 2025-26; San Francisco 7 and 9; California 1,237 and 2,208.","With one measure for every year (discharges other than death, over all discharges), the California middle hospice discharged 24% of its patients alive in 2015 and 26% in 2016, against 35% in 2019 and 37% in 2021 (the later forms drop this table; on the diagnosis table used for the case the middle went from 35% in 2019 to 48% in 2025). 12% of 2015 discharges came after 180 days or more. Hospices filing a report: 661 in 2015 (53 in the Bay Area), 1637 in 2025 (110). None of the case hospices is in the 2015 or 2016 files. A Plus Hospice, Inc. (2016: 20 patients), a separate licence in the same Pleasanton building as A Plus Prime Care, does appear.","23 of the 42 case homes reported under a different name in 2015 or 2016; 3 named no parent; 5 named a person as parent (not shown).","Checked; nothing that bears on a case. San Francisco hospitals together wrote off 0.74% of gross patient revenue as charity in 2015-16 and 1.51% in 2024-25 ($555.5 million); together they reported a net loss of $203.7 million in 2024-25. No hospital is named in an open case; the files add two baseline years to the hospital series.","Joining the December 2019 to 2025 editions with October 2026, 60 homes (San Francisco, the related-balance case and the homes named in other cases) carry $3.4 million of federal fines; $2.7 million of it, at 40 homes, is dated before October 2023 and so is missing from the current file. San Francisco homes: $2.1 million in all, $1.8 million before October 2023; largest Laguna Honda Hospital & Rehabilitation Ctr D/p Snf ($1.2 million, 5 fines). Largest overall: Laguna Honda Hospital & Rehabilitation Ctr D/p Snf, San Francisco ($1.2 million).","3 of 21 San Francisco homes show more than one registered operator name across the December 2019 to October 2026 federal editions: Central Gardens Post Acute (Central Gardens, INC. / Golden California Healthcare, LLC); City View Post Acute (Ghc of San Fran 180, LLC / Pine Street Snf LLC); San Francisco Towers (Covia Communities / Front Porch Communities and Services).","4 of the case's hospices are in the November 2026 federal file. 1 score below the California middle Hospice Care Index of 9.0 (out of 10); 0 have no index published. Home Connect Hospice, LLC: index 5.0 (3.6% of California hospices score as low or lower), Medicare spending per patient $25,014 (national percentile 80); Grace and Glory Hospice, Inc: index 9.0 (75.6% of California hospices score as low or lower), Medicare spending per patient $39,935 (national percentile 98); Best Hospice Provider, INC.: index 9.0 (75.6% of California hospices score as low or lower), Medicare spending per patient $22,523 (national percentile 70); Physicians Hospice Group: index 10.0 (100.0% of California hospices score as low or lower), Medicare spending per patient $28,599 (national percentile 89). The federal quality file lists Attaie Healthcare Inc and A Plus Prime Care Hospice Inc under trade names; the federal enrolment file carries their registered names and the same certification numbers.","41 Bay Area street addresses carry two or more separately named hospices or home-health agencies in the November 2026 federal files; 0 involve an agency in the hospice-surge case. 14 telephone numbers are given by more than one agency. Largest: 4075 Evergreen Village Square, San Jose (3 agencies).","Of 126 provider numbers named in Health billing cases and the medical screens, 3 appear as San Francisco clinicians in the October 2026 federal file. The rest are organizations (pharmacies, suppliers, clinics), which the clinician file does not list, or are not enrolled in Medicare in San Francisco. The file holds 152,910 California clinicians and 8,859 with a San Francisco practice address.","64 San Francisco street addresses carry two or more separately enrolled Medicare group practices; the most is 2100 Webster St, San Francisco 94115 with 12 practices and 97 clinicians. The top addresses are hospital and medical-office campuses; nothing here resembles the single-apartment registrations of the San José case.","Checked; nothing tied to a case. 12 dialysis facilities (Davita: 5, Independent: 3, Fresenius Medical Care: 2) and 13 hospitals are in the November 2026 files; 0 dialysis facilities are rated worse than expected on patient survival. No dialysis or hospital operator appears in a current case.","40 San Francisco pharmacies and medical-supply companies drew $100,000 or more of Medicaid drug money in 2018-2024. 9 have no Board of Pharmacy permit of any kind at their address in the November 2026 file ($17.1 million). 6 of them are medical-supply companies ($15.2 million, led by SUNRISE MEDICAL SUPPLIES INC ($4.9 million, 2018-2024); SF ROYAL MEDICAL SUPPLY, INC ($4.4 million, 2018-2024)); the file has no licence type for medical-device sellers, so for them the absence shows nothing. The 3 pharmacies without a permit in the file: SAN FRANCISCO STATE UNIVERSITY STUDENT HEALTH SERVICES ($955,250, 2018-2024); WALGREEN CO. ($647,385, 2018-2020); WALGREEN CO ($214,586, 2018-2024). 2 sit at an address whose only permit was issued after their Medicaid drug money ended: ALTO PHARMACY LLC ($1.9 million, 2020-2024); MISSION WELLNESS HEALTHCARE LLC ($923,542, 2018-2023). 1 more holds a permit issued after their drug money began, and 4 Walgreens stores carry the chain's 28 August 2025 permit date (screen 'boards-walgreens-reissue').","486 of the 488 Walgreens retail-pharmacy permits in the Board of Pharmacy file carry the original issue date 28 August 2025, including 32 of the 32 in San Francisco. California retail-pharmacy permits issued in 2025: 817, against 284 the year before. Stores that Medicaid paid in 2018-2024 therefore show a permit newer than the payments.","Of 6,037 San Francisco physicians and nurse practitioners whose federal licence number is in the State files, 27 were paid by Medicare in a data year after their licence's listed expiry, with the licence now lapsed (\"delinquent\"): $2.2 million in those years. For physicians: 18 of 4,946, $2.0 million. Another 205 gave a licence number the boards' files do not list at all (113 of them paid in 2023 or 2024); the files omit revoked, surrendered and cancelled licences, and some federal entries carry a mistyped number. Nobody is named here.","Medical Board (physicians): complaints 8,340 to 9,280, accusations filed 299 to 253, licences revoked 39 to 22, discipline per 1,000 new cases 39.8 to 33.9; Board of Registered Nursing: complaints 3,383 to 5,704, accusations filed 1,133 to 740, licences revoked 429 to 219, discipline per 1,000 new cases 168.6 to 71.8; Board of Pharmacy: complaints 2,671 to 3,246, accusations filed 299 to 135, licences revoked 132 to 62, discipline per 1,000 new cases 80.7 to 65.4. The Division of Investigation, which investigates for the boards without their own investigators, had 817 open cases at 7/31/2016 and 1,041 at 10/31/2025; in 112 of 112 months the average age of the cases it closed was above its 180-day target (latest 379 days).","Checked; the files give a count, not a test. By address of record, the Medical Board file holds 8,201 current San Francisco physician licences and 484 medical-group name permits (155 lapsed). None of the three files has a discipline, probation or accusation field, and none lists revoked, surrendered or cancelled licences, so a check of discipline needs the Medical Board's bulk file, which was not obtained. No licensee or permit in any of the three files gives the San José apartment address of the 62 new billing companies in the case 'one-apartment-62-billing-numbers'.","The HHS notice of July 21, 2026 puts the California deferral at about $867.5 million (Minnesota $199 million), the same day as the deferral letter for the quarter ended 31 March 2026. The April 2026 guidance repeats the $1.8 billion of questionable claims across eight States with State-only programs, and the September 2025 letter ends federal money in health-plan payments for this group from January 2027. The September 2025 letter describes the 2024 audit as covering an 18-month period, but the dates it gives (October 1, 2018 to June 30, 2019) span 9 months.","Federal enrolment, owner and revocation files, State licence and enforcement files, nursing homes' own financial reports and the City ledger, joined on the numbers each regulator assigns. Each screen is a place to look and never a finding; where only a name matches, the screen says the records do not show whether it is the same party.","402 of the 479 hospices on the federal list of revoked Medicare enrolments are in California. Medicare had paid 339 of them $1,264.7 million over fiscal 2018 to 2024, $411.2 million in 2024 alone (9.5% of all Medicare hospice payments in the State). The most common reasons: abuse of billing privileges: pattern or practice (179); affiliation that poses an undue risk (140); on-site review (78). 86 of the 402 still appear in the State's listing of open licensed hospices dated 16 October 2026 (those 86 were paid $109.6 million in 2024). Hospices enrolled in Medicare in California fell from 2,212 in the 2023-04 file to 1,142 in 2026-10; 395 of the 1,481 that left are on the revoked list. 43 California home-health agencies are on the same list (paid $286.5 million over 2018 to 2024). In the six Bay Area counties: 1 (Devotion Hospice Care Inc, Hayward, revoked 2025-04-18, paid $2.5 million).","175 hospices and home-health agencies still enrolled in Medicare in October 2026 have an owner whose federal owner number also held an ownership interest in a revoked California agency; Medicare paid those 175 $303.1 million in 2024. Counting officers, directors and managing employees as well, 409 still-enrolled agencies ($528.2 million in 2024) share a numbered person or company with a revoked one. 4 are in the six Bay Area counties: Sojourn Hospice & Palliative Care -East Bay, LLC. (Pleasanton, hospice, $10.8 million in 2024; shares 1 federal owner number(s) with Optimal Home Health Care Inc.); Owen Healthcare LLC (San Francisco, hospice, $4.3 million in 2024; shares 2 federal owner number(s) with Los Robles Healthcare LLC); Blossom Ridge Health LLC (San Francisco, home-health agency, $2.7 million in 2024; shares 4 federal owner number(s) with Blossom Ridge Home Health Agency LLC); Owen Healthcare LLC (Oakland, home-health agency, $2.4 million in 2024; shares 2 federal owner number(s) with Los Robles Healthcare LLC).","16 of 67 Bay Area hospices certified for Medicare since 2018, with an owner on file, show every present owner arriving after the hospice was certified for Medicare, the first between 6 and 36 months later; Medicare paid them $39.3 million in 2024. Across California the count is 747 of 1,857 certified since 2018 ($805.1 million in 2024). Largest in the Bay Area: A Plus Prime Care Hospice Inc (Pleasanton): certified 2021-12-11, present owners from 2022-10-01 (9 months later), $8.8 million in 2024; Aveem Inc (Concord): certified 2020-11-18, present owners from 2021-08-25 (9 months later), $4.0 million in 2024; Alameda Care Hospice (Walnut Creek): certified 2021-03-10, present owners from 2022-09-15 (18 months later), $4.0 million in 2024; Home Connect Hospice, LLC. (Fremont): certified 2022-02-09, present owners from 2023-09-01 (18 months later), $4.0 million in 2024.","6 of the 6 hospices named in the case are in the federal owner files. For 3 of them every present owner arrived after Medicare certification: A Plus Prime Care Hospice Inc (certified 2021-12-11, present owners from 2022-10-01, 9 months later; $8.8 million in 2024); Home Connect Hospice, LLC. (trading as Valor Hospice) (certified 2022-02-09, present owners from 2023-09-01, 18 months later; $4.0 million in 2024); Seasons Hospice & Palliative Care Of California -Oakland LLC (trading as Accentcare Hospice & Palliative Care Of California - Oakland) (certified 2019-01-27, present owners from 2020-12-21, 22 months later; $2.4 million in 2024). 5 share a federal owner or manager number with another hospice or home-health enrolment: A Plus Prime Care Hospice Inc with Merced Hospice Inc; Best Hospice Providers Inc with Best Home Health Providers, Inc.; Home Connect Hospice, LLC. (trading as Valor Hospice) with Wondrous Love Hospice Care LLC; Attaie Healthcare Inc with Attaie Healthcare Inc.  No two of the case hospices share a number with each other. None is on the federal revoked list.","48 Bay Area nursing homes changed owner between 2019 and 2024 and filed a full-year State report on each side of the sale. Their nursing hours per resident day moved by a middle figure of 6.2% from the last year before the sale to the latest year after, against 2.8% for Bay Area homes that did not change hands over the same years; 23 of the 48 did worse than those peers. Rent and lease cost per resident day moved 58.0% at the sold homes against 3.7% at peers; in dollars, the 48 homes reported $35.6 million of rent and lease cost in their last year before the sale and $67.3 million in their latest year. 15 homes show both nursing hours below peers and rent above peers. At 25 the amount owed by related companies rose by $1 million or more. State citations of class AA, A or B in the two years before and after the sale, where both windows are on file (48 homes): 27 before, 34 after. Furthest below peers on nursing hours: Mountain View Healthcare Center (Mountain View; to Cirby Holdings LLC on 2022-10-01; 4.73 to 3.65 hours); City View Post Acute (San Francisco; to Pine Street SNF LLC on 2023-05-01; 4.9 to 3.93 hours); Atherton Park Post-Acute (Menlo Park; to Carpinteria LLC on 2019-08-01; 4.09 to 3.64 hours).","23 City payees paid $100,000 or more are confirmed as the operator of a State-licensed health facility by two keys: the payee's own City business registration sits at the licensed address, and that registration carries the facility's or the licensee's name. The City paid them $1,466.0 million. 6 of them hold sites with State enforcement actions since 2021 (21 actions, 2 citations of class AA or A, $324,000 in final penalties). Largest private operators with a regulator's record or a large related-company balance: CRESTWOOD BEHAVIORAL HEALTH INC ($191.1 million from DPH; 1 confirmed and 22 other sites under the same licensee; 12 State action(s), 0 of class AA or A; net income $13.6 million on revenue of $118.9 million at its 5 site(s) filing a State nursing-home report for 2025); Sequoia Living ($12.8 million from HSA, HOM, MYR; 1 confirmed and 2 other sites under the same licensee; 1 State action(s), 0 of class AA or A; net income $-2.2 million on revenue of $13.6 million at its 3 site(s) filing a State nursing-home report for 2025; $1.2 million owed by related companies); Planned Parenthood Northern California ($637,000 from DPH; 1 confirmed and 16 other sites under the same licensee; 2 State action(s), 0 of class AA or A); Garfield Nursing Home Inc ($581,859 from DPH; 2 confirmed and 0 other sites under the same licensee; 3 State action(s), 2 of class AA or A; net income $2.5 million on revenue of $27.7 million at its 2 site(s) filing a State nursing-home report for 2025; $55.4 million owed by related companies); River Vista Behavioral Health ($369,113 from DPH; 1 confirmed and 0 other sites under the same licensee; 2 State action(s), 0 of class AA or A); MEDICAL HILL HEALTHCARE CENTER ($336,220 from DPH; 1 confirmed and 0 other sites under the same licensee; 1 State action(s), 0 of class AA or A; net income $5.1 million on revenue of $30.2 million at its 1 site(s) filing a State nursing-home report for 2025; $45.2 million owed by related companies). The middle California nursing home with $2 million or more of revenue reported net income of 7.3% of revenue in 2025. 1 payee(s) began their latest City contract within 24 months after a class AA or A citation or an immediate-jeopardy penalty at a confirmed site: Garfield Nursing Home Inc (DPH - Skilled Nursing Facility (no. 1000038024), from 2026-07-01 to 2029-03-31, $6.6 million agreed; 2025-10-23: class A citation at Garfield Neurobehavioral Center, $25,000, under appeal; 2025-11-24: class AA citation at Garfield Neurobehavioral Center, $75,000, under appeal).","Of 3,546 businesses on the federal exclusion list, 1 carries the exact name of a City payee paid $10,000 or more; of 1,278 organizations on the State Medi-Cal suspended list, 0; of 5,130 organization entries on the federal revoked list, 4 payee names. 1 of these 5 name matches has a second key: Hometown Medical Supplies LLC (federal revoked medicare enrolments, San Jose, CA, from 2024-06-02; dme supplier - medical supply company: noncompliance (dme standards not met)) is City payee HOMETOWN MEDICAL SUPPLIES, paid $29,907 under fiscal-year labels 2017 to 2024. For the other 4 the records do not show whether it is the same organisation or two with the same name: Health Education Services (federal exclusion list, El Reno, OK, 1995-08-03) and City payee HEALTH EDUCATION SERVICES ($19,689, 2017 to 2025). The rest are national chains or universities with one store or clinic enrolment revoked. No City payment under a later fiscal-year label than the listing date is shown for any confirmed match.","Between April 2023 and October 2026, 1,481 California hospice enrolments and 555 home-health enrolments left the Medicare files. At 21 suites a differently named agency of the same kind first appeared in the files after the one that left (21 pairs). 0 of the pairs follow an agency on the federal revoked list; 7 pairs share a federal owner or manager number; 0 do both. 2 pairs are in the six Bay Area counties: Seva Homecare, LLC. (last in the 2023-04 file; not on the revoked list) then Mission Home Health Of East Bay LLC (from 2023-07) at 5976 W Las Positas Blvd Ste 118, Pleasanton; no owner or manager number in common; Dc Giron Healthcare Services Inc (last in the 2024-01 file; not on the revoked list) then 1Health Home Health Inc (from 2026-07) at 3170 Crow Canyon Pl Ste 130, San Ramon; no owner or manager number in common. No revoked agency is followed at its suite by a new agency sharing an owner or manager number.","The State's four-year complaint, deficiency and enforcement tables for 79 Bay Area nursing homes, set against each home's size. A complaint is an allegation, and a citation under appeal has not been upheld.","79 homes (42 case homes, 47 homes sold in 2019 to 2024, 10 both): 6,765 complaint and incident allegations in 2023 to 2025 by the site's count, from 5,011 distinct complaints and facility-reported incidents; 0.878 allegations per 1,000 resident days pooled (middle home 0.676); 533 complaints and incidents substantiated with deficiencies. Highest per resident day: Eden Healthcare Center (2.689; 346 allegations, 182 complaints and incidents, 13 substantiated); Herman Health Care Center (2.308; 241 allegations, 234 complaints and incidents, 16 substantiated); Bay Marina Post Acute (2.237; 217 allegations, 128 complaints and incidents, 22 substantiated). 166 State enforcement actions 2023 to 2026 to date, 23 of them class AA or A citations; 13 under appeal.","Per 1,000 resident days, 2023 to 2025 (allegations / distinct complaints and incidents / substantiated with deficiencies): case homes below the California middle on nurse hours 0.831 / 0.624 / 0.0693, other case homes 0.703 / 0.567 / 0.0416. Sold homes that are not case homes 0.985 / 0.701 / 0.081, case homes not sold 0.859 / 0.702 / 0.0663; State actions per 100 beds, 2023 to 2026: 2.09 and 2.4. Life Generations' four homes: 0.516 / 0.423 / 0.0232, 3 State actions, none of class AA or A.","77 homes with both figures. Lowest quarter on nurse hours: 0.891 allegations and 0.0836 substantiated complaints and incidents per 1,000 resident days, 2.13 State actions per 100 beds; highest quarter: 0.812, 0.0558 and 2.41. Rank correlation of nurse hours with the allegation rate -0.10, with the substantiated rate -0.25.","166 State actions at the 79 homes, 2023 to 2026 to date: 86 citations and 80 administrative penalties. 23 citations of class AA or A; 17 of them not appealed or with the appeal closed, 6 under appeal. 13 actions in all are appealed and still open. Garfield Neurobehavioral Center: 3 citations, all appealed and open (class B 2025-04-15 $3,000; class A 2025-10-23 $25,000; class AA 2025-11-24 $75,000).","At 79 of 79 homes the site's official count of complaints and reported incidents for 2023 to 2026 equals the number of allegation rows in the complaint table, not the number of distinct complaints: 8,543 allegations from 6,286 distinct complaints and incidents. Enforcement rows equal the site's official count of actions at 79 homes and the State dashboard export (issued 2023 to 13 November 2026) at 79.","47 of the 47 sold homes have a facility export. At 33 the current property owner dates from the same day as the current licensee; at 1 (The Ridge Post-Acute) the property owner is among the companies the export lists as owning the licensee, and at 3 it is the licensee itself. Of all 79 homes' exports, 36 homes hold a \"Patient Needs Waiver of 2.4\" and 17 a \"Workforce Shortage Waiver of 2.4 only\" for July 2026 to June 2027.","Zuckerberg San Francisco General, the City's own hospital, declared the most diversion hours: 3,852 in 2024 (42% of all hospitals), 2,868 in 2025 (36% of all hospitals), 2,033 in 2026 (40% of all hospitals). Diversion was suspended city-wide 721 times in 2024 and 371 in 2025."],["Errors, investigations and fraud found, against other counties.","Program by program: what the law allows and what auditors found."],["Overtime beyond the hours in a year.","Employers paid $685.3 million into the San Francisco employees’ pension fund in the year to June 2025, against $592.6 million in 2015; the bill peaked at $836.6 million in 2021. Pensions paid out rose from $1.12 billion to $2.00 billion (79% more), and in every one of the 11 years pensions paid were larger than employer and worker contributions together, by $871.9 million in 2025; investment income has to cover that. There were 1.12 working members for each person drawing a pension in 2015 and 1.08 in 2025. By the fund’s own smoothed measure it held 85.6% of what it has promised in 2015 and 96.8% in 2025.","The fund earned less than the return it assumed in 5 of the 11 years to June 2025. Over the whole period it earned 8.0% a year on average against 7.4% assumed; that average leans on one year, 2021, when it earned 35.4%, and without it the other 10 years average 5.6%. The worst year was 2022 (-6.2%). The assumed return was cut from 7.50% to 7.20% over the period.","In the State Controller’s table for fiscal 2024, San Francisco’s fund reports 96.6% of its promises funded (valuation of 2023-07-01), rank 6 of the 17 local funds pulled; the middle fund reports 88.0%. San Francisco’s share was 86.3% in the fiscal 2017 table. The lowest is Golden Gate Transit District Amalgamated Retirement Plan at 48.0%.","Of 6,280 full-time employees who left the payroll between fiscal 2021 and 2025 and can be followed for the three years before their last two, 66 (1.1%) were paid at least 1.5 times their usual pay, and at least $40,000 more, in one of their last two years: it is uncommon. The rise was mostly overtime for 31, mostly regular salary for 22 and mostly other pay for 13. The pension fund’s plan summary and the payroll file both indicate that overtime does not count toward a San Francisco pension, so the overtime cases measure cash paid, not a larger pension; a rise in regular salary, as in the other 22 cases, is the kind that counts toward one.","Police has the most cases (14 of 569 leavers followed), then Fire Department (13) and Municipal Transportation Agcy (9). Among departments with at least 50 leavers followed, the highest share is Fire Department: 3.5%, 6 of its 13 cases mostly overtime.","Across 972 job classes the top hourly step rose 48.3% between fiscal 2014-15 and 2025-26 in the typical class, and nine classes in ten rose less than 55.7%. 72 classes rose at least ten points more than typical; 25 of them gained extra steps at the top of the scale and 47 rose that far on the same or fewer steps. The largest rise is Architectural Administrator (job class 5120): $57.01 to $109.12 an hour, up 91%.","Citywide, filled positions rose 3.7% between fiscal 2019 and fiscal 2026 (34,936 to 36,211) while spending rose 39%; the typical job class’s top pay step rose 31% over the same years. In 10 departments positions fell while spending rose, and in 8 of those spending rose by a quarter or more: Sheriff (positions -13%, spending +51%); War Memorial (positions -9%, spending +51%); Juvenile Probation (positions -20%, spending +39%); Police (positions -15%, spending +40%); Adult Probation (positions -10%, spending +39%).","The City payroll for fiscal 2013 to 2026 shows 120 job classes with a single holder, 56 classes that first appeared with one or two people already in them, 70 employees paid far above the top of their class’s published scale year after year, and 3,290 permanent appointments that went to the person already doing the job. Each is a measured oddity; what would settle any of them is the job announcement and the appointment paperwork, which the payroll does not contain.","In fiscal 2026, 120 of the 955 job classes in use had exactly one paid holder in the whole City and 79 had two; together they were paid $46.7 million. In 38 of the one-holder classes the present holder is the only person the payroll has ever named in it; 16 have had the same single holder in every year since fiscal 2017, the first year with names. 50 of the one-holder posts are exempt from civil-service hiring and 7 of the one- and two-holder classes carry a generic title. One-holder classes numbered 170 in fiscal 2013.","Of the 955 job classes in use in fiscal 2026, 561 were used by a single department and 175 of those had one or two holders. Municipal Transportation Agency has the most small department-only classes (20), followed by Public Health (20) and City Administrator (14).","People paid in generic-title classes rose from 1,829 in fiscal 2013 to 3,555 in fiscal 2026, and their share of all City pay from 6.7% to 10.7% ($604.0 million in fiscal 2026). The Manager grades grew from 812 to 1,361 people (+68%) and the Administrative Analyst and Management Assistant grades from 758 to 1,760 (+132%), while the number of people paid by the City overall grew by 8%. The largest added cost is in Public Health (Manager I to VIII: 106 to 213 people, $30.4 million more a year).","184 job classes were paid for the first time after fiscal 2013, the first year of the file. Of those first paid from fiscal 2018 on, when holders can be named, 56 started with one or two holders: 12 went to people who were not on the payroll the year before, 37 were exempt from civil-service hiring and 6 carry a generic title; the number with no rate in either published pay table is 1. The class pages and classification actions date 44 of the 56: 20 were created in the fiscal year they were first paid or the one before, so the class was written and filled together, and 24 were older classes that had stood unused. For the other 12 no creation date is published; 10 of them had a pay rate in the published tables more than a year before anyone was paid in them. In all, 34 of the 56 are older classes.","17 job classes were paid in only one or two consecutive fiscal years between 2014 and 2025 and never again; 12 of them had a single holder. Together they were paid $2.4 million. 8 of the 17 carry the tag OCII in their title, a block of classes that entered the file together in fiscal 2015.","Of 21,675 people who first appear on the payroll between fiscal 2019 and fiscal 2026, 785 (3.6%) started at an hourly rate in the best-paid twentieth of City staff ($239,116 a year or more in fiscal 2026). 477 of them are physicians, dentists and nurses, 121 are in legal classes and 123 in management classes; 670 hold exempt appointments. Outside medicine, the most top-tier entrants are in City Attorney (74), Public Health (48) and District Attorney (35); the most into management classes are in Public Health (29) and Public Utilities Commission (16).","21 new entrants carry a rare name that also appears in a connected record: 14 were paid as campaign staff or consultants by the committee of an elected official in whose department they then started (Board of Supervisors: 8, Mayor: 4, Sheriff: 1, District Attorney: 1), 3 had been paid as a board or commission member under the department that then put them on staff, and 4 of the 7 people already listed as coming from a funded organization started in the best-paid twentieth. 5 of the 21 started in the best-paid twentieth. Every row is a name match with a second link, not a confirmed identity.","70 employees were paid regular salary at least a quarter above the highest published rate for their job class, and a quarter above their classmates, in two or more fiscal years since 2017; 43 of them in five years or more and 40 still in fiscal 2026. The pay above the top of the scale adds up to $27.2 million. 61 of the 70 are in the file’s engineering job families, which include the architecture classes, and the departments are Public Utilities Commission (25), Airport Commission (21), Public Works (14), Municipal Transportation Agency (4). 19 of the 40 still above the line in fiscal 2026 were paid one of 6 identical salaries (the commonest, $286,278, went to 8 people), which points to a second pay schedule that the payroll file does not name. A further 117 employees were above the line in a single year only.","Pay more than a quarter above the published ceiling was found in 111 combinations of class and department since fiscal 2017. The largest is Engineer (class 5241) in Public Utilities Commission: 10 people over 59 employee-years, $3.7 million above the ceiling; then Assoc Engineer (class 5207) in Public Utilities Commission (9 people, $3.5 million) and Engineer (class 5241) in Airport Commission (7 people, $2.2 million).","This is uncommon. Across the City, pay recorded as “other” was 5.8% of regular salary in fiscal 2026 ($282.5 million). Only 13 job classes with five or more holders were above a fifth that year, and 1 of those had been above it routinely (in eight of the ten years, or in every year the class had five holders): Director (class 1115), Retirement System (53.1%). The largest in money is Sergeant 3 (class Q052): $18.3 million for 412 holders, 23.0% of salary, up from 8.0% in fiscal 2017. 5 of the 13 classes were under a tenth when first counted.","6 job classes with 688 paid holders in fiscal 2026 have no rate in either published pay table; they were paid $21.4 million. The largest are Technology Expert I (class 9976) (64 holders, $11.0 million), Public Service Trainee (class 9910) (612, $7.9 million) and Transitional Department Head (class 0971) (4, $1.1 million).","Of 20,023 employees who can be followed by name across a four-year window, 291 (1.5%) held three or more classes in it with regular salary up by half or more; 85 of them started in a trainee, apprentice, aide or junior class. For comparison, the middle rise over four years for people who stayed in one class was 14.9%. Of the other 206, 89 ended in an exempt appointment and 5 in a class with one or two holders. Among departments with 100 or more followed employees the rate is highest in Homelessness Services (6.2%), Port (5.5%), Emergency Management (4.0%).","54 followed employees moved, between fiscal 2018 and 2026, into a job class in which they were the only paid holder in the City that year. In 42 cases nobody had been paid in the class the year before, and in 22 of those the person’s rate of pay rose by a tenth or more. The middle rise across all 54 moves was 10.2%.","Citywide, people paid in management classes rose from 1,091 in fiscal 2013 to 1,727 in fiscal 2026: from 3.1 to 4.6 for every 100 other employees, and from 4.9% to 6.5% of all pay ($365.3 million in fiscal 2026). The share of staff in management classes rose most in Human Rights Commission (2.9% to 17.7%), Health Service System (6.1% to 18.9%) and Retirement System (18.7% to 30.4%).","Between fiscal 2018 and 2025, 20 one-person posts changed hands inside a fiscal year with the outgoing and incoming holders’ paid hours adding to about one full year (1,950 to 2,250 hours), which leaves no room for a vacancy. That is few. In 4 of them the newcomer was not on the City payroll before that year, and none of those was a permanent civil-service appointment, the kind that needs an examination and a list; 3 were exempt appointments. For scale, the civil grand jury put the middle time to fill a City job at 255 days in 2023.","87 positions can be paired this way; the middle gap between one holder leaving and the next taking up the position is 43 days. In 35 the successor’s date is within a week of the departure or before it, and in 4 of those the successor’s name first appears on the City payroll in that same fiscal year.","Of 22,243 permanent civil-service appointments since October 2018 that can be matched to the payroll, 3,290 (14.8%) went to someone already paid in that class in that department the fiscal year before on another footing: 1,922 as temporary exempt, 930 as permanent exempt and 438 as provisional. 325 of those 3,290 were the only person ever appointed from their eligible list, and 683 were ranked first on it. Among departments with 50 or more appointments the share is highest in Technology (50.3%), Mayor (40.8%) and Homelessness Services (36.9%). 9,005 appointments (40.5%) went to people not on the payroll the year before.","The payroll file does record a type of appointment for every row from fiscal 2017. On that record, exempt appointments were 14.0% of City staff time in fiscal 2017 and 13.2% in fiscal 2026: 2,570 full-time equivalents on permanent exempt appointments (2,379 in fiscal 2017) and 2,165 on temporary exempt ones (2,304 in fiscal 2017). Provisional appointments went from 48 to 194 full-time equivalents. 102 people were paid in classes the file marks as unrepresented. The exempt share rose most in Human Rights Commission (14.9% to 66.8%) and Emergency Management (4.8% to 20.3%).","816 people were paid as temporary exempt employees for at least 1,040 hours a year in the same class and department for four or more consecutive fiscal years since 2017; 258 of those runs were still going in fiscal 2026. The largest groups are Special Nurse (class P103) in Public Health (169), Public Svc Aide-Public Works (class 9916) in Public Works (57) and Custodian (class 2708) in City Administrator (25).","473 followed employees worked at least two full years on a permanent appointment and were later paid as temporary exempt staff for 1,000 hours or fewer in a year, with no return to permanent full-time work; 248 came back in the same job class they had held. 254 were being paid this way in fiscal 2026, for 146,463 hours and $12.5 million. The most are in Public Health (91), Police (89) and Public Utilities Commission (53). 63 of the 473 were paid for more than 960 hours in a fiscal year of return. The Charter allows a retiree 120 working days or 960 hours a fiscal year, whichever is greater, so more than 960 hours is within the limit if worked in 120 working days or fewer; the payroll cannot say who is retired or how many days anyone worked.","9 reports from 2020 to 2026 in the project’s library make a finding about hiring. Two themes recur. Hiring is slow: the civil grand jury wrote in 2023 that the “time it takes the City to fill a job is 255 days”. And where reviewers found favoritism, it ran through the routes these screens measure: a promotion made “through a very unusual process”, a temporary Special Assistant post for a departing director, hiring panels with an interested party on them, and complaints about managers’ hiring that were sent on to the Civil Service Commission.","The City’s careers site listed 182 postings on 14 November 2026: 102 Permanent Civil Service, 37 Temporary Exempt, 33 Permanent Exempt, 10 Temporary Provisional Permanent Civil Service. 60 have no closing date and 94 give one that can be measured. The middle posting with a date is open 15 days (Permanent Civil Service: 17, Temporary Exempt: 14, Permanent Exempt: 14, Temporary Provisional Permanent Civil Service: 14). 12 are open five days or fewer, 6 of them exempt appointments and 3 permanent civil-service ones. 6 permanent civil-service or provisional postings show fewer than three days between the two dates, the minimum the Commission’s rule sets in business days. 3 of the dated postings are for a class with no more than two paid holders in fiscal 2026. This is the only direct test of “filled basically immediately” that the public record allows, and it covers current postings only: past announcements are not published.","Classification actions established 171 job classes between fiscal 2015 and 2026. 80 were first paid in the fiscal year of the action or the next; 42 of those started with one or two holders and 31 with exactly one, and 6 of the small ones went to people not on the payroll the year before. 66 have never had a paid holder, and 7 already had paid holders before the action, which marks a class being re-established or renumbered.","The Annual Salary Ordinance authorized 38,470 positions in job classes for fiscal 2026; the payroll shows 34,060 full-time equivalents paid on permanent civil-service, permanent exempt and provisional appointments, 11.5% fewer. The ordinance carries a further 907 full-time equivalents on lines titled “Temporary - Miscellaneous” and “Temporary - Nurses” (wording normalised from the scans), against 2,222 actually paid on temporary and other appointments. The largest gaps are in Public Health (1,164 of 8,301), Municipal Transportation Agency (928 of 6,416) and Public Utilities Commission (580 of 2,798). 84 class-and-department lines carried at least one authorized position in each of the five years to 2026 with nobody paid in them (147 positions in 2026), and 20 were paid well above their authorized count.","84 class-and-department lines were authorized at one position or more in every year from fiscal 2022 to 2026 with nobody paid in them there; the largest is Community Paramedic (class H009) in Fire Department (16 positions). 20 lines were paid at least three full-time equivalents, and half as much again, above their count; the largest is Assistant Engineer (class 5203) in Public Works (72 paid against 1 authorized). The table includes every qualifying class and department in both groups.","As of 30 June 2026 Human Resources reported 1,847 appointments in Charter category 16 (temporary and seasonal), 83 in category 17 (substitutes) and 2,309 in category 18 (special projects), 4,239 in all, outside the transportation agency. The Charter limits are half-time in a fiscal year (1,040 hours) for category 16, two years for 17 and three years for 18. Human Resources listed 5 appointments past the limit in its report of 15 September 2025, 6 appointments past the limit in its report of 5 October 2026, and wrote in 2 earlier reports that none had exceeded it. The payroll, on a different footing, shows 740 people outside the transportation agency paid as temporary exempt for more than 1,040 hours a year in the same class and department for four or more fiscal years running. The share of employees in the capped categories (1, 2 and 4 to 12) was reported as 1.56% on 30 June 2026.","The Commission’s own audits of recruitments mostly found compliance, with two named lapses in verifying an appointee’s qualifications, one at the Department of Building Inspection and one in the Mayor’s Office. Requests for an inspection of merit-system practice rose from 55 in fiscal 2021-2022 to 112 in fiscal 2024-2025. Between 7 November 2022 and 2 February 2026 the Commission’s minutes record 52 agenda items on appeals of an examination or a rejected application: 41 denied, 6 put off to a later meeting, 5 granted in whole or part; appellants are counted, never named. Human Resources confirmed to the Commission that hiring someone as exempt first and examining afterwards was the standing practice where no eligible list existed.","The Commission’s minutes from 5 August 2024 to 2 February 2026 print tables listing 282 proposed personal-services contracts worth $3.03 billion, all entered as new requests; the Commission’s packets for the same meetings show that about a quarter were amendments of earlier approvals, so for those the minutes’ amount may be the cumulative ceiling rather than the amount added, and the total may count earlier approvals again. Airport Commission accounts for $778.4 million across 28 contracts, Public Health for $713.2 million across 66 and Public Utilities Commission for $564.8 million across 46. Over the whole period from November 2022 the minutes carry 160 agenda items on such contracts. The tables print no reason for contracting out; the request forms, which do, are read in the screen of contracting-out requests (csc_records).","The 100 highest-paid City employees averaged $562,109 in fiscal 2026, 103% more than in fiscal 2013, against 77% for the middle employee, and overtime is now 44% of their pay; pay recorded outside salary and overtime reached $282.5 million. At organizations the City funds, tax returns report 186 bonuses to listed officers and staff in years when the City supplied a quarter or more of revenue, and 68 bonus or raise years that coincide with a deficit, a staff cut, audit findings or a missed target; high pay is not misconduct.","The 100 highest-paid people averaged $277,570 in fiscal 2013 and $562,109 in fiscal 2026, a rise of 103%; the middle person on the payroll went from $72,135 to $127,713, a rise of 77%. Stopping at fiscal 2025, the last year of ordinary length, the rises are 82% and 61%. The top 100 were paid 3.8 times the middle person in fiscal 2013 and 4.4 times in fiscal 2026. What changed is overtime: it was 17% of the top 100’s pay in fiscal 2013 and 44% in fiscal 2026, while regular salary fell from 66% to 46% of it. It took $489,350 to be in the top 100 in fiscal 2026, against $245,674 in fiscal 2013.","In fiscal 2026 the 100 highest-paid people were paid $56.2 million between them. For 73 of the 100, overtime was the largest part of their pay; for 27 it was regular salary. They work in Police (58), Sheriff (16), Public Health (11), Fire Department (8), Retirement System (6) and 1 other department. The largest overtime figure is $522,802 (Frank Harrell, Sergeant 3 (class Q052), on 6,132 paid hours). The highest total, $1.5 million, belongs to a row whose regular salary alone is $1.1 million for the class Deputy Sheriff (class 8304): that is far above anything the class is paid and looks like a one-time entry such as back pay rather than a rate of pay. All qualifying rows are listed.","In fiscal 2013 the top 100 came mostly from Fire Department (51), Police (28) and Public Health (5); in fiscal 2026 from Police (58), Sheriff (16) and Public Health (11). The largest single source in fiscal 2026 is Sergeant 3 (class Q052) in Police: 24 of the 100, with overtime 54% of their pay. Over the 14 years, 91 pairings of department and class have supplied at least one of the 1,400 places; 27 supplied one in fiscal 2026.","Pay recorded as “other” came to $282.5 million in fiscal 2026, 5.8% of regular salary; it was $135.5 million (4.5%) in fiscal 2017 and $152.1 million (6.2%) in fiscal 2013. Police, Public Health and Fire Department paid 61% of it. In Police it rose from $19.1 million (5.9% of salary) in fiscal 2017 to $63.2 million (15.1%) in fiscal 2026, about $20,940 for each person paid. The payroll file does not say what the payments were; the budget analyst wrote in 2024 that the police agreement in force from July 2023 brought “wage increases, retention pay, step adjustments, and signing bonus”.","Ten job classes received 52% of all other pay in fiscal 2026. The largest is Police Officer 3 (class Q004): $27.1 million for 1,144 holders, $23,654 each and 17.2% of their salary (it was 6.2% in fiscal 2017). Then Registered Nurse (class 2320) ($26.5 million) and Firefighter (class H002) ($21.3 million). For each holder the largest among classes with five or more is Director (class 1115), $162,844 each, followed by Asst Chf of Dept (Fire Dept) (class H050) ($61,205) and Battalion Chief, Fire Suppress (class H040) ($59,458).","431 people with full-year hours received $50,000 or more in other pay in fiscal 2026, 38 of them $100,000 or more. They are in Police (213), Fire Department (94), Public Health (66) and Retirement System (13); the commonest classes are Sergeant 3 (91), Police Officer 3 (54), Lieutenant 3 (40) and Battalion Chief, Fire Suppress (27). The largest is $304,434 on a salary of $454,815 (Alison Romano, Chf Executive & Investment Ofc (class 1119), Retirement System). 13 of the 431 are at the Retirement System, in its executive and investment classes (Chf Executive & Investment Ofc, Director, Managing Director and Senior Portfolio Manager); 9 of them were paid $50,000 or more in other pay the year before as well. The file does not say what it is or how it is set.","Since fiscal 2017 there are 1,411 cases of other pay of $25,000 or more that was also larger than the person’s regular salary for the year, $70.2 million in all. In 568 the person does not appear on the payroll again and in 149 only briefly, which is the pattern of a payout on leaving; in 17 the person worked on; 116 are in the latest year and 561 carry names too common to follow. Only 5 of the 1,411 had full-year hours in the year itself. The departments are Fire Department (335), Police (323), Public Health (246) and Public Utilities Commission (98). The largest is $329,750 against a salary of $262,161 (Mark Gonzales, Dep Chf of Dept (Fire Dept) (class 0150), fiscal 2019).","Of 27,654 employees who can be followed for four years or more, 242 had at least one year in which other pay was five times their usual level and $50,000 or more above it (291 such years, $25.1 million): it is uncommon. 135 of the years were on leaving ($12.0 million), 70 were followed by more work ($5.9 million) and 86 are in fiscal 2026, too recent to tell. The mid-career ones are concentrated: 52 of the 70 are in Police, 63 of the 70 fall in fiscal 2023 to 2025, and the commonest classes are Lieutenant 3 (20), Sergeant 3 (16) and Police Officer 3 (10). 33 people have two or more such years, which is a new level of pay rather than a spike. Largest mid-career: $308,317 against a usual $4,017 (a line attorney at the Public Defender, Attorney (Civil/Criminal) (class 8177), fiscal 2023). Largest on leaving: $325,556 (William Coaker Jr., Chf Executive & Investment Ofc (class 1119), fiscal 2022).","29 departments have at least one spike. Police has 52 of the 70 mid-career ones and 145 in all ($11.7 million); next come Public Health (31, of which 17 on leaving), Public Defender (4, of which 1 on leaving) and District Attorney (13, of which 10 on leaving). Outside Police, 18 of 146 spike years were followed by more work; the rest are payouts on leaving or fall in the latest year.","7,675 employees can be followed through six full years in one job class; 5,910 of them began a five-year stretch already at the top of what their classmates were paid. For those, salary typically moved -0.3 points against the published top step: it tracked the scale. 59 people rose at least 20 points faster than both the published top step and their classmates. They are in Fire Department (25), Police (12), Municipal Transportation Agency (10) and Public Works (4); the commonest classes are Firefighter (13), Police Officer (8), Transit Operator (7) and Captain, Emergency Med Svcs (5). The largest: Brice Peoples, Lieutenant, Fire Suppression (class H020), Fire Department, from $134,806 in fiscal 2017 to $253,496 in fiscal 2022, up 88% while the scale rose 11%.","Across 972 job classes the top published step rose 48.3% between fiscal 2014-15 and fiscal 2025-26 in the typical class; 72 classes rose at least ten points more. 65 of those had paid holders in fiscal 2026: 4,877 people with regular salaries of $430.8 million. Had those classes risen only as fast as the typical class, the salary bill would be about $39.6 million a year lower. Most of that is Transit Operator (class 9163) (2,528 holders, top step up 63%, about $18.5 million a year), IS Business Analyst-Principal (class 1054) (182, $4.0 million) and Parking Control Officer (class 8214) (352, $2.9 million).","Benefits cost $1.79 billion in fiscal 2026, 36.7% of regular salary and $43,754 for each person paid; in fiscal 2013 they cost $978.9 million, 40.0% of salary and $26,196 a person. The share fell because the City’s retirement payment did: it peaked at 23.0% of salary in fiscal 2021 and was 15.1% in fiscal 2026. Health and dental cover rose from $9,470 to $17,488 a person (85%), about in step with a rise of 82% in regular salary for each person paid.","Among 42 departments with 50 or more people paid in fiscal 2026, benefits run from 30.2% of regular salary in City Attorney to 56.8% in Sheriff, followed by Adult Probation (49.1%) and Juvenile Probation (47.4%). For each person the highest is Sheriff at $72,164. The spread comes mostly from the retirement column: 38.3% of salary at the top against 10.9% at the bottom.","115 job classes had 50 or more full-year holders in fiscal 2026 and 52 of them are used by two or more departments with ten holders each. In the typical one the best-paying department’s middle holder is paid 2.5% more than the lowest’s: the same class is paid much the same wherever it sits. 6 classes show a gap of a tenth or more. The widest is Attorney (Civil/Criminal) (class 8177): $282,075 in City Attorney against $219,122 in Public Defender (29%), then Automotive Service Worker (class 7410) (Municipal Transportation Agency over Police, 18%).","Of 19,968 full-year holders of the 115 largest classes in fiscal 2026, 36 were paid at least 1.4 times the middle holder of their own class in salary and other pay together. For 27 the difference is mainly regular salary and for 9 mainly other pay; 24 have a regular salary more than a quarter above the top of the class’s published scale. The commonest classes are Assoc Engineer (7), Engineer (5), Registered Nurse (4) and EMT/Paramedic/Firefighter (4). Furthest above: Scott Neu, Deputy Sheriff (class 8304), Sheriff, $1.3 million against a class middle of $174,603.","1,405 returns from 352 City-paid organizations for tax years ending 2020 to 2026 carry a compensation schedule, and they report 2,484 bonus or incentive payments at 135 organizations, $538.1 million in all. 81% of the money is at Dignity Health Dba St Mary's Medical Ctr ($285.0 million), Delta Dental Plan of California ($113.3 million) and California Pacific Medical Center ($37.8 million), where City payments were never more than 0.05% of revenue in any year on file: 1,425 of the payments are at organizations where the City supplied under 1% of revenue that year. The largest single figure is $17.0 million (Lloyd H Dean, Chief Executive Officer, Dignity Health Dba St Mary's Medical Ctr, year ended 30 June 2022). The next table keeps only organizations for which the City is a main funder.","186 bonus or incentive payments, $4.6 million in all, were reported by 38 organizations in tax years when City payments were a quarter or more of their revenue. The largest is $198,400 on base pay of $407,517 (Karen J Hanrahan, President/CEO (Thru 2/23), Glide Foundation, year ended 30 June 2023, when the City supplied 33% of revenue). In 8 cases the bonus was half of base pay or more. The organizations reporting most are Low Income Investment Fund (21), Meals On Wheels (19), Larkin Street Youth Services (14) and Safe & Sound (13). The Controller’s cost rules for nonprofit contracts list “Bonuses paid to staff.” among costs that may not be charged to the City, so a bonus at a City-funded organization has to be paid from other money; the returns do not show which money paid it.","132 organizations reported at least one bonus from their own funds; 35 did so on every return on file (three or more). 38 of the 132 did so in a year when the City supplied a quarter or more of their revenue, and 5 of those on every return: Low Income Investment Fund, Main Street Launch, San Francisco Aids Foundation, Project Open Hand and Family Caregiver Alliance. On the form’s questions about how pay is set, 4 organizations answered yes in at least one year to pay that depends on revenue (10 returns), 2 to pay that depends on net earnings (3 returns) and 64 to payments not fixed in advance (177 returns). Among organizations with a bonus on file, those answering yes on revenue or net earnings are Delta Dental Plan of California, California Academy of Sciences, SF Community Clinic Consortium, Caremessage and Flourish Agenda, Inc.","Across 1,029 organization-years in which City payments were a quarter or more of revenue, 68 at 54 organizations combine a bonus or a raise of a tenth or more for the top officer with at least one measurable difficulty in the same year: a deficit of 2% of spending or more (51), employees down by a tenth or more (23), federal audit findings (6) or a programme below target with a City monitor (2); 14 show two or more. 22 involve bonuses and 48 a raise. Of 360 top-officer pay changes that could be tested, 121 were rises of a tenth or more. First on the list: Main Street Launch, year ended 30 June 2024 (spent $3.1 million more than it took in; employees down from 20 to 17). A further 17 bonus or raise years are at organizations among the 60 most-paid for which no result recorded by anyone else was found; they are listed last.","The top officer’s pay could be set against size at 413 City-paid organizations; the middle figure runs from $95,746 for those with revenue under $1 million to $625,623 for those with $100 million or more. Of 186 for which the City supplied a quarter or more of revenue, 17 pay their top officer at least one and a half times the middle figure for their size. Furthest above: The Transgender Advocates For Justice An, $280,261 on revenue of $761,690 (2.9 times its peers; 37% of revenue), then San Francisco Clean City Coalition ($212,724, 2.2 times) and Arriba Juntos - Ial ($521,378, 1.9 times). At 21 of them, pay to all listed officers and key employees is a fifth or more of all spending, led by San Francisco Clean City Coalition (42%). At 1 more the listed pay is larger than everything the return says was spent, which points to pay carried by another body or to an error on the return rather than to high pay. 18 organizations whose top officer is paid mainly by a related organization are left out here and appear in the table on related organizations.","21 organizations answered yes at least once to one of the eight listed benefits: first-class or charter travel (10), travel for companions (6), tax gross-up payments (4), a discretionary spending account (2), a housing allowance or residence (7), payments for business use of a personal residence (1), health or social club dues (6) and personal services such as a driver or chef (4). 67 reported a severance or change-of-control payment and 25 a supplemental retirement plan; 10 show $5,000 or more owed to the organization by its officers or other insiders and 29 money lent to it by them. Where the City supplied a quarter or more of revenue, 3 organizations disclose a listed benefit (Homeless Children's Network, Alive & Free and Glide Foundation), 14 a severance payment and 19 a balance with officers or other insiders. Those organizations are listed first.","320 current officers, directors and key employees at 33 City-paid organizations were reported as receiving $10,000 or more from a related organization in at least one year (681 person-years); 260 were paid by the filing organization as well in their latest year and 60 only by the related one. 34% of them are at three organizations: Adventist Health St Helena (44), Dignity Health Dba St Mary's Medical Ctr (33) and California Pacific Medical Center (32). Where the City supplied a quarter or more of the filer’s revenue there are 40 at 9 organizations: the best paid is Kenneth Lombard, President of Bridge Regional Partners Inc, with $1.1 million from related organizations and $45,067 from the organization itself in the year ended 31 December 2024.","The payroll file describes the column as irregular payments “including premium pay, incentive pay, or other one-time payments” and gives no breakdown. Other City documents on disk name the pieces without sizing them: premium pay and holiday pay in the police budget; retention pay and a signing bonus under the police agreement from July 2023; and unused leave, which the City’s accounts say “consist of vacation, sick, compensatory time, and other holidays and leaves”. No document on disk says how much of the yearly total is premiums, how much is leave paid out and how much is bonus or incentive pay, so the screens in this file do not say either.","8 documents from 2022 to 2026 in the project’s library make a finding or set a rule about pay. On nonprofits the record is specific: the Controller’s cost rules list “Bonuses paid to staff.” as a cost that may not be charged to the City, its audit of one housing provider is headed “More Than $200,000 in Bonuses Were Unplanned and Paid”, and the City Attorney alleged, of payments another grantee had billed to a City grant, that “these stipends were in fact performance bonuses”. On boards, the budget analyst found that the society running the Zoo had not reviewed its chief executive’s performance from 2019 until the chief executive left. On City pay the library holds less: the budget analyst’s account of what the police agreement added, and its finding on sick leave. No report on disk reviews leave payouts on retirement, premium pay citywide or the Retirement System’s pay.","The State Controller’s pay files put San Francisco beside every other public employer in California, and add the payrolls of the school district, City College, BART, the bridge district and the court, which the City’s own file leaves out. They list positions, not people.","Taken whole, no. In 2024 San Francisco paid overtime equal to 10.6% of regular pay, 11,486 dollars per paid position; it ranks 7 of 8 among itself and the 7 largest other city payrolls (Los Angeles, San Diego, San Jose, Long Beach, Oakland, Sacramento, Fresno), whose middle figure is 15.3%. Its overtime share moved from 9.4% in 2019 to 10.4% in 2025. Other pay was 4.9% of regular pay and lump sums 0.5%.","In 2025, San Francisco’s police overtime was 37.7% of regular pay (50,098 dollars per paid position), ranking 1 of 7; the middle of the others was 28.4% (31,292 dollars per position). Highest: San Francisco, Long Beach (city), Oakland (city). In 2019 San Francisco’s share was 18.2%, rank 4 of 7 (middle of the others 19.0%). 193 of its positions were paid more in overtime than in regular pay (70.93 per 1,000 salaried positions).","In 2025, San Francisco’s sheriff and jails overtime was 47.6% of regular pay (55,706 dollars per paid position), ranking 1 of 13; the middle of the others was 24.9% (24,258 dollars per position). Highest: San Francisco, Alameda (county), San Mateo (county). In 2019 San Francisco’s share was 30.3%, rank 2 of 13 (middle of the others 18.3%). 106 of its positions were paid more in overtime than in regular pay (111.46 per 1,000 salaried positions).","In 2025, San Francisco’s fire overtime was 23.6% of regular pay (32,289 dollars per paid position), ranking 13 of 14; the middle of the others was 42.2% (42,385 dollars per position). Highest: San Diego (city), Los Angeles (county), Los Angeles (city). In 2019 San Francisco’s share was 17.9%, rank 12 of 14 (middle of the others 40.0%). 27 of its positions were paid more in overtime than in regular pay (14.17 per 1,000 salaried positions).","In 2024 San Francisco’s other pay plus lump sums came to 5.4% of regular pay (5,871 dollars per paid position), 10 of 13 against the 12 county payrolls compared; highest: Ventura 13.0%, Sacramento 12.2%, Contra Costa 8.9%.","In 2024 BART paid overtime equal to 16.2% of regular pay, 16,162 dollars per paid position, and 48 positions were paid more in overtime than in regular pay; it ranks 1 of 13 transit payrolls compared. Golden Gate Bridge district: 7.4%. Muni, inside the City payroll: 13.1%. Highest: San Francisco Bay Area Rapid Transit District 16.2%, San Diego Transit Corporation 15.7%, Sacramento Regional Transit System 14.6%.","8 public bodies in San Francisco report to the State but are not in the City’s payroll file. In the latest year on file: San Francisco Unified School District 2021: 10,664 paid positions, $623.9 million in wages, overtime 0.3% and other pay 27.1% of regular pay; City College of San Francisco (Community College District) 2022: 1,836 paid positions, $120.8 million in wages, overtime 12.2% and other pay 1.6% of regular pay; BART 2025: 4,573 paid positions, $619.5 million in wages, overtime 17.6% and other pay 10.5% of regular pay; Golden Gate Bridge, Highway and Transportation District 2025: 898 paid positions, $88.5 million in wages, overtime 8.0% and other pay 2.7% of regular pay; San Francisco Superior Court 2025: 547 paid positions, $60.3 million in wages, overtime 0.1% and other pay 6.6% of regular pay. Years missing from the export as saved: San Francisco Unified School District 2020, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025; City College of San Francisco (Community College District) 2023, 2024, 2025; Golden Gate Bridge, Highway and Transportation District 2020. The 2025 file for school districts was not among the files saved. The San Francisco Housing Authority does not appear in any of the files.","In 2021 San Francisco Unified recorded other pay equal to 26.8% of regular pay (12,324 dollars of other pay per paid position), rank 1 of 11 districts with 5,000 or more paid positions; the middle of the others was 7.3%. It is spread across ordinary jobs: Regular Classroom Teacher $22.0 million other and lump-sum pay on $90.3 million regular pay (1,428 positions); ELD Classroom Teacher $13.0 million other and lump-sum pay on $56.8 million regular pay (886 positions); Sped IA Sh -All Impair $7.6 million other and lump-sum pay on $15.8 million regular pay (782 positions).","Highest total wages in the latest year of each body: Asst Gm Finance, Interim (BART, 2025) $673,318; Police Sergeant (84 Hr) (BART, 2025) $601,013; Sr Police Officer-Adv. (84 Hr) (BART, 2025) $560,607; Police Sergeant (84 Hr) (BART, 2025) $485,852; General Manager (BART, 2025) $477,893; Police Sergeant (84 Hr) (BART, 2025) $474,421.","444 position-years from 2019 to 2025 had overtime at least equal to regular pay of $40,000 or more: BART 440, City College of San Francisco (Community College District) 3, Golden Gate Bridge, Highway and Transportation District 1. At BART the commonest positions are Train Operator (69), Senior Operations Foreworker (50), Electrician (47). Largest: Electrical Foreworker (BART, 2021), overtime $299,931 on regular pay of $114,840.","9,903 position-years at the City and the other San Francisco bodies had regular pay more than 10% and $10,000 above the reported range maximum. 7,123 of them sit in department-years where 40% or more of rows are above the range, which points to the range reported rather than the pay: San Francisco, Fire Department: 6,493 rows in 7 years (as much as 51.9% of the department); Golden Gate Bridge, Highway and Transportation District, Bus Operators: 601 rows in 5 years (as much as 68.6% of the department); City College of San Francisco (Community College District), Ccsf-Custodial Services: 29 rows in 1 year (as much as 42.6% of the department). The other 2,780 (1,161 of them rows that also name a second position): San Francisco 2,340; Golden Gate Bridge, Highway and Transportation District 251; City College of San Francisco (Community College District) 69; San Francisco Superior Court 59; BART 36; San Francisco Unified School District 24; Transbay Joint Powers Authority 1. At the City the largest groups are Public Health (1189), Police (260), Public Utilities Commission (201). Largest gap: Police Officer 3 (San Francisco, 2024), regular pay $634,859 against a top of $154,726.","Of 40,298 City rows in the State export for 2025, 37,027 carry a job title found in the Department of Human Resources rate table for fiscal 2025-26 (1,067 titles). 366 of them had regular pay more than 10% and $10,000 above 26 times the highest biweekly maximum listed for that title; 155 of them are rows that also name a second position, whose regular pay may include the second job, most in Public Health (159), Fire Department (76), Police (45). Largest: Senior Physician Specialist (Public Health), $602,798 against a top of $364,728.","12,376 rows from 2019 to 2025 name another position. The commonest: San Francisco, Special Nurse: 7,273 rows; San Francisco Unified School District, Interschool Athletics: 374 rows; San Francisco Unified School District, Sped Ia Sh -All Impair: 276 rows; San Francisco Unified School District, Summer Session Teacher: 252 rows. City rows that also list Special Nurse: 7,273; the largest total wages among them: Registered Nurse (2023) $590,692.","338 paid rows carry a position title naming a retiree or annuitant, $4.1 million in all; San Francisco Unified School District 2019 Day To Day Sub Teacher Retired: 172 rows, $2.2 million; San Francisco Unified School District 2021 Day To Day Sub Teacher Retired: 115 rows, $1.1 million; San Francisco Unified School District 2019 Retired Administrators Per Con: 14 rows, $306,118. Largest single row: Retired Administrators Per Con (San Francisco Unified School District, 2021) $59,505.","Not testable. The 76 State Controller files saved list positions and pay but no names or employee numbers, so no person can be followed from one employer to another.","San Francisco Unified’s average scheduled teacher salary was $103,472 in 2024-25, rank 8 of 15 districts and county offices with 2,000 or more salary-schedule full-time equivalents (middle of the others $104,440); in 2017-18 it was $73,379, rank 11 of 12. Salary-schedule full-time equivalents: 3,636 then, 3,263 now; average daily attendance 50,411 then, 45,172 now.","The Commission’s list for 16 June 2025 holds 11 requests worth $95.8 million (cumulative $98.2 million); Public Health asked for 4 of them, $80.2 million. 8 say the department had contracted out the same or similar services in the last three years, and in 4 the department answered that the City has employees or classes with the skills. The largest, $30.0 million over 60 months for unarmed guards at the public hospitals and clinics, is marked a temporary basis; the department wrote that the Sheriff’s Office used to do the work: “The San Francisco Sheriff's Office has provided these services but acknowledged that they are experiencing significant staffing challenges, and are unable to provide uninterrupted …”.","Charter section 10.104 lists 19 categories of exempt position. Categories 1 to 12 (Mayor’s and City Administrator’s policy staff, elected officers’ chief deputies, department heads and deputies, one secretary per commission, and others) share one overall cap: their proportion of full-time staff may not exceed the 1 July 1994 level. Categories 16 (temporary and seasonal, half-time a year), 17 (substitutes, two years) and 18 (special projects, three years) need Civil Service Commission approval and cannot be renewed. The section was last amended by Proposition C, approved 11/5/2024.","Charter A8.511(c)(2) lets a retired member work in a post requiring special skills for no more than 120 working days or 960 hours, whichever is greater, in one fiscal year, and bars using the retiree to replace a permanent civil-service employee. Section 10.105 caps provisional appointments at three years unless the Board approves a renewal.","City pay split by the union that bargains for each job: in fiscal 2026, five units drew 70.7% of $513.4 million in overtime. Union giving and pay growth are set side by side; bargaining and political giving are permitted, and timing is a question, not proof.","In fiscal 2026 the City paid $513.4 million in overtime and $283.9 million in other pay on $4.92 billion of regular pay. Five units drew 70.7% of the overtime: POA (unit 911) $156.8 million (47.1% of its regular pay, 70,190 dollars per person paid); Firefighters,Local 798, Unit 1 (unit 798) $63.9 million (23.8% of its regular pay, 34,097 dollars per person paid); SEIU, Local 1021, Misc (unit 790) $53.1 million (6.1% of its regular pay, 4,776 dollars per person paid); Deputy Sheriffs' Assoc (DSA) (unit 498) $47.8 million (50.0% of its regular pay, 63,366 dollars per person paid); TWU, Local 250-A, TransitOpr (unit 253) $41.2 million (20.6% of its regular pay, 16,239 dollars per person paid). Among units with $5 million or more of overtime, the fastest growth since fiscal 2019 was Sheriffs' Mgrs and Supv (MSA) (unit 499) (2.65 times) and POA (unit 911) (2.57 times).","Police overtime was $163.4 million in fiscal 2026 (against $62.8 million in fiscal 2019); 95.9% of it went to POA (unit 911). Sheriff overtime was $62.2 million (fiscal 2019: $31.2 million); 76.9% went to Deputy Sheriffs' Assoc (DSA) (unit 498). Fire overtime was $68.9 million, 92.6% to Firefighters,Local 798, Unit 1 (unit 798).","Year by year: POA $27.7 million in 2014, $60.9 million in 2019, $156.8 million in 2026; Firefighters,Local 798, Unit 1 $40.8 million in 2014, $37.5 million in 2019, $63.9 million in 2026; SEIU, Local 1021, Misc $16.4 million in 2014, $34.7 million in 2019, $53.1 million in 2026; Deputy Sheriffs' Assoc (DSA) $9.3 million in 2014, $25.5 million in 2019, $47.8 million in 2026.","In fiscal 2026, 545 rows were paid more than 10% and $10,000 above the top of their job code's scale, $18.6 million above it in all (fiscal 2019: 201 rows, $6.2 million). Most by amount: Firefighters,Local 798, Unit 1 (unit 798) 151 rows, $5.1 million; Prof & Tech Eng, Local 21 (unit 021) 82 rows, $5.1 million; POA (unit 911) 72 rows, $2.1 million. Largest job codes: Firefighter (job H002, Firefighters,Local 798, Unit 1): 54 rows, $1.6 million above a yearly top of $151,918; Transit Operator (job 9163, TWU, Local 250-A, TransitOpr): 103 rows, $1.5 million above a yearly top of $100,360; Lieutenant, Fire Suppression (job H020, Firefighters,Local 798, Unit 1): 39 rows, $1.3 million above a yearly top of $176,592.","4 of 11 requests drew a union objection, worth $80.2 million. The largest, $30.0 million for unarmed guards at the public hospitals and clinics, named one class, 8300 Sheriff's Cadet, and one union, 790 SEIU, Local 1021, Misc; 94 people held that class in fiscal 2026 (Sheriff 94 rows; none in Public Health), and they were paid $3.3 million of overtime. The same year the Sheriff paid $62.2 million of overtime, 76.9% of it to Deputy Sheriffs' Assoc (DSA) (unit 498), a unit the request does not name.","20 classes marked exempt were paid $50,000 or more of overtime in fiscal 2026, $5.9 million in all; largest: Protective Services Worker (job 2940, Human Services Agency) $1.5 million to 194 people; Captain 3 (job Q082, Police) $779,710 to 36 people; Protective Services Supervisor (job 2944, Human Services Agency) $767,953 to 44 people. By unit: SEIU, Local 1021, Misc (unit 535) $2.3 million; SEIU, Local 1021, Misc (unit 790) $1.0 million; POA (unit 911) $779,710.","Checked, nothing unusual: 1 of 39,822 rows whose job code is in the FY2025-26 table (0.0%) carry a different union code, so the payroll's units are the manual's units. Largest by pay: Public Svc Aide-Public Works (job 9916): payroll Miscellaneous Unrepresented Employees (unit 001), manual Laborers, Local 261 (unit 261), 1 rows.","The Budget and Legislative Analyst's audit of December 2024 found “approximately 51,000 ineligible 10B overtime hours were worked by employees with high sick leave usage between 2020 and 2023”, and that “SFPD has not adhered to key provisions of the Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) with the Police Officers’ Association related to sick leave”. In fiscal 2026 the unit was paid $156.8 million of overtime, 47.1% of its regular pay.","The City's labor agreements and codes fix crew sizes, minimum staffing, premiums and call-back pay. This section lists those rules from the agreement texts, prices them where the pay records allow, and tests two examples the owner raised.","29 rule entries (29 distinct rules) across 5 bargaining units plus 4 Charter or Administrative Code sections. The firefighters' agreement fixes crews of one officer and three firefighters on engines and squads and one officer and four on trucks (in 5 of 5 full texts since 2018); the deputies' agreement carries a jail-by-jail minimum table; electrical and craft agreements set minimum crews for specific jobs.","171 premium or guarantee entries in 35 units. Call-back pay with a minimum number of hours appears for 22 units (minimum 4 hours: 21, minimum 3 hours: 1). Police retention pay reaches 23% of base in the 2026-2030 agreement.","Overtime of the four fire suppression ranks that fill the fixed crews was $57.4 million in fiscal 2026 ($38.9 million in 2014; peak $75.4 million in fiscal 2022). Apparatus operator pay on the 261 driver and tiller positions is estimated at $2.0 million a year, $455,754 of it for 60 tiller positions; one seat around the clock on 20 trucks is about $10.5 million in base pay. 5 rules cannot be priced from the files on disk.","124 premium, allowance or differential lines priced across 33 distinct Controller cost letters; 39 are for fire, police or sheriff units. Largest: Retention Pay (San Francisco Police Officers Association, $9.9 million in the last year); Peace Officers Standards Trainings (POST) Rank Premium (San Francisco Police Officers' Association, $6.6 million in the last year); Training and Education (San Francisco Fire Fighters Union Local 798, $6.0 million in the last year).","14 passages from 9 official documents. The Controller (2004) listed San Francisco, Milwaukee and San Jose as staffing trucks with five and wrote that the remaining jurisdictions used four; the Budget and Legislative Analyst (2014) wrote that Proposition F locked in the 2004 response system. No review on disk calls a fire, police or sheriff staffing rule outdated; the Board's 2015 resolution called the 1,971-officer police minimum outdated in the sense of too low.","No labor agreement mentions wooden ladders. Two Fire Department files do: the Strategic Plan (2017) names \"wooden ladder repair and construction\" as a Central Shops service, and 2016 Commission minutes record a wooden ladder raise at the 150th-anniversary event. The rear driver exists as the tiller operator: the firefighters' agreement pays tiller operators a 5% premium, the 2026 vacancy list shows 60 tiller positions on 20 trucks, and the words \"second driver\" appear in none of the 3133 documents.","Police retention pay: highest step 7% in the first full text on disk, 23% in the 2026-2030 agreement. Fire training and education pay: highest rate 9% then 12%. 5 tracked values changed; the Charter's fixed 1,971-officer police minimum, quoted in 2015, is no longer in the Charter text on disk.","21 passages in 104 distinct bargaining summaries use \"outdated\" or \"obsolete\"; 13 concern pay or premiums (for example a physicians' premium built into steps 11-20, police retention and POST certificate language). Most are clean-up of language already superseded.","Departments must ask the Civil Service Commission before they hire outside firms for work City employees could do, and the request forms say why. These screens read four years of those requests, the Commission’s own five-year report and two hiring follow-ups, with police, sheriff and fire overtime set against other employers back to 2015.","Appeals and hearing requests before the Commission (new plus carried over) went from 95 in 2020-2021 to 116 in 2024-2025; in the last year 38 were about examinations, 39 about discrimination and 35 about bars on future City employment. Only 3 appeals in five years were about personal-services contracts. Inspection-service requests in the report’s chart went from 43 to 112; in 2024-2025 32 concerned certification and selection, 10 conflicts of interest and 7 appointments. The report lists the complaints as including favoritism, nepotism, appointees not meeting the minimum qualifications, order of layoff, probationary periods, reversion rights, out-of-class assignments, status grant, disqualification after appointment to the position, ….","Public Utilities Commission (2022-12-05): 9 of 10 hires were temporary exempt; 1 was permanent civil service. Emergency Management (2023-09-18): 122 filled of 189 authorized (35% vacant); staffing model 165. Both are the departments’ own accounts.","Police overtime was 14.3% of regular pay in 2015, rank 4 of 7 large-city police payrolls (middle of the others 15.0%); 18.2% in 2019 and 37.7% in 2025 (rank 1 of 7). Sheriff and jails: 20.2% in 2015, rank 2 of 13 (middle 16.0%), and 47.6% in 2025, rank 1 of 13. Fire: 23.6% in 2015, rank 12 of 14.","The civilian complaint files for the Police Department and the Sheriff's Office, read for allegations about pay, time, money and property. None concerns overtime or pay; the nearest are rarely sustained, like complaints in general.","None of 7,350 police allegations (2,837 cases) or 357 Sheriff's Office allegations (90 cases) concerns overtime, timecards, pay or outside work. The nearest, 26 allegations of absence from post, inattention, alcohol on duty or misuse of City equipment, had none sustained of 11 decided on the merits. Of 13 police complaints saying an officer took money or property or sought a bribe, none was sustained (8 had a merits finding; at the 6.0% case baseline, none sustained has a 61% chance); 4 went to the Police Department's Internal Affairs Division.","Police: 4.4% of allegations decided on the merits were sustained in 2024, 4.0% in 2025 and 2.9% in 2026 to 20 November; the median closed case took 98, 56 and 47 days. Sheriff's Office cases ran slower: median 308 days in 2026, with 9.8% sustained.","From fiscal 2019 to 2026 the adopted budgets set aside $1.83 billion for overtime and the ledger records $2.79 billion paid; paid overtime exceeded the budget in 8 of 8 years. Police paid 2.12 times its overtime budget over the eight years and the Sheriff 1.83 times; 6 of the 12 departments with at least $1 million a year of overtime budget went over in every year.","The Sheriff's General Fund salaries ran over the adopted budget in 7 of 8 years from fiscal 2019 to 2026: $173.4 million against $156.5 million in fiscal 2024 ($16.9 million over), and $202.7 million against $188.9 million in fiscal 2026. Over the eight years its overtime ran $143.0 million over budget while permanent salaries came in $80.3 million under; for Police the figures are $391.0 million and $270.1 million.","The Human Resources Director posts a notice each time a job class is created, changed, abolished or a position is moved into another class. Three hundred and thirty-nine of the older notices, fiscal 2008-09 to 2015-16, were saved in November 2026 (one of them twice, so 338 are read here); the City’s index lists no actions at all for fiscal 2013-14.","1 class number in the classification actions of fiscal 2009 to 2027 was established, abolished and established again. Class 0385 was first established as Crime Scene Investigations Manager (fiscal 2016), abolished in fiscal 2024, and established again as Chief of Police (Transitional) (fiscal 2025); the payroll names a holder only in fiscal 2025. A count that dates the class from its first establishment calls the second job nine years old when it was created and filled in the same year.","11 notices of fiscal 2009 to 2012 grant permanent status 22 times: 20 grants name employees (48 names in all, a few of them twice), and 2 cover every permanent employee of a hospital laundry class under an arbitration award. 5 moved a single employee into a management class; 5 grants were dated back, the longest by 10.4 years.","The notices of fiscal 2009 to 2011 allocate 25 management positions in 11 departments, each with its working title; the most are in Police (6), Public Health (6), Human Services (5). These are the only notices that name the department behind an action; notices that create a new class do not.","The saved notices establish 147 classes. Of the 79 established from fiscal 2013, when the payroll file begins, 34 have never had a paid holder and 20 were first paid within a year by one or two people. 34 carry an existing title with the tag SFERS added and were set up in December 2011 to February 2013; one notice’s file name reads “PERS to SFERS classes”."],["Of 711 comparable funding requests, 697 matched to the cent and 14 differed. At San Francisco Unified, 311 of 315 matched. The largest difference came from FY2021 request 2199036752.","Of 711 comparable funding requests, 697 matched to the cent and 14 differed. At San Francisco Unified, 311 of 315 matched. The largest difference came from FY2021 request 2199036752.","Find the original federal audit and compare selected findings with later reports documenting repayments, approvals and corrective action.","119 distinct finding references across 64 reports in this selection. Rows link to individual audit reports; the 2021 MEDA filing uses a labeled mirror.","110 distinct finding references across 61 reports in this selection. Rows link to individual audit reports; the 2021 MEDA filing uses a labeled mirror.","394 distinct finding references across 206 reports in this selection. Rows link to individual audit reports; the 2021 MEDA filing uses a labeled mirror.","11 selected San Francisco recipients have paired audit reports. The table identifies the exact amount reviewed, the action taken and where the follow-up appears.","Compare the money Caltrain budgeted and spent with passenger trips and capital project milestones.","FY2025 operating expenses were $225.254 million against $247.628 million budgeted. Passenger fares covered 26.1% of reported expenses, compared with 69.7% in FY2019. FY2021 was over budget, including $22.278 million in contract services and $8.920 million in professional services above budget.","Caltrain estimates 9.1 million trips in FY2025 and 12.6 million in FY2026, up 37.6% using unrounded counts. Weekend service increased from 32 to 66 trains per day. June 2026 ridership reached 81.6% of June 2019.","Two of 15 reported project phases forecast more than their current budget. South Linden and Scott grade separation forecast $10.928 million against $6.281 million. Mini-high platforms forecast $7.671 million against $7.271 million.","6 of 34 milestones with paired dates were completed later than planned as of the June 2026 project report.","21 of 34 milestones with paired dates were forecast later than planned as of the June 2026 project report.","The Phase One review attributed $516 million in cumulative operating cost savings to BART and more than $76 million to Caltrain from July 2019 through June 2025. That was about 9% and 7% of their respective operating costs on the report’s basis.","Compare budgets with actual spending, trace changes in service and ridership, and read the inspector general’s findings on individual investigations and contractor recoveries.","BART’s FY2025 total operating uses finished $20.2 million above its final budget. FY2026 uses finished about $10.8 million below budget. Capital spending was $1.063 billion in FY2025 and $806 million in FY2026.","Heavy rail carried 125.1 million unlinked trips in FY2019, 17.1 million in FY2021 and 56.6 million in FY2025. FY2025 railcar revenue hours were 2.17 million versus 2.23 million in FY2019. Reported operating expenses increased from $651.0 million to $885.6 million over those six years.","BART recovered $11,900 on power washing and pursued $16,713 for sweeping. The FY2025 annual report gives $28,613 in settlements with two contractors for unperformed services. BART later confirmed a separate $57,655 recovery for medical crews.","The archive includes unsupported billing, contract splitting, gaps in inventory records, and allegations the inspector general cleared. Each row links the issuer’s report and identifies its conclusion.","The catalogue lists 108 source URLs. Reviewers opened 103 PDFs. Two pairs contain identical files, leaving 101 distinct PDFs. Five links to general auditing standards are identified separately.","2 firms on the City’s suspended-and-debarred list appear in 9 board items of other bodies worth $96.1 million as read; 2 items ($4.3 million) are dated after the City acted. The largest is ProVen Management, Inc.: 8 BART items, $75.6 million, of which 2 ($4.3 million) are change orders and settlements dated after the City suspended it.","27 organisations that carry an official finding, a charged or proven case, or (for nonprofits) three or more warning signs in the City data also appear in 51 board items of other bodies, $63.6 million as read; the largest with an official finding at the City is Sunset Scavenger Company, with 2 items at City College worth $271,000.","The school board’s agendas carry 84 items that call a contract “retroactive” (work begun before approval), besides 123 routine batch ratifications; BART’s board was asked to approve 54 sole- or single-source purchases worth $1.60 billion as read; the bridge district ratified 19 emergency actions by its general manager worth $12.9 million.","4,508 board items of seven bodies were read, 2,498 with a dollar figure; the largest single authorisation naming a firm is $863.9 million to Halmar International, LLC (Golden Gate Bridge district).","The Housing Authority's audits reported findings in every one of 7 years (22 in all, 19 marked as repeats). The school district had 3 findings for 2022, the year its auditor gave no opinion on its financial statements. The City's own audit reported 2 findings across 7 years, while the federal money it covers rose from $447 million (2019) to $1.27 billion (2025).","The school district and City College are separate governments that together spend well over a billion dollars a year of public money, none of it in the City's ledger. This file sets the district's money, staff and results against its enrolment and against ten like districts, and gathers what State and local overseers have already found.","Day-to-day spending by the school district and its county office rose from $777 million in fiscal 2015 to $1.24 billion in fiscal 2024 (+60%) while pupils in all San Francisco public schools went from 59,105 to 56,508 (-4%). Per pupil that is $13,154 to $21,985 (+67%); the same measure for all California school systems rose 80%. Counting only the district's own pupils, fiscal 2024 spending was $25,249 each, and all revenue was $1.53 billion. The city's children aged 5 to 17 numbered 74,976 in 2014 and 76,044 in 2023; public-school pupils were 79% and then 74% of that number.","Instruction took 65% of the $465 million growth in day-to-day spending between fiscal 2015 and fiscal 2024 ($404 million to $707 million). The fastest-growing line was business, central and other support services, +146% ($25.5 million to $62.9 million). Support for teaching staff fell $35.9 million; Construction and other capital spending fell $111 million. Long-term debt outstanding was $781 million at the end of fiscal 2015 and $933 million at the end of fiscal 2024.","In fiscal 2024 San Francisco spent $25,249 per pupil day to day, rank 3 of 11 (the middle of the ten peers: $23,161). Furthest above the peer middle: general administration $400 against $244 (+64%); pupil transport $763 against $468 (+63%); pupil support services $2,573 against $1,656 (+55%); other support services $231 against $150 (+54%). Furthest below: running and upkeep of buildings $1,639 against $2,248 (-27%). Its money comes from a different place: $17,492 per pupil from local sources against $7,978 at the middle peer, and rank 9 of 11 for State money.","In fiscal 2024 instruction took 56.9% of San Francisco's day-to-day spending against 59.1% at the middle peer (rank 9 of 11, 1 = highest share), and general plus school administration 7.0% against 6.8% (rank 4). It had 20.6 pupils per teacher against 20.8 and 222 pupils per administrator against 260. Salaries and benefits came to $150,100 per full-time-equivalent employee against $154,970 (rank 8).","Between 2014-15 and 2024-25 pupils in San Francisco public schools fell 4% while all staff went from 6,387 to 6,693 full-time-equivalents (+5%), or 108 to 118 per 1,000 pupils. Grew: all other staff +24%; classroom aides +3%. Shrank: school administrators -36%; office support staff -11%; teachers -3%; district administrators unchanged. The State's own file, 2019-20 to 2024-25, shows the same direction: pupils -9%; teachers -14%; administrators -35%; pupil-services staff -46%; other staff +66%.","The share of San Francisco pupils meeting the State standard in English was 56.5% in 2018-19 and 53.5% in 2025-26 (State: 51.1% to 49.3%); in mathematics 50.5% and 45.8% (State: 39.7% to 38.4%). Its rank among the eleven districts went from 2 to 3 in English and 1 to 2 in mathematics. Chronic absenteeism was 18.8% in 2018-19 and 28.8% in 2024-25 (State 19.4%; rank 8 of 11, 1 = least absence). Four-year graduation at regular schools was 87.3% against 92.1% statewide.","In 2023-24 San Francisco was number 3 of 11 for day-to-day spending per pupil ($25,249) and number 2 for English results (53.7% meeting the standard). Across the eleven districts the higher spenders tended to have the lower English results (rank correlation -0.51): the highest spender, Oakland Unified, had 33.1% meeting the standard and the lowest, Elk Grove Unified, 51.7%. That is the pattern the State's funding formula produces by itself, because it sends more money to districts with more high-need pupils.","The two central-office categories together cost $866 per pupil in fiscal 2019 (peer middle $644; rank 2 of 11) and $1,679 in fiscal 2024 (peer middle $1,128; rank 2 of 11), $82.6 million in all. They peaked at $1,937 per pupil in 2022-23, which was also the year of the payroll-system repair; the survey does not say what the money bought. Business and central services alone: $584 to $1,278 per pupil (peer middle $488 to $864).","6,649 agenda items since January 2018 concern contracts, but only 266 carry a dollar figure that could be read, and most of those are the approval threshold or a bond ceiling, not a contract price: the contract lists sit on item pages that could not be downloaded, so the largest contracts by vendor cannot be ranked from these records. 336 items call a contract \"retroactive\" (work begun before approval): 168 in 2021-22, 5 in 2025-26 and 27 in the first four months of 2026-27. Who brought them: individual schools 119; other central offices 98; buildings and construction 48; special education services 45; unit not stated in the title 26. That is 252 more than the 84 counted on the other-public-bodies screen, which read a shorter extract. 22 organizations that carry findings or warning signs in the City data also appear on school or City College agendas; they are listed on the other-public-bodies screens.","The district switched on a new payroll system on 3 January 2022, declared a payroll state of emergency ten months later, and in February 2024 announced it would replace the system; the replacement started in July 2025. The repair was bought through a consulting contract first estimated at $2.8 million and then enlarged by up to $8,837,750. The Civil Grand Jury cited a $14 million figure from a newspaper headline; the implementation contract papers, saved in November 2026, give the contract's own amounts (screen schools_records.payroll-contract-prices). The State fiscal-crisis team wrote in 2024 that the errors' cost was something \"the district is unable to estimate\". The board was asked to act 17 times on contracts and resolutions for the two systems; 1 of those, extending the old system's supplier agreement, was on agendas after the new system started (2026-10-13).","44 findings from 7 overseers were read: 20 on the school district, 22 on City College and 2 on the county office. The State fiscal-crisis team rated the district at high risk of insolvency three times running (scores 38.4% in 2022, 50.7% in 2024, 42.9% in 2026). For the year to June 2022 the district's auditor gave no opinion on its accounts, with $184 million of federal money under audit. City College's auditors doubted in 2020 and 2021 that it could keep operating; its accreditor monitored its finances for three years, warned it in January 2024 and cleared it in June 2025.","The State team answered \"no\" (a failing) to 54 questions in 2022, 64 in 2024 and 60 in 2026. 16 questions were answered \"no\" in all three reviews, among them: audit findings left uncorrected; staffing records not used to build the budget; actual revenue and spending out of line with the budget; deficiencies named in State oversight letters not addressed; no board-approved plan to control transfers from the general fund; falling enrolment; school buildings not fully used. 10 of the 20 sections had more failings in 2026 than in 2022; between 2024 and 2026 the count rose in budget development and adoption, charter schools, deficit spending (unrestricted general fund), general fund - current year, information systems and data management, leadership and stability, special education.","The State directory lists 23 charter schools ever opened in San Francisco, 13 of them active. They enrolled 6,207 pupils in 2015-16 and 7,226 in 2025-26: 10.4% and 12.9% of all public-school pupils. 3,487 of the 2025-26 total are in the Five Keys schools, which the district authorised and which teach adults and people in custody; without them the share is 6.5% and 7.1%. The district authorised 19 of the 23; schools authorised by the State Board of Education enrolled 461 pupils in 2025-26.","The Controller reports that in fiscal 2025 the City's education enrichment fund contributed $96.3 million to the school district, a second voter-approved fund $35.0 million and a parcel tax for educators' pay $54.0 million: about $3,268 per pupil. The City's open vendor ledger for the same year shows $15.0 million paid to the district under its name, and the district reported $27.0 million from cities and counties to the federal survey for fiscal 2024: the main transfers cannot be followed in either. City departments paid City College $27.4 million in fiscal 2020 and $12.2 million in fiscal 2025, mostly from the children's department, whose Free City College programme the Controller audits; a further $326 million shown against the college's name in fiscal 2025 is coded to a department and fund named for the college district itself, and appears to be the college's own money passing through the City treasury.","City College reported 21,930 full-time-equivalent students in 2015-16, 22,309 at the peak in 2017-18 and 13,508 in 2022-23, while its unrestricted general fund spent $209 million and then $179 million: $9,531 and $13,255 per student. Its year-end balance fell to $8.7 million (5.3% of a year's spending) in 2019-20, the period in which its auditors doubted it could keep operating, and stood at $19.1 million in 2022-23. The federal employment survey counts 1,209 full-time and 1,678 part-time employees in 2017 and 1,039 and 950 in 2025. The college's own reports disagree about 2 of these years' revenue, by as much as $39.2 million.","The federal housing department’s yearly file shows the Authority funded for 8,847 vouchers in 2015 and 16,111 in 2025. The share in use fell to 82% in 2022, leaving about 2,843 funded vouchers unused, and was 96% in 2025. Federal spending per voucher rose from $1,470 a month to $2,735 (86% more). The Authority’s own monthly reports show $440.8 million of housing assistance payments in 2025 against $331.4 million in 2022, and running costs of $23.9 million (5.4% of the payments). Its unrestricted reserve for the program was below zero in 48 of the 48 months reported, moving from minus $28.3 million in 2022-01 to minus $284,147 in 2025-12.","The Authority’s audits reported findings on its federal programs in 7 of the 7 years from 2019 to 2025: 22 findings, 19 of them marked as repeats of an earlier year, while the federal money it spent rose from $330.0 million to $525.6 million. Findings fell from 5 in 2019 to 2 in 2025. Federal inspectors scored its public-housing developments 38 times in the files on disk; 15 of those scores were below 60 out of 100, and the number of developments inspected fell from 23 in the busiest year to 1 in 2025 as buildings left the public-housing program.","The June 2024 budget forecast the general fund would end 2024-25 at $187.1 million after a $148.5 million drawdown; a year later the estimate was $295.9 million, and the June 2026 forms put the unaudited balance at $428.9 million, $241.9 million more than budgeted. For 2023-24 the estimate of $335.6 million became $473.9 million unaudited ($138.3 million more).","The June 2026 forms show the district’s general fund spending $1.31 billion in 2025-26 (estimated) and budgeting $1.36 billion for 2026-27. The unrestricted fund fell by $133.4 million in 2025-26, of which $111.5 million was a transfer out to another fund; the 2026-27 budget holds a reserve for economic uncertainties of $27.2 million.","2024-25 adopted budget (June 2024): 15 items flagged (1 Average Daily Attendance, 3 ADA to Enrollment, 4 Local Control Funding Formula (LCFF), 6b Other Expenditures, 7 Ongoing and Major Maintenance Account, S5 Contributions); 2025-26 adopted budget (June 2025): 10 items flagged (3 ADA to Enrollment, 4 Local Control Funding Formula (LCFF), 5 Salaries and Benefits, 6b Other Expenditures, S5 Contributions, S7b Other Self-insurance Benefits); 2026-27 recommended budget (June 2026): 11 items flagged (5 Salaries and Benefits, 6b Other Expenditures, S4 Contingent Revenues, S5 Contributions, S6 Long-term Commitments, S7b Other Self-insurance Benefits); 2026-27 County Office of Education recommended budget (June 2026): 10 items flagged (1b ADA - County Programs, 2 Local Control Funding Formula (LCFF), 3 Salaries and Benefits, 4b Other Expenditures, 5 Ongoing and Major Maintenance, S7b Other Self-insurance Benefits).","Of the $133.4 million fall in the district’s unrestricted general fund in 2025-26, $111.5 million was a transfer into the special reserve fund (Fund 17), which carries that sum as a reserve for economic uncertainties in 2026-27 and $0 in the two projection years. Counted the way the budget narrative describes the cap (unassigned general-fund balance plus Fund 17), reserves come to $163.1 million against 10% of spending, $136.2 million; the narrative says the district expects to meet the cap. It also projects an unrestricted shortfall of $26 million in 2028-29.","All 12 audits for fiscal 2014 through 2025 gave unmodified opinions on the financial statements and on compliance for the major federal Highway Planning and Construction program. Each findings schedule reported no financial statement findings or federal award findings and questioned costs.","The school district's own contract papers, interim reports and State lists, and City College's audits and purchase orders, read line by line. They show what the payroll contracts cost, how far forecasts missed, which contracts were approved after work began, and which City College contracts grew most.","The implementation contract with Infosys, approved in October 2019 at $9.5 million, was raised three times in twelve months to $16,482,810 by June 2022 (+74%), each time to push go-live back or to stabilise the system after it went live. After the November 2022 emergency an audit team found about $4.3 million of overpayments in over 1,200 pay claims. A separate staffing agreement with Protiviti carried a $1.5 million cap for 2023-24 while its rate table priced 40 people full-time at $8.0 million; the 2024-25 agreement on disk leaves its price clause as template text, with $5 million on the budget-code line.","Item 14 of 9 June 2026 approved 41 contracts over $119,100 worth $25,825,002 in a single consent vote; the item's own total is $26,221,802, $396,800 more than its attachments add up to (exactly the amount of one attachment, Real Options For City Kids SFSD-Vis. Valley MS, which may be counted twice). 30 of them, $20,002,118, are school-site programmes run through the Student and Family Services Division; Bay Area Community Resources alone has 15 worth $11,028,600. 3 are with individual contractors, $1,081,000, two of them in the Superintendent's Office at $425,000 and $450,000.","11 of 13 sample contracts reached the board after their start date, by a median of 105 days. The longest: Pacific Northwest Enterprise (Buildings & Grounds), $109,000, due to start 2023-07-01 and on the agenda 2025-10-14, 836 days later; then Warman Security, 378 days.","4 sample contracts are priced within 10% under the bid limit their own text states, the closest at 99.7% of it (Pacific Northwest Enterprise, $109,000 against $109,300). Two Buildings and Grounds contracts cover the same door-access and intercom upkeep: Pacific Northwest Enterprise, $109,000 for 2023-2026, and Warman Security, $114,000 for 2024-2027; together $223,000, 195% of the limit.","2 of the 50 board attachments read leave the price clause as \"INSERT DOLLAR AMOUNT IN WORDS\": 24-25-FINAL--ISA - ROBERT HALF PROTIVITI GOVERNMENT SERVICES FY 2024-2025 v.1 (2024-06-25); 00006 After School Enrichment Program (McKinley) $57,000.01 (2026-06-09).","In March 2025 the district certified it might not meet its obligations (a negative certification) on a forecast that the general fund would end 2024-25 at $269.4 million; the unaudited books show $428.9 million, $159.5 million more (unrestricted part: $132.5 million forecast, $221.2 million actual). For 2025-26 the first interim forecast $215.1 million and the books show $301.8 million (+$86.7 million), although the adopted budget had put it higher, at $329.7 million. The unrestricted balance did fall, from $221.2 million to $83.5 million, mostly through a $111.5 million transfer the interim planned into the special reserve fund.","Of 22 interim reports from 2015-16 to 2025-26, the State listed the district 3 times with a negative certification (may not meet its obligations this year or next) and 5 times with a qualified one (may not meet them in the following three years). The negative run: 2023-24 second, 2024-25 first, 2024-25 second. In the two years of the payroll breakdown and the audit without an opinion (2021-22 and 2022-23) the district was not listed: it certified itself positive in all 4 reports.","The State's current expense of education per pupil in average daily attendance was $12,148 in 2016-17 and $25,643 in 2023-24 (+111%), while attendance fell from 50,753 to 45,287 (-11%). Among unified districts with 20,000 or more in attendance it ranked 10 of 56 in 2016-17 and 3 of 43 in 2023-24 (middle that year: $18,747). The district reported 55.74% of that cost going to classroom pay in 2024-25 and 55.51% in 2025-26, against the Education Code minimum (section 41372) of 55% for unified districts.","Pupils in district-run schools went from 52,565 in 2014-15 to 48,722 in 2022-23 (-7.3%). Of 99 schools open in both years with at least 100 pupils at the start, 14 lost a quarter or more; the steepest fall was Carver (George Washington) Elementary, 239 to 101. The State fiscal-crisis team has three times listed school buildings not fully used among the district's failings.","In 2020–21, ED Data Express reported $12,479,657. California’s revised final allocation was $12,648,592. Its payment schedule shows $13,254,641 had been paid and the state recovered $606,049 by offset. California subsequently reported full payment of the $13,867,852 final 2022–23 allocation and the $16,867,733 final 2023–24 allocation.","36 findings in 7 audits. 12 findings were made in two or more of the years read: Financial Condition of the District (2018, 2019, 2020, 2021); Load Banking (2018, 2019, 2020, 2021); Student Success and Support Program (2016, 2017, 2018); Year End Closing (2014, 2015); Capital Assets (2014, 2015); Preparation of Schedule of Federal Awards (2014, 2015). Load banking lets faculty teach extra now for paid time off later; the auditors found faculty who had been paid full salary while teaching less than a full load, without the banked hours to cover it, and the deficit owed back by faculty stood at $707,924 at June 2021. Questioned costs named in these years total $501,648.","62 contractors on the papers (school-district contracts of $50,000 or more, or City College orders of $100,000 or more) carry the same name as City payees paid $100,000 or more; 7 of them have an organization profile on this site. Largest City payees among them: Medline Industries, Inc. (City $262.5 million); Alliant Insurance Services, Inc. (City $218.2 million); McCarthy Building Companies Inc. (City $217.6 million); YMCA of San Francisco (City $209.5 million).","Yes for the school district: other pay was 25.5% of regular pay in 2015, rank 1 of 16 California districts with 5,000 or more paid positions (middle 5.4%), and 27.0% in 2018, rank 1 of 14. BART’s overtime as a share of regular pay by year: 2015 16.8%, 2016 17.3%, 2017 16.8%, 2018 19.4%, 2019 19.4%, 2020 12.4%, 2021 14.8%, 2022 20.5%, 2023 17.3%, 2024 16.2%, 2025 17.6%.","The State Controller files on disk are pay files, not financial statements: they list positions and pay, with no revenue or spending. 9 San Francisco public bodies in them were paid $1.70 billion by the City in fiscal 2019 to 2026; 6 have a gap. The San Francisco Community Health Authority (the State file gives its website as sfhp.org; the City ledger pays “San Francisco Health Plan”, $219.3 million in these years) lists 10 governing-board positions and no pay in 2025; in no year from 2019 to 2025 does the file hold a staff position for it. Transbay Joint Powers Authority: pay total under a fifth of its usual level in 2022 (likely a reporting error); San Francisco Unified: no report for 2020, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2025; San Francisco Community College District: no report for 2023, 2024, 2025; Metropolitan Transportation Commission: no report for 2019; Golden Gate Bridge Highway and Transportation District: no report for 2020.","On the records saved, very little: 11,327 contributions since 2018 to 98 school-board and college-board candidate committees were checked against the 96 vendor names read from the school-district and City College papers on disk, and 8 contributions matched ($950 in all: Bay Area Community Resources, 2; Mission Graduates, 2; Kitchell Corporation, 1; Seneca Family of Agencies, 1; YMCA of San Francisco, 1). The test is weak because the vendor list is short, not because the answer is known.","The school board’s own pages for seven vendors’ contracts, 2018-2026, set the meeting date beside the dates the papers give for the work, the processing and the signing. The same vendors’ campaign money and gifts are set against those meetings, with the count the calendar alone gives.","116 item pages were saved; 115 ask the board to act (107 on the consent calendar, 8 as separate items). None of the 115 pages records a vote or an outcome; the official minutes of 27 of the meetings were saved on 22 November 2026 and give the outcome of 53 of these items (screen board-votes-from-minutes). 75 pages give a date for the work or for processing, and in 74 of them that date comes before the meeting, by a median of 45 days. 67 are list items titled as contracts “processed between” two dates; the window closes a median of 18 days before the meeting (at most 60), and 33 of these lists are titled as ratifications.","10 of the 115 action items are titled retroactive; 6 state when services began, a median of 92 days and at most 223 days before the meeting. One later item corrects an earlier one: the retroactive Protiviti agreement approved on 2023-09-12 had stated a start of 1 May 2023; the amendment says the work ran from 2023-02-01.","274 contract and amendment papers were read; 104 state a start date that could be read (102 as a date in the term clause, 2 as a fiscal-year term). Of the 67 first contracts among them, 35 begin before the meeting, by a median of 67 days and at most 354; the other 32 begin after it. 34 of the 37 amendments restate a start before their meeting, which is the original contract’s term, not new work begun early. 73 of the 274 say they take effect only on board approval. For 170 papers no start date could be read (scans, or forms without a term clause).","The posted copies show little of signing. Of 274 contract and amendment papers, 24 carry a typed or stamped signature date (24 before the meeting); other signatures are handwritten or blank and were not dated here. 63 of the 65 that are “dated for convenience” carry a date before the meeting, which is the drafting date, not a signature. Where the signature block has the line “approved/ratified by the Board of Education on”, it is blank in 37 of 39 papers read; 2 have a board date written in, and in 1 of those it is not the meeting the paper is posted for.","The official minutes of 27 meetings were saved (29 files); 53 of the 115 action items fall on those meetings. 49 were adopted that night: 3 on a recorded vote of their own, the rest with the consent calendar, none with a nay recorded. No item among them was pulled, continued or voted down, so the vote date is the meeting date. For 4 (2024-06-25) the minutes list the item on the consent calendar but record no adoption of it before adjournment. Set against the vote date, 18 of the 19 adopted items that give a date for the work or the processing put it before the vote (median 45 days, at most 223); 25 of the 54 first contracts posted with them state a start before the vote (median 41 days); 12 of 12 papers with a typed signature date carry one before the vote, and the board-date line is blank in 9 of the 10 signature blocks read.","Yes, on two keys. 40 of the 127 school-district papers read for it print the contractor’s address as 171 Carlos Drive, San Rafael, CA 94903; the City’s own contract approval notices for Bay Area Community Resources (17 filings, 2018-2025, Board files 184681-22, 200463, 210768, 220458, 230413, 241101) give the same address. Name and address both match. The City payment file itself carries a payee name only, so the tie to the City’s payments runs through the City’s contracts with that name.","No. Its 14,533 rows (2010 to 2021) are parking and traffic orders: parking 9,339, traffic 2,512, transit 1,172, pedestrian 860, bicycle 299. 0 rows mention a contract, vendor, procurement or dollar sign."],["Filing, issuance, completion and open age for an individual permit.","Fast approvals, one inspector per contractor, complaints that go nowhere.","232 consecutive monthly reports cover 2007-07 through 2026-10. 150 PDFs were recovered from archived copies of the state website. The extracted row count agrees with the printed footer in 231 reports.","232 consecutive monthly reports cover 2007-07 through 2026-10. 150 PDFs were recovered from archived copies of the state website. The extracted row count agrees with the printed footer in 231 reports.","10 permits carried an exact contractor licence number during this window. 6 had an owner-builder or a different contractor listed by issuance. 3 building permits still listed the number in a signed revocation order. 1 requires the state history between two decisions.","295 distinct permits and license number pairs met the date screen across both register series. This reproduces the original screen. The separate issuance-window table covers events that arose during review.","245 pairs had a documented license reissue between the prior revocation and local filing. This before-filing group contributed 4 trade permits at three properties. The other 46 need dated status or renewal records.","32,908 distinct permit, type and state-number combinations share either identifying text or number evidence across all date relationships. 160 include an exact number in at least one additional City contact role. The rows can overlap.","Follow each building application from filing to issuance or its last recorded status, and compare department review visits with the targets in effect.","398,278 applications filed in 2012–2024 had at least 365 days of follow-up. In the 2024 cohort, 73% of additions and alterations, 38% of new construction, 87% of other permit types, 95% of over-the-counter alterations received issuance within one year.","On November 24, 10,126 applications filed since 2012 still had an active pre-issuance status, a valid filing date and no issuance or completion date. 7,623 were at least one year old and 5,639 at least three years old.","The City recorded 11,686 eligible first station reviews. 7,630 finished by their due dates, 3,201 finished later and 324 still lacked a finish date after the target passed. Another 531 had inconsistent dates.","Across 1,996,708 recorded station visits on permits filed since 2012, 169,036 permit–addendum–station groups had a second or later visit.","Project records and related Planning records have separate identifiers and workflows. The table counts observed closures, selected active statuses and records with a final-looking status but no closure date.","The November 17 extract contains 67,798 published stage observations. It flags 336 for potential data error. Rows without an outcome are shown separately.","Of 439,809 unique applications filed since 2012, 405,851 had a usable approval interval; 404,685 also had an issuance date on or after approval.","San Francisco's own permit records show how one job turns into many permits across several departments, how often a permit must be bought again just to close the first one, where plans sit, and who moves faster. This page shows where the process stacks, loops, stalls and treats like cases differently, and where official reviewers have already said so.","Since 2015, 8,860 building permits (2.6% of all filed) were taken out only to get a final inspection on, renew or reinstate an earlier permit; the earlier permit was a median 4.4 years old, and 44% were five or more years old. 541 lots needed three or more such permits. Of full-review permits issued for filings from 2012 to 2019, 6% were later named by a finishing or renewal permit and 23% still show no final sign-off.","Building Inspection issued a first notice of violation on 47,664 complaints since 2015. Clearing one took a median 77 days, and 8% of notices from 2015 to 2023 are still open. 561 notices cite an expired or cancelled permit and 6,103 cite work without a permit. 10,067 building permits were filed to answer a notice or complaint; 103 of those are also permits to finish or renew an earlier permit. 4,587 lots drew notices on three or more separate complaints.","The records show 77,490 instances since 2015 of a permit that points back at, or waits on, another permit or approval (one permit can appear in more than one row): 12,686 Public Works street permits quote a building permit application, 3,855 building projects were approved as a site permit followed by 7,403 separate sets of construction drawings, each routed for approval again, 8,636 building permits carry a reviewer's note that Public Works must sign off or issue its own permit before the job can be closed, and 8,860 building permits exist only to finish or renew an earlier one.","A finished home remodel costing $100,000 to $1 million carried a median 7 separate permits and records from 4 departments, and took a median 565 days from first filing to final sign-off, building time included; one in ten carried 17 or more. A new building with five or more homes carried a median 45 permits and records across 6 departments and took 6.2 years.","A full-review building permit filed since 2015 passed through a median 5 technical review stations in 4 departments, and 65% were put on hold at least once (median 106 days on hold). Of the days plans sat at a station, 35% passed before a reviewer started. The three stations with the most plan-days were Planning Department zoning check, Building plan check and Permit processing center (routes the plans). On jobs of $1 million to $20 million, plans reached building plan check a median 52 days after filing, the Fire Department after 82, and the water and street desks only after 159 and 162: the stations are mostly visited one after another.","Full-review alteration permits issued in 2019 took a median 292 days from filing; in 2023, 328; in 2025, 173. New buildings issued in 2025 took a median 711 days. 2,261 full-review applications filed 2015 to 2023 (11.1%) were withdrawn, cancelled or expired after a year or more without ever being issued, and 2,250 filed by 2023 are still waiting.","12,196 building permits filed 2015 to 2024 list a permit consultant or expediter (4.3% of all; 30% of full-review jobs of $5 million and over, 9.7% of those under $50,000). Set against like permits of the same kind, cost band and year, the answer is mixed: on jobs sent for full plan review the consultant's permit came out ahead (229 days against 289), but across all permits it was faster only 44% of the time (alterations approved at the counter: median 14 days against 15 for like permits without one; alterations sent for full plan review: median 229 days against 289 for like permits without one; new buildings: median 303 days against 306 for like permits without one). In their own filings, 46 consultants and firms reported $6.7 million in client fees.","Across 462 quarterly reports that could be read, 46 permit consultants and firms reported $6.7 million in fees from clients for permit consulting.","164 companies and organizations are named as paying clients on the consultants' filings; 9 of them are nonprofits the City itself funds, which between them were reported as paying $1,002,057 in permit-consulting fees. A further 307 clients are private owners, counted here but not named.","A plain count finds 28 pairs where one City plan reviewer handled 35% or more of a permit consultant's permits at a technical review station. Holding the year and the kind of permit equal, and setting aside single projects filed in one batch, leaves 1: Lau (Nelson) Chi Chiu handled 31 of the 56 permits listing Bin Lei at building plan check, where about 3 would be expected. On the published records, repeat pairings between consultants and plan reviewers are almost wholly explained by timing and specialty.","Five departments collected $1.75 billion in permit, plan-check, inspection and licence fees from FY2015 to FY2026 under 72 separately named fees. Building Inspection took in $80.6 million on 71,577 permits in FY2019 ($1,126 a permit) and $95.6 million on 53,662 in FY2026 ($1,781 a permit). Fees to extend an application or permit rose from $218,824 to $890,174 and penalties for work without a permit from $584,745 to $2,488,060; premium plan review, a paid faster service, brought in $5,287,975 at its peak and $414,741 in FY2026. Homes finished went from 3,047 to 1,355.","The Controller's ledger shows 76 separately named permit, review, inspection, licence and penalty fees that each brought in $250,000 or more since FY2015 across five departments.","Since 2015 Public Works issued 7,501 separate sidewalk table-and-chair permits for 2,700 addresses, an average of 2.7 permits per address and as many as 16 for one. A business sign needs a building permit, which took a median 15 days and passed 4 review stations. 205 of 693 venues with an entertainment permit hold three or more different kinds of City approval at the same address. 140 of the 500 food-truck locations on the current list are still only 'requested'.","57 venues with an active entertainment permit hold four or more different kinds of City approval at the same address in these files alone.","Of 8,791 review steps the Planning Department has scored against a deadline, 2,868 (33%) ran over. First plan review missed its 30-day deadline 60% of the time in 2023 and 12% in 2026. From an accepted application to approval took a median 160 days for applications accepted since 2021.","Since 2015 members of the public filed 887 requests for discretionary review, a hearing on a project that otherwise meets the code. Each took a median 135 days to close; the Planning Commission changed the project in 27%, left it unchanged in 23%, and 33% were withdrawn. Projects that drew one took a median 525 days at Planning against 352 for other projects needing a hearing or review. The City's published records name the person who asked on 4 of the 887, so whether a few people file many cannot be checked.","For full-review alterations to one- and two-family houses costing $50,000 to $1 million, issued 2019 to 2026, the citywide median wait was 408 days; it was 530 in Oceanview/Merced/Ingleside and 273 in Presidio Heights. Among counter permits with plans worth $25,000 or more, 26% were issued within a day when the applicant was a licensed contractor and 6% when a permit consultant.","The City's own published rules, fee tables and ordinances, read for what a person must do, in what order, and at what printed price to get a permit. The rules stack many permits one behind another and send applicants to licensed professionals, insurers and other departments along the way, but no published rule was found that makes two permits each wait on the other.","Of 1,449 sentences pulled from City permit pages, 98 state a real dependency, giving 108 distinct pairs: 60 say one thing must come before the other, and 48 say both are needed without giving an order. The building permit has the most: 21 stated prerequisites, 16 of them required before it is applied for or issued.","52 permits or approvals have at least one stated prerequisite. Building permit: 21 (16 before); State-streamlined housing application (ministerial approval): 4 (4 before); Health permit (Public Health): 4 (2 before); Food truck permit (Public Works): 4 (4 before). The things most often required by other permits are business registration (6 permits), building permit (5 permits), planning approval (4 permits).","The longest string of stated \"before\" requirements runs 4 steps: Fire alarm permit  needs  Electrical permit  needs  Building permit  needs  Project Application filed with Planning  needs  Pre-application meeting with neighbors. Its links apply only to: solar systems; projects needing intake review, neighbor notice or an entitlement; certain kinds of project. 5 chains reach that length, and 96 start-to-end chains run two steps or more.","No. Among the 60 \"before\" pairs no permit is stated to need, directly or through a chain, a permit that needs it first. 6 pairs of pages that point at each other were read side by side; in every one the pages state which comes first. The published pages describe many permits stacked in sequence, not a circle.","57 published requirements were found: 18 to use a licensed architect, engineer or contractor, 19 to hold insurance or post a bond, 4 for a meeting before applying, 8 for a notice to neighbors, and 7 for a separate street or sidewalk permit. 36 of them send money to a private professional, insurer or surety, whose prices the City does not set or print. An applicant may run the required community outreach meeting. Optional City facilitation is listed at $1,407. The current neighborhood notification schedule lists Building Permit Neighborhood Notification $123 + $4.36 per envelope. The other tables list Pre-application Plan Review Fee: $602 per hour - Minimum Four Hours Per Project and Street Space $209.00 per 20 LF/ month. One page states the opposite: the Entertainment Commission requires no insurance for its own event permits.","Of 11 open points, the published rules confirm 7, contradict 0 and are silent on 4. Confirmed: Planning approval comes before the building permit application; a permit lasts 365 days and an application 730; work without a permit costs nine times the issuance fee; premium plan review is sold at half the plan review fee plus $1,000; public discretionary review is listed at $807 for FY2026–27, subject to the neighborhood organization waiver. Not found: any true catch-22, who may buy premium review, how reviewers are assigned, and what permit consultants must report.","The Building Inspection table effective July 12, 2026 sets the building permit fee as a plan review fee plus a permit issuance fee, each rising with job value across 10 bands: $181 for the first $500 (plan review) and $206 for the first $500 (permit issuance) at the bottom. Extensions, penalties, premium review, Planning's discretionary review and notification fees, and Public Works street charges are quoted line by line below. No total for a typical job is given, because the tables do not print every component (trade permits, other departments' reviews, school and impact fees and surcharges are charged on top).","Building Inspection's hourly inspection rate was $158.10 before the 2023 fee ordinance, which wrote in $181.82; the 2024 ordinance struck that out and wrote in $461 (+154%); the table effective July 12, 2026 prints $519, 3.3 times the figure of three years earlier. The hourly plan review rate went from $173.91 to $526 over the same ordinances. 59 Planning fee lines appear in the FY2025–26 schedule and the FY2026–27 preview, with a median change of +2.4%. The published calculator confirms 57 later amounts. The solar and returned-payment figures appear in the preview only. 54 development impact fee lines that could be followed through five or more yearly registers rose by a middle figure of +24% from first to last. Most other fees are published for the current year only.","953 ordinances touching permits, fees or the codes that govern them were enacted from 2018 to 2026, about 106 a year. By the wording of their titles, 219 add a requirement, restriction or fee and 205 streamline, waive or exempt (59 do both). The balance has shifted: through 2021, 24% of ordinances added and 17% removed; since 2023, 21% added and 28% removed. 64 raise or create a fee and 78 waive, cut or defer one. 87 are landmark designations, which add a review for one building, and 128 are budget, bond, lawsuit or agreement items left unsorted.","The Planning Code is amended most often: 337 ordinances in 9 years (56 adding a requirement or fee, 95 removing, waiving or exempting), followed by the Administrative Code (120) and the Police Code (65). The Building Code was amended by 54 and the Public Works Code by 55.","28 sponsors are recorded. Rafael Mandelman is named on 318 ordinances, Aaron Peskin on 184 and Ahsha Safai on 160; the Mayor is recorded on 98. 200 ordinances carry no sponsor in the index.","Across report years 2018 to 2025, San Francisco's median was 377 days from application to entitlement (rank 2 of 9, 1 being slowest), 254 days from entitlement to building permit (rank 3 of 9) and 561 days from permit to completion (rank 2 of 9). The slowest city on each measure was San Jose (433 days), San Jose (579) and Oakland (591). Oakland, Fresno reported the same date for application and entitlement on most projects, a difference in reporting rather than in speed. San Francisco reported 29,819 homes completed, 4.43 per 1,000 residents a year (rank 3 of 8 with a population figure, 1 being most).","San Francisco was the slowest of the cities with enough linked projects in 5 of 7 years for application to entitlement, 5 of 7 for entitlement to permit and 1 of 8 for permit to completion. In 2025 its medians were 304 days, 266 days and 645 days. The city reported 4,034 homes completed in 2018 and 2,844 in 2025.","17 findings are drawn from 6 official documents. The State housing department's 2023 review (9 findings here) said San Francisco has the longest housing timelines in California, an average of 523 days to entitle a project and 605 more to issue a building permit on 2022 data, and ordered the City to remove discretion from code-compliant housing approvals and to standardize permitting after entitlement. The City's own annual reports record review targets of 21, 30 and 14 days at Planning and 63% of counter permits handled within two business days at Building Inspection.","9 published targets or limits could be set beside a measured figure. Planning's first plan review has a 30-day target and took a median 42 days in 2023 (60% over the deadline) and 28 in 2026 (12% over). A permit is now written to last 365 days, while permits taken out only to finish an earlier one found that earlier permit a median 4.4 years old. The table “permit types” prints a time for 145 permit pages, but almost all are advice on when to apply; only the State housing deadline matches a measured step.","7 people or firms are on the list as published on the page dated Monday, October 6, 2025, placed between 2021-11-18 and 2025-04-03. In the City's words the program, created in 2021, covers \"A significant violation includes serious permit misuse, a conviction for fraud, misrepresentation, bribery\" and failure to disclose unauthorized dwelling units. For anyone listed, every permit that names them goes through extra review by senior plan review staff, a licensed contractor must be named on the permit, and the department must \"Require site inspection by DBI and the Planning Department prior to permit issuance for projects associated with the listee.\" \"They will remain on the list for a period of five years, provided they are not found to be responsible for subsequent Building Code Violations.\"","Measured against like permits of the same type, review path, cost and year, 60 firms and licensed professionals get San Francisco building permits consistently faster and 93 consistently slower, far more than chance would produce, and the edge mostly lasts year after year. 37 of the 60 faster firms still stand out after a control for the kind of work, but connected firms as a group are no faster than unconnected ones, and the speed test does not pick out the cases the courts have already proven.","60 of 1,157 firms and professionals tested meet the test for consistently faster building permits, where chance alone would produce about 0.05; 37 of them still stand out after also matching the kind of work, and 11 do more than half their jobs in a single kind of work. At the usual one-in-twenty threshold 517 firms differ from like permits against about 58 expected by luck, so who files a permit is strongly tied to how fast it is issued. Strongest after the control: E. J. Weber Elect.Co Inc, faster on 88% of 99 jobs (2015–2024), a median 0 days against 10 for like permits. 9 further individuals who are neither licensed nor registered meet the test and are counted, not named.","93 of 1,157 firms and professionals tested are consistently slower than like permits, where chance alone would produce about 0.05; 63 still stand out after also matching the kind of work. Slowest after the control: Jim Parr dba Plumbing, faster on only 7% of 142 jobs (2012–2023), a median 30 days against 0 for like permits. 4 further individuals are counted, not named.","The edge is mostly a standing feature of the firm, not an episode. Taking the 60 consistently faster firms together, they beat like permits on 65% of jobs filed through 2019, 63% in 2020 and 2021, and 66% from 2022. Of the 51 named, 38 held the edge on both sides of the 2020 and 2021 cases, 3 saw it fade afterwards, 0 saw it grow, 3 had stopped filing by 2022 and 7 have too few jobs on one side to say. Across all 346 tested firms with at least 20 jobs in both periods, the share faster before 2020 and the share faster from 2022 move together (correlation 0.54): 49 of the 61 firms that beat like permits on more than 60% of jobs before 2020 still beat them on more than half afterwards.","Of 128,375 issued alterations with drawings filed 2012 to 2024, 86% were approved at the counter without full plan review: 67% of jobs costing $250,000 to $1 million and 62% of jobs of $1 million and over. 4 of 915 firms tested take the counter path far more often than like jobs of the same cost, kind of work and building use, year after year (10 points or more above like jobs, beyond 4.0 standard errors, in three quarters of active years); 45 are sent to full review far more often. Largest gap: Halyard Construction Inc., at the counter on 89% of 298 jobs where like jobs went there 76% of the time.","76% of 17,864 full-review permits issued through 2022 were put on hold at least once, and 78% of full-review applications filed 2012 to 2021 have been issued. Against like permits, 5 of 87 firms tested were held far less often year after year and 0 far more often; 16 of 115 reached issue through at least half a review station fewer than like permits (the average is 4.8); and 2 of 87 saw their applications reach issue far more often. Held least against expectation: Clear Channel Outdoor, on hold on 6% of 50 permits where like permits were held 46% of the time.","Electrical and plumbing permits leave almost no room for a fast lane: 96% and 97% are issued the day they are filed, and only 1 and 1 of 821 and 914 contractors tested meet the test. Where waits exist the test finds firms on both sides. Fire Department permits: 8 consistently faster and 10 consistently slower of 82 tested; Planning cases: 1 consistently faster and 4 consistently slower of 28 tested; Public Works street permits: 19 consistently faster and 49 consistently slower of 157 tested. 1 firm is consistently fast in two or more systems (B-Side Inc.), and none of the firms that are consistently fast on building permits is also consistently fast in another system. The three largest filers of street permits: Pacific Gas and Electric (6,862 filing days, faster on 58%); J. Flores Construction Company Inc (2,239 filing days, faster on 55%); PODS, Inc (1,820 filing days, faster on 54%).","The City issued 37,290 building permits in 2019 and 22,872 in 2025; construction value permitted peaked at $5.4 billion in 2017 and was $2.7 billion in 2025; net new homes on issued permits went from 4,899 in 2019 to 2,216 in 2025. A full-review alteration issued in 2019 had waited a median 292 days, in 2023 328, in 2025 173. The ten largest contractors were named on permits carrying 31% of permitted value in 2012 to 2014 and 32% in 2023 to 2025, and on 5.2% and 8.5% of permits by number. By value the share swings with a few very large projects and shows no trend; by number of permits the ten largest contractors' share rose, most of the change coming in 2020 when the number of permits issued fell by 47%.","Of 105 named firms that are consistently fast on any measure, 44 have at least one recorded connection and 61 have none. 36 have staff or owners who gave to candidates for City office since 2012 ($135,242 in all), 7 are clients of registered lobbyists, 3 gave a gift to a City official or department, 4 are on the Ethics Commission's permit-consultant register, 16 were paid $100,000 or more by the City since fiscal 2019, 0 employ a person matched by name to former City staff, and 1 is named in a case file or on Building Inspection's extra-scrutiny list. Each of these connections is an exact name match.","Turned around, the answer is no. Of the 1,157 firms in the speed test, 348 have at least one recorded connection and 809 have none. The average connected firm beats like permits on 48% of its jobs; like firms in the same role without a connection manage 47% (no difference beyond chance). By kind of connection: registered permit consultants (29 firms), 46% against 48%, no difference beyond chance; firms whose staff gave to candidates (301 firms), 47% against 47%, no difference beyond chance; clients of registered lobbyists (21 firms), 51% against 46%, no difference beyond chance; gift givers (10 firms), 48% against 47%, no difference beyond chance; City vendors (60 firms), 49% against 48%, no difference beyond chance; firms in a case file or on the extra-scrutiny list (13 firms), 46% against 47%, no difference beyond chance. 18 of the connected firms are consistently faster and 28 consistently slower; among the 809 unconnected firms the counts are 42 and 65.","The speed test does not pick out the proven cases. Of 8 people and firms from the case files and the extra-scrutiny list with enough permits to test, 0 meet the test for consistently faster in the years the conduct covered. Rodrigo Santos (2012–2019): faster on 51% of 1,307 jobs, not distinguishable from like permits. SIA Consulting (2012–2021): faster on 42% of 347 jobs, somewhat slower: beyond the usual threshold only. 1 favourable result on another measure: SIA Consulting's full-review applications reached issue 83% of the time against 71% for like applications, enough to be listed in the screen 'Always approved, never held'. 1 result runs the other way: Harold Howell (Mercury Engineering Group, Inc.) is consistently slower on building permits (faster than like permits on only 36% of jobs). A method that cannot see the cases the courts have already proven cannot be read as clearing anyone it does not flag.","In 2019 the Department of Building Inspection issued 37,290 building permits, 22,921 of them (61%) on the day they were filed; in 2025, 12,408 of 22,872 (54%). By declared construction value the same-day share is far smaller: $287.2 million of $4.19 billion in 2019 (6.8%) and $304.0 million of $2.73 billion in 2025 (11%). From 2019 to 2025 same-day permits carried $1.81 billion of $20.70 billion in declared value. Almost none of the permits sent for plan review (1 of 981 in 2025) or for new buildings (0 of 45) were issued the same day. How long the rest take, and who gets through fastest, is in the site’s “How the permit process works” page.","Building Inspection publishes elapsed review intervals by station. The 2025 PermitSF performance targets can also be applied retrospectively to older records.","Among 127,033 finished first station reviews of permits filed in 2015–2026, 77% took more than 30 calendar days from filing. Of 88,730 finished rechecks, 18% exceeded 14 calendar days. The City announced these performance targets in May 2025. Among stations with 1,000 or more first reviews, Structural plan check fell within the benchmark least often (17%) and Mechanical and electrical plan check most often (43%). Building plan check met it on 19% of first reviews and the Planning zoning check on 23%. 4,965 review steps on permits filed in these years have no finish date yet.","The share of first reviews within the 30-calendar-day benchmark was lowest for permits filed in 2021 (11%) and highest in 2025 (73%). Recent years include only reviews that have already finished, which flatters them.","First reviews by permit type and cost band: additions alterations or repairs $5 million and over: 23% on time, median 104 days from filing; new construction $5 million and over: 24% on time, median 114 days from filing; additions alterations or repairs under $10,000: 30% on time, median 77 days from filing; demolitions under $10,000: 28% on time, median 123 days from filing.","2,269 scored permits list a permit consultant or expediter. Their reviews sat at an average place of 0.50 among like reviews (0 = fastest, 0.5 = typical, 1 = slowest), against 0.50 for permits without one. additions alterations or repairs: 0.49 against 0.51 (-2.5 standard errors), 3.8 rechecks per permit against 3.4. new construction wood frame: 0.51 against 0.50 (+1.4 standard errors), 5.3 rechecks per permit against 6.2. new construction: 0.52 against 0.51 (+0.5 standard errors), 8.9 rechecks per permit against 12.1.","131 firms and professionals are listed on 40 or more scored jobs. 7 are faster than like reviews beyond chance and 7 slower (cut 3.6 standard errors, so about 0.05 would pass by luck). Fastest: Comstock Crosser & Assoc Develp Co Inc. (contractor), average place 0.34 on 73 jobs, 26% of its review visits finished the day they arrived.","Of 17 consistently faster firms that could be tested in the City’s review table, 5 are also faster at the review desks (2 standard errors or more) and 2 are slower there. Of 29 consistently slower firms tested, 4 are slower at the desks too. 66 firms have fewer than 10 permits in the review table (most fast-lane firms work on counter permits, which this table does not cover).","Of 10,015 permits issued 2020 to 2026 that appear in both tables, 13 have a review step that arrived before the issue date but finished a day or more after it (13 steps), and 2 were issued with a step that arrived before issue and still has no finish date.","Of 9,451 permits issued 2020 to 2026 after in-house review that have review rows, additions alterations or repairs: 6,970 issued, 1,235 with no Planning step (18%), 9 with no Building Inspection step, 3,160 with no Fire step; type 9 (no name in the table; most descriptions begin “solar permit:”): 440 issued, 440 with no Planning step (100%), 0 with no Building Inspection step, 0 with no Fire step; demolitions: 374 issued, 25 with no Planning step (7%), 0 with no Building Inspection step, 275 with no Fire step.","The issuance table lists 151,732 permits issued 2020 to 2026: 138,904 over the counter and 12,828 after in-house review. Alterations with in-house review took a median 282.5 days in 2020 and 187 in 2026. Counter alterations: 2 and 2 days. In-house alterations listing a permit consultant took a median 246 days against 305 for those without (841 and 7,265 permits).","6,994 completeness checks are on file; 97% of those judged met the City’s 21-day deadline. 1,636 submissions were found incomplete and 1,405 rejected (by the Central Permit Bureau or Planning).","For full-review alterations and new buildings filed 2015 to 2026, the review-metrics table shows Building plan check: 2.38 visits per permit (57% seen more than once) against 2.44 in the routing table; Planning zoning check: 1.86 visits per permit (50% seen more than once) against 1.9 in the routing table; Mechanical plan check: 1.73 visits per permit (39% seen more than once) against 1.78 in the routing table; Public Works street-use and mapping: 2.12 visits per permit (53% seen more than once) against 2.17 in the routing table.","The full text of the San Francisco codes settles several rules the other screens had to leave open. Permits expire on fixed clocks, consultants report only on large jobs, reviews must follow the order of receipt, and the condominium lottery gives more tickets to buildings that keep losing.","10 rules were found and quoted. Among them: an unissued application expires after 730 days and an issued permit after 365 days without work; a permit to finish expired work is charged on the unfinished part only; paid premium review is open to anyone and refunded if late; work without a permit costs nine times the issuance fee plus the permit fee; consultants report only on jobs over $1,000,000; and the departments must review material in the order received unless a written finding says otherwise.","5 of the questions raised by the permit and jobs screens are not settled here: State deadlines, fee history, small-business permit renewals, discretionary-review rules and reviewer assignment.","Of 18 firms faster than like reviews at the permit desks, 8 also appear as a lobbyist, lobbyist client, campaign or ballot donor, commissioner's business or affiliate of a certified local firm. Most links: Clear Channel Outdoor (5): paid $4.0 million at an official's request (2020-08-27), Board decision 302 days later; people giving it as employer gave $2,550 in 9 campaign gifts to 8 committees (2012–2021); rent or guarantee to the City reduced (2021-06-25) and 2 more.","15 letters on file. 4 record an adverse finding: 2021-11-22 San Francisco – Letter of Inquiry and Technical Support (violation found); 2022-12-29 3832 18th Street Project – Notice of Violation (violation found); 2023-10-26 Review of San Francisco’s Accessory Dwelling Unit (ADU) Ordinance unde (does not comply); 2023-11-28 City and County of San Francisco – Corrective Action Letter (overdue actions found; corrective action required). The 28 November 2023 findings were closed on 16 January 2024, when the State found the overdue actions done."],["Every special planning approval decided since 2015, sorted by what was proposed and where, then set against who lives there and who filed it. The tables show measured differences and what survives a like-for-like comparison; they do not show motive.","Of 5,392 applications decided since 2015, 73.1% were approved, 1.9% refused and 24.9% withdrawn or cancelled; the share not approved runs from 21.7% for new housing, 50 or more homes to 56.0% for removing or merging homes. Outright refusals are rare (104); most applications that fail are withdrawn. Of conditional-use requests filed in 2015-17, 32.2% were not approved; of those filed in 2021-23, 31.3% (variances: 27.8% and 31.5%). Projects of 10 or more homes moved most: 19.2% of 245 filed in 2015-18 were not approved and 39.5% of 124 filed in 2019-23 (84.4% of that later group has an outcome so far, and the ones still open are more likely to end in approval).","After giving every district the same mix of proposals, the share not approved runs from 23.3% in District 7 (214 decided) to 33.6% in District 11 (157) against 26.9% citywide; 1 of 11 districts sits further from what its own mix predicts than chance would explain (District 11: 70 not approved against 52 expected).","Among 28 neighborhoods with at least 50 decided applications, the adjusted share not approved runs from 21.7% (West of Twin Peaks) to 42.5% (Outer Mission); 1 is beyond what chance would explain: Outer Mission (31 of 59 not approved against 19 expected). Neighbors’ review requests run from 9 per 1,000 full residential permits in Western Addition to 115 in Marina (city 46).","In the lowest-income third of neighborhoods 28.0% of 1,660 not approved against 25.8% expected; in the highest-income third 24.0% of 2,077 not approved against 26.0% expected. In the third with the fewest white residents 30.9% of 1,315 not approved against 26.9% expected; in the third with the most, 27.1% of 1,497 not approved against 29.2% expected. Outright refusals run the other way (1.8% against 2.9%), so the gap is made of withdrawals and cancellations. Applications that mention an enforcement notice fail more often (38.7% of 326 against 26.1% of the rest) and are 6.2% of cases in the fewest-white third against 7.0% in the most-white third; allowing for that, the fewest-white third’s gap is +4.2% and still beyond chance (South of Market: 24 of the 52 more than expected). 2 of 24 groups sit further from their expected rate than chance would explain: lowest third by the share of white residents (+4.0% on 1,315); highest third by the share of Asian residents (+2.6% on 1,848). Neighbors ask for a review 71 times per 1,000 residential permits in the third with the most white residents and 20 in the third with the fewest; by income, 74 in the highest third and 21 in the lowest.","Counted plainly, no resident characteristic is linked to the share of applications not approved beyond what chance would produce (income -0.37 across 28 neighborhoods). Once every neighborhood is given the same mix of proposals, non-approval is lower where incomes are higher: rank correlation -0.52 (moderate), -0.58 measured against each neighborhood’s own mix, and -0.57 when the share filed through firms is also made equal - all three beyond chance. Measured against its own mix only, the link also shows for the share of white residents (-0.55), the share born abroad (+0.58); those do not hold in the like-for-like rate. Using a firm to file does not explain it: the share of applications filed through a named firm has no link with income (-0.05). Neighbors’ requests for a review are the strongest pattern: the share of white residents +0.63; household income +0.61; the share below the poverty line -0.61; the share of Asian residents -0.53 (26 neighborhoods).","Since 2015 projects adding 68,290 homes were filed; District 6, District 5, District 9 account for 62.7% of them. Proposals run from 21 homes per 1,000 residents in District 1 to 418 in District 6. Of the homes in projects with a recorded outcome, 29.3% were in projects denied or withdrawn. Across neighborhoods, proposals per resident show a weak link with income (-0.39).","Among 35 case planners with 60 or more decided applications since 2015, the share not approved runs from 7.5% to 50.0%; 2 of them sit further from what the mix of cases predicts than chance would explain, and in those cases every failed application was a withdrawal or cancellation, not a refusal.","Filed through a registered permit consultant: 22.9% of 807 not approved against 27.3% expected for the same kinds of case; lobbied projects: 24.2% of 1,279 not approved against 26.6% expected for the same kinds of case; firms whose staff gave to supervisor or mayoral campaigns in the prior two years: 24.4% of 853 not approved against 27.7% expected for the same kinds of case; no firm named: 28.0% of 1,602 not approved against 26.7% expected for the same kinds of case.","Of $750 million in add-backs read from FY2014-15 to FY2027-28, $109 million (14.5%) is tagged to a district. Individual items carry a district tag only in the lists for FY2014-15 to FY2018-19; from 2018 the Board instead set aside a roughly equal lump per district. Counting only the items that competed, District 2 received $99 per resident and District 6 $54; with the lumps the range is $167 to $111. Across the 11 districts the rank correlation between that figure and household income is -0.01: too few districts to read anything into it.","Of 21,196 such applications since 2015, 12.8% were denied, withdrawn, cancelled or voided (1.5% denied outright). After equalizing the mix of permit types the share not granted runs from 8.4% in Potrero Hill (736) to 22.3% in South of Market (975); 2 of 32 neighborhoods are beyond chance. Across 32 neighborhoods the link with income is -0.18 and with the share of residents born abroad +0.34.","Who voted how on land-use matters at the Board of Supervisors, the Planning and Historic Preservation Commissions and the Board of Appeals, who appealed, and who won, set against the district, the kind of project and the Census profile of the surrounding tract. The tables show measured differences and whether they survive a like-for-like comparison; they do not show motive, and a difference between areas is not proof of bias.","On 575 full-Board roll calls on matters whose title names a district (2015 to November 2026), the other members cast 97.9% of their votes the same way as that district’s supervisor, though 95.0% of all 1,618 land-use roll calls had no dissent at all. In the 32 divided ones the district supervisor was on the winning side 21 times (65.6%), against 80.3% for colleagues on the same votes.","The district’s own supervisor was on the losing side of 11 roll calls on matters naming that district, out of 32 divided ones and 575 in all (appeal of a conditional-use decision 6; appeal of an environmental-review decision 5). Most often: Katy Tang (4); Matt Haney (3); Hillary Ronen (2); Danny Sauter (1).","Of 251 appeals filed with the Board from 2015 to 2026, 164 were decided by a vote and 40 (24.4%) were upheld - 24 overturning the decision and 16 approving the project again under the Board’s own conditions; 87 never reached a vote. Conditional-use appeals were upheld in 59.6% of 47 decided and environmental-review appeals in 12.0% of 100; named organizations (24.7% of 73) and individuals (25.0% of 72) fared alike, and no district with 20 or more decided appeals differs from what its mix of appeal kinds predicts by more than chance.","On 142 decided appeals in a known district, the Board’s result matched the district supervisor’s vote 136 times (95.8%): 34 of the 37 they voted to uphold were upheld and 102 of the 105 they voted to reject were rejected. District supervisors voted to uphold at about the same rate (26.1%) as the other members on those same appeals (27.1% of 1,375 votes), and were excused or absent on 5 more.","Supervisors cast 20,840 aye or no votes on land-use matters at the Board and its committees from 2015 to 2026 and 232 (1.1%) were no votes, from 0.0% (Alan Wong, 284 votes) to 6.1% (John Avalos, 198). Planning Commissioners voted no in 3.2% of 9,716 votes in the 2019-2022 minutes, from 0.9% (Diamond) to 8.3% (Ruiz).","At the full Board 81 of 1,618 land-use roll calls were divided (5.0%), most often on appeal of a conditional-use decision (13.0% of 131) and zoning map change or planning law for a named site (10.6% of 198). At the Planning Commission 169 of 897 decisions with a recorded vote were divided (18.8%), most often on new housing, 1 to 9 homes, or an in-law unit (35.7% of 42); by district of the address the share runs from 6.8% (District 7, 44) to 24.5% (District 2, 102).","Of 218 appeals decided (September 2022 to November 2026), the department’s decision stood in 125 (57.3%), was changed by conditions in 65 and overturned in 28; when someone other than the holder appealed a building permit, the holder kept it unchanged in 66 of 120, had conditions added in 43 and lost it in 11. By district the share changed or overturned runs from 27.3% (District 9, 22) to 65.0% (District 8, 40; 49.9% expected from its mix of permits), and no district is beyond what chance would produce.","The minutes record 293 votes with a printed tally (September 2022 to November 2026); 34 (11.6%) had a dissent, and members’ dissent shares run from 1.3% (Jose Lopez, 235 votes) to 4.3% (Rebecca Saroyan, 69). A motion failed for lack of votes in 7 appeals and in 6 of 45 requests for a rehearing or late filing, and 5 appeals ended with the department’s decision standing by default.","The minutes (January 2019 to August 2022, with 1 earlier hearing in April 2018) record 1,226 decisions and 731 continuances (35.9% of actions): 28 decisions were disapprovals (2.3%) and 307 approvals with changes (25.0%). The share changed or disapproved runs from 17.5% (business: shop, restaurant, bar, cannabis, office, hotel, sign) to 46.7% (new housing, 1 to 9 homes, or an in-law unit) and by district from 17.2% (District 6; 22.0% expected from its mix of cases) to 42.9% (District 4; 40.2% expected); like for like, no district or neighborhood sits beyond what chance would produce.","The 2019-2022 minutes hold 234 requests for discretionary review: 148 were heard and review was taken in 83 (56.1%), with the permit refused in 8; 61 were withdrawn. Across 872 projects at an address, 32.8% were continued at least once - 0.67 continuances per project, from 0.41 in District 7 to 0.87 in District 11.","Of 1,718 decisions placed in a census tract, 46.2% of 517 in the lowest-income third of tracts went against the project or were continued (44.6% expected from the mix of cases) and 49.4% of 644 in the highest-income third (50.1% expected). The neighborhood-level link in the planning-applications screen (non-approval lower where incomes are higher, -0.52) points the same way but is within what chance would produce at tract level, and on no Census measure do the lowest and highest thirds differ, like for like, by more than chance would produce.","Per 100,000 households, 18 review requests came from the lowest-income third of tracts and 106 from the highest (+0.38 across 232 tracts: a weak link, beyond what chance would produce); 18 from the third with the fewest white residents and 120 from the third with the most (+0.41 across 237 tracts: a moderate link, beyond what chance would produce). Against the neighborhood-level links in the planning-applications screen (0.61 with income, 0.63 with the white share), at tract level the link with income holds and the link with the white share holds.","701 organizations and firms are named in these records; 95 match a name in a campaign, lobbying, City-payment or warning-sign file (a name match is not proof of identity), and 7 have a link to a member who voted on their matter in the two years before - 3 through donations to that supervisor’s committee, 5 as the client in a lobbyist’s reported contact. Counting each organization on each decided Board of Supervisors appeal, the linked ones were on the winning side in 4 of 7 and the rest in 20 of 102, too few to compare; recusals on record number 77 in commission minutes and 6 at the Board of Appeals, and supervisors were excused on 44 deciding appeal votes.","At the commissions 188 of 873 decisions at an address followed a lobbyist’s reported contact with planning staff or commissioners about that address: 33.5% were changed or disapproved against 26.1% expected for like cases, and where the contact was with a commissioner who then voted (120 decisions) 35.8% against 26.2% (beyond what chance would produce) - changed more often than like cases, not less. Lobbied projects were continued 1.11 times each against 0.52 for the rest; at the Board of Supervisors 64.4% of 59 lobbied appeals were rejected against 79.0% of the other 76 (within what chance would produce, like for like).","The Entertainment Commission’s minutes (July 2022 to December 2025) record 287 permit items: none denied, 14 approved with limits added at the hearing and 35 put off or moved; by district the share not approved outright runs from 0.0% (District 10, 24) to 23.6% (District 9, 55). Of 135 legacy-business applications before the Small Business Commission (August 2022 to February 2026), 113 print a passing vote and none a denial, and the grant lists (325 Arts Commission grants, 89 Community Challenge Grants) show only winners, so refusals cannot be compared by area.","San Francisco and the State hand out licences, homes, selling spaces, grants and task work through at least 37 drawings, queues, scored rounds and pools; only 7 of them publish both who entered and who won. Where a test is possible the same names do not win against the odds: the one multi-year drawing favoured those who had waited longest, scored rounds mostly followed their scores, and the sharpest unevenness is in how pool work is shared.","37 such systems were found: 7 publish both entrants and winners, 1 published entrants but needed no drawing, 3 publish pool members and spending, 2 publish ticket numbers only, 6 publish winners only, and 14 publish counts or nothing. 4 more are listed for completeness and turned out not to be drawings. The rule is quoted from the publisher’s own document in 17 rows and summarised in the rest.","Only one drawing on disk publishes entrants and winners for more than one year: the condominium-conversion lottery of 2001 to 2013. There the same buildings did not keep winning: 804 of 1,996 buildings won once, 7 are marked winners twice and 1,185 never won. What stands out is the reverse: a first entry won 3.3% of the time and a building on its 8th entry 71.6%. The longer a building had been entering, the better its chance; that is what the code prescribes, since Subdivision Code section 1396.3 gives a building one extra ticket for every lottery it lost, with no cap. Even with tickets weighted that way, about 85 first entries would have been expected to win and 54 did, and about 118 sixth-or-later entries against 237: the remaining gap is in the direction expected from the separate Pool A, which converts buildings that lost three or more times ahead of the ticket draw. The State’s 2026 neighborhood liquor-licence drawing had 8 entrants for 5 licences and is the only year online, so no entrant can be seen winning twice. The taxi medallion list was a queue, not a draw.","Arts Commission grants to organizations, fiscal 2020–2022: 169 awards to 123 organizations, the ten most frequent holding 15.4%; 212 more awards went to 199 individual artists, 13 of whom won twice. The older cultural-equity file (fiscal 2004–2016): 1,341 awards to 320 organizations, top ten 14.8%, led by Asian Pacific Islander Cultural Center (with 1 related name) with 35. Community Challenge Grants (fiscal 2021, 2022, 2023 and 2026): 94 awards to 77 groups, top ten 26.6%. The 2026 Storefront Opportunity Grant list names 38 recipients, each once. Without the applicant lists this shows concentration, not unfairness.","Scooter permits, 2019: 4 of 11 applicants were permitted, the 4 highest scores, with no lower score preferred over a higher one. State bond and tax-credit rounds with an award list, 2021 to 2026: San Francisco projects made 64 applications and 33 were funded: 1 of 21 in the first 4 rounds (applications of February 2021 to March 2022), when 45% of all applications statewide were funded, and 32 of 43 since, against 54% statewide. In 2 cases a San Francisco application lost while a lower-scored San Francisco one in the same pool won. Every San Francisco applicant was funded in 9 rounds. Community Challenge Grants, 2023: 24 of 28 applications were funded, in a round whose scoring the Controller later found unreliable.","Scooter permits: 8 firms applied in two or more of the 3 rounds; Scoot was permitted in 2 of 2, Lime was permitted in 1 of 2, Spin was permitted in 1 of 2, JUMP was permitted in 1 of 2, Skip was permitted in 1 of 2 rounds with a published decision. State housing credits: 15 sponsors each stand behind two or more San Francisco applications; the most frequent are Mercy Housing California (17 applications, 9 funded); Related Affordable (15 applications, 9 funded); San Francisco Housing Development Corporation (10 applications, 6 funded). 4 developments applied three or more times before being funded or are still trying.","64 pools with two or more members and enough spending to compare were found in Public Works’ monthly status reports, the Port’s contracting reports and the City’s contract file. In 17 of them one member took half or more of a pool of three or more, the most lopsided being Lee Incorporated with 81.3% of $7.1 million in \"PUC Speclzed Tech AsNeeded Srv\" (Public Utilities Commission; the pool name is the contract title as the City’s file abbreviates it). 26 member places across these pools show no spending at all. In Public Works’ own reports the widest gap is Smith-Emery San Francisco at 69.8% of \"Special Inspection & Testing\", against an even share of 20%. Neither department’s quoted rule promises rotation or an even split.","Housing credits: 11 times one parent company stood behind two or more separately named applicants in the same State round, the most being 4. Community grants: San Francisco Parks Alliance was the fiscal sponsor of 11 of the 19 awards in fiscal 2022, and of 27 awards worth $2.3 million over the four award years on disk. Public Works pools: in 14 cases (10 firms) a firm holds its own contract in a pool and also sits on another member’s team in the same pool. Owner-groups from the aliases analysis tie 2 sets of differently named winners in one round. The 2026 liquor lists, where the State accepts one application per applicant, show no two entrants sharing a registered owner account or business address.","20 organizations and firms appear as a winner or member in two or more different places. 4 of the 8 entrants in the 2026 neighborhood liquor-licence drawing are also on the 2026 Storefront Opportunity Grant list (4 joined through the business registry, not by name alone), 2 of them ranked within the licences available; 3 firms hold places in three or more Public Works pools at once; 13 organizations won in more than one grant or credit program. 9 of them are profiled City payees and 7 have staff recorded as campaign donors.","74 listings on the City’s housing portal offered 689 homes and drew 128,010 applications; the middle listing had 260 applications per home and the most contested 2,925. Holders of a Certificate of Preference filed 0.44 applications for each home open to them, and in 50 of 74 listings there were at least as many such homes as certificate applicants; live-or-work applicants filed 101 per home. In every one of the 74 listings applicants with no preference could not reach a home by arithmetic, because applicants holding a preference alone outnumbered the homes.","19 passages from audits, reviews and enforcement releases are on disk, 5 of them about below-market-rate housing. On housing, the City Attorney reported in December 2018: “City enforcement has so far returned 21 homes to their intended purpose — providing affordable housing to San Franciscans who need it”. On grants, the Controller’s 2023 review of the Community Challenge Grant round found 4 separate failures in how applications were scored. No review of the condominium-conversion lottery’s drawings was found; what is on disk about it is the program’s own status reports.","Taxi medallions: free medallions went down a waiting list that held 3,029 people in 2012, by when it was closed to new names; from 2010 the City offered medallions for sale instead. Of 722 medallions now held as purchased, 302 (41.8%) are marked foreclosed, and 302 purchased medallions are held in the name of 2 credit unions, so the buyers are not shown to have gained. Condominium lottery: 199 homes a year won conversion until 2013, when 593 entered buildings lost the last drawing; the fee-based program that replaced it converted 1,915 homes, 1,155 of them in its first year, by eligibility and fee, with no drawing.","25 of the 37 systems cannot be tested for repeat winners from what is published: 12 publish nothing usable, 2 publish counts or the entrant pool only, 6 publish winners without applicants, 2 publish ticket numbers that change every drawing, and 3 publish pool spending without the task orders. The download also recorded 173 links it could not fetch.","Condominium lottery (2001 to 2013): 1,996 buildings made 6,987 entries and 818 wins; 7 building(s) show as a winner twice, and 2 registry owner(s) had a property business at two or more buildings entered in one drawing. Taxi medallions: 18 of 801 holder names hold more than one of the 1,145 medallions, the largest being SFFCU (San Francisco Federal Credit Union) Taxi Medallion Holder with 292. Cannabis: 17 owner-group(s) hold State licences under two or more legal names, and 60 more pairs share only an owner’s name. Grant rounds: 8 pair(s) of tied organizations were funded in the same round. Mobile food and street vending: 1 group(s) under two applicant names; 4 vending names appear twice.","67,798 Planning rows; 9,140 were scored against a deadline and 3,207 (35%) ran over. the site’s “How the permit process works” page, built from the earlier copy of this table, counted 8,791 judged steps with 33% over; the new copy has 9,140 judged with 35% over. 13,373 first-plan-review rows have no end event at all, and Planning itself flags 336 rows as a potential error (missing revision letter: 229, missing completeness letter: 40, review days negative: 34, duplicate revision received: 24, completeness days negative: 9).","At most 200 units a year are chosen by lottery (Subdivision Code 1396). The seniority procedure in 1396.3, which prevails over the original 1396.1, gives each losing building an extra ticket for every lottery it lost, with no cap (1396.1 had capped tickets at five), and puts buildings that lost three or more times into a Pool A of up to 100 units ranked by how often they lost. So a rising chance of winning for repeat entrants is what the rule is designed to produce. No lottery could be held before 1 January 2024 (1396.5).","The 210 hearings in the minutes saved for 2022-08-17 to 2026-07-09 record 1,195 decisions and 476 continuances (27.8% of actions, against 35.8% in 2019-01 to 2022-08). 11 decisions were disapprovals (0.9%, against 2.3% before) and 209 approvals with changes; the share changed or disapproved is 18.4% against 27.4% before. By year the share continued runs from 16.2% to 31.5%.","The 2022-08 to 2026-07 minutes hold 127 discretionary-review cases: 93 were decided and review was taken in 38 (40.9%, against 56.1% in 2019-01 to 2022-08), with the permit refused in 0; 29 were withdrawn and 5 were only continued in these files. Of 742 projects at an address, 22.0% were continued at least once (0.44 continuances per project, against 0.65 before) and 38 were continued three or more times.","38 projects at an address were continued on three or more hearing dates; the most, 10 times (A residential property (address not printed)). 15 had no decision in these minutes and 2 ended withdrawn; 6 were presented at some point by a registered lobbyist or permit consultant."],["Gifts from people the official’s department pays.","Contractor money around the vote.","Grantees that fund campaigns.","Lobbyists’ money for the officials they lobby.","Many donors, one employer, one day.","Union money in, union money out, and the committees that carry a union’s name.","What officials and donors were fined for.","Follow disclosed lobbying to the original monthly filings, the actual Board action and the contract provisions at stake.","Eight comparisons distinguish final enactments, contract ceilings, an amended permit and a proposal still in committee.","329 disclosed union-client contacts cite 51 matched Board files. The table retains filed and pending proposals alongside enactments.","1,482 contact entries include monitoring, support, opposition and requests for delay.","54 other contact records explicitly cite one of the same files.","1,125 entries report $3,592,156.70 of compensation, including zero-dollar disclosures.","A union-sponsored committee settled two mailer disclaimer violations. A separate committee settled filing violations involving three elections.","Four places where public money is decided outside the City’s main ledger: the Board of Supervisors’ own June spending list, the bid sheets for construction contracts, the State’s contractor-licence file, and the boards of the school district, City College, BART and four other bodies. Every row comes from a document the body itself published, and every pattern here is a question to check, not a finding of wrongdoing.","Across 13 budgets the Board’s lists add $454.2 million for the first year alone, peaking at $99.0 million in June 2021 and falling to $19.3 million in June 2026; of 2,036 lines, 16 name an organisation the City pays.","40% of itemised add-back dollars are placed in three departments (Mayor’s Office (housing and community development), Children, Youth and Their Families, Economic and Workforce Development); these are departments that hand most of their program money to outside organisations by grant.","In the 2014–2017 lists district-marked lines ranged from $2.7 million (District 1) to $4.5 million (District 10); from 2018 the lists set an equal sum per district, about $0.5–1 million each a year.","At least 35 purposes appear in two or more of the thirteen lists (6 in three), worth $19.1 million in first-year money; 3 carried a one-time mark in one of those years. The largest is “Expansion of the Code Enforcement Outreach program”, $5.5 million across 2014, 2023, 2024.","17 items name 14 organisations the City pays, $2.7 million over both budget years; 11 of those organisations carry warning signs elsewhere in this file, led by Collective Impact (14 signs; $150,000 in June 2017).","Federal records give a second view of the organisations San Francisco pays: what they borrowed and were forgiven in the pandemic, what the City told Washington it did with relief grants, who settled with federal prosecutors, and who lobbies or gives in federal politics. Most of it checks out; the tables below start with the parts that do not add up.","4 City payees are named as federal lobbying clients, with $3.9 million reported by their lobbyists since 2013; every charity among them also reports lobbying on its own tax returns. City payees gave $883,106 to national political groups registered under section 527, $732,048 of it from Pacific Gas & Electric Co and Orrick Herrington & Sutcliffe.","San Francisco public bodies have paid federal lobbyists $17.9 million since 2014 by the lobbyists' own reports, $11.6 million of it from the City, the airport and the water utility. The two records broadly agree: the firms reported $8.8 million from those three for 2017 to 2025, and the City ledger shows $9.2 million paid to the same firms in fiscal years 2017 to 2026, which also covers any other work they did.","Little to see. Staff of 125 private organisations paid $1 million or more by the City gave $4.0 million to federal committees between 2017 and 2026, and $1.3 million of that (32%) was payroll giving to the employer's own political committee. Only 3 same-day groups of three or more people from one employer went to a single candidate. Their staff gave $8,460 of the $523,915 that San Francisco residents gave Supervisor Connie Chan's 2026 campaign for Congress, and $28,725 of the $2,213,556 given to State Senator Scott Wiener's.","State records give a third view of the organizations San Francisco pays: what they give and lobby for in Sacramento, which State officials ask donors to fund them, and what State grants they hold beside City and federal money. They also hold the State's own scorecard on what San Francisco did with its homelessness grants.","State officials reported $62.7 million in payments made at their request, since 2015, to 21 organizations the City also pays. $61.9 million of it is in 11 rows filed by Gavin Newsom, the largest $45.6 million to Enterprise Community Partners Inc. (\"To support Project Homekey.\"). The pattern that stands out is local: Matt Haney (Assembly) reported 50 payments totalling $387,602 to Center For New Music San Francisco Inc between 2023 and 2026, from 35 payors including Amazon.com Services LLC $50,000; Northern California Carpenters Regional Council $45,000; Uber Technologies Inc. $25,000; CA Apartment Association $25,000, most often filed under the purpose \"Legislative\" and described as \"Donation to Center for New Music San Francisco 501 (c)(3) -- Joy to the City Event\". The City paid that organization $3.2 million in fiscal 2019 to 2026, and it carries this sign here: Federal tax exemption shown as revoked 2022-02-15, not reinstated.","13 nonprofits among the City's largest payees filed lobbyist-employer reports with the Secretary of State since 2015, showing $4.3 million paid for lobbying. 7 of them answer no to the lobbying question on tax returns covering periods in which their own State reports show lobbying payments: The Exploratorium ($184,000); Project Open Hand ($139,500); PRC ($114,189); National Harm Reduction Coalition ($73,500); Glide Foundation ($72,250); Mission Neighborhood Centers Inc ($34,500). Much of the lobbying is for State budget money: several reports name a budget request for the organization's own project.","The organizations' own money goes to committees, not candidates. Since 2015 organizations the City pays put $1.1 million of their own money into State political committees; the largest recipient was NPH Action Fund Political Issues Committee ($625,000 from 4 of them); Abode Services gave $325,545; Mercy Housing California gave $320,000; Sequoia Living gave $125,000; Low Income Investment Fund gave $120,000. Their staff gave $379,359 ($210,259 from nonprofit staff, the rest from school, college and university employees), of which $48,466 went to the Assembly and Senate committees of San Francisco's own legislators. Only 3 same-day groups of three or more co-workers appear, the largest 5 people giving $1,450 to Jane Kim for Senate 2016 on 2015-12-27. 5 officer-level gifts of $5,000 or more in a cycle were found. No organization that received a grant from the State Treasurer's or Attorney General's office shows gifts to candidates for that office.","The City ledger shows $6.0 million paid in fiscal years 2019 to 2026 to the 2 firms that hold its State lobbying contracts: Edelstein Gilbert Robson & Smith LLC $3.5 million; Shaw Yoder Antwih Schmelzer & Lange Inc $2.5 million. The paying departments, as the ledger names them, are: Public Utilities Commsn; Mayor; Airport Commission; City Attorney; Municipal Transprtn Agncy. The school district separately reported $587,082 to the State for the same years, which brings the public total on record to $6.6 million; the Transbay Joint Powers Authority also awarded a legislative advocacy contract to 2 firm(s) in 2023 but publishes no amounts.","The same parties turn up in many separate records (a donation, a gift, a lobbying contact, a payment made at an official’s request, a contract approval, a hire), and this file puts them in date order and asks whether the order is more than chance. It then tests the project’s own warning signs against the cases that were later proven or charged, and ranks who carries the same signs today as a guide to where to look first, not as evidence. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","103 of 5,371 payee-and-official pairs show three or more different kinds of dated dealing inside two years; so do 19 payee-and-department pairs that include a gift, a lobbying contact or a hire. The commonest orders are: donation → approval → amendment (15); approval → amendment → donation (12); lobbying contact → approval → amendment (11); approval → donation → amendment (10). Where a chain holds both a gift, donation or requested payment and a decision, the money came first in 33 and the decision first in 42 (44% money first; screen 3 tests whether that is more than chance).","Across 3,760 approvals and amendments (2,443 with a named deciding or sponsoring official), the payee’s side dealt with the deciding official as follows. Donations: 37 in the 180 days before and 50 in the 180 days after (42% before). Gifts and trips: 2 in the 180 days before and 5 in the 180 days after (29% before). Lobbying contacts: 7 in the 180 days before and 5 in the 180 days after (58% before). With other supervisors sitting when the Board decided: donations: 149 in the 180 days before and 163 in the 180 days after (48% before). With officials and staff who neither decided, sponsored nor voted on it: donations: 742 in the 180 days before and 799 in the 180 days after (48% before); lobbying contacts: 993 in the 180 days before and 908 in the 180 days after (52% before). An even split is what unrelated timing would give.","Counting only the official who decided or sponsored a decision, a gift or donation from the payee’s side falls in the 90 days before it 21 times; with the decision dates shuffled 2,000 times the average is 21.9 and 57% of the shuffles show as many or more: no more than chance would produce. Counting every supervisor who sat when the Board approved it, the real count is 116 against a shuffled average of 102.3 (16% of the shuffles show as many or more): no more than chance would produce. Both are null results. Lobbying contacts, which should cluster before decisions if the method can see timing at all, number 123 against a shuffled average of 84.7 (within what the shuffles produce).","Of 170 payments of $1,000 or more that organizations made at a City official’s request (2013 to late 2025), 9 were followed within a year by a Board or commission decision naming the payor, and 7 were preceded by one in the year before. For requests by State officials the figures are 62 followed and 57 preceded, of 1,805. Decisions are about as common after a payment as before it. In 1 of them the official who asked was also a sponsor of the later Board file: Pacific Gas & Electric Co paid $5,000 at the request of Malia Cohen on 2017-08-11, and 133 days later the Board passed “Grant of Easement - Pacific Gas and Electric Company - 68.25 Square Feet at Nort”.","Of the 8 name matches of a funded organization’s listed staff who then joined the department that funds it, 5 have a new or enlarged contract from that department to that organization dated in the year before or during the fiscal year they joined. In the other direction, of 11 former employees later listed at an organization their old department funds, 7 have such a contract between the year before they left and their first listing. Numbers this small show no pattern either way; organizations with standing yearly contracts will nearly always have one in any window.","216 case files at those three levels name 72 organizations that can be matched to a City payee; 150 further names (firms the City never paid under that name, unnamed parties and groups) cannot be tested at all. For 39 of the 72, at least one sign is dated to a fiscal year before the matter became public, the earliest by a middle figure of 6 years; 33 show nothing firmly dated before it, 12 of them carrying no sign at all today. Counting only the 21 kinds of sign that the next screen finds informative, 32 had at least one dated before, the earliest by a middle figure of 5 years. But a sign before the date is common everywhere: 65% of the 355 other profiled nonprofits carry one dated before fiscal 2025, against 79% of the 42 known-case nonprofits. What differs is how many: a middle figure of 4 such signs for the known cases against 1 for the rest. The informative signs most often dated before: A gift, donation or requested payment in the year one of its contracts was approved (20); Identical odd amounts paid twice in one year (15); Three or more kinds of dated dealing with one official inside two years (this file) (12); Agreements that at least doubled through amendments (7).","72 of 5,149 City-paid organizations (1.4%) are in a known case, and 42 of the 444 profiled nonprofits (9.5%). Of 253 kinds of sign, 40 are carried by at least three known-case organizations at twice or more the rate of other organizations, nonprofits included, even with one case taken away, and 21 of those were also datable before at least two cases became public, led by: Monitoring findings left open in three or more years, or published targets missed (15 of 33 carriers); Monitoring findings left unresolved two years running (9 of 14 carriers); A gift, donation or requested payment in the year one of its contracts was approved (24 of 154 carriers); Identical odd amounts paid twice in one year (20 of 151 carriers); One owner, several names, all paid by the City (17 of 355 carriers). 30 kinds with fifteen or more carriers pick out no more than two known cases, led by: Ten or more purchase orders just under the bidding limit (1 of 106); Large payments soon after registering (2 of 94); Tax return discloses business with an officer or director (2 of 43); The same names on the funder's board and the recipient's (1 of 40); and 17 mark size rather than cases, such as The scorecard: the 60 nonprofits paid most, and what anyone else recorded about their results.","This is a way to choose where to look first; it is not evidence about any organization. 605 organizations in no case file carry at least one of the 21 informative signs; the highest weighted count is 8 of a possible 23, held by Bayview Opera House, and the fiftieth is 2. The known-case organizations themselves have a middle figure of 1 on the same sum, and 605 organizations outside any case file match or exceed it. The highest-scoring fifty received $3.53 billion from the City in fiscal 2019 to 2026.","This ranks paths for a first look; it is not evidence about any organization. 29 known-case nonprofits have a full four-year record before their matter became public. By the fourth year the middle one was paid 1.43 times its first-year figure (other nonprofits, fiscal 2023 to 2026: 1.03 times); 21% of their money on average was awarded without competition (others: 7%); and monitoring left findings open in 34% of the years they were monitored (others: 12%). The average known-case path and the average ordinary path are 0.76 apart, and only 18 of the 29 known cases sit nearer their fellow cases’ average than the ordinary one, so the path is a weak marker: most known cases did not look unusual on these measures before they became public. 54 of 268 nonprofits in no case file sit nearer the known-case path than the ordinary one; the closest is Shanti Project at 0.33 (its nearest single case: Baker Places Inc).","Clients have paid registered lobbyists $173.3 million since 2010 to contact City officials 66,677 times; about $1.2 million from those lobbyists, their colleagues, their clients and the clients’ officers reached the campaign committees of City officials and candidates. This file follows that money from client to lobbyist to official to the measure and back to the client, and tests whether votes and sponsorship move with it more than chance: votes do not (on 307 lobbied measures, land-use matters and money files with a final Board roll call on disk, 23 of them divided), sponsorship of lobbied measures does not, and sponsorship of contracts and leases naming a lobbying client does, which shows association and not cause. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","Lobbyists reported $173.3 million from 2,520 clients between January 2010 and October 2026: $6.0 million in 2010, a high of $13.7 million in 2019 and $12.7 million in 2025. The ten largest clients account for 12.4%; the largest are Prologis ($2.7 million), Recology ($2.5 million), Golden State Warriors ($2.5 million). Separately, 22 organizations that registered because they spend on campaigns to sway City decisions reported $3.3 million of such spending since 2019, led by Juul Labs, Inc. ($780,646), Mission for All, LLC ($756,446).","329 firms, sole practitioners and in-house teams reported payments; 88 of them sell lobbying to three or more outside clients and took 96.4% of the total. Five names account for 68.2%: Lighthouse Public Affairs ($33.1 million), HMS Associates ($24.7 million), Noyola Piccini Group ($22.1 million), New Deal Advisers ($19.9 million), Platinum Advisors ($18.5 million).","86 lobbying clients are nonprofits the City funds or payees of $1 million or more since fiscal 2019; lobbyists reported $14.0 million from them, $3.1 million of it from 30 City-funded nonprofits. 681 of their reported contacts mention a contract, grant, lease, budget or funding. The largest payers among the nonprofits: California Pacific Medical Center ($870,340), Exploratorium ($337,500), Corporation of Fine Arts Museums ($255,000).","Lobbyists reported 66,677 contacts with 2,873 named officials and staff from 2010 to October 2026. 33.7% were with the Planning Department and its commission, 21.6% with the Board of Supervisors and 10.9% with the Retirement System, where investment managers pitch for pension money. 11,967 contacts were with an elected official or a candidate for City office; the most contacted are Aaron Peskin (1,235), David Chiu (748), Mark Farrell (718), London N. Breed (673).","Lobbyists wrote a Board file number on contacts about 294 files; 258 are ordinances, resolutions, motions or charter amendments introduced since 2015. Across those files a supervisor who was contacted about the file was a sponsor in 169 of 629 cases (26.9%), against 398 of 2,277 (17.5%) for sitting supervisors who were not contacted. For 3 of 33 supervisors the gap is larger than chance would give (about 1.7 expected by chance alone). A final Board roll call is on disk for 214 of the 258 files: on those, supervisors who had been contacted about the file cast 522 ayes and 14 noes, and in 521 of those 536 votes the contact was dated on or before the day of the vote.","Campaign filings since 2010 show $1.2 million in 2,925 contributions to the committees of City officials and candidates from lobbyists’ circles: $264,180 from registered lobbyists themselves, $225,225 from other people at lobbying firms and the firms, $114,028 from clients’ own funds and $570,279 from clients’ officers. Registered lobbyists made 635 contributions ($210,673) to City-office committees before the 2018 ban and 57 ($14,286) after it, 15 of the later ones to an official their firm reported contacting within two years. Lobbyists’ own reports list 205 contributions since 2018 ($124,975), 169 of them to City-office committees.","124 links run from a lobbying client or firm, through a committee no candidate controls, to spending in support of a City official or candidate; the givers’ proportional share of that spending comes to $533,828. In 34 links ($306,195) the giver’s lobbyists also reported contacting that official within two years. The largest: DoorDash gave $100,000 to Forward Action SF, Supporting London Breed for Mayor 2024, Sponsored by Abundance Network, which spent $3.2 million supporting London N. Breed in 2024.","Lobbyists reported 86 expenses for officials worth $6,256 in all since 2010, mostly event tickets and meals, plus 61 disclosures of pay to an official’s family member. Officials and staff reported 294 gifts worth $66,443 from lobbying firms and from clients then paying a lobbyist; for 38 of them the giver’s lobbyists reported contacting the same person within two years before or a year after. Lobbying clients and firms made 83 payments worth $4.0 million to third parties at an elected official’s request, and paid for 5 trips.","For 52 officials and candidates the four channels together come to $5.4 million since 2010: $898,128 into their own committees, $533,828 as the circle’s share of independent spending for them, $4.0 million paid to others at their request and $29,547 in gifts and travel. Money into their own committees is 1.9% of the $47.6 million those committees took in (typical official 2.3%; highest Katy Tang, 11.4%). The largest totals: Edwin Lee ($3.8 million), London N. Breed ($410,420), Mark Farrell ($245,195), Daniel Lurie ($118,098).","Of 66,677 reported contacts, 36,045 concern planning or building approval for a project and 13,008 other, or general discussion. Lobbyists sought approval or support in 54.9% of contacts and opposed something in 2.6%; the rest say “monitor”, ask for changes or give no position. Only 2,291 contacts (3.4%) carry a Board file number that matches a file on record, covering 294 files; a further 250 can be tied to 36 enacted ordinances by the words of their subject.","294 Board files are named by number in lobbyists’ reports and 216 of them passed. Where every client sought support, 84.1% of 132 files passed; where every client was opposed, 58.1% of 31 passed; 4 had clients on both sides. The clients paid the firms involved $12.8 million in the years of those contacts. For 28 files the circle of a lobbying client or firm gave to a sponsor’s committees in the two years before introduction or while the file was pending ($53,901 in all). A final Board roll call is on disk for 215 of the 294 files: 191 had no vote against and 24 were divided; on 1 the motion failed. 5 of the roll calls were read from the Clerk’s minutes because the vote page lists no member, and for 1 more file the Board voted but neither source settles how each member voted.","Matching the subject of each contact to the titles of 953 ordinances enacted from 2018 to 2026 ties a further 250 contacts to 36 ordinances. This is an inference from wording, kept apart from the file-number matches. For 6 of them the lobbying circle gave to a sponsor’s committees; the clients paid the firms involved $3.1 million in the years of contact. Most contacts: Planning Code - Jobs Housing Linkage Fee and Inclusionary Housing (33 contacts).","Across every final Board roll call on disk where a lobbying client’s side can be read (307 files, 3,297 member votes: lobbied land-use matters, other measures a lobbyist cited by number, and contracts, leases and other money files naming a client), members voted the client’s way in 95.5% of 426 votes where the client’s circle had given to them in the two years before, and in 91.5% of 2,871 where it had not: no more than chance would produce once members voting on the same roll call are compared. Only 23 of those 307 roll calls were divided; on them the figures are 78.1% of 32 against 75.3% of 215 (within chance). The client’s side carried on 288 of the 308 sides taken. Clients opposed a measure in 18 cases and saw it defeated in 1; in 16 the measure they opposed passed with no member voting no. In the other 290 cases (a client seeking passage, or taking a side on an appeal) the client’s side lost 3 times. Taken apart: measures cited by file number, 96.3% of 134 against 83.2% of 1,207 (within chance; on their 11 divided roll calls, within chance); money files naming a client, 98.0% of 146 against 98.8% of 1,205 (within chance; on their 9 divided roll calls, within chance); committee votes on the same files, 97.1% of 69 against 91.7% of 567 (within chance; 3 divided, too few votes on each side to test). Members a client’s lobbyist had contacted voted the client’s way in 86.1% of 776 votes against 93.8% of 2,521 for the rest (within chance). Land-use matters alone, the test as first run: 137 land-use matters with a final roll call were lobbied on, and the client’s side can be read for 79 of them (841 member votes). Members voted the client’s way in 94.0% of 184 votes where the client’s circle had given to them in the two years before, and in 93.3% of 657 votes where it had not: no more than chance would produce once members voting on the same matter are compared. On the 5 matters with a divided roll call the figures are 81.8% of 11 against 78.6% of 42 (within chance).","Across every final Board roll call on disk where a client’s side can be read, 20 supervisors have at least five votes on each side to compare; 1 voted the client’s way more often when the circle had given, beyond the 5% chance level, where chance alone would produce about 1.0. That one is London N. Breed: 97.3% of 37 votes against 83.7% of 49, but 8 of the 9 votes against a client were cast on roll calls with no dissent, where every member voted against the client, so the gap mostly reflects which clients’ circles had given, not votes that differed from colleagues’. On divided roll calls alone 0 supervisors have five votes on each side, and 0 cross that level. Across all 33 supervisors the votes cast against a lobbying client number 264 of 3,297, 60 of them on divided roll calls. On lobbied land-use matters alone, the test as first run, 12 supervisors could be compared and 0 went beyond chance (about 0.6 expected).","46 divided roll calls are set out in 46 rows (a file with clients on both sides has a row for each): 36 final Board votes, 2 other Board votes on the same measures where a member voted differently, and 8 committee votes. A client’s side can be read for 23 of the final votes, in 23 rows: the client’s side won 22 and lost 1. On those final votes, members who had received money from the client’s circle in the two years before voted the client’s way 25 of 32 times (78.1%); members who had not, 162 of 215 (75.3%); by the test in the screen on votes that difference is within chance. The client’s side lost on 200455 (2020-06-23, 10 to 1). In 20 rows the clients only monitored the measure or asked for changes, so no side can be read.","Across 97 lobbied measures whose clients sought passage, a sitting supervisor was a sponsor in 46 of 111 cases where the clients’ circle had given to that supervisor in the two years before introduction (41.4%) and in 293 of 977 where it had not (30.0%); shuffling the giving among the supervisors sitting for each measure gives 41.4 on average against 46 observed, within chance. 1 of 10 supervisors with enough cases show a gap beyond chance on their own (about 0.5 expected by chance). Most lobbied measures sponsored: Rafael Mandelman (84 of 2,279), Aaron Peskin (60 of 1,046), Ahsha Safai (60 of 1,140).","136 passed Board files about a contract, lease, agreement or other money matter name as counterparty a client that was paying a lobbyist around that time; 49 of them name a sponsor (the rest came from departments with none). In 13 the client’s lobbyists had reported contacting the sponsor in the three years before passage, in 8 the client’s circle had given to the sponsor’s committees, and in 2 both. All four links (fees paid, contact with the sponsor, giving to the sponsor, sponsorship of a file with a dollar amount) are present in 1 sponsor-and-client pair covering 1 file, with $1,000 of giving beside $10.0 million stated in the file. Supervisors the circle had given to were sponsors in 23.8% of 21 cases against 8.8% of 411 for other sitting supervisors (a gap beyond chance: 5 against 1.5 expected when the giving is shuffled among the supervisors sitting for each file). A final Board roll call is on disk for 126 of the 136 files (7 of them read from the Clerk’s minutes; 10 of the others passed before 2015): 117 passed with no vote against and 9 were divided. In the rows below, a sponsor who was on that roll call voted aye in 17 of 17 cases, no in 0, and was excused or absent in 0; in 4 more the sponsor was not on the roll call (4 of them files whose sponsor of record is “Mayor”). 38 more files are gifts or grants a lobbying client made to the City; they are listed but not counted. 2 of the 17 budget add-backs that name an organization name a lobbying client.","51 passed measures that a client lobbied for are followed by something on record for the client (54 client rows). In 6 the measure itself names the client as counterparty, and the titles of those measures name $248.7 million in all; that sum adds different kinds of amount (contract ceilings, settlements and other terms as each title gives them), not money paid. At 10 rezoned or specially zoned sites, building permits with a stated cost of $117.4 million were issued in the three years after. Of 34 clients with City payments on both sides of the passage date, 27 were paid more in the three years after than in the three before.","Campaign committees of 8 officials and candidates paid $649,254 since 2010 to firms that sell lobbying (8 official-and-firm pairs of $1,000 or more). In 5 pairs ($397,453) the same firm’s lobbyists reported contacting that official, and in 5 a payment and a contact fall within two years of each other. 13 registered lobbyists carry the name of someone lobbyists had earlier reported contacting ten or more times as an official and then lobbied that same office; 0 did so within a year of their last recorded contact as an official. 9 City officials or employees report income from a firm that sells lobbying.","4 firms that sell lobbying appear by name in the City’s payment ledger, paid $1.4 million since fiscal 2019, the largest Hanson Bridgett LLP ($662,737). 7 firm-and-nonprofit pairs appear on City-funded nonprofits’ tax returns among the highest-paid contractors ($4.6 million), and lobbyists’ own reports show $2.9 million in lobbying fees from nonprofits the City funds. Of 10 names that file as lobbyists on the City’s behalf to other governments, 0 also appear as a firm lobbying City officials.","Of 32,251 original monthly reports, 3,039 (9.4%) were received after the 15th of the following month and 409 more than thirty days after it. 178 contacts about 25 Board files were first disclosed in a late report that arrived after the Board’s final vote on the file. 55 lobbyists who registered from 2019 on reported 233 contacts dated more than a week before their registration. 24 permit consultants ticked the box saying they also qualify as lobbyists, and 22 of those names are found among registered lobbyists. The Ethics Commission’s penalty list shows 12 lobbying cases since 2012, with penalties of $58,168 in all.","The Ethics Commission’s penalty list holds 12 lobbying cases ($58,168 in penalties), 10 gift cases and 14 cases about contribution limits or contributions from City contractors. The largest lobbying penalty is $16,000 (Gwyneth Borden, 2023). The document library also holds 33 announcements by federal and local prosecutors and the City Attorney of charges, pleas or sentences for bribes and gratuities to City officials.","The Board of Supervisors votes on the contracts, leases, grants, settlements and appropriations that need its approval, and 96.4% of the 3,483 with a final vote on record since 2015 passed without a single no. These screens show where the few no votes fall, who casts them, what happens in committee, what is hurried, what fails, and which votes the record does not show; none of it is a finding against anyone. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","From 2015 to 9 November 2026 the Board cast a recorded final vote on 3,483 contracts, leases, grants, settlements and appropriations. 3,359 (96.4%) passed without a single no vote, 2,703 of them with every listed member voting aye. 121 passed over at least one no and 3 were voted down. By year the share passed with no dissent runs from 92.6% (2016) to 99.7% (2019), with no steady rise or fall. The 124 files with a no vote were decided on 82 separate vote lists: 47 of them are one member’s no repeated down 9 same-day lists of 3 or more files.","Appropriation and budget ordinances draw a no most often: 42 of 257 (16.3%), against 82 of 3,226 (2.5%) for every other kind together. The largest group, contract or other agreement, has 38 of 1,089 (3.5%). Grant or gift the City accepts: 17 of 900 (1.9%). Lease, licence or concession: 13 of 625 (2.1%). Grant agreement: 6 of 203 (3.0%). Emergency declaration or contract: 3 of 56 (5.4%). Property purchase or sale: 3 of 54 (5.6%). Settlement with a business: 1 of 197 (0.5%). Loan: 1 of 102 (1.0%). All 3,483 files together name $66.59 billion in their titles.","Among the 27 departments with 20 or more files, the share with a no vote is highest for Controller (10 of 35, 28.6%); Public Library (5 of 22, 22.7%); Sheriff (5 of 24, 20.8%); Economic and Workforce Development (3 of 40, 7.5%). 9 of the 27 had none at all, among them Office of Contract Administration (130 files), Port (94 files), District Attorney (76 files), City Administrator (34 files). The numbers are small: 67 files with a no across those departments, 27 of them part of a same-day list. What sets the top of the list apart is a handful of recurring items (Controller: 8 of its 10 are certifications that a service can be done by a private contractor for less than by City employees; Public Library: 3 of its 5 name one organization, Friends of the San Francisco Public Library; Sheriff: 3 of its 5 name one organization, San Francisco Pretrial Diversion Project).","A little, on small numbers. Files naming $10 million or more drew a no in 39 of 1,064 cases (3.7%); files naming less, in 28 of 1,236 (2.3%). Leaving out noes that were part of a same-day list, the figures are 26 of 1,064 (2.4%) and 18 of 1,236 (1.5%); chance alone would give a gap that wide about 9% of the time, so it is suggestive at most. Files with no single amount in the title, which include the annual budget, have the highest share: 57 of 1,183 (4.8%). Larger files are also the ones that come with a written analyst’s report: 89.2% of those of $100 million or more introduced since 2019, against 10.4% of those under $1 million.","124 money files drew at least one no at their final vote from 2015 to 9 November 2026. 3 were voted down: file 141301 (Appropriation - Cost of Doing Business for Non-Profit Agencies - General City Responsibility - …), 5 to 6 on 3 March 2015; file 151182 (Sale of City Property - 30 Van Ness Holdings, LLC - 30 Van Ness …), 4 to 7 on 8 December 2015; file 230915 (Amended and Restated Office Lease - Retroactive - The Lighthouse Building LLC - 1155 …), 0 to 11 on 26 September 2023. 10 more passed with a margin of three votes or fewer. At the other end, 81 of the 124 had a single no, and 47 belong to same-day lists on which one member voted no to every file (all 9 of those lists fall on a day the Board voted on the annual budget ordinance, with the same member voting no on it). One more belongs here but is not counted, because the minutes print its final passage twice: file 160358 (Appropriation and De-Appropriation - Surplus Revenue and Expenditures - Supporting Increased Overtime …): printed twice: 6 to 5 (noes: Avalos, Campos, Cohen, Kim, Peskin), then 7 to 4 (noes: Avalos, Campos, Kim, Peskin), so it passed over at least 4 noes whichever is the vote on record; it is set out on the screen about the hole in the record.","Supervisors cast 37,421 aye or no votes at the final vote on money files from 2015 to 9 November 2026; 215 (0.6%) were no votes. Among the 33 members with 100 or more such votes the share runs from 0.0% to 3.2% (Dean Preston, 50 of 1,540). The most no votes: Dean Preston 50 (43 as the only no, on 19 meeting days); Aaron Peskin 30 (13 as the only no, on 27 meeting days); Catherine Stefani 18 (12 as the only no, on 11 meeting days); Jane Kim 14 (0 as the only no, on 13 meeting days). 6 of the 33 never voted no on a money file: Alan Wong, Bilal Mahmood, Chyanne Chen, Danny Sauter, Stephen Sherrill, Vallie Brown. At the committee stage 20 of 10,184 votes on a recommendation were noes.","9 organizations other than public bodies are named in two or more money files that drew a no at the final vote: San Francisco Pretrial Diversion Project, Inc. (4 of its 5 files, on 4 days); Friends of the San Francisco Public Library (4 of its 14 files, on 3 days); New Cingular Wireless PCS, LLC (3 of its 6 files, on 3 days); Oryx Development I, LLC (2 of its 2 files, on 2 days); Intersection Media, LLC (2 of its 2 files, on 2 days); Bank of America, N.A. (2 of its 3 files, on 1 day). In every case the files passed. 2 of the 9 owe at least one of those noes to a same-day list on which one member voted against every file. Set against 1,368 organizations named in files with a final vote, repeat dissent is rare.","Committees took 3,462 roll calls to recommend a money file or send it on, covering 3,455 files; the Budget and Finance Committee took 2,417 of them. A member voted no in 16 (0.5%), and in 3 the motion to recommend failed. Of the 16 files with a divided committee vote, 13 went on to a Board vote and 8 of those drew a no there as well. 66 roll calls sent a file on without a recommendation and 1 with a recommendation of “do not pass”; 209 sent it as a committee report.","Committees sent 67 money files to the Board without a recommendation or, once, with a recommendation of “do not pass”. 62 reached a recorded Board vote: 47 passed with no dissent, 13 passed over at least one no and 2 were voted down. That is a no vote on 24.2% of them, against 3.2% for all other money files (chance alone would give a gap that wide less than 1% of the time). 5 never reached a recorded Board vote. The one “do not pass” file, an office lease, was then voted down by all eleven members.","206 of the 3,419 money files that a committee recommended and the Board then voted on were sent “as a committee report” (6.0%), naming $3.33 billion in their titles. The Board’s Rules of Order (Rule 4.20, edition effective June 30, 2026) allow it as an exception: “legislation deemed by a committee to be of an urgent nature, which is heard after 9:00 a.m. Thursday may be considered by the Board” as a committee report, if the committee chair asks the Clerk that morning to put it on the printed agenda. The ordinary rule is that a meeting’s new business is what committees reported out before 9:00 a.m. on the Thursday before the Tuesday meeting. In the record these files were heard on a Thursday (123), Friday (45) or Monday (35) and voted by the Board a median 5 days later; 36 were voted the next day. Other files waited a median 6 days, and 1,185 of them 12 days or more. The share peaked at 19.5% in 2017, a year in which other files waited a median 12 days, and was 1.1% in 2026; 121 of the 206 came in June, July or December. Dissent is not clearly more common on them: 9 of 206 drew a no at the final vote (4.4%) against 109 of 3,213 (3.4%), a gap chance alone would give about 43% of the time.","By kind, the 206 committee reports are mostly contract or other agreement (95, 8.9% of that kind); lease, licence or concession (37, 6.0% of that kind); appropriation or budget ordinance (18, 7.2% of that kind); settlement with a business (17, 8.8% of that kind). By department: Public Health (37, $1.23 billion); Real Estate Division (22, $107.0 million); Homelessness and Supportive Housing (20, $700.3 million); Airport (10, $126.9 million); Public Utilities Commission (8, $40.9 million). By committee the share is highest at the Homelessness and Behavioral Health Select Committee, which sent 28 of its 34 files this way (82.4%). No kind or department stands out for dissent: the no votes on committee reports are spread across 4 kinds of file.","2,983 of the 3,483 final votes (85.6%) sit in groups of 5 or more money files decided on the same day with the identical line-up of votes, naming $54.60 billion of the $66.59 billion in all titles. There are 312 such groups, on 303 of the 429 meeting days with a final vote on a money file; the largest is 44 files on 8 December 2015 (11 to 0, $886.2 million named). Only 134 files (3.8%) have a vote list no other money file shared that day, and 69 of those had a no. None of the 117 groups of ten or more contains a no vote. The one direct check on disk points to separate votes. In the minutes of the 14 Board meetings read for votes missing from the vote pages (133 of the 136 Board roll calls taken from them are dated 13 April 2016 to 26 July 2016), money files appear 133 times: 95 under “new business”; 25 under “unfinished business”; 13 under “committee reports”; none under the consent agenda, and every vote read is printed under its own item. 88 of the final votes in these groups were read from those minutes.","Very little fails. Of 3,483 money files with a final vote the Board voted down 3: Appropriation - Cost of Doing Business for Non-Profit Agencies - General City Responsibility - … (5 to 6, 2015); Sale of City Property - 30 Van Ness Holdings, LLC - 30 Van Ness … (4 to 7, 2015); Amended and Restated Office Lease - Retroactive - The Lighthouse Building LLC - 1155 … (0 to 11, 2023). The Mayor vetoed 3 appropriations the Board had passed by 6 to 5, 7 to 4, 7 to 4, and the record shows no later roll call on any of them. In committee a motion to recommend failed 3 times, and 19 files were tabled, 16 of them in committee. For 2 of these files a later money file names the same organization or street address. The vote tables print the result “Fail” on 16 roll calls on money files in all: 7 are motions to amend, continue or pass part of a file, and 3 look like slips in the record.","The files are too few to say. 20 agreement files that passed over at least one no sit in the chains of linked Board files; 5 of them (25.0%) were followed by a later file that raised the amount. Of 1,125 that passed with no dissent, 211 (18.8%) were. Chance alone would give a gap that wide about 56% of the time, so no difference can be claimed either way. The 5 divided files with a later increase belong to 3 organizations: Mid-Market Foundation; San Francisco Pretrial Diversion Project; TEGSCO, LLC.","For 332 roll calls on money files and lobbied measures the Clerk’s vote page announces a vote and names no member; 306 of them are dated 13 April 2016 to 26 July 2016. The Clerk’s published minutes of the same meetings supplied the names for 315, 278 of them on money files. That restores the final Board vote on 104 money files naming $3.43 billion in their titles: 98 passed with no dissent and 6 drew a no (file 160295, 8 to 3; file 160296, 8 to 3; file 160357, 10 to 1; file 160466, 10 to 1; file 160539, 10 to 1; file 160628, 10 to 1). 17 roll calls remain without member votes: 14 where the minutes also print no names, 2 where the minutes print the action twice with different votes, and 1 that the minutes do not print. 15 of the 17 are on money files, and for 14 of those the file’s final vote is on record all the same. 1 money file is left out of every count because its final passage is one of them: file 160358 (Appropriation and De-Appropriation - Surplus Revenue and Expenditures - Supporting Increased Overtime …): printed twice: 6 to 5 (noes: Avalos, Campos, Cohen, Kim, Peskin), then 7 to 4 (noes: Avalos, Campos, Kim, Peskin), so it passed over at least 4 noes whichever is the vote on record. Separately, 77 pages on money files record a step with no vote wording at all, such as a voice vote.","A campaign cheque and a registered lobbyist are the front door of influence; this page follows five side doors that appear in San Francisco's proven cases or in the rules written after them: gifts to departments and their \"friends of\" organizations, commissioners' own businesses, ballot-measure committees, public campaign money, and how the rules are enforced. Almost everything listed is disclosed, and each table is a place to look, not a finding.","Departments reported 1,933 gifts valued at $39.7 million between 2024-11-01 and 2026-10-31; Police (POL) received $15.3 million and Mayor's Office (MYR) $7.7 million. 93 donor organizations, giving $6.4 million (16% of the value), hold a contract, grant, permit or lease with the receiving department by the department's own entry on the form or by its payment record; the largest is San Francisco Symphony ($2.2 million to Arts Commission (ART)). For 14 of them a contract with that department began within a year of a gift.","The Controller's June 2026 audit lists 12 support organizations as of November 2025 (9 active, 2 developing, 1 defunct), against the 19 \"Friends of\" organizations departments reported in 2020; the defunct one is the San Francisco Parks Alliance, which held the Public Works accounts the Controller reviewed in 2020. Its returns show net assets falling from $17.2 million at June 2020 to minus $566,155 at June 2024, while the City paid it $22.8 million from fiscal 2017. The largest giver named for the department it supports is on neither Controller list: San Francisco Police Community Foundation gave Police gifts valued at $13.4 million in the two years of gift reports. Foundations in the grants file paid the organizations in the table $143.2 million. For 1 of them a funder, or a payor who gave at an official's request, is also paid by the same department: for the Parks Alliance, Pacific Gas and Electric Company ($16.3 million); Recology San Francisco ($9.1 million); Golden State Warriors ($124,040).","406 recusal notices were filed by 93 members of 17 bodies from 2019 to 2026; Rent Board members filed 141 of them and Planning Commission members 60. The notice was signed a median 1 day after the recusal (some members sign before the meeting, which shows as a negative number), and 24 were signed more than 30 days after. The minutes of the Planning Commission, Historic Preservation Commission and Board of Appeals print 69 meeting days on which a member was recused; a notice from that member for that day is on file for 54. 96 notices name a business, investment or income source the member had declared on a Form 700.","Of 2,134 yearly statements filed by board and commission members for 2021 to 2025, 124 (6%) were filed after April 10 of the following year, when the first list of non-filers is drawn up. The Ethics Commission's own count for all filers in 2025 is 337 late (5.7%) and 79 not filed (1.3%) of 5,958, and its list of June 2, 2025 still showed 6 board members and commissioners without a Form 700; it opened cases on 21 of 234 people still out of compliance after a grace period. 13 penalties on record are for not filing the form, $33,945 in all.","16 measures could be followed: 11 bonds and 5 dedicated funds. Their campaigns raised $27.4 million; the payment file shows $5.33 billion paid from funds named for them, $16.9 million of it before July 2018 and the rest since. 35 organizations and firms gave $1.0 million to those campaigns and were later paid $383.0 million from the same measures' funds; the measure with most such money is the Dignity Fund for seniors and adults with disabilities (15 donors, paid $175.5 million, 34.5% of what the file shows paid from the fund).","39 donor-and-measure pairs: $1.0 million given, $383.0 million later paid. The largest payee is Charles Pankow Builders, LTD, which gave $2,500 to the campaign for the Public Health and Safety bond and was paid $79.7 million from its fund; the largest gift is Friends of the Urban Forest's $485,000 for the Street tree maintenance fund ($1.5 million paid). A further 19 individual donors, giving $13,220, named an employer that was paid from the fund; they are counted and not listed.","127 gifts of $5,000 or more from organizations, firms and other committees, $8.6 million in all, went to committees tied to 17 officials: $3.4 million reported as given at an official's request, the rest to committees carrying an official's name. The Ethics Commission's November 2024 release on a mayoral candidate's two committees cites a $500 limit on contributions to a candidate's committee; measure committees have no limit, and the largest gift here is $550,000 from Emerson Collective LLC. Most went to committees tied to Daniel Lurie ($5.6 million) and London Breed ($1.1 million). 13 of the gifts ($1.3 million) came from donors the City paid $100,000 or more around the gift, or whose lobbyist contacted that official within a year, or whose contract that official or board approved within a year. Individuals gave a further $4.8 million at officials' request; they are counted (59) and not listed.","Campaign statements show $21.7 million of public funds received by 90 candidate committees for elections from 2014 to 2026, $8.6 million of it by 26 committees for 2024; the Ethics Commission reports $8.8 million approved for 27 candidates that year. 26 committees reported returning $246,104 to the City, and 10 are named as respondents in a later penalty on the Commission's list ($125,780 in all, for any violation). The Commission says it was unable to complete the required audits of publicly financed candidates for the 2020 and 2022 elections, and that all 27 audits for 2024 were delivered about a year after the election. The audit reports themselves are not in the document folder, so their findings (disallowed claims, repayments owed) cannot be tabulated.","90 publicly financed campaigns since 2014 reported 75,925 itemized contributions of $500 or less from individuals, 35,960 of them $150 or less. 45 same-day, same-employer blocks of five or more donors were found in 22 campaigns, covering 334 contributions (0.4% of the total); the highest share in one campaign is 6% (JEFF SHEEHY FOR SUPERVISOR JUNE 2018, 2018). Donors are counted and not named.","The Commission's file lists 164 penalties from 2003 to 2026, $1.0 million in all; the median is $1,500 and the largest $108,180. A year for the events could be read for 112: the median penalty came 2 years later, 47 came three or more years later and 14 five or more. For example, in case 24-000817 (a candidate's two committees) the count lines add up to $192,216 of prohibited contributions against a penalty of $108,180; and in case 21-22-000463 a commissioner sought a City subcontract the stipulation says was worth more than $2.7 million and was penalised $9,500. The sum is sometimes the amount at issue and often only the size of the campaign, contract or decision around it, so each row prints the words beside the figure.","Campaign cases are 108 of the 164 penalties and $666,418 of the $1.0 million. 23 penalties ($159,200) fell on appointed board and commission members, 5 on elected officials in ethics cases and 11 on lobbyists. 14 respondent names appear in two or more cases, among them Stacy Owens (6 cases, 2020/2021), Norman Yee (3 cases, 2013/2021), Jane Kim (3 cases, 2015/2019), Wendy Aragon (2 cases, 2014/2015).","Departments filed 387 monthly gift reports valued at $41.9 million on original filings. This section checks them against the gifts itemised under them, names the organizations that gave to a department that pays them, and sets each of the Board's 29 waivers beside the gifts reported afterwards.","387 cover pages from 36 departments (2024-11 to 2026-11) report 2,463 gifts valued at $41.9 million on original filings and $1.8 million more on 49 amendments. The largest: Police (POL) $15.3 million, Recreation and Parks (REC) $8.2 million, Mayor's Office (MYR) $7.7 million. 9 cover pages ($365,513) are new since the download of 12 November and are not yet in the itemised list on disk, which ends 2026-10-31.","Of 318 department-months within the period the itemised list covers, 271 match their itemised gifts in count and value (within 1%, counting the original alone or with its amendments) and 47 do not; in 1 the cover pages report gifts and the list holds none for that month. The largest gap: Recreation and Parks (REC), 2026-05, cover $750,632 against $577 itemised. In 39 of the 47, the gifts itemised equal one amendment alone. In 2 of those the amendment is a correction and nothing is missing (Public Health (DPH), 2025-02; District Attorney (DAT), 2025-10): its explanation says it removes gifts from the original, to be reported in a later month, or that the originals were sent twice. In the other 37 the original filing's gifts, $2.5 million on the cover pages, are missing from the published list.","18 donor-and-department pairs where an organization gave the department gifts valued at $2.6 million in all while the same department paid it $25,000 or more in the fiscal years around the gift. Largest: San Francisco Symphony to Arts Commission (ART), $2.2 million given, $13.5 million paid. By department: Arts Commission (ART) $2.2 million; War Memorial (WAR) $247,689; Airport (AIR/SFO) $37,548; Recreation and Parks (REC) $32,800. 1,389 gifts from donors whose names read as individuals are counted and not matched.","The export lists 29 waivers approved 2023-02-09 to 2026-10-02 (5 in 2023, 4 in 2024, 16 in 2025, 4 in 2026); 0 are new since the 12 November download. 2 name \"City contractors\" as the payors. The departments they cover itemised gifts after the approval dates for 28 of the waivers; the gift reports name the donor, the waiver list does not.","49 of 387 cover pages are amendments, carrying $1.8 million in gifts; most from Recreation and Parks (REC) (14), City Administrator (ADM) (11), Library (LIB) (4). 0 give no reason.","11 of 16 organizations that gave gifts to a department paying them also appear in a lobbying, commissioner, requested-payment or contract screen. Most: San Francisco Public Health Foundation (9 influence and 16 contract screens): boardvotes: The money files that drew a no vote; boardvotes: What did not pass; chains: Chains of dealings between one payee and one official and 3 more.","Per person represented, the largest givers were Firefighters Local 798 ($3.5 million since 2015, 1,800 dollars per person paid in fiscal 2026; overtime 1.73 times 2019, pay per person 1.46 times, median top of scale 25.1% higher); Deputy Sheriffs’ Association ($1.2 million since 2015, 1,595 dollars per person paid in fiscal 2026; overtime 1.87 times 2019, pay per person 1.48 times, median top of scale 31.2% higher); Police Officers Association ($3.1 million since 2015, 1,371 dollars per person paid in fiscal 2026; overtime 2.57 times 2019, pay per person 1.74 times, median top of scale 26.4% higher). Across 11 union families the rank correlation of giving per person with overtime growth is 0.727, with the level of overtime (share of regular pay) 0.65, with pay growth per person 0.364 and with scale growth -0.205 (11 families): the scale rose by about the same for nearly every unit, while overtime and premiums did not. That is a description of 11 union families, not a test of cause.","4 such measures are among the unions' largest targets, $1.8 million in all; largest: Firefighters Local 798 $1.6 million for “Measure H: RETIREMENT BENEFITS FOR FIREFIGHTERS”; SEIU Local 1021 $122,000 for “Measure I: Retirement Benefits for Nurses and 911 Operators”; Police Officers Association $100,000 for “Measure F: Police Staffing and Voluntary Deferred Retirement: Option Program for the Police Department”.","218 ordinances adopting or implementing a labor agreement are in the Board's legislation records; 1 has a roll call on disk, and it drew a no: file 220594 (San Francisco Police Officers Association), finally passed 9-1, no from Dean Preston. Timing (a question only): Carpenters gave $291,000 a year in agreement years and the year before, against $63,046 in other years.","Carpenters: $291,000 a year around its agreements (2024), $63,046 in other years; Deputy Sheriffs’ Association: $161,044 a year around its agreements (2017, 2019, 2024), $39,622 in other years; IFPTE Local 21: $230,441 a year around its agreements (2016, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2022, 2024, 2026), $59,510 in other years; Municipal Executives’ Association: $2,868 a year around its agreements (2017, 2018, 2019, 2020, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2026), $2,500 in other years. 4 of 13 families gave more around agreement years, 9 gave less.","Yes. Organisations gave $1.6 million to the Yes on L committee (Better Roads and Transit) in 2022-23; 8 of them are firms in the Authority's award memos on disk, $132,500 together: AECOM $35,000; Arup $25,000; HNTB Corporation $25,000; WSP USA Inc. $20,000; WMH Corporation $10,000; TY Lin International $10,000; DeSilva Gates Construction $5,000. WMH Corporation's $10,000 came on 3 June 2022, the month after the Board gave it the $2.7 million Hillcrest design contract, and it filed the City's contractor-contribution form for it; its three Authority contracts have since been amended upward.","75 of 404 items at an address that were heard with speakers were presented by one of 20 people whose full name is in the Ethics Commission's lobbyist or permit-consultant registry; Reuben, Junius & Rose, LLP people appear on 49 of them (10 of its registrants). Their items were changed or disapproved in 31.0% of decisions against 22.7% for other items, and continued in 5.3% of cases against 9.4% expected for items of the same kinds. 3 were disapproved.","Of 683 decisions at a street address, 85 came within a year after a lobbyist reported contacting a commissioner who voted on it about that address. Those commissioners cast 293 votes on them and 280 were yes votes. The lobbied projects were changed or disapproved in 23.5% of decisions, against 13.9% for the rest.","Of the 29 waivers (5 in 2023, 4 in 2024, 16 in 2025, 4 in 2026), 19 carry a written promise to report donations to the Board, all of them from 2025 on; the reporting date had passed for 15 by 20 November 2026. Reports are attached to 5 Board files, and the Clerk's lists of communications to the Board record 3 more that are not in this project's files (Ordinance 010-25, listed 2026-02-13; Resolution 126-25, listed 2025-12-30; Resolution 569-25, listed 2026-06-24); 8 files whose reporting date has passed have no report on record. The reports read name 2 donors in all: The Prado Group, Inc., $51,000.00 for Downtown Development Corp. (File 251083); Fidelity Charitable Perspectives (Google), $1,000,000.00 for Breaking the Cycle (File 250754). The other three reports say no interested party was solicited. Late: File 250105 (Resolution 115-25) 91 days after the due date.","The papers name 70 organizations (and 2 private individuals, counted, not named); 32 of them are City payees under the same or a similar name. The homelessness department's November 2025 memo lists the San Francisco Housing Accelerator Fund among interested parties engaged under the previous waiver and says any entity contracting with it is one; the City has paid that fund $55.9 million since fiscal 2019 (MYR $54.5 million, HOM $1.0 million, DEM $337,996). Its October 2024 request says of the philanthropies then named that \"none of the partners above are contractors with the department\", while its March 2024 slide lists among partnership names Larkin Street Youth Services (the soliciting offices' departments, HOM, MYR, paid it $119.6 million together) and Urban Alchemy (the soliciting offices' departments, HOM, MYR, paid it $72.5 million together). Of the Civic Bridge partner companies (waiver payor class \"Various City contractors\"), the largest City payees, leaving aside the employees' health plan (Blue Shield) and the University of California, are AECOM $216.9 million; AT&T $47.1 million; Deloitte $26.9 million; California State University–San Francisco $6.9 million; Accenture $5.9 million.","The published list carries none of the 52 signed filings that a later filing replaced (0 envelopes appear in it). On 42 of them, 494 gifts worth $2.9 million in 34 department-months appear on no filing the list holds; in 10 the filing the list carries repeats the gifts. The largest losses: Recreation and Parks (REC), May 2026 $750,055; Recreation and Parks (REC), November 2024 $565,000; Recreation and Parks (REC), August 2026 $338,944; Recreation and Parks (REC), February 2026 $338,337. 4 gifts worth $393,750 that an amendment removed are itemised under a later month of the same department and are not counted as lost. This confirms with the signed PDFs what the site’s “Gifts to departments” page inferred from the cover pages: the list keeps only the last filing of each month, and an amendment that adds a late gift erases the original's donors from public view.","69 organization-and-department pairs gave $2.5 million on the replaced filings; 389 gifts worth $381,080 from individuals are counted and not named. One apparent loss was not a loss: Public Health's original February 2025 filing listed 4 gifts from Epic Systems Corporation, $393,750 in all, that an amendment removed because they still needed the Board's approval; the list carries them under May 2025 and June 2025, after the Board accepted them by Resolutions 238-25, 239-25, 290-25 and 291-25. In all the list shows 12 gifts from Epic to Public Health, $879,251; the department paid Epic State and Local Government $57.2 million since fiscal 2023. Another Planet Entertainment, which holds the Outside Lands use permit, gave Recreation and Park festival tickets and passes worth $72,855 (Aug 2026), answering \"Yes\" to a contract, an issued permit and a financial interest; none of these items is in the list. The Sheriff's December 2025 original lists the donor of $36,894 for first-responder events as the Sheriff himself, the official who signed the form; the November gift for a multi-jurisdictional training exercise came from the Sheriff's Foundation.","11 department-months carry more than one original filing although the form allows one (7 of them the same total filed twice); 2 replaced filings were signed before the month they report (mislabelled months); one filing names the signing department head as the donor; 10 replaced filings were true replacements whose gifts the later filing repeats.","44 City people named as directors on notices while in City service where the organization’s return says otherwise: 21 named after the return says the seat ended, 23 never listed on a return for those years. More widely, 265 director names at 74 organizations stay on 720 notices dated more than a year after the organization’s return shows them gone (most: Chinatown Community Development Center, 25 names, latest notice 2026-09-25).","These screens line up the dates of donations, gifts and requested payments with the dates officials approved contracts and cast votes, and say each time what the calendar alone would produce. The clearest result is about paperwork, not donors: the notices that tell the public whose donations an approving official may not take are often filed late, some after the ban they announce has ended.","Of 7,047 original notices for approvals dated from January 2019 to 2026-11-06, 2,975 (42.2%) were filed more than five business days after the approval date typed on them. The Mayor’s notices account for most: 2,497 of 3,830 (65.2%) were late, 695 by more than 90 days and 186 by more than a year, with a typical (median) gap of 13 days. The Board of Supervisors’ notices, filed by its Clerk, were late in 210 of 2,513 cases (8.4%). All 169 notices for Retirement Board approvals were late, typically by 71 days.","196 notices were filed more than 365 days after the approval they report, 186 of them from the Mayor’s office; they name 126 contractors and grantees, and 166 give the Mayor’s Office of Housing and Community Development as the filer’s department. Most were signed in batches: 69 in 2023-05, 42 in 2024-10, 14 in 2022-12. The longest gap is 838 days (Homeless Prenatal Program, Inc, approved 2021-10-01, notice signed 2024-01-17). Across all notices, 2 donations ($500) by people named on a notice, who gave the contractor as their employer, went to a committee of the approving office after the approval and before the notice naming them was filed; 0 of those concern the contractors listed here.","Nearly all do. Of 719 contract, grant, lease and loan files of $100,000 or more that passed from 2020 to 2026, 687 (95.5%) are cited by file number on a notice. 32 are not: 20 name only a public body and 12 name a private party, the largest being File 220145 (Tumbleweed Energy Storage LLC, $65,000,000). 3 of the 12 concern an organization that already has a case file on this site.","Campaign filings from 2017 to 2026 hold 248 donations to a committee of the approving office by a person who is named on a contractor’s notice and gave that contractor as employer. 53 of them ($13,400) fall from 180 days before to 365 days after an approval, and 26 fall in the 365 days after one, the part of the period that needs no guess about when a proposal went in. Because many of these contractors have an approval every year, their windows cover much of the calendar: if these donors gave on the same days as every other donor to the same committees, 52 of the 248 would fall inside. The count is no more than the calendar alone gives. 5 of the 53 are followed by a refund entry for the same donor and committee.","Rarely. Campaign filings from 2017 to 2026 hold 6 contributions from an organization whose name is the contractor’s on a notice to a committee of the approving office (a supervisor’s candidate committee, or a committee carrying the approving officer’s surname); 2 fall from 180 days before to 365 days after an approval and 1 in the 365 days after one, where the calendar alone would put about 0.2 inside. The largest by far: Autodesk gave $75,000 on 2026-03-18 to “Daniel Lurie Ballot Measure Committee - Safe, Affordable MUNI for a Thriving San Francisco”, 232 days after the Mayor’s approval dated 2025-07-29 (notice filed 2025-08-22).","No bunching shows. 40 organizations named as counterparty in 91 passed Board files gave, in their own name or through donors naming them as employer, 245 contributions to a voting supervisor’s own committees within a year either side of the final vote. 30 fall within 30 days of the vote; the same supervisors’ whole donor calendars put 26 there, so the count is no more than the calendar alone gives. In the 30 days before the vote: 17 against 14 expected; in the 30 days after: 13 against 13. The rows list the 15 organization-and-supervisor pairs with a contribution inside the 30 days, by amount.","The reports for 2015 to 2023 give 388 named organizations as payor or recipient of a payment requested by a supervisor or mayor. For 75 of them a Board file naming the organization was decided within a year either side of the payment; moving each payment two years earlier or later gives 76, so the closeness in time is about what the Board’s routine yearly files would give. In 147 payment-and-file pairs the official who asked also sponsored the file, voted on it or was Mayor on the day (12 where the payor had the file, 0 where the recipient was then paid by the City, 135 where the recipient then made a gift the Board accepted); 42 of those fall within 90 days. The largest within 90 days: $125,000 paid to San Francisco Parks Alliance at Mark Farrell’s request on 2015-10-16, and File 151196 (grant the City accepts) decided 60 days later, on which the official is recorded as: sponsor; voted yes (11 to 0).","From January 2023 to 2026-10-14, 25 contributions from organizations, $1,165,000 in all, went to ballot-measure committees at the request of a supervisor or mayor. 1 followed, within twelve months, a Board decision on a contract, lease or grant naming the giver: Autodesk gave $75,000 to “Stronger Muni for All, Mayor Lurie's Ballot Measure Committee” at Daniel Lurie’s request on 2026-03-18, 232 days after File 250673 (lease or property). 6 came from organizations that City offices’ own waiver papers name, under the same name or one beginning the same way, as parties they engaged or solicited ($637,500): Anthropic ($500,000, Daniel Lurie, 2026-03-23); Salesforce.com, Inc. ($50,000, Matt Dorsey, 2024-10-30); JPMorgan Chase & Co. PAC (FEC ID: C00128512) ($50,000, Daniel Lurie, 2026-08-25); The Prado Group ($17,500, Daniel Lurie, 2026-05-26); The Prado Group, Inc. ($10,000, Bilal Mahmood, 2026-05-14); Shorenstein Company, LLC ($10,000, Bilal Mahmood, 2026-05-29).","126 reported gifts and travel payments come from a giver that holds, at some time, a contract with the filer’s own department. For 27 of them a contract between the two began within 90 days of the gift; moving every gift one year earlier or later gives 24, so gifts are no closer to contract starts than the calendar alone gives. The largest is a travel payment worth $5,124 from San Francisco AIDS Foundation to a filer in EOBCDH_Public Health Commission on 2018-07-22; contract 1000009852: HIV Testing ($930,317) began on 2018-07-01.","Of 420 reported gifts and travel payments from named organizations, 23 have a Board file naming the giver decided within a year either side; moving each gift two years earlier or later gives 16. In 35 gift-and-file pairs the official sponsored the file, is printed on its final roll call or was Mayor on the day: 21 where the gift came first and 14 where the vote came first. The official is printed Excused in 1 of them. Largest: Shamann Walton, Bay Area Community Resources, travel payment worth $1,679 on 2023-10-09; File 240739 decided 2024-09-03 (voted yes (11 to 0)).","Lobbyists’ reports since January 2018 list 119 contributions tied to an official the same lobbyist reported contacting within a year. 24 of them (20.2%) are within 30 days of a contact and 4 within a week; moving each contribution a year earlier or later puts 11 within 30 days, so the two are closer together than the calendar alone gives. 16 of the 24 within 30 days went to a City-office campaign committee, the channel the ban covers; for 19 of the 24 the report names someone other than the lobbyist as the source of the money.","Very little money, and no more inside the window than the calendar gives. 6,911 contributions since 2018 to 59 school-board candidate committees were checked; 9 contributions ($1,050) came from donors naming one of the seven vendors as employer, and none from a vendor itself. 6 fall within 180 days before to 365 days after a board item on that vendor, against 6.0 if they had been given on the same days as everyone else’s contributions to the same committees; 5 within 30 days, against 3.1. 1 of those inside the window went to a candidate who sat on the board at the item’s meeting. 9 contributions are too few to read as a pattern either way.","No. Against the 48 vendor-vote dates the minutes record, 6 of the 9 vendor-linked contributions fall within 180 days before to 365 days after a vote on that vendor, against 5.9 the campaign calendar gives; 2 within 30 days, against 1.1; 1 of those inside the window went to a sitting member. Against agenda dates (screen vendor-donors-near-board-items) the figures were 6 against 6.0. With 9 contributions the test can show only that nothing stands out.","0 gifts or paid trips from the seven vendors are reported by members while they sat on the Board of Education. 2 more ($3,297, from Bay Area Community Resources) appear on statements that list the Board of Education among several offices, filed by Walton, Shamann while holding a supervisor post; they belong to that office (case walton-bacr-trip-and-vote), not to a school-board vote.","Gift-of-travel forms, statements of economic interests, campaign filings, public financing and tax returns describe the same people and money from different sides. These screens ask where the sides do not meet. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","Of 143 gift-of-travel filings from 2015 to 2026 (amendments and duplicates set aside), 116 of the 124 that can be checked appear as a gift or travel payment on the officer’s Form 700 (93.5%). 8 trips worth $15,718.38, filed by 7 officers, are missing from a Form 700 that is on file for the period. Another 10 trips ($59,340.49) have no statement on file for the period at all, all of them Mayor Edwin Lee’s 2017 trips; 7 trips of 2026 are not yet due. 2 filings ($1,113.00) were made on the elected officers’ form by a Public Health staff member, who is not named, and are not on that person’s statements either.","In the elections of 2012-2020 covered by the third-party disclosure summaries, 60 candidate races took $11,186,502 in public financing, and outside committees reported $7,850,831 of spending to support those same candidates. In 14 races the outside support was larger than the public money. The two largest: London Breed, June 2018, $975,000 public and $1,275,240 outside, most of it from the committee sponsored by Firefighters Local 798; Ahsha Safaí, November 2016, $145,743 public and $780,327 outside.","The donor tables (Form 3620) hold 0 filings and 0 payee rows; both were last updated in June 2019. The recipient tables (Form 3630) hold 1 filing, signed 2020-11-02: a recipient reporting $125,000 requested by the District Attorney, with no expenditure or proceeding rows. All six tables are labelled Historical.","Who travels with elected officers on trips others pay for, who owes the Ethics Commission late-filing fees, how much public money candidates received, and what the transit board's resolutions dataset holds. Each is measured against a baseline where one exists.","34 Gifts of Travel filings list companions (375 rows). 17 list registered lobbyists or campaign consultants, or their staff; registered lobbyists filled 56 places, most on San Francisco Chamber of Commerce trips. In the lobbying contact log those lobbyists reported 15 contacts with the same officer in the year after the trip and 15 in the year before: no rise.","79 filings, $167,977 in all. The largest totals: Edwin Lee $83,123; Malia Cohen $26,002; Jose Cisneros $17,941. Most are sister-city and economic missions, conferences and speaking invitations.","113 entries owe late-filing fees; the rows sum to $49,225 (the file's own total line says $49,265): 30 campaign committee entries ($31,315), 65 economic-interest statement entries ($4,710; filers not named, agency column blank), 17 lobbyist entries and 1 consultant. 2 owing names are also City payees: San Francisco Travel Association owes $250, City payments $14,559,828 (2017-2027); Exploratorium owes $25, City payments $754,099 (2017-2017). A separate list names 9 campaign committees delinquent in filing, all from 2010 to 2016 elections.","$23,771,782 approved in 546 approvals from 2012 to 2026; the November 2024 election took $8,840,187. The 2014 and 2015 disbursement files agree with the approvals for those years ($194,710 and $307,500). 21 transfer-of-funds forms: 20 move money between a San Francisco candidate's own committees; 1 is a stray filing between federal-style committee numbers (offices 'House' and 'Homeland', June 2025).","The statements of organization show which committees name a Mayor as their controlling officeholder, and from when. The Ethics Commission’s yearly lists show how many officials and staff were still missing their financial statement months after the deadline.","11 committees’ forms were read (40 statements of organization, 81 campaign statements). 4, all ballot-measure committees, name a Mayor as controlling officeholder; the 2018 committee that backed London Breed’s candidacy under the firefighters’ name names none and lists the union as sponsor. The Proposition E committee’s amended statement of organization e-filed on 2024-01-10 already names London Breed as controlling officeholder, although the Commission’s filing list shows the committee under a name without hers until 12 February 2024. 5 name a union as sponsor; ‘Supporting Our Firefighters - Yes on Proposition A’ names neither a sponsor nor a controlling officeholder.","The 2023 list named 365 people on 6 June 2023; on 26 December 2023, nine months after the deadline, it still named 327, all of them already on the June list, among them 10 board or commission members. The lists for 2024, 2025 and 2026 name 96, 229 and 171. 89 people (same name and department) appear on two or more lists and 16 on three or more. The Commission’s penalty file holds 9 penalties for not filing since 2023, $11,150 in all. Members of the SoMa Community Stabilization Fund advisory committee appear on the 2023, 2025 and 2026 lists (5 people, 2 of them on two lists).","Executed agreements attached to Board files show how close departments took each one to the $10 million line before the Board saw it. Appeal papers and lobbyists’ reports show who stood behind the projects the Board heard on appeal.","Of 164 appeals decided by a vote, 64 concern a file or address a lobbyist reported contacting officials about; the Board upheld 23 of them (35.9%) against 17 of the other 100 (17.0%). Holding the kind of appeal fixed, 17.4 would be expected; 1.6% of 5,000 shuffles reach 23. The papers identify the project sponsor in 216 of 251 hearing files."],["Officials paid by, or invested in, an organization the City pays.","Names that recur across case files.","The same names turn up on the City payroll, on lobbyist and permit-consultant registers, and on the tax returns of organizations the City funds. This page lists only the overlaps that have a second link beyond the name, and says each time whether the match is confirmed.","Of 12,109 people with testable names who left the payroll between fiscal 2017 and 2025, 11 share a name with a registered lobbyist active afterwards and 5 of those reported contacts with the very department that had paid them: 0 inside the one-year ban on the dates available, 0 too close to call, and 2 whose first contact came within two months after the year ran out. 1 became a paid permit consultant, and 11 turn up at an organization their former department funds: 6 as a paid officer or employee, 1 as an outside contractor and 4 on the board without pay.","8 people new to the payroll since fiscal 2019 share a name with a paid officer or key employee of an organization that their new department went on to pay at least $100,000; 1 is confirmed by their own Form 700.","5 people are on the City payroll for at least $50,000 in a year when a funded organization’s return lists them with pay of $25,000 or more and their own department paid that organization. 0 appear on both in two or more years: every case found is a single overlapping year, which is what a job change looks like.","11 pairs of City-funded organizations that do not report each other as related share two or more listed people in the same year; 7 pairs are paid by the same department, 1 pass grants between them and 0 file from the same address.","15 rare names appear on the returns of three or more unrelated City-funded organizations in a single year; the organizations behind the top entry received $39.4 million from the City in fiscal 2026.","The Ethics Commission lists 25 approved waivers since 2004, 8 of them since 2018. Over the same years since 2018 this screen finds 5 rare-name matches of former employees lobbying their own former department within three years.","Everything the other screens flagged was joined into one graph of 4,822 organizations, firms, people and addresses and 4,106 recorded links, and 112 linked groups came out once banks, law firms, office towers and other hubs were removed. Being linked is not being involved: a group is a place to look, ranked by how many different warning signs sit inside it.","112 linked groups were found among 2,305 flagged organizations, firms and addresses. The top-ranked group is named after Felton Institute: 6 members carrying 46 different kinds of warning sign and $426.9 million of City money since fiscal 2019; of its members none is a party to a proven or charged case, one is the subject of an official finding of its own, and 3 are named in the file of a proven or charged case without being a party; attached to it by one route: Gwendolyn Westbrook, party to a charged case (charged; not decided, 2026-02-23). Across all 112 groups, 4 hold a member that is itself a party to a proven or charged case, 18 hold a member that is the subject of an official finding, and 4 hold a member that is only named in such a case file.","The twenty top groups hold 228 recorded links between 170 members (attached members included); this part lists links 1 to 60. The commonest kinds are pays (grant, contract or rent) (75), shares an officer or director (name match) (74), same street address (29).","The twenty top groups hold 228 recorded links between 170 members (attached members included); this part lists links 61 to 120. The commonest kinds are pays (grant, contract or rent) (75), shares an officer or director (name match) (74), same street address (29).","The twenty top groups hold 228 recorded links between 170 members (attached members included); this part lists links 121 to 180. The commonest kinds are pays (grant, contract or rent) (75), shares an officer or director (name match) (74), same street address (29).","The twenty top groups hold 228 recorded links between 170 members (attached members included); this part lists links 181 to 228. The commonest kinds are pays (grant, contract or rent) (75), shares an officer or director (name match) (74), same street address (29).","60 organizations and firms that the City paid in fiscal 2026 or 2027 are within two links of a person or organization named in the file of one of the 30 proven or charged cases. 3 are parties to a case in the record of case outcomes; 7 are named in a case file without being a party; 50 are not in any of those files and sit one or two links away. Together they were paid $1.19 billion in fiscal years that began after the case became public; $95.9 million of that went to parties.","65 members or neighbours touch two or more groups. Yes on G, the Affordable Housing Opportunity Fund touches the most (7): 3. Chinatown Community Development Center; 4. Young Community Developers Inc; 9. Tenderloin Neighborhood Development Corp; 10. Mission Neighborhood Health Center; 17. Mission Economic Development Agency; 21. The SF Housing Accelerator Fund; and 1 more. 35 of them are the only tie found between at least one pair of groups.","23 firms are named as a largest contractor, landlord or grant recipient on the tax returns of three or more City-funded nonprofits, receiving $129.8 million in all. 9 are audit, payroll, insurance or similar firms that serve many clients by nature; of the other 14, the one with most paying nonprofits is Block by Block (5 nonprofits, $17.6 million). 5 are also paid by the City directly, 10 have staff who gave to local campaigns, none pays registered lobbyists, and none shares an address or an officer's name with a nonprofit that pays it.","87 paths were found: 39 of the kind “grant onward to an organization that shares a leader”; 24 of the kind “officer or director paid as an outside contractor”; 14 of the kind “grants in both directions between two city-funded organizations”; 8 of the kind “rent paid to the organization’s own insider”; 2 of the kind “contractor’s staff gave to the supervisor who sponsored the nonprofit’s funding”. The largest is Low Income Investment Fund to Felton Institute, $7.9 million (2021-2025). Of 20 pairs of a nonprofit and a supervisor who sponsored its funding, 2 had a contractor whose staff gave to that supervisor within three years, $750 in all: on that test nothing of size was found.","6 addresses outside the large office buildings hold a City-funded nonprofit together with a contractor or grantee paid by a City-funded nonprofit at the same address; 3 of them also hold a party that pays registered lobbyists or gives to political committees. The largest by money passed on is 934 Brannan St: Asian Pacific Islander Cultural Center, paid by Somarts: $506,437 (2021-2024); Queer Cultural Center Qcc, paid by Somarts: $287,770 (2024-2025).","62 rare names appear in three or more of the five roles. Only 1 has a second, independent link for every pairing of three roles and is listed; 6 have such a link for at least two pairings. On this test almost nothing unusual was found.","68 of the 112 groups have no member that is a party to a case, the subject of an official finding, or named in any case file. The largest is named after Tef Design Min Design: 9 members, 11 linked pairs, 7 kinds of unreviewed warning sign and $10.2 million of City money since fiscal 2019.","359 hubs were removed before grouping: 203 by name (public bodies, banks, utilities, title, payroll and insurance firms, law and accounting firms, universities and hospitals), 69 because one kind of link tied them to more than 12 others, and 87 addresses that are office buildings or mail drops. Without the removals the largest connected set would have swallowed most of the graph; with them it still holds 689 of the 1032 linked nodes, which is why large sets are split into cores.","Starting from each supervisor and mayor since 2015 rather than from an organization: the Board files that carry their name, the organizations those files name, and every recorded link from those organizations, from staff and from appointees back to the official’s own campaign, gift, requested-payment and lobbying records, with the vote each supervisor cast where a roll call is on disk (land-use matters, appointments, and from 2015 money files and measures a lobbyist cited by number). A connection is not evidence of wrongdoing, so each table prints the comparison (this official against the Board, connected against unconnected) and says when a difference is within chance. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","The roster holds 36 officials: 34 supervisors and 4 mayors, 2 of whom were both. Their candidate committees report $28.2 million raised from others; 4.4% of it came from organizations, or donors naming an employer, that are counterparties of a Board money file or clients of lobbyists who contacted that official. London N. Breed raised the most ($4.0 million); the highest share from such parties among those who raised $100,000 or more is 7.2% (Matt Dorsey). 13 officials appear in an Ethics Commission penalty.","6,984 Board files name a sponsor: 6,168 of the 10,373 introduced since 2019, and 816 of the 6,507 introduced from 2014 to 2018, where the record on disk carries sponsors almost only for money and land-use files (760 of those 816). Rafael Mandelman is named on the most (2,368). Of the 997 sponsored contracts, grant agreements, leases and loans, 283 (28.4%) carry the Mayor as sponsor: London N. Breed 167 files naming $4.05 billion; Daniel Lurie 59 files naming $1.25 billion; Edwin Lee 51 files naming $805.3 million. District add-back lines add $71.3 million across 1,025 lines. The final Board roll call is on disk for 1,926 of the 2,128 sponsored money files (appropriations and grants the City accepts included): 85 drew at least one no vote and 1 failed. A supervisor who sponsored a money file sat on the committee that sent it to the Board in 918 of 2,130 cases (43.1%); the same supervisors’ share of committee seats on all money files sent on in those years would give about 813 (38.2%): more than chance would produce.","Sponsored contracts, grant agreements, leases and loans that passed name 461 outside organizations in 813 official-and-beneficiary pairs. In 53 pairs (6.5%) the beneficiary, its staff or a committee it funded appears in the sponsor’s own money records, and in 19 more the only link is a registered lobbyist’s reported contact. The largest money link is Pacific Gas and Electric with Mark Farrell ($52,400; 1 sponsored file naming $10.0 million).","Across all sponsors, 7.0% of sponsored money files (7.4% of the dollars in their titles) name a beneficiary with a money link to the sponsor; the same officials’ money-linked organizations appear in 4.5% of the files they did not sponsor while in office (more than chance would produce). 3 officials of 29 show a higher share in their own files than in others’ by more than chance (Mark Farrell, David Campos, Myrna Melgar), and 0 a lower one; for the rest the difference is within chance or too small to test.","No more than chance: across three sets of divided roll calls, 42 member-and-party pairs had the party already in the member’s donor circle, and the member voted the party’s way in 31 of them (73.8%), where voting like the other members on the same roll calls would have given about 32 (7,848 of 10,000 shuffles reach 31 or more). Land-use matters: of 71 divided roll calls (2015 to 2026, one per appeal), 18 involve an organization whose wanted outcome is on record (it filed the appeal, or a lobbyist’s client asked for support or opposition by file number), giving 251 member-and-party pairs, 10 of them (from 2 roll calls: Perkins Coie LLP; all lobbyists’ clients seeking passage) with the party already in the member’s donor circle. The member voted the party’s way in 8 of those (80.0%), against 64.9% of the 37 pairs with the same kind of party outside the circle: within what chance would produce; the other members on the same roll calls voted that way 80.0% of the time. Money files: of 3,483 final Board votes on disk (2015 to 2026), 123 were divided and 45 of those name an outside organization, which wants the file passed, giving 533 member-and-party pairs, 14 of them (from 7 roll calls: Accenture, Pacific Gas and Electric, Planned Parenthood Northern California and others; all organizations the file names as its counterparty) with the party already in the member’s donor circle. The member voted the party’s way in 11 of those (78.6%), against 82.7% of the 519 pairs with the same kind of party outside the circle: within what chance would produce; the other members on the same roll calls voted that way 87.3% of the time. Other lobbied measures that are not land-use matters: 21 divided final votes, 9 with a lobbyist’s client whose side is on record, giving 96 member-and-party pairs, 18 of them (from 5 roll calls: San Francisco Chamber of Commerce, San Francisco Deputy Sheriff's Association, San Francisco Travel Association; all lobbyists’ clients seeking defeat and lobbyists’ clients seeking passage) with the party already in the member’s donor circle. The member voted the party’s way in 12 of those (66.7%), against 68.0% of the 78 pairs with the same kind of party outside the circle: within what chance would produce; the other members on the same roll calls voted that way 54.0% of the time. 1 of the 14 roll calls with a donor-circle party was decided by one or two votes (0 of the 2 land-use ones). No organization that filed an appeal was in the donor circle of a member who voted on it (204 pairs). Member by member, 1 of 33 has enough pairs on both sides to test; 1 differs by more than chance (Shamann Walton, who voted with donor-circle parties less often than with others).","Of 123 divided final Board votes on money files from 2015 to 2026, 45 name an outside organization as counterparty, and on 7 of those the organization was already in the donor circle of at least one member who voted: 14 member-and-vote pairs in all, 11 ayes and 3 noes. Every link is money from donors who named the organization as their employer; the largest link is $950. The set is too small to test on its own; the comparison with members outside the donor circle is in the screen “Divided votes: with a donor’s side, and with everyone else’s”.","On money files (2015 to 2026, Board and committees), a supervisor is printed Excused on one item while voting on the others that day 25 times (most: Ahsha Safai 5, Shamann Walton 5, Aaron Peskin 3, Mark Farrell 3), and on two to four items 58 times. 43 of those 83 items name an outside organization, and in 3 of them that organization had a money link to the member before the vote (Catherine Stefani and Pacific Gas and Electric: only individual donors who wrote it as their employer, which is the donors’ own text and not money from the company; Shamann Walton and Bay Area Community Resources: a reported source of income or gifts; Shamann Walton and Young Community Developers Inc: a reported source of income or gifts). “Excused” is also printed for a member who is simply out of the room, and a recusal notice on file covers only 1 of the 83 (Ahsha Safai, File 210031). On land-use items the same count is 183 (most: Ahsha Safai 37, Shamann Walton 29, Aaron Peskin 25, Malia Cohen 17), and 1 recusal notice from a supervisor is on file with the Ethics Commission. Against 3,045 declared gifts, income sources and investments, 32 passed Board files name the same organization as counterparty within the declaration period or the twelve months after: 19 follow a gift or travel payment, the largest being travel payment worth $1,679 on 2023-10-09 from Bay Area Community Resources to Shamann Walton before File 240739. The final roll call is now on disk for 32 of the 32 that passed while the official sat on the Board: the member voted yes on 31, no on 0, and was printed Excused on 1 and Absent on 0; 21 of the yes and no votes were on a file naming a reported source of income or gifts, the rest on a file naming a company in which the member reported an investment. None of those files is the subject of a recusal notice by the same official; whether one was required depends on amounts and timing that only the Ethics Commission can rule on.","The committees of the 36 officials report $77.8 million in itemised payments to 4,408 firms and organizations and 2,157 individuals. 40 official-and-firm pairs are lobbying firms whose lobbyists later reported contacting the same official, 6 are individual registered lobbyists who did, 12 are campaign staff who then started work in the official’s department, 1 shares a rare name with a person the same official later appointed as Mayor, and 69 are firms the City went on to pay.","The payroll names 178 legislative aides at the Board of Supervisors and 204 Mayoral staff between fiscal 2017 and 2026 (names that behave as one person). The Clerk’s Board Roster and the supervisors’ own pages (2026) tie 45 current staff to 12 offices, 33 of whom are found on the payroll; with campaign payments and filings that name an aide as an official’s contact, 62 of the payroll’s aides and Mayoral staff can be placed with an official. Lobbyists’ reports name 24 of the listed staff as the person contacted, 439 times in all. With a second link, 4 later appear as registered lobbyists contacting their old department, 1 at an organization that department funds, 12 came from the campaign of an official in the department that hired them, and 13 hold or held a board or commission seat under the same Form 700 account; 115 went on to another City department and 41 came from one. Lobbyists reported 2,377 contacts with 119 of these staff by name.","The Clerk’s vacancy notices list 943 seats on 100 bodies: 483 filled by the Board, 211 by the Mayor, 20 by the President of the Board and 229 by other officers, jointly or without a printed authority. Of 183 people now holding a Mayor’s seat, the appointing Mayor can be named for 63 (Daniel Lurie 35, London N. Breed 27, Edwin Lee 1). Board files from 2015 record 1,197 approved appointments of a person to a body: 237 by the Mayor, 29 by the President of the Board, 890 by the Board itself and 41 on the nomination of another officer or with none printed. Of 174 mayoral appointees with a rare name, 55 (31.6%) share that name with a donor to the appointing mayor’s committees; 49 (28.2%) share it with a donor to a different mayor’s committees, the comparison that shows how politically active appointees are in general. 24 mayoral appointees are confirmed as donors by a second link. On no body with three or more mayoral appointees do more than half share a name with a donor to the appointing mayor. The President who made the appointment can be named for 27 of 29 presidential appointees; 4 of the 17 with a rare name share it with a donor to that President (5 with a donor to a different President). Of 659 Board appointees with a rare name, 224 (34.0%) share it with a donor to some supervisor.","The Clerk of the Board’s vacancy notices (dated 2025-01-29 to 2026-10-20) list 943 seats on 100 bodies: 240 are printed as vacant and 63 more as vacant with the former member holding over. Of 483 seats the Board fills, 153 are vacant and 45 held over (41.0% not filled). Of 211 seats the Mayor fills, 48 are vacant and 13 held over (28.9% not filled). Of 20 seats the President of the Board of Supervisors fills, 1 is vacant and 1 held over (10.0% not filled). 53 of the 62 holdovers with a printed expiry date had run more than a year past it when the notice was dated; the longest is 4,650 days (Sweatfree Procurement Advisory Group, term expired 2012-05-07). 7 vacant seats are first appointments to a new body, and 24 seats print a named holder whose term end is earlier than the notice’s own date without calling the member a holdover. The Commission Streamlining Task Force’s workbook of January 2026 counts 230 vacant seats among 1,533 on the 121 bodies for which it gives both numbers.","The Board and its committees took 2,435 roll calls on 1,115 appointment files from 2015 to 2026; 105 were divided (66 of 1,093 at the full Board, 6.0%), the only ones that say anything about one member. Hillary Ronen voted against a nominee most often (20 of 45 divided votes on a nominee). The Board did not approve 39 files (23 tabled or left to lapse, 7 the motion to approve failed, 6 withdrawn, 3 the Board passed a motion rejecting the nomination); 37 of them were the Mayor’s nominations, out of 328 mayoral files. 53 files were continued at least once. On the 23 divided roll calls (21 files, 12 of them the Mayor’s nominations and 4 naming more than one person) where a nominee shared a rare name with an earlier donor to at least one voting member, those members voted for the nominee in 49 of 53 cases (92.5%) and the other members in 111 of 160 (69.4%). Had the recipients voted like the whole Board on each of those roll calls, about 39 of the 53 would have voted for the nominee; shuffling which members were the recipients within each roll call 10,000 times reached 49 or more in 0 shuffles (more than chance would produce). 10 of the donor pairs are confirmed by a second link, and 9 of those voted for the nominee.","From 2015 to 2026 the Board’s agendas and rosters give 67 supervisor-years in the chair of one of the four committees that hear money and land-use files. A chair was named in a typical 48 lobbying contacts a year, against 44 for the other supervisors sitting in the same years; the chairs’ typical figure was the higher one in 6 of 12 years. Comparing each supervisor with themselves, 8 had more contacts a year while chairing than while not and 7 had fewer (within what chance would produce). On the Land Use committee, the chair had more permit-and-planning contacts than the typical other supervisor in 10 of 12 years. Contributions from lobbyists, their firms’ staff and their clients’ staff came to a typical $200 in a chair-year and $2,000 in another supervisor’s year; 6 supervisors drew more of it while chairing and 9 less (within what chance would produce). The committees’ own roll calls on money files and lobbied measures (915 meetings of the four committees, 2015 to 2026) list 187 of the 217 member-years the agendas give, and add 4 member-years in which a supervisor sat at three or more meetings of a committee without being on an agenda on disk. In 573 cases a money file sent on by a committee named an organization already in the donor circle of a supervisor then on the Board; that supervisor sat on the committee in 129 of them (22.5%), where the same supervisors’ share of committee seats on files of the same kind in those years would give about 141 (24.6%): within what chance would produce. Member by member, 9 sat more often than their seat share predicts by more than half a file and 13 less often.","The Ethics Commission’s campaign-consultant registers name 340 consultants; 96 of them list a committee of one of the 36 officials as a client, in 164 consultant-and-official pairs. In 16 pairs the consultant also has business before the same official: 11 have another client that is named in a file the official sponsored or is the client in a lobbyist’s contact with the official, and in 5 the consultant’s own firm has registered lobbyists who reported contacting the official (most contacts: Dilger, Rosemary with London N. Breed, 36). All of it is registered and disclosed.","Supervisors and mayors on the roster reported $12.7 million paid to third parties at their request and $7.8 million given to ballot committees at their request (the two together, most: Edwin Lee $11.3 million, Daniel Lurie $3.8 million, London N. Breed $1.7 million), and $598,725 in gifts and travel (most: Edwin Lee $174,651, London N. Breed $148,816, Rafael Mandelman $50,845). In 227 of 872 official-and-source pairs the payor, recipient or giver also has recorded business with the same official: a lobbyist’s contact on its behalf, a sponsored file naming it, or campaign money. 29 further requested-payment rows were filed by department heads and other elected officers and are not shown here.","170 decisions at the three bodies have a party whose wanted outcome is on record (a lobbyist’s client citing the case number, or an organization named as appellant or permit holder), giving 1,131 member votes. Comparing like with like, for members whose appointing Mayor or President of the Board can be named: at the Planning Commission members voted the party’s way in 78 of 78 votes where the party was in the appointing official’s donor circle (100.0%) and in 98.0% of the other 500 (within what chance would produce); at the Historic Preservation Commission members voted the party’s way in 4 of 4 votes where the party was in the appointing official’s donor circle (100.0%) and in 100.0% of the other 26 (too few cases to test); at the Board of Appeals members voted the party’s way in 0 of 2 votes where the party was in the appointing official’s donor circle (0.0%) and in 55.6% of the other 234 (too few cases to test). In 0 votes the party was an income source the member had declared; 85 recusals are printed in the same minutes.","Of 5,751 measures that passed with a named sponsor, 137 (2.4%) are cited by file number in a lobbyist’s reported contact with a City official. Among sponsors of 50 or more, the share runs up to 6.1% (Sandra Lee Fewer: 18 of 297). The clients on those 137 measures were named as the counterparty in titles worth $89.0 million, and the City paid the same 75 clients $342.5 million in the fiscal years after the first such measure passed, for all purposes. A further 46 sponsor-and-measure pairs match a contact only by subject words, which is an inference and is kept apart.","The Ethics Commission’s register of penalties lists 22 resolved cases naming 13 of the 36 officials or a committee carrying their name, with $207,033 in penalties; the largest is $108,180 on 2024-11-08 (Mayor Mark Farrell for Yes on Prop D, Mark Farrell for Mayor 2024, Mark Farrell, and Roy Herrera). 17 of them are filed under “Campaign Finance”. Of 179 City Attorney actions, federal charging papers, pleas and probable-cause findings in this project’s document library, 0 name a roster official as a subject. 15 of the project’s own case files name one.","Where a drawing, a bid list, a purchase limit or a pool assumes that separate names are separate parties, the files on disk tie 101 sets of differently named City payees to one owner on record evidence and 199 more pairs by address alone. One owner running several businesses is ordinary; each table says what joins the names and what would settle it.","2 City-paid companies are declared as related corporations on the tax returns of City-funded nonprofits (apart from 63 related housing partnerships, which are in the first screen). 0 more row(s) rest on a name: a person on a funded nonprofit’s return has the same rare name as the registered owner of a business, with a second link (same paying department, same address, or the business on the nonprofit’s contractor list). 3 name match(es) whose only second link is a shared paying department are counted but not listed, because a department that pays hundreds of parties is too weak a link to name a person on. 5 name matches to City-paid businesses with no second link are not shown, nor are 6 businesses registered at a nonprofit’s address that neither the City nor the nonprofit is recorded as paying, nor 5 partnerships of the nonprofit itself that are registered in an officer’s name. The largest declared company: Caritas Management Corp (Mission Housing Development Corp.), $3,677,050.","Of 300 sets of differently named City payees, 99 rest on a record that names the owner, the licensed person or the relationship (tiers 1 to 3; $1.23 billion paid under second and later names), 2 on an address plus a telephone or named owner (tier 4; $407,543), and 199 are address-only or surname leads (tiers 5 and 6; $366.7 million to the smaller side of each pair). Within tier 3, 23 groups ($764.7 million under second and later names) are nonprofits and the related organizations they declare on their own tax returns. Beyond payees, the groups file holds 410 groups with two or more names of interest and 326 address leads.","1,332 people have filed a Form 700 for a seat on a City board or commission. 194 member-and-payee pairs were found in which a declared business, employer or other income source was paid $25,000 or more by the City in the years declared; in 12 the member listed the payee on the schedule for businesses owned 10% or more, and in 21 the payments came from the member's own department ($103.2 million in those years), the largest being Low Income Investment Fund (Elizabeth Winograd, Children & Families First Commission: $70.1 million). In 1 of those the payee is on the member's ownership schedule: Architectural Resources Group, Inc. (Aaron Hyland, Historic Preservation Commission: $617,800). 26 of the payees were also a registered lobbyist's client in those years.","2,336 recorded votes were tested against the declarations of 60 members. 3 votes named a declared interest, none of them a business the member owns or is paid by: all 3 concern shares in a large public company whose branch or shop was the subject of the application. In 1 matter naming a declared interest the member is recorded as recused. On this test the record shows nothing unusual.","5,300 searchable Board attachments were checked. 2,458 pages identified a single application tied to a dated roll call and a member disclosure. 4 of those pages also named a declared entity.","The Clerk of the Board's annual listings name every seat on the City's boards and commissions. Here they are set against the officers and staff of organizations the City pays, naming a member only where the member's own disclosure confirms the tie.","The 2025 listing shows 731 seats on 83 bodies: 598 holder names, 269 printed \"VACANT\", 140 printed \"Holdover\" and 117 printed \"Term Expired\"; 558 different people, 32 of them on two or more bodies. The 2026 listing shows 740 seats on 83 bodies: 596 holder names, 259 printed \"VACANT\", 107 printed \"Holdover\" and 86 printed \"Term Expired\"; 553 different people, 33 of them on two or more bodies. 417 people appear in both listings.","2 seat holders in the listings are named on the tax return of a City-paid organization and name the same organization on their own Form 700; for 0 of them the body they sit on oversees a department that paid the organization $25,000 or more since fiscal 2019. 120 more holders share a full name with one person on such a return without that confirmation (11 where the body oversees a paying department); they are counted and not named.","The eleven groups added on 17 November were each built from their own files. Here they are crossed with each other and with the rest of the site, to find organizations and buildings that only stand out when two records are put side by side.","2 organizations have an officer confirmed in a commission seat (rosters). Mercy Housing California also appears in 22 other screen groups; members of Residential Rent Stabilization and Arbitration Board declare it on their Form 700; it paid $25,000 to a ballot committee at an official's request; borrowers tied to it hold $351.5 million of City loans.","13 organizations carry a signal in a new group and either a second new group (0) or eight or more older groups. No organization carries byOrg signals in two of the new groups: the new files touch different parts of the City. Most older groups: San Francisco Public Health Foundation (16).","The 4 individual prescribers behind pharmacies drawing most of their Medicaid drug money through one number were searched by full name in every other screen: 0 found. Of 11 private drug billers flagged by the new drug screens, 11 appear outside the Medicaid screens: CBHS PHARMACY SERVICES (health_records: Drug money and the State pharmacy permit at the same address); KAISER FOUNDATION HEALTH PLAN INC (fedmoney: Firms the City pays that settled or admitted federal fraud matters; fedmoney: Federal campaign gifts from the staff of City payees; health_records: Drug money and the State pharmacy permit at the same address and 7 more); MISSION AREA HEALTH ASSOCIATES, INC (health_records: San Francisco addresses with the most separate Medicare group practices); NORTH EAST MEDICAL SERVICES (chains: Would the tests have caught the known cases?; exceptions: The largest contracts awarded without competition; exceptions: Declared the only possible source and 10 more); PLANNED PARENTHOOD SHASTA DIABLO, INC. (rings: The linked groups; rings: Linked groups where nobody has looked; statemoney: State grants to organizations that already carry warning signs); SAN FRANCISCO STATE UNIVERSITY STUDENT HEALTH SERVICES (health_records: Drug money and the State pharmacy permit at the same address; rings: What was removed as a hub, and why); SCRIPTSITE PHARMACY PARTNERS LLC (health_records: Drug money and the State pharmacy permit at the same address); SF ROYAL MEDICAL SUPPLY, INC (health_records: Drug money and the State pharmacy permit at the same address); SOLANO PHARMACY (health_records: Drug money and the State pharmacy permit at the same address); WALGREEN CO (health_more: Who receives industry research money in San Francisco; health_records: Drug money and the State pharmacy permit at the same address; leaks: Settlements in which the City is paid and 6 more); WALGREEN CO. (health_more: Who receives industry research money in San Francisco; health_records: Drug money and the State pharmacy permit at the same address; leaks: Settlements in which the City is paid and 6 more).","The 2022-08-17 to 2026-07-09 minutes print 64 meeting days on which a member of the Planning or Historic Preservation Commission was recused from an item; a notice from that member dated that day (or the day either side) is on file for 34. Wright (surname as printed) (Historic Preservation Commission) has the most recused days without a notice (17).","The 2019 listing yields 484 seat lines (83 vacant) on 61 bodies and 381 holder names. The 2020 listing yields 613 seat lines (129 vacant) on 89 bodies and 451 holder names. The 2022 listing yields 795 seat lines (118 vacant) on 76 bodies and 517 holder names. The 2024 listing yields 745 seat lines (110 vacant) on 68 bodies and 438 holder names. 3 holders are confirmed by their own Form 700 as connected to a City-paid organization on whose return they appear; 2 of them are not in the site’s “Who sits on boards and commissions” page, and for 0 the body oversees a department that paid the organization $25,000 or more since fiscal 2019. 203 name-only matches are counted and not named.","Every contractor whose deal goes to an elected official files a list of its directors, officers and owners; this page reads those lists as a register of people and sets them beside the City payroll, the Form 700s, the tax returns and the case files. Each row is a name on two records, with what else agrees and what would settle it.","15 employee-and-organization pairs with a rare name: the contractor’s list of directors and officers, filed when its contract with a department was approved, carries the name of a person that department was paying in the same fiscal year. 12 are not in the older screens; in 4 of those the organization’s own tax return lists the same name, and the departments paid those 12 organizations $16.7 million in the overlapping years. By department: Public Health 8, Mayor 6, City Administrator 1. A further 25 pairs with common names: counted, not listed.","8 member-and-organization pairs with a rare name; 0 are confirmed by the member’s own Form 700, which names the organization. The departments paid these organizations $13.7 million in the years of the notices. Of 13 people who have filed a Form 700 as members of the SoMa Community Stabilization Fund advisory committee (the panel in the United Playaz case), 7 share a name with a director or officer on a notice for that office’s grants or on a funded organization’s return in the same years. 1 pair with common names: counted, not listed.","6 former employees with a rare name: 5 not in the older screens, 3 of them first named within a year of leaving. Their former departments have paid those 5 contractors $43.3 million since the first notice. A further 17 former employees appear only as unpaid directors, and 1 common-name pair: counted, not listed.","Files that were read one at a time are joined here on the things that do not change with a name: a telephone, an email domain, a suite, a parcel, a registration number. The joins show which separately named City contractors sit behind one desk, what the Tax Collector and the assessor record at the places the City pays for, and who was paid after a registration lapsed.","1,725 contractors appear on 99,274 notice rows. 491 pairs whose names are alike were set aside as one family by name; of the rest, 317 pairs of differently named contractors give the same telephone, company email domain or suite. 26 pairs are already one owner-group in the owner-groups screen and 23 are joint ventures or law-firm teams. 22 pairs of City payees are left that no other screen ties together, 12 of them on two or more kinds of shared detail (32 further pairs involve a project or housing company). The strongest: Crestwood Behavioral Health Inc. ($173.3 million paid since fiscal 2019) and Helios Healthcare LLC ($24.7 million): the same telephone; the same email domain (cbhi.net); 4 identically named officers or owners.","City payees are set against the Tax Collector's business registry, the City's address file, the assessor's roll and Planning's land-use file. The joins test where a payee is registered, when its registration opened and closed, and whether its trade fits what the City bought.","59 payees paid $1 million or more are registered only on parcels the assessor classes as homes or vacant land (the places screen's test, re-run on the 23 November address file). Planning's land-use file disagrees for 19 of them: it records commercial space or homes on the parcel, or the class is a live/work unit (Bar Association of San Francisco, 936 Geary LP, Accend Networks, Mission Asset Fund, Transbay 2 Senior L.P., Aramark Correctional Services LLC and others). For 31 both records show housing only, 10 of them confirmed by a contract notice or directory address: a firm run from a home, which is ordinary.","Of 5,770 payees paid $100,000 or more since fiscal 2019, 3,148 match exactly one registry account by name. 21 of those (0.7%) opened that account after a fiscal year in which the City had already paid them, $26.4 million in those years. The largest: Triage LLC, paid $22.1 million by Public Health from fiscal 2021 before an account that opens on 2024-04-22. 2 rows are confirmed by a second address; the rest are name matches.","106 of the 3,148 payees matched to one account (3.4%) were paid $126.9 million in fiscal years after that account closed, with no open account under a like name or at the same address. 8 accounts were closed administratively by the Tax Collector. The largest is Harrison Assets LLC ($34.9 million after 2011-06-20). Many rows are abbreviations of a name the payee also registered in full; they are a place to look, not a finding.","822 payee-and-purchase pairs of $250,000 or more were tested, using the registry's current industry code or, where it is blank, the archived code. 12 do not fit (1.5%). The contract title beside each row shows the usual reason: a pass-through such as a tenant-improvement allowance, a manufacturer's code on a sale, or rent booked to a firm registered as a consultant. The largest: Moscone Center Joint Venture ($23.4 million for construction; registry code 7220-7229, 722310).","25 of the 34 notice pairs could be found in the registry under both names; 12 share a registered street address. Pairs tied on the notices by officers' names alone find no shared registry address, so they stay unconfirmed."],["Spending against the official counts, system by system.","Disaster aid, recovery money and how San Francisco compares.","San Francisco told the Treasury it spent $25.4 million and helped 2,211 households with an average of $7,021 each, which comes to $15.5 million, or 61% of the spending. The other $9.9 million is not explained in the federal file. Among the 35 other California cities and counties in the same report the figure is 90% at the median and never below 81% (Stanislaus County); San Francisco is the lowest of 36.","The City received $153.8 million. Its final report gives one line with no recipient named for $116.8 million (76%); the remaining lines name 13 recipients for $37.0 million, including $12.2 million to 4 protective-equipment suppliers that the City ledger shows were paid $35.2 million in fiscal 2020 and 2021. Taken with the $624.8 million recovery grant reported as a single line, $741.7 million of the $778.6 million in the City's two largest pandemic grants (95%) has no named recipient in the federal files. For the recovery grant, 30 of the 39 other large California cities and counties reported 1,417 named subawards to the Treasury by mid-2023; San Francisco reported none.","For most it does: across 236 City-funded nonprofits the median loan implies 69% of the payroll on the borrower's return. But 7 loans imply at least 1.25 times the largest payroll the borrower reported for 2020 to 2022, and 4 imply 1.5 times or more, together $12.1 million; the widest gap is Filipino-American Development Foundation, whose $385,801 loan implies $1.9 million of yearly payroll against $792,725 on its return.","9 City payees had a forgiven paycheck-protection loan of $100,000 or more and at least one other federal emergency source; for 7 of them City payments also rose 10% or more between fiscal 2019 and 2021. Those 7 had $19.3 million forgiven and $14.0 million from the other sources.","What the City’s pension fund, payroll, audited accounts, State filings and lease list say when they are laid side by side and set against comparable places. These are official figures read as published; where a pattern stands out it is a question to ask, not a finding of wrongdoing.","In fiscal 2025 San Francisco spent $10,517 per resident on general public services. The middle of 7 other large California cities was $2,582, which makes San Francisco look 4.07 times as costly; adding each city’s county, the middle of 6 city-plus-county pairs was $5,855, and San Francisco is 1.8 times that (rank 1 of 7). Against the pairs it is furthest above the middle on debt payments (2.94 times) and health, welfare and sanitation (2.56 times), and lowest on buildings and equipment (0.91 times). It collected $7,062 in taxes per resident against a pair middle of $2,456.","San Francisco’s general public-service spending per resident went from $6,005 in fiscal 2017 to $10,517 in 2025 (75% more); the middle city-plus-county pair went from $3,207 to $5,855 (83% more). It ranked first against the pairs in every year. In fiscal 2024 its unfunded pension promise came to $4,668 per resident (rank 4 of 7; pair middle $4,187) and its retiree-health promise to $4,641 (rank 1; pair middle $1,572). Debt owed by its general funds was $5,465 per resident in fiscal 2025 (rank 1; pair middle $1,125).","In fiscal 2024 San Francisco owed $4,668 per resident in unfunded pension promises, $4,641 in retiree-health promises and $4,915 in general-fund debt: $14,224 together. Among the 6 city-plus-county pairs the middle total was $7,698 and the highest was Oakland + Alameda County at $10,295. San Francisco’s retiree-health promise per resident is 3.0 times the pair middle; its pension promise is 1.11 times the pair middle.","For each large stream of City money paid to nonprofits: the result the City said it expected, the result someone other than the provider recorded, and what one unit of that result cost against an outside benchmark. Where nobody independent records a result at all, the money is counted and shown.","The City publishes yearly targets for 65 measures of services that nonprofits mostly deliver. Across 399 measure-years with both a target and a result, 56% met the target; in FY2024-25, 37 of 65 did (57%). Every measure met its target at least once, but 10 are on an unbroken run of three or more missed years that was still running in FY2024-25; the longest run is 11 years (Percentage of graffiti requests abated within 48 hours (public property), FY2014-15 to FY2024-25).","Of 174 missed years that were followed by a published target, the next target was made easier 52 times (30%), kept the same 102 times and made harder 20 times. After a year in which the target was met it was eased 7% of the time. In 21 of the 52 cases the new target was set at or below the result already achieved in the missed year, so the following year started as a pass. 46 of the 52 cases fall outside the two pandemic years. 0 measures have no published target after the last missed year.","Public Health's contract monitors have published results for 240 programme reviews at 33 nonprofits covering fiscal years 2020 to 2023. Of 236 reviews that give a figure, 38% met every contracted performance objective and 72 met under 80%; of 61 given an overall score, 6 were scored below standard (1 or 2 on a 4-point scale). Those 33 nonprofits received $252.2 million of the $636.2 million Public Health paid nonprofits in fiscal year 2023. For homelessness contracts, 108 contract memos carry a performance history; 17 of them say whether contract objectives were met (8 met, 7 not all met or not fully reported) and none gives the figures.","37 provider-years fell below the threshold and have payment figures for the following year; in 21 of them the department paid the provider more the next year, $48.4 million more in total. 13 of the 21 were reviews of the two pandemic years. Top of the list by weight of the monitor's finding: 3rd Street Youth Center & Clinic, $509,942 in fiscal year 2022 and $1.0 million the year after (overall score 1).","The 2020 to 2025 plan filed with the federal housing department carries 106 numbered indicators under 29 goals. By the City's own final report, 59 (56%) reached the five-year goal and 8 ended below half of it. The plan expected $4.01 billion across those goals and reports $4.09 billion spent. The furthest short among indicators with a goal in at least three years: # of housing-office affordable housing tenants at risk of eviction that receive notification of eviction support services (126 against a five-year goal of 4,812).","Per exit to permanent housing, State count: $76,258 in 2020 and $126,109 in 2025 (+65%), as the money went from $181.5 million to $489.4 million (+170%) and the count from 2,380 to 3,881 (+63%). Per exit to permanent housing, City count: $67,747 in 2023 and $97,322 in 2025 (+44%), as the money went from $361.1 million to $489.4 million (+36%) and the count from 5,330 to 5,029 (-6%).","Per household moved into supportive housing or rapid rehousing: $129,153 in 2019 and $212,345 in 2026 (+64%), as the money went from $158.9 million to $518.3 million (+226%) and the count from 1,230 to 2,441 (+98%).","Per bed of any kind (shelter, transitional, supportive, rapid rehousing, other permanent): $10,687 in 2019 and $21,096 in 2025 (+97%), as the money went from $158.9 million to $489.4 million (+208%) and the count from 14,864 to 23,200 (+56%). Per shelter, transitional or safe-haven bed: $46,682 in 2019 and $101,185 in 2025 (+117%), as the money went from $158.9 million to $489.4 million (+208%) and the count from 3,403 to 4,837 (+42%).","Per person receiving a mental-health or substance-use service: $5,008 in 2020 and $7,264 in 2025 (+45%), as the money went from $267.4 million to $387.3 million (+45%) and the count from 53,406 to 53,317 (-0%).","Per meal (home-delivered and served at centres): $6.74 in 2019 and $9.44 in 2025 (+40%), as the money went from $19.4 million to $28.7 million (+48%) and the count from 2,873,470 to 3,039,789 (+6%).","Per child or youth in funded programmes: $2,018 in 2019 and $4,183 in 2025 (+107%), as the money went from $84.0 million to $131.5 million (+57%) and the count from 41,622 to 31,433 (-24%).","The data on disk cannot support a cost per job placement over time. Human Rights Commission payments to the Japanese Community Youth Council per Opportunities for All placement: $4,564 in 2025 ($7.2 million for 1,584); one year only, so no trend can be drawn.","A cost per visit can be computed only for some centers in some years. Asian And Pacific Islander Wellness Center: $299 in 2022 and $698 in 2025 (+133%), as the money went from $4.1 million to $18.1 million (+339%) and the count from 13,766 to 25,923 (+88%). San Francisco Community Clinic Consortium: $329 in 2018 and $406 in 2020 (+24%), as the money went from $33.4 million to $37.9 million (+13%) and the count from 101,668 to 93,360 (-8%). Healthright 360: $465 in 2022 and $450 in 2024 (-3%), as the money went from $4.1 million to $5.9 million (+42%) and the count from 8,901 to 13,071 (+47%). Lyon-Martin Community Health Services: $584 in 2024 and $709 in 2025 (+21%), as the money went from $4.1 million to $8.9 million (+120%) and the count from 6,962 to 12,614 (+81%). Mission Neighborhood Health Center: $491 in 2024 and $506 in 2025 (+3%), as the money went from $34.5 million to $40.8 million (+18%) and the count from 70,192 to 80,575 (+15%).","In federal fiscal year 2024, 17% of people leaving San Francisco's shelters, transitional housing and rapid rehousing went to permanent housing (891 of 5,131), against a middle figure of 36% in ten peer areas: rank 11 of 11, where 1 is best. Its federal homelessness grant of $56.2 million works out at $63,047 for each such exit, against a peer middle of $16,182 (rank 11 of 11, 1 being the fewest dollars). Among people already in permanent housing, 97% stayed housed, against 96%. In 2019, before many more shelter programmes began entering records, San Francisco reported 46% (521 of 1,143).","7 health centers and look-alikes based in San Francisco filed a federal report for 2025. Nationally, health centers screened 45% of eligible adults for colorectal cancer and screened 75% of patients for depression with a follow-up plan; 6 of the 7 San Francisco centers were below the national figure on at least one of the two, led by Asian & Pacific Islander Wellness Center (25% and 53%). Where cost is published, the middle San Francisco center spent $4,205 per patient (3 centers), against $3,369 at 7 other Bay Area centers.","The State employment department prints a goal and a result for San Francisco on 43 indicator-years; San Francisco fell short of the goal on 15 (35%). In the year July 2025 to June 2026, 10 of 15 indicators met the goal. The widest miss: Youth: Credential attainment rate, July 2025 to June 2026, 8.3% against a goal of 50.0%.","The homelessness department's 2017 and 2023 plans and Public Health's 2022 overdose plan state 38 dated goals and targets; a reported result could be found for 12 of them. Two years into the five-year homelessness plan, the department reports 10,300 people moved into permanent housing against a goal of 30,000 (five-year goal), and 15,186 given prevention help against 18,000 (five-year goal).","The Mayor's budget book for fiscal years 2020 and 2021 printed targets for 127 measures in the departments that fund most nonprofit services. 61 of them (48%) no longer appear under the same name in the Controller's performance dataset. For 15 of those, the department's own projection for fiscal year 2019 was already below that year's target when the book was printed.","The contracts and grant appendices on file for all of the 40 most-paid nonprofits list 2,836 distinct objectives, 1,283 of them with a percentage target. For 16 of those organizations, paid $431.0 million in fiscal year 2026 (a sum that includes money passing through them to others), no result naming them was found in the commission papers, monitoring tables or federal counts on disk. For the other 24 a summary exists (a monitor's score, a share of objectives met, a sentence, or beds occupied on the federal count night) but never the figure achieved against each objective. Of 108 homelessness contract memos that carry both the objectives and a performance history, 17 say whether objectives were met.","Of 30 published benchmarks for supportive housing, rapid rehousing, shelter, treatment, workforce, violence interruption, after-school and meal programmes, a San Francisco figure of the same kind could be found on disk for 6. Home-delivered meals: benchmark $11.06 total (incl. volunteer and donated value); San Francisco $9.44 of City money per meal in fiscal year 2025 (home-delivered and centre meals together; providers add federal, State and donated money on top). Permanent supportive housing / Housing First: benchmark $16,479 median (middle half $13,120 to $26,452); San Francisco $17,000 per unit vs $13,748 for Bay Area tax-credit projects (building operating cost only, services not included). Permanent supportive housing / Housing First: benchmark 86% housed (vs 36% usual care); San Francisco 97% of people in permanent housing stayed or left for other permanent housing (federal fiscal year 2024). For the other 24, including treatment completion and every violence-interruption and after-school measure, nothing published lets San Francisco be compared.","The 60 were paid $7.11 billion from fiscal year 2019 to 2026. 16 carry the verdict “independent measure, target missed” ($2.44 billion); 14 carry the verdict “independent measure, target met” ($1.95 billion); 5 carry the verdict “independent measure, no target” ($537.6 million); 15 carry the verdict “only its own reports” ($1.38 billion); 10 carry the verdict “no result on record” ($804.1 million). For 25 of the 60, paid $2.18 billion (31% of the money), no result recorded by anyone other than the organization itself was found.","Of $2.09 billion paid to nonprofits in fiscal year 2026, $1.37 billion (66%) went to providers for which no provider-level result recorded by anyone else is on disk. Published provider results exist only in Public Health (40% of its payments go to a provider with at least one published review) and Homelessness (78%). 24 departments that paid nonprofits $91.5 million have no City target for those services, no outside count and no provider result at all, led by City Administrator ($29.1 million), Arts Commission ($15.6 million), Sheriff ($12.5 million), Recreation and Parks ($6.9 million).","151 findings from 54 evaluations of 70 San Francisco programmes are on file. 58 findings come from an evaluator independent of the operator, 34 from the operator itself or someone it paid, and for 59 independence is unclear, mostly where the Controller compiles figures the departments report. Few studies have a comparison group, so most say what happened, not what the programme caused.","17 reviews issued between 2018 and 2026 reach the same conclusion from different directions: the City mostly counts activity, not results, and in several departments could not say which providers were effective. None of them found that a programme does not work; they found that the records needed to tell were missing, inconsistent or unverified.","Of 17 objectives in the two final evaluations, 8 were not met, almost met, not reported or had no data. The grant-funded treatment beds ran well below the 90% occupancy target (Cohort 1: 66% against a 90% target; Cohort 2: 47% against a 90% target). A recidivism objective in the first grant was scored 'met' on six people with a prior conviction.","13 of 28 San Francisco results for program years 2023 and 2024 fall in the bottom quarter of California's boards. The youth program reported 14 participants in PY2023 and 208 in PY2024. Lowest ranks: Dislocated Worker PY2023 median quarterly earnings (45 of 45); Youth PY2024 employed 2nd quarter after exit (45 of 46); Youth PY2024 employed 4th quarter after exit (44 of 46).","Shelter: 3,058 of 3,396 beds occupied on 20 November 2026 (91%). Supportive housing: 92% occupied in July-August 2026; the inventory lists 9,649 site-based units, 451 more than the vacancy dashboard counts. Every scorecard target reads 'under review'. The exits page prints the same fiscal-year-to-date total for this year and last.","San Francisco's Continuum of Care awards rose from $30,968,697 in 2015 (59 projects) to $60,766,781 in 2025 (35 projects). Supportive housing's share fell from 87% to 79%. The FY2025 total agrees with the three FY2025 round totals already on disk.","HUD allocated $32,078,940 across four programs in FY2026. The FY2025 CDBG award was $18,798,493. Treasury reported $11,362,066.55 in cumulative outlays for that award when checked on November 27, 2026.","Of the 83 City-paid organisations based outside San Francisco, 34 have direct federal awards (not loans) with award activity in fiscal 2019-2026, $4.02 billion in all. 20 took at least $1 million from both: $3.74 billion federal (most of it to four national research, health and lending bodies with little City money) and $383.8 million from the City in fiscal 2020-2026. The largest overlaps: Seneca Family of Agencies ($86.8 million federal, $74.8 million City); Abode Services ($41.4 million federal, $74.1 million City); Mercy Housing California ($64.7 million federal, $27.1 million City); Native American Health Center ($47.1 million federal, $16.4 million City); Lutheran Social Services of Northern Cal ($11.3 million federal, $30.2 million City). None of the $41.4 million in federal homelessness (Continuum of Care) grants to 3 of these organisations carries San Francisco's continuum code, which is on every one of the City's own 1,047 such award ids.","9 federal awards to 5 of these organisations name San Francisco in their description, $7.8 million in all. Public Health Institute: $2.1 million from the Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration (2017); Public Health Institute: $1.5 million from the National Institutes of Health (2017); Public Health Institute: $994,407 from the Environmental Protection Agency (2021); Main Street Launch: $800,000 from the Administration for Children and Families (2024). These are the awards where a federal and a City grant could pay for the same San Francisco programme; the grant budgets would show whether costs are split.","35 of them took 45 paycheck-protection loans worth $53.0 million (face value). 27 borrowed $150,000 or more; for 8 of those, with $23.2 million in loans, City payments in fiscal 2021 were at least 10% above fiscal 2019. Largest: Seneca Family of Agencies $10.0 million; Bay Area Community Resources $6.1 million; Reading Partners $5.0 million; Playworks Education Energized $4.9 million.","Yes. Of 2,281 award rows the download kept, 33 ($4.9 million) belong to recipients in states where the City-paid organisation has no other rows, mostly small pandemic loans: Family Support Services (SC (4), MD (3), KY (2), AL (2), NJ (2), GA (1)); Fresh Approach (AZ (4), TX (1), FL (1)); Horizon Services, Inc (TX (3), GA (1), OK (1)); Each One Reach One Inc (TX (2), FL (2)). They are left out of every figure here, so the download's own total of $4.08 billion overstates what these organisations received.","The 5 decision texts now on disk cover 5 tracker rows. The two pandemic decisions (May and June 2026) refuse $64,132 for portable toilets and sinks: the City first claimed $127,562, cut that by half on appeal to the units it said stood near testing and vaccination sites, and FEMA found they were rented under the Public Works Pit Stop programme, away from those sites, which had restrooms. The State emergency office declined to support either appeal. The older Rim Fire decision refused $38,147.93 for a power-line survey and insulator cleaning that found no damage; a 2021 overrun appeal was dismissed as filed too early. In all, final denials on file since 2015 total $102,279. One older decision is on file too: on 2006-03-31 FEMA denied a second appeal for $3.8 million over the January 1997 storms (disaster 1155). The City built a $3,743,269 wall instead of the $164,016 slope repair FEMA had approved, asked for the difference almost three and a half years after finishing, and FEMA could not clear the work environmentally after the fact. A changed scope of work and a late request are the reasons given.","8 organizations: $13.3 million of payroll loans forgiven, and $44.6 million of federal emergency money through the City in audit years 2020 to 2022. The largest: Episcopal Community Svcs of SF Inc ($3.4 million forgiven; 2021: Disaster Grants - Public Assistance $22.7 million; 2022: Disaster Grants - Public Assistance $10.4 million); Eviction Defense Collaborative Inc ($507,537 forgiven; 2022: Emergency Rental Assistance Program $5.4 million); Meals On Wheels ($1.4 million forgiven; 2021: Disaster Grants - Public Assistance $2.5 million; 2021: Special Programs for the Aging Title III, Part B $414,104; 2020: Special Programs for the Aging Title III, Part B $34,486).","The housing office's published service records set against its own reports to HUD, and the youth directory, schools list and neighbourhood corridors. One in ten published service records is a second copy; the HUD reports count once.","The Our415 tables can show little about activity. 399 of the 3,281 services with categories have any weekly schedule; 20 schedule rows carry an end date before the day the data were drawn. The 2,363 events come from 2 City departments (SF Rec Park, SF Public Library), not from grantees. The organization and service names are in tables not published here, so no entry can be joined to a grant. The City's contract for the site, 1000033328, was for $224,770; $51,840 was paid against it.","Neither file bears on a grant on its own. The schools list holds 235 schools, 234 active and the rest pending; it has no closed schools to set against program sites. The corridors file maps 25 commercial corridors and was last updated 2016-06-29; it carries no grant or spending.","The Human Rights Commission's adopted budget shows a grant line of $1.55 million for fiscal 2024; the ledger records $19.21 million of grants that year, and $71.7 million against $12.0 million budgeted over fiscal 2019 to 2026. Most of the difference was appropriated as an unitemised 'Programmatic Projects' amount ($13.1 million in fiscal 2024, $35.2 million in 2025) or came from grants received. Public Health budgets its nonprofit work as services, not grants, so its ratio says nothing."],["Every U.S. Attorney announcement since 2015, by subject.","Follow documented refunds, disputed assessments, corrected records and tests that found no charge due.","30 focused reviews compare reported figures to their underlying records and identify the documents that could change each conclusion.","The City lists 165 decisions. The archive review matched 1,260 enforcement-related passages to their records or other dispositions, including 87 distinct Ethics proceedings. Recovered image text matched 42 further proceedings. All 165 listed source documents were located.","The eight 2020 quarterly returns identify 12 exact building-permit numbers. Seven have an issue date, ranging from 2 to 1,087 calendar days. Two permits at 150 Eureka cover different work, a seismic upgrade and a church conversion.","14 follow-ups distinguish confirmed transfers, payments, accounting adjustments and unresolved receipts.","The church conversion at 150 Eureka took 1,087 days. One of 99 other issued permits in its group took at least as long. The same address’s seismic permit took two days, the median among 13,734 comparable over-the-counter permits.","Two state compliance findings, one court-reviewed appeal and a system overrun disclose different consequences. Current workload figures require hearing and waiver records before individual deadlines can be assessed.","Six cases and three school votes now identify the underlying approval, payment evidence or confirmed rate.","121 cases connect source records across stages of a public decision.","The San Cristina rehabilitation was completed. The two FY2020 formula grants were paid to their corrected final amounts.","San Francisco documented an invoicing delay. The customs agreement reached the Board before its tenth anniversary. Hospital collection fees varied by payer.","FY2026 category amounts add to $2,139,566, or 79.2% of the $2.7 million budget. The $437,084 consulting total already includes all seven listed projects.","The published records trace a change to seven-car staffing, a proposed two-year shift in operator procurement, and a full-shift charge for shorter third-party work. Measuring the effects requires payroll, bids and invoices.","The register identifies 165 decisions. In fiscal 2025 its nine settlement rows reconcile to $170,708.99. The annual chart counts a separate merits hearing.","Auditors, grand juries, prosecutors and the City's own watchdogs have already written a great deal down. This page gathers what they found about the organizations and public bodies on this site, and points to the document each time.","1,281 documents were searched for 1,002 names. 323 organizations are named in at least one official document, and 248 in an audit, integrity review, grand-jury report, City Attorney action or prosecutor's record. Documents naming 37 people who appear in case files are indexed too, and are listed on those cases rather than ranked here.","11 organizations paid $1 million or more since FY2019 (together $267 million) are named in the title of, or at least three times in, an audit, integrity review, grand-jury report, City Attorney action or prosecutor's record, and have no case file on this site.","The Controller's program received 817 reports in FY2026, up from 732 in FY2022. It investigated and closed 422; 115 (27%) led a department or contractor to act, the lowest share in the 5 reports. Of the 124 actions that followed, 74 were policy or procedure changes and 38 were personnel actions still pending; 1 employee was terminated, 0 suspended, and no repayment was recorded.","In FY2026, Building Inspection had 26 reports investigated, 7.34 times its share of the City workforce; Homelessness had 14 reports investigated, 4.75 times its share of the City workforce; Human Resources had 5 reports investigated, 1.7 times its share of the City workforce.","16 State Auditor reports issued from 2018 to 2026 examine or draw on San Francisco; 4 list a San Francisco body among those audited.","10 firms or groups on the City ledger appear in 16 Justice Department announcements since 2015. Local matters include the teacher residency program in San Francisco schools and two other federal cases. Tetra Tech EC agreed in 2026 to pay $57.0 million over soil tests at the Hunters Point shipyard (allegations only; the City has paid Tetra Tech companies $582,047 since fiscal 2019), and three San Francisco refuse companies admitted bribery and paid $36.0 million in 2021 (the City has paid Recology companies $114.4 million). The rest concern federal business elsewhere; the largest City payee among them is Kaiser Permanente affiliates ($3.92 billion, 100% of it paid by the Health Service System). Separately, the airport repaid the Federal Aviation Administration $809,554 in 2015 for costs wrongly billed to federal grants.","The auditor gave a clean opinion on the City’s accounts in all 11 years from fiscal 2015 to 2025. The yearly audit reports list 11 findings over the period, 4 on the accounts and 7 on grants; 2 were rated a material weakness, the most serious grade. 6 years had no finding at all (2017, 2019, 2021, 2022, 2024, 2025). The opinion on the use of federal grants was not clean once, in fiscal 2020 (qualified for one programme: Disaster Grants – Public Assistance (Presidentially Declared Disasters)). The accounts were signed off between 141 days (fiscal 2016) and 268 days (fiscal 2018) after the year closed.","11 findings in 11 years. 2 were rated a material weakness: 2016-002 (Special Tests and Provision: Maximum Per-Unit Subsidy and Underwriting Requirements); 2020-002 (Period of Performance). The heading that recurs most is “Procurement and Suspension and Debarment”: findings in fiscal 2015 and 2016, after one under the same heading the year before (2014-004), about different programmes (Port Security Grant Program; Assistance to Firefighters Grant). The following year’s report said corrective action had been taken on 11 of the 11 findings it followed up. 1 finding was a repeat (2015-001, repeating a fiscal 2014 finding the report said had not been fixed). 3 findings give dollar figures under the “questioned costs” heading; the rest say there were none or leave it blank.","Nothing adverse. The 16 reports that examine San Francisco are in the official-documents screen; the remaining 4 that mention the city often are 3 audits of the State Bar, a State body whose head office was in San Francisco, and 1 audit of Anaheim's stadium lease, which uses the Port of San Francisco's lease with the Giants as the better example.","The Ethics Commission opened 713 enforcement matters in fiscal 2022 to 2026 and issued 52 penalties in the same years, about one penalty for every 14 matters opened. The Controller's whistleblower programme received 817 reports in fiscal 2026; of 422 investigations closed, 115 (27%) led a department or contractor to act. The Commission resolved 23 whistleblower-retaliation matters in fiscal 2025 and imposed no penalty in any of them.","16 passages from 14 documents dated 2019 to 2026: the Controller on money raised for departments outside City books and on support organizations' record-keeping; settled cases on a friends-of gift, a commissioner's votes and a measure committee that paid a candidate's campaign; the Ethics Commission on audits of publicly financed campaigns it could not complete; and the yearly counts of non-filers and whistleblower reports.","The minutes record 6 annual audits (2015-16 to 2020-21). One had a finding: 2018-19, a compliance finding on commissioners' conflict-of-interest filings, reported resolved the next year. The others report no findings. The fund balance printed in the minutes was $29.7 million in 2015-16 and $32.8 million in 2018-19, 1.26 times that year's expenditures; the minutes report it rising again in 2019-20 and 2020-21.","The minutes record at least 19 distinct recusals under Government Code 1090 between 2016 and 2022, mostly on budget rows for the commissioner's own department (for example the youth department's IT help desk and the health department's mental-health consultation rows). Nothing here suggests a vote was cast where a recusal was due.","Departments told the Civil Grand Jury they would act on dozens of recommendations and many are still pending years later. The school district budgets deficits its later books do not show, and the Central Subway cost far more and opened years later than its federal grant assumed.","Across 16 grand-jury reports from the 2021-22 to 2024-25 juries, 77 department responses promised to carry out a recommendation at some point. 31 of them were still reported as 'will be implemented in the future' or 'requires further analysis' at the latest report, 15 of them from the 2021-22 to 2023-24 juries, whose reports are at least two years old. Most: Mayor (10), Superintendent of Schools (4), Office of the Mayor (3).","Of 506 responses, the latest answer was 'implemented' for 81, 'will not be implemented' for 66, 'will be implemented in the future' for 33, 'requires further analysis' for 30, and no response required (implemented or abandoned) for 290.","49 of the 66 responses whose latest answer is 'will not be implemented' concern audits, oversight, contracts, performance measures, cost or public reporting: contracts 23, cost 18, performance 14, accountability 9, audit 4, oversight 1.","21 responses in the 2025 column record that the department did not respond: Mayor (16), Office of the Mayor (5).","The meeting record lists 98 items on 92 files; 36 files name a dollar amount, the largest $473.4 million (file 151076). Of 84 recorded action results, 83 were Pass and 1 Fail; 6 files are described as retroactive.","Federal files show what Washington paid to City-funded organisations based outside the city, why FEMA refused City claims, and how two federally funded transit projects slipped. They also show who competed for County Transportation Authority contracts and what federal auditors found at City grantees.","The City subaward file has 1,102 records. 1,102 carry a usable recipient ID and 0 match the national exclusion snapshot. Across the broader San Francisco prime-award extract, 1 recipient record matches and 0 began during a listed exclusion.","3 program-and-finding combinations at 2 recipients meet the screen. PRC’s later audit records its 2022 payroll finding as resolved.","The Planning and Historic Preservation Commission minutes from August 2022 to July 2026, read item by item with who spoke for each project, set against the lobbyist and permit-consultant registries. State wage judgments, the State public-works register, the Airport's posted bids and the State directory of disadvantaged businesses are set against what the City pays.","The Labor Commissioner's judgment search lists 462 judgments from 2015 to November 2026 against defendants with a San Francisco address ($23.1 million in all; 340 open/unpaid, 68 closed-claimant judgment, 41 closed - satisfied, 10 open - partial payment/satisfaction, 1 stayed - bankruptcy, 1 stayed - other, 1 pending/open). 341 name a company; 14 of those companies match by name a City payee since fiscal 2019 or a member of a City contract team, 7 of them with the judgment still shown as open or unpaid; the largest City payee among them is Episcopal Community Services of San Francisco, Inc. ($440.8 million, judgment J-89738, $10,307).","Records saved on 20 November 2026: the Board's behested-payment waivers and their reports, signed gift reports the public list dropped, charity and company registries, and court registers. Each is set against what the City paid and what the existing cases say.","A grand jury indictment was filed on 2025-11-18 (case CRI-25025111) naming Stanley Ellicott with Lanita Henriquez (#25023975) and Dwayne Jones (#25024194) as co-defendants; bail on the 2023 complaints was exonerated on 2025-11-19 (Jones) and 2025-11-20 (Henriquez). Ellicott's 2024 case shows orders paying restitution from frozen accounts at Axos, Fidelity, Green Dot Bank, Patelco Credit Union, Voya Financial (latest 2026-02-19) and an abstract of judgment filed 2026-02-23. Westbrook's register shows 4 entries \"preliminary hearing ... not waived\" (2026-03-09, 2026-04-17, 2026-07-29, 2026-10-21) and no plea; Davis's and Spingola's last minute orders are dated 2026-11-13 and 2026-11-13; the two Providence defendants' last minute orders are dated 2026-11-03. In the City's suit against the Santos firm, an amended judgment after trial was entered on 2026-01-28, the City was awarded attorney fees, the remaining property-owner defendant appealed (A175716), and an abstract of judgment issued to the City on 2026-07-31. The City Attorney's writ petition against the Controller's hearing officer (Collective Impact) was filed 2025-12-24; the real party was served on 2026-07-30, and no hearing or ruling appears.","72 names from the case files were searched in the contract notices and the tax returns. 5 appear at an organization their case does not name, in 11 person-and-organization pairs; 0 pairs carried the same three-part name as the case file; the rest are the name alone.","The City’s own audited schedules say it provided $775.0 million of federal money to subrecipients in fiscal 2021 to 2025 ($158.5 million in 2021; $153.5 million in 2022; $160.2 million in 2023; $135.4 million in 2024; $167.4 million in 2025). The subawards it reported to the federal register with dates in the same five years come to $199.2 million: 26%. For the Continuum of Care homeless-housing programme the schedules show $177.0 million provided to subrecipients and the register shows $1.5 million ($7.5 million counting every City report on file, 2015 to 2021). 13 programmes with $5 million or more provided show no report at all, led by 97.067 Homeland Security Grant Program ($60.9 million); 93.558 Temporary Assistance for Needy Families ($40.4 million); 93.959 Block Grants for Prevention and Treatment of Substance ($36.1 million); 93.658 Foster Care Title IV-E ($30.0 million). Recipients’ own audits record $638.8 million received through the City in the same years, far more than the register. In 6 programmes the recipients’ auditors record more than the City says it provided to subrecipients at all: 93.558 Temporary Assistance for Needy Families ($83.1 million in recipients’ audits, $40.4 million in the City’s); 93.778 Medical Assistance Program ($57.6 million in recipients’ audits, $17.4 million in the City’s); 97.036 Disaster Grants - Public Assistance ($38.3 million in recipients’ audits, $2.1 million in the City’s), which suggests the City treats as contractors bodies whose auditors treat the money as a federal award.","305 companies and nonprofits named as actors in the 128 cases were tested by exact name; 29 have at least one hit. A hit is a place to look and for most of them repeats what the case already says; the \"found\" column lists each one so that the case text can be checked against it.","How often the District Attorney files public-corruption and public-fund fraud charges, how those cases end and how long they take. The charged cases on this site, as the District Attorney's own files record them.","Of 102,791 new criminal cases the District Attorney filed on 2011-2026 arrests, 59 carry a public-corruption charge (misappropriation by an officer, false claim to a public body, bribery of an officer, or a Government Code conflict of interest): 42 from 2011-2019 arrests and 17 from 2020-2026, 12 of them from 2024-2026. Of the 38 resolved, 24 ended in conviction (63%), against 51% of all resolved cases and 63% of felony theft and forgery cases; the median case took 640 days from filing to resolution, against 183 for all cases. 9 are still pending. Fraud on public or insurance funds: 225 cases, 83 of 116 resolved convicted (72%).","Two of the thirteen court numbers are resolved, both as convictions: 22001657 (filed 18 Feb 2022, resolved 29 Sep 2023, 588 days) and 24004990 (filed 21 Mar 2024, State prison sentence 5 Jan 2026, 655 days). Four are pending in the prosecuted file (25028484, 25028488, 26004009, 26007118). 23013637 is listed as a new filing with 23 Government Code 1090 counts and no status. 23013720, 24001435, 25025111 and 26007115 are in neither the prosecuted nor the resolutions file; nor are 25023975 and 25024194, the alternate numbers the court lists for the 2023 co-defendants, which the arrests file marks \"Discharged\" on arrest dates two weeks before the 18 November 2025 indictment. For 26007118 the prosecuted file lists only three perjury counts (118(a)); the arrests file listed 424(a)(1) and three 118(a).","The City's adopted budget set against what its ledger shows was spent, fiscal 2019 to 2026. Overtime, the Sheriff's salaries, the homelessness budget and grant lines are where the two part most.","18 department lines of the operating funds missed their adopted budget by more than 10% over, or 20% under, in at least six of the eight years and by more than $20 million in all. By chance alone a line of at least $5 million is more than 10% over in 14% of years and more than 20% under in 10%. Most of the largest gaps are bookkeeping (debt service, retirement investment fees and court benefits recorded in a different place from where they were budgeted).","In 256 of 268 permit-type years every month's revenue is the trip count times one fee, so the revenue column is computed from the trip counts. The ledger's ground-transport trip fees run above the trip table every year ($4.9 million more in fiscal 2026, taxis left out of both). Shared-ride vans made 445,519 recorded trips with $21 of per-trip revenue. 37 of 1872 active fleet permits have no active vehicle on file.","10 links were re-tested. 4 gain a second key (a contract number or a shared address), 2 are strengthened or narrowed, and the rest are unchanged."]];
